ons.\n\n### 3. Cleaning and Component Care (8.5)\n\nThe general rule for cleaning is **daily cleaning** combined with thorough periodic servicing.\n\n| Component / Area | Cleaning Concern | Action Required | Frequency |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base/Foundation** | Accumulated residue, overflow from filler screw, plane surface. | Thorough cleaning, scrubbing, and removal of all debris. | As needed (due to overflow/residue). |\n| **Fluid Containment** | Residue, contamination, or accumulated particulates. | Flushing and removal of debris. | Daily/As required. |\n| **Fluid Handling** | Potential sources of leaks or improper runoff. | Ensure runoff is directed only to appropriate effluent/collection points. | Ongoing Inspection. |\n\n### 🌐 Maintenance Workflow Diagram\n\n**(Note: As no diagram was provided, this structured flowchart represents the required safety and maintenance sequence.)**\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Identify Need for Maintenance/Cleaning] --> B{Safety Check};\n    B --> C[POWER OFF: Turn off Main Control Power];\n    C --> D[Disassemble/Prepare Component];\n    D --> E[Inspection/Cleaning Process];\n    E --> F{Address Specific Component Needs};\n    F --> G[Reassemble Component];\n    G --> H[Test System Operation];\n    H --> I[Maintenance Complete];\n```\n\n### 4. Key Takeaways and Critical Warnings\n\n*   **Handling Fluid:** While the working fluid itself is not poisonous, **discharged fluid** containing machining chips and waste must be handled carefully. It should never be allowed to flow directly into the sewer or natural outdoor drain.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** Inspection should be comprehensive, covering not just the primary moving parts but also the *support structure* (the base and carrier) for accumulated grime.\n*   **Proactive Cleaning:** Dirt accumulation on the base or carrier can restrict movement and impede clean operation. Daily cleaning is mandatory.\n\n[Page 33]\nThis page is an excerpt from	unknown_source	[]	2026-