Summary)\n\nThe page provides a structured diagnostic flow (Section 9) designed to identify the root cause when the "Rotating head does not work." This process is sequential, meaning one check must be passed before proceeding to the next.\n\n#### ➡️ Troubleshooting Flowchart\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[START: Rotating head does not work] --> B{Is the connector in good contact?};\n    B -- No --> C[Action: Check and fix the motor, driver, and pulse power board connectors.];\n    B -- Yes --> D{Is the indicator light normal?};\n    D -- No --> E[Action: Check if proper power and pulse power board light is on.];\n    D -- Yes --> F{Is the rotary switch on and off normally?};\n    F -- No --> G[Action: Replace the rotary switch.];\n    F -- Yes --> H{Does the motor have a sound?};\n    H -- No --> I[Action: Replace the motor.];\n    H -- Yes --> J{Motor speed is abnormal or stuck?};\n    J -- No --> K[Potential Issues: 1. Connector not in good contact. 2. Motor bearing wear.];\n    J -- Yes --> Z[Resolution: Continue basic inspection/maintenance];\n```\n\n### IV. Key Data and Action Items Table\n\nThis table summarizes the immediate corrective actions taken at each stage of the troubleshooting process.\n\n| Fault Diagnosed | Condition Checked | Root Cause / Necessary Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Connector Contact | Check/fix motor, driver, and pulse power board connectors. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Indicator Light | Confirm proper power and pulse power board illumination. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Rotary Switch Operation | Replace the rotary switch. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Motor Sound/Function | Replace the motor. |\n| **Abnormal Speed/Stuck** | Motor Speed | 1. Check connector contact. **OR** 2. Check motor bearing wear. |\n\n***\n*Note: The instruction for the troubleshooting process explicitly states: "Make sure that the rotary head is clockwise during machining (view from above)."*\n\n[Page 34]\nThis document is a detailed **