ecks (Focus on Steps 2, 4, 5)\n\n*   **Voltage Checks (Servo Motor):** The diagnostic must verify that the voltage provided to the servo motor is within the acceptable operational range.\n*   **Servo Board Check:** If processing is abnormal, the diagnostic requires a thorough check of:\n    *   The motor connector.\n    *   The servo board connector.\n    *   The status of the soldering (must be fixed/secure).\n    *   *Note:* The system indicates that if the servo board detects an issue, the **speeed adjustment button** should not be used for diagnosing, as this may be unrelated to the primary failure.\n*   **Motor Health:** If the motor is hot, it suggests immediate failure: either the motor itself is damaged, or there is a fluid/electrical leakage.\n\n#### 2. Mechanical & Structural Checks (Focus on Steps 1, 3, 6)\n\n*   **Connectors:** The initial check focuses on simple physical connectivity (loose connectors).\n*   **Limit Switch:** If the switch is damaged, it must be repaired or replaced to ensure proper positional feedback to the system.\n*   **Shaft Interferences:** The final mechanical check involves ensuring that the main shaft and all parts surrounding it are free of obstructions or physical binding.\n    *   **Common Causes:** Loose couplings or foreign objects causing friction.\n\n#### 3. Advanced Electrical Troubleshooting (Implied Checks/Data)\n\nWhile not always asked in the flow, the diagram contains specific technical advice that implies necessary knowledge of the system components:\n\n*   **Voltage Specifics:** If the servo board is found to be faulty, the following components and voltages must be checked:\n    *   **DC Voltage:** 32V\n    *   **Components:** Bridge stack, filter capacitor, and voltage transformer.\n    *   **Secondary Check:** The AC output must be checked for proper function.\n\n#### Summary Conclusion\n\nThe diagnostic process is comprehensive, moving logically from simple fixes (loose cables, loose couplings) to complex electrical failures (faulty servo boards, motor leakage). The severity of the required intervention increases as the diagnostic proceeds down the flowchart.\n\n[Page 35]\nThis page is a highly detailed **Troubleshooting and Maintenance Flowchart** for a complex industrial fluid or electro-chemical spray system (likely involving a high-pressure pump and electrode mechanism).\n\nThe purpose of the document is to guide a technician through sequential checks to identify the root cause of system failure, ranging from low fluid levels to component blockages.\n\nSince the source is a flowchart, the summary is presented using a structured table format to replicate the decision-making logic, alongside key warnings and data points.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary: System Troubleshooting Guide\n\n### I. General Operational Warnings and Key Data\n\nThe document emphasizes several critical maintenance actions that must be performed regardless of the specific failure point:\n\n| Area | Action Required | Specification/Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Oil Change** | Pump Oil | Must be changed regularly. |\n| **Pressure Regulator** | Adjustment | Must be adjusted to a set value of **6 MPa**. |\n| **Leaks** | Joints Check | Check all joints for potential water leakage. |\n| **Fluid Addition** | Working Fluid | Use either **tap water** or **distilled water**. |\n\n### II. Troubleshooting Flowchart (	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:35:25	2026-04-24 04:57:35
