d filter. | Blockage is the primary cause. |\n| **Fluid Volume** | Is there enough working fluid? | **Action:** Fill the working fluid. <br> **Check:** Ensure the inlet filter sinks to the bottom of the container. | Insufficient fluid volume. |\n| **Electrode Blockage** | Whether the copper tube electrode hole is blocked? | **Action:** Use special scissors to cut the front of the copper tube electrode. | Electrode tip blockage. |\n\n---\n\n### III. Summary Flow Diagram (Conceptual)\n\nThe entire guide can be visualized as a checklist, moving from electrical/mechanical failures (Motor/Pump) to fluid/system failures (Pressure/Filter/Level).\n\n**START $\\rightarrow$** *Is Fluid Spraying Normally?*\n$\\downarrow$\n**[Electrical/Mechanical Check]** (Motor $\\rightarrow$ Pump $\\rightarrow$ Water Line)\n$\\downarrow$\n**[Performance Check]** (Pressure $\\rightarrow$ Filters $\\rightarrow$ Fluid Level $\\rightarrow$ Electrode)\n$\\downarrow$\n**END / CORRECTIVE ACTION**\n\n### IV. Key Data Table (At-a-Glance Checklist)\n\n| Component | Failure Symptom | Diagnosis / Cause | Recommended Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor** | Failure to run | Blown fuse, broken contactor, broken motor. | Inspect/Replace electrical components. |\n| **Pump** | No rotary sound | Detached key, worn shaft, broken pump. | Check physical connections and mechanical integrity. |\n| **Filter Element** | Low pressure | Blocked filter. | Clean the filter element. |\n| **Working Fluid** | Poor performance | Low fluid level. | Fill the working fluid (use tap or distilled water). |\n| **Electrode Tip** | Poor spray pattern | Blocked electrode hole. | Use special scissors to cut the front of the copper tube electrode. |\n| **System Integrity** | Leakage/Failure | Loose joints or incorrect pressure setting. | Check all joints for leaks. Adjust regulator to **6 MPa**. |\n\n[Page 36]\nThis page details a comprehensive **Troubleshooting Flowchart** used to diagnose the root causes of **Insufficient Processing Performance** in a machining or cutting process. The information is structured as a sequential decision tree, guiding the user through a series of checks until the problem source is identified.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Diagnostic Flowchart: Insufficient Processing Performance\n\nThe diagram outlines eight key sequential checks, starting from the initial observation of poor performance and narrowing down the potential failure points (Workpiece, Fluid, Tooling, Machinery).\n\n### 🔍 Step 1: Initial Checks (Conditions & Workpiece)\n\n| Diagnostic Check | Condition | Action / Conclusion |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Processing Conditions** | $\\text{No}$ (Not Normal) | View the machining parameters to determine the set value. |\n| **Workpiece Appropriateness** | $\\text{Yes}$ (Appropriate) | Proceed to material checks. |\n| **Workpiece Material** | Super Hard | (Implies specific material requirements/wear considerations). |\n\n### 🛠️ Step 2: Operational Components Check (Fluid, Electrode, Discharge)\n\nThis section focuses on the consumables and immediate physical environment.\n\n#### A. Working Fluid Pressure\n*   **Check:** Is the working fluid pressure normal?\n*   **If No (Abnormal):**\n    *   **Action:** Adjust the fluid pressure to the set value.\n    *   **Note:** Potential causes include using tap water or distilled water (indicating fluid	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:35:25	2026-04-24 04:57:35
