l.\n\n***\n*Note: No diagrams were provided on this page; therefore, the summary is presented using structured text and tables to replicate the hierarchical, instructional nature of the original technical document.*\n\n[Page 69]\nThis page provides comprehensive instructions on **Fault Clearance, Safety Protocols, and Troubleshooting** for conveyor systems. Given the nature of the text, the "key data" are contained within the mandatory safety warnings and the sequential troubleshooting steps, which are structured below for maximum clarity.\n\n***\n\n## 🚧 10. Fault Clearance and Safety Instructions\n\nThis section emphasizes that all procedures must be followed diligently to prevent injury and ensure equipment safety.\n\n### I. Mandatory Safety Protocols (The "Must Know")\n\nThe manual utilizes strong warning symbols to categorize hazards, requiring extreme caution:\n\n| Warning Type | Risk Level | Description / Required Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **DANGER** (Red Symbol) | Immediate, Fatal Risk | *Example:* Danger of death due to electric shock. Never bypass protective devices. The machine must be entirely de-energized before service. |\n| **WARNING** (Orange Symbol) | Serious Injury Potential | *Example:* Injury due to human misconduct or lack of qualifications. Never perform servicing unless qualified personnel are present. |\n| **General Safety** | Operational Requirement | Always follow safety instructions in Chapter 3 ("Safety Instructions"). |\n\n### II. General Fault Action Principles\n\n*   **Preparation:** The operator must be fully qualified to address faults.\n*   **Fault Documentation:** Operators are advised to consult the "Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)" page for more detailed troubleshooting guidance.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ Troubleshooting Procedures (Key Data)\n\nThe manual separates fault clearance into two distinct phases: operational fault clearance (10.1) and technical troubleshooting (10.2).\n\n### 10.1. Procedure in Case of Operational Malfunctions\n\nThis sequence outlines the necessary steps for initial, operational fault clearance, requiring caution and documentation:\n\n| Step | Action | Key Requirement / Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.** | Disconnect the conveyor system from the power supply (turn off the main switch). | Essential first step for safety. |\n| **2.** | Secure the conveyor system against restart. | Physical lock-out procedure. |\n| **3.** | Attach warning signs to the conveyor system. | Alerting personnel to the inactive state. |\n| **4.** | Troubleshoot by qualified personnel. | Requires specialized expertise to proceed. |\n| **5.** | Restart with a test run. | Gradual re-energization and testing. |\n| **6.** | Turn over to operating personnel. | Final handover after successful testing. |\n\n### 10.2. Technical Troubleshooting\n\nThis sequence is highly technical and focuses on deeper electrical and system investigation. It requires complete system isolation.\n\n| Step | Action | Critical Focus / Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.** | Disconnect the power supply. | *Repeat disconnection* for thorough safety. |\n| **2.** | Secure against restart. | Maintain physical lock-out. |\n| **3.** | Check that the power is off. | Verification step. |\n| **4.** | Ground and bypass. | Advanced electrical safety procedures. |\n| **5.** | Isolate/cut off adjacent conducting parts. | Prevents residual power from neighboring equipment. |\n| **6.** | Interrupt protective circuits (conveyors and adjacent machines). | Mandatory isolation of protective features. |\n| **7.** | Remedy fault. | Only after all previous steps are completed and the cause is identified. |\n\n***\n\n## 📞 Technical Support and Summary\n\n*   **Manufacturer Contact:** For complex faults or issues that cannot be resolved internally, the operator **must contact the manufacturer** of the conveyor system.\n*   **Key Principle:** Never bypass safety or protective circuits. Always prioritize qualified personnel and adhe