Safety Instructions and Hazard Identification (3.2)\n\nThis section details the inherent dangers and the mandatory use of specialized safety equipment.\n\n#### ⚠️ Safety Icon Legend\n| Icon/Symbol | Meaning | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **🟡 WARNING** | **Caution/Risk Warning** | Indicates a potential hazard (e.g., electrical shock, moving parts). Read the surrounding text for specific actions. |\n| **🔵 CAUTION** | **Procedural Warning** | Indicates that residual energy or system components remain active even after primary power is cut off. |\n| **🔴 DANGER** | **Immediate Danger** | Indicates an immediate threat of severe injury or death (e.g., lifting machinery, structural collapse). Requires immediate stop. |\n| **🛑 EMERGENCY STOP** | **Mandatory Action** | Must be utilized immediately upon sensing an immediate, critical danger. |\n\n#### 🚧 Danger Point: Electrocution and Structural Damage\n*   **Source:** Any electrical circuit, lifting equipment, or structural rigging.\n*   **Hazard:** Accidental physical or electrical injury, or structural collapse.\n*   **Rule:** Never bypass safety mechanisms or modify load-bearing structures.\n\n### IV. Residual Energy and Risk Protocols\n\nThis is the most technical section, detailing the risks associated with various energy sources that persist even after the main power is off.\n\n#### ⚡ Hazard Table: Residual/Stored Energy\nResidual energy must be considered from all potential sources. **Never assume the system is completely inert.**\n\n| Energy Source | Potential Hazard | Example | Critical Safety Protocol |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical** | Electric shock, residual voltage, stored charge. | Capacitor discharge, grounding issues. | Disconnect all power sources and verify zero voltage before touch. |\n| **Pneumatic** | Sudden blast, pressurized gas release. | Compressed air lines, hydraulic lines. | Use appropriate bleed-off procedures and secure lines. |\n| **Hydraulic** | Sudden release of high-pressure fluid. | Hydraulic cylinders, fluid reservoirs. | Pressure must be relieved according to SOP before inspection. |\n\n#### 🚨 Critical Safety Protocols\n1.  **Isolation:** Always follow proper lock-out/tag-out (LOTO) procedures for *all* energy sources before intervention.\n2.  **Energy Release:** If an energy source (pneumatic, hydraulic) is involved, its energy *must* be safely and fully released (bled down) before maintenance.\n3.  **Emergency Action:** In the event of an immediate danger (e.g., crushing, high pressure), the Emergency Stop button must be used immediately, and the area must be isolated.\n\n[Page 11]\nThis page provides critical **Safety Instructions** for operating the Conveyor System, focusing on identifying potential hazards (Emissions and Mechanical Energy) and defining mandatory safety protocols.\n\nSince the original document uses visual cues and mandated lists, the summary is presented using descriptive text, warning symbols (as placeholders for the actual diagrams), and organized tables to capture the detailed information.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Safety Instructions Summary (Page 3)\n\n### I. General Operational Hazards and Warnings\n\n| Hazard Type | ⚠️ Warning Message | Key Action / Precaution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Heat/Thermal** | Danger of injury due to hot surfaces. | Caution must be maintained when handling or operating the machine. |\n| **Falling** | Danger of falling when transporting people. | Ensure operators are trained and follow proper transport protocols. |\n| **Noise** | Risk of injury due to noise. | Mandatory use of **hearing protection** is required at all times. |\n\n### II. Section 3.2.1 Hazards - Emissions\n\nThis section focuses on hazards related to the operation and byproducts of the conveyor system.\n\n*   **Heat/T	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 04:45:31	2026-04-24 04:57:33
