ed Summary: Flat Belt Characteristics\n\n**Context and Purpose:**\nThe sheet serves as a comprehensive reference guide for engineers and purchasers, allowing them to select the correct flat belt (based on part numbers like HBLLT, HBNT, etc.) depending on the required application, operating conditions, material resistance, and required performance metrics (such as friction and tensile strength).\n\n**Format:**\nThe core information is presented in a single, expansive table containing over 12 columns of technical specifications.\n\n### 🔍 Key Data Categories and Parameters\n\nThe technical data can be grouped into the following critical categories:\n\n**1. Product Identification & Use:**\n*   **MISUMI Part Numbers:** Unique identifiers for each specific belt variant.\n*   **Application:** Defines the intended use (e.g., Lifting, All-purpose, Oil-resistant, Smooth operation).\n*   **Material:** Specifies the composition of the belt (Front side and Core side). Common materials include Polyurethane (UP) and various synthetic/rubber compounds.\n\n**2. Physical and Mechanical Properties:**\n*   **Thickness (mm):** The physical gauge of the belt.\n*   **Colour:** The visible color of the belt material.\n*   **Tensile Strength ($\\text{kg/cm}$):** Indicates the maximum force the belt can withstand before breaking. (Generally ranges from 1.5 to 2.5 for listed belts).\n*   **Permissible Stress ($\\text{kg/cm}^2$):** The maximum operating stress the belt can safely endure.\n\n**3. Operational Performance Metrics (Key Data):**\n*   **Min. Curvature Radius (mm):** The smallest radius the belt can bend around while operating safely.\n*   **Damping (g):** A measurement of the belt’s energy absorption capabilities.\n*   **Coefficient of Friction ($\\mu$):** This is a crucial measurement, defining the grip between the belt and the pulley/surface.\n    *   **Coefficient of Friction (polished with):** Friction when the belt surface is polished.\n    *   **Coefficient of Friction (plain side):** Friction when the belt surface is left unpolished (raw).\n\n***\n\n### 💡 Analysis of Belt Variations and Performance\n\nThe data shows distinct variations based on the material and application:\n\n| Application Type | Typical Material | Key Performance Characteristics | Example Part Number |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **General Conveying** | Polyurethane (UP) | Good overall strength and moderate friction (e.g., $\\mu$ of 0.5 to 1.0). | HBLLT, HBLLTWH |\n| **Oil-Resistant** | Specialized Polyurethane | Designed for wet, oily environments. Often maintains strong coefficients of friction even under challenging conditions. | HBLLT/UP (Oil-resistant) |\n| **Smooth Operation** | Polyurethane | Designed to minimize vibration and ensure consistent, quiet movement. | HNSBLT |\n| **High-Grip/Polyurethane** | Polyurethane | Generally shows high friction values (e.g., $\\mu$ of 0.7 to 1.0), making them ideal for traction. | HBNT, HNSB |\n\n**Key Observations:**\n\n1.  **Material Consistency:** Poly	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:23:10	2026-04-24 04:57:36
