 occurs.\n\n| Step | Action | Details / Key Data |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.** | Set the conveyor onto a flat, stable surface. | If necessary, secure the conveyor by fixing it to the floor. |\n| **2.** | Adjust the conveyor horizontally. | Use a level to check the alignment. |\n| **3.** | Loosen the adjustment screws. | Loosen the screws on the pulley on the side of the slippage. If there is slight slippage, tighten the screw. **Caution:** Keep in mind that you need to loosen the screw again if it is too tight and the belt moves in the opposite direction. |\n| **4.** | Observe the belt routing. | Repeat these steps if the slippage is still happening. |\n| **5.** | Tighten the nut. | |\n| **6.** | Operate the conveyor. | Run the conveyor for some time to stabilize the belt routing. |\n| **7.** | Adjust the tension. | Adjust the tension by turning the adjustment screw on the pulley bracket until the previous setting is restored. |\n\n### 🟢 8.3.3 Alignment Correction (Integrated Drive)\n\n**Purpose:** Same as 8.3.2, for models with an integrated drive mechanism.\n\n| Step | Action | Details / Key Data |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.** | Set the conveyor onto a flat, stable surface. | Secure the conveyor by fixing it to the floor if necessary. |\n| **2.** | Adjust the conveyor horizontally. | Use a level to check the alignment. |\n| **3.** | Loosen the adjustment screws. | Loosen the screws on the pulley on the side of the slippage. If there is slight slippage, tighten the screw. **Caution:** Follow the same procedure as 8.3.2. |\n| **4.** | Observe the belt routing. | Repeat the process if there is still slippage. |\n| **5.** | Tighten the nut. | |\n| **6.** | Operate the conveyor. | Run the conveyor for some time to stabilize the belt routing. |\n\n***\n\n## 🔩 Section 8.4: Set-up and Accessories - Tensioning Belts\n\nThis section details the procedure for tensioning the belt, which varies depending on the type of drive mechanism used (Head, Centre, or Integrated).\n\n### **Key Procedure: Tensioning the Belt**\n\nThe general goal is to ensure the belt has the correct tension for proper operation.\n\n#### **Diagrammatic Flow (Drive Type Comparison)**\n\nThe adjustment process involves using the tensioning screw to regulate the belt's tightness around the pulley.\n\n| Drive Type | Procedure Description | Visual Diagram/Key Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Head Drive** | To tension the belt, turn the tensioning screw clockwise until the belt pulls tautly. | **Action:** Turn tensioning screw at the idler pulley (clockwise). |\n| **Centre Drive** | To tension the belt, turn the tensioning screw clockwise until the belt is adequately tight. | **Action:** Turn tensioning screw at the idler pulley (clockwise). |\n| **Integrated Drive** | The process is the same as the Head and Centre drives, requiring secure tensioning at the pulley. | **Action:** Turn tensioning screw at the idler pulley (clockwise). |\n\n***\n\n## ✅ Section 8.5: Recommissioning (Post-Maintenance Check)\n\nRecommissioning is a final checklist ensuring the machine is safe and ready for operation after maintenance or setup.\n\n**Critical Steps Checklist:**\n\n*   **Connection Check:** Check the ground wire connections for tightness.\n*   **Belting/Belt Check:** Install all removed nameplates after	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 04:45:31	2026-04-24 04:57:33
