veyor system. The system is fundamentally designed for the linear transport of **solid materials**.\n\n**Key Principles of Use:**\n1. **Adherence to Specifications:** All operations must strictly follow the manufacturer's specified operating and maintenance protocols.\n2. **Purpose:** The primary function is material transfer (loading, transporting, unloading, etc.) while ensuring the material remains stable throughout the journey.\n\n**Intended Applications (Use Examples):**\n* **Transport:** General linear conveyance of materials.\n* **Handling:** Loading, unloading, moving materials (within specified limits).\n* **Maintenance:** The systems are designed to facilitate proper inspections and routine maintenance tasks.\n\n### II. 🔴 Section 1.3: Improper Use (What MUST NOT be done)\n\nThis section outlines severe prohibitions to maintain safety and system integrity. The primary rule is to **never exceed the operational limits** specified by the manufacturer.\n\n**Prohibited Practices (Do Not Use For):**\n| Category | Restrictions | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Material Composition** | Liquids, gases, or powders (unless specifically approved). | Risk of leakage, spillage, or clogging; damages the system. |\n| **Chemical/Energy** | Flammable materials, explosives, or highly reactive substances. | Extreme safety hazard (Fire/Explosion risk). |\n| **Mechanical Limits** | Overloading, exceeding maximum speeds, or using materials that bind/jam. | Risk of equipment failure, overheating, or entanglement. |\n| **Modification** | Any alteration to the core system mechanics or electrical components without written permission. | Compromises safety and structural integrity. |\n\n### III. ⚠️ Critical Safety Warnings and Identification Protocols\n\nThe document places extreme emphasis on safety and unauthorized changes, providing four distinct warning types.\n\n#### 🚨 WARNING: General Operational Safety\n\n*   **Personnel Requirement:** Only highly trained and authorize