ety & Technical Data (Section 4.1)\n\n#### A. Safety Instructions\nUsers are instructed to follow all safety instructions detailed in **Chapter 3, "Safety Instructions."**\n\n#### B. Key Technical Specifications (Table Data)\n\n| Parameter | Specification |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Rating** | 230 V |\n| **Tolerance** | $\\pm 10 \\%$ |\n| **Frequency** | 50 Hz |\n| **Motor Protection** | Includes multiple combinations (e.g., 60 W / 1 A, 250 W / 0.6 A, 400 W / 3 A, 600 W / 1 A, etc.) |\n| **Connection Type** | Fixed connection |\n| **Electromagnetic Compatibility**| Compliant with the EMC Directive and applicable EMC standards. |\n\n### II. ⚙️ Drive Motor Installation Options (Section 4.2.1)\n\nThe system offers three distinct methods for installing the drive motor unit, depending on the required layout and structural design:\n\n| Installation Option | Description | Visual Representation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Head Drive** | The drive unit is mounted at the start (head) of the conveyor line. | (Diagram showing the drive unit at one end.) |\n| **Centre Drive** | The drive unit is positioned in the middle (center) of the conveyor line. | (Diagram showing the drive unit in the middle.) |\n| **Integrated Drive** | The drive unit is fully incorporated or integrated within the main structure of the conveyor. | (Diagram showing the drive unit integrated into the framework.) |\n\n### III. 🔗 Conveyor System Components (Section 4.2.2)\n\n#### A. Conveyor System Types\nThe system can be configured using three main types of conveyors:\n1. **Belt conveyor:** Used for carrying goods via a continuous belt.\n2. **Chain conveyor:** Used when robust, high-load transport is needed (often utilizing a chain system).\n3. **Timing belt conveyor:** Used for precision timing and transport.\n\n**Common Components:**\nThe system assembly consists of the following components:\n*   A drive unit\n*   Idler pulley\n*   Roller unit\n*   Conveyor belt\n\n#### B. Mechanical Structure Diagram\nA schematic diagram illustrates the mechanical relationship between the rollers and pulleys that drive the belt.\n\n#### C. Special Features Key (Legend)\nThe following table defines the numbering system used in the mechanical structure diagram, detailing the function of each part:\n\n| No. | Special Feature | Component Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective cover | Shields mechanical elements. |\n| **2** | Frame | The main structural support for the unit. |\n| **3** | Drive guard | A safety barrier for the drive mechanism. |\n| **4** | Conveyor belt | The primary material transport surface. |\n| **5** | Drive unit | The motor and gearbox assembly (the driving power source). |\n| **6** | Roller unit | The support rollers that guide the belt. |\n\n***\n*(Note: The text also mentions that conveyor systems can be run by motor circuits, electric controllers, or other advanced control methods.)*\n\n[Page 14]\nThis page provides detailed technical information on two primary types of conveyor systems: **Chain Conveyors** and **Timing Belt Conveyors**, along with general notes on system configurations and accessories.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ I. 4.2.3 Chain Conveyor\n\nThis section details the structure and operational features of a heavy-duty chain conveyor.\n\n### A. Mechanical Structure (Diagram and Key)\n\nThe diagram illustrates a cross-section of the conveyor components.\n\n| Component No. | Component Name | Description |\n| :---: | :	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:23:10	2026-04-24 04:57:36
