 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(1) Placement Ground** | Must be structurally robust. | The foundation must be capable of withstanding the entire weight of the machine. |\n| **(2) Vibration Countermeasure** | Must ensure stability during operation. | Place the machine on a stable ground that is free of vibrations. If adjacent machines cause vibration, install a **rubber buffer** system. |\n| **(3) Environment** | Must be stable and protected. | The ideal location requires: <ul><li>Minimal temperature fluctuation.</li><li>Low levels of dust or debris (garbage).</li><li>Protection from direct sunlight.</li></ul> |\n| **(4) Level Adjustment** | Must ensure optimal operational alignment. | Use the adjustable feet system (as described in Section 6) to guarantee the machine is perfectly level. |\n\n***\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaways (Summary Points)\n\n*   **Stability is Paramount:** The most emphasized safety and operational concern is maintaining a stable foundation, free from vibration and able to handle the machine's full weight.\n*   **Accessibility:** The design incorporates dedicated points for **forklift loading and unloading**, making logistics integral to the setup.\n*   **Precision Setup:** The machine cannot be operated until the adjustable feet have been properly set to achieve perfect level and alignment.\n*   **Proactive Mitigation:** Users are instructed to proactively mitigate environmental risks (temperature swings, dust, external vibrations) to prevent operational failure and maintain accuracy.\n\n[Page 12]\nThis page provides detailed operational guidelines, technical specifications, and schematic diagrams for setting up and running a machine, focusing specifically on the Water Circulation and Water Pump systems.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including key data, diagrams, and technical tables.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Operational Guidelines and Setup\n\n### A. Initial Adjustment Procedure\nWhen performing the horizontal adjustment, the following steps are mandatory	unk