et is ready (checking required permissions/permits). | **Inspection / Documents** | Ensure operational clearance is granted. |\n| **3. System Voltage Check (c)** | Use the voltmeter to ensure the voltage meets all machine system requirements. | **Voltmeter** | Prevent system damage due to low/high voltage. |\n| **4. Activation (d)** | Release the emergency stop switch. | **Physical Action** | Energize the machine safely after all checks are complete. |\n\n### 🟢 Phase 3: Operational and Phase Sequencing Checks\n\nOnce power is applied, functional checks must confirm correct movement and power distribution.\n\n#### 1. Phase Sequence and Direction Check\nThis is a critical step to ensure the machine operates correctly and safely.\n\n*   **Procedure:** Before the machine is fully energized, verify the rotational direction of the secondary stroke (W-axis).\n*   **Test:** The technician must click the **UP button** and physically move the **W axis up**.\n*   **Goal:** Confirm the movement direction and operational range.\n\n#### 2. Power Phase Sequence Confirmation\nThe machine must be confirmed to run using the correct electrical phase sequence.\n\n*   **Verification:** The proper **three-phase power supply** must be confirmed (JOG the DW button and move the W axis down).\n*   **Outcome:** Upon successful completion of all steps, the machine display will show the initial coordinates and operation panel indicators.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Simulated Flow Diagram: Startup Process\n\nThis diagram summarizes the sequence, ensuring that electrical checks precede physical activation.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Start: Initial Inspection] --> B{Electrical Checks};\n    B --> B1[Check Resistance (Ohmmeter): Motors, Grounds, Short Circuits];\n    B --> B2[Check Voltage (Voltmeter): Mains Switch, System Requirements];\n    B2 --> C[Confirm Power Supply Cabinet Readiness];\n    C --> D[Release E-Stop Switch];\n    D --> E{Functional Checks};\n    E --> E1[Verify Phase Sequence: 3-Phase Power Check (W-Axis Down)];\n    E --> E2[Verify Direction: UP button / W-Axis Up];\n    E2 --> F[System Ready: Display Initial Coordinates];\n```\n\n[Page 17]\nThis document appears to be an excerpt from a technical troubleshooting and maintenance manual, providing instructions for resolving operational issues related to machinery axes (specifically the W-axis and U/V axes) and proper power sequence checking.\n\nSince the source material is text-only, the summary will use highly structured formatting (tables, detailed lists, and bullet points) to simulate diagrams and organize the key technical data.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary and Analysis of Technical Procedures\n\n### I. 🚨 Troubleshooting Guide (Power and Movement Issues)\n\nThe text addresses two main operational failures: the inability to move the UP button and the requirement to check the power phase sequence.\n\n#### 1. Why Can't I Press the UP Button from the Beginning?\n\n| Cause | Description | Solution/Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **W-axis Support Blockage** | The W-axis may have a support pad located in the lower part of the W-axis. | **Check/Clear:** Ensure the W-axis support pad is functioning correctly. |\n| **Functionality Issue** | The system requires the three-phase power supply to be connected, and the limit switch must be activated/working. | **Power & Safety Check:** Verify the three-phase power and confirm the status of the limit switches. |\n\n#### 2. Why Check the Power Phase Sequence?\n\n| Condition/Failure | Importance | Root Cause Check |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Three-Phase Power Supply** | Improper sequence can damage components and cause failure. | **Check Sequence:** Verify the phase sequence of the three-phase power supply. |\n|	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:35:25	2026-04-24 04:57:35
