equence adjustments.\n2.  **Re-locking:**\n    *   Use the Allen key to **re-fix the X-axis left-hand pallet sheet metal fixing screw**.\n3.  **Testing the Function:**\n    *   **Goal:** To confirm proper operation after reassembly.\n    *   **Action:** After the phase sequence adjustment of the three-phase power supply, **please debug the limit switch** again to work normally.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Key Data and Summary Table\n\n| Component/System | Function/Role | Critical Check Point | Failure Implication |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Three-Phase Power** | Provides necessary power for the W-axis and related movements. | **Phase Sequence** (Crucial) | Incorrect sequence leads to erratic or failed movement. |\n| **W-axis Support Pad** | Supports the W-axis in its lower part. | **Clearance/Physical Integrity** | Prevents the UP button from being activated/moving. |\n| **Limit Switch** | Safety and positional feedback mechanism. | **Functionality/Debugging** | Must be confirmed working after any major maintenance/power adjustment. |\n| **Locking Plate/Screw** | Secures the X-axis carriage base to the machine structure. | **Tightness/Fixing** | Must be properly reinstalled and secured to ensure stability. |\n\n### ⭐ Overall Summary Takeaways\n\n1.  **Prioritize Power:** All movement issues are fundamentally linked to the **three-phase power supply** and its **phase sequence**. This must be checked first.\n2.  **Sequential Process:** Maintenance (like removing/replacing the locking plate) must follow a strict sequence (Power Off $\\rightarrow$ Unlock $\\rightarrow$ Remove $\\rightarrow$ Adjust $\\rightarrow$ Re-lock $\\rightarrow$ Test).\n3.  **Troubleshooting Flow:** If the axis is non-responsive, check for physical blockage (support pads, switches). If the axis moves incorrectly, check the electrical input (power sequence).\n\n[Page 18]\nThis page describes the structure and function of the **Electrical Control System** used in machinery, detailing two primary subsystems: the Pulse Power Supply and the Spindle Servo System.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including structured components, a functional diagram, and key data points.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Summary: Electrical Control System\n\n**System Overview:** The electrical control system is the operational brain of the machine. It is responsible for controlling the movement, power distribution, and performance of the main components, including the rotary head device, rectifier unit, and the spindle.\n\n### I. Key System Components (The Parts)\n\n| Component | Function | Primary Role |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rectifier Unit** | Converts incoming electrical power to usable forms for the system. | Power Conditioning |\n| **Pulse Power Supply** | Generates and manages precise electrical pulses (pulses and pulse channels) necessary for operation. | Pulse Generation / Actuation |\n| **Machine Tool / Spindle** | The mechanical end effector that performs the work. | Work Execution |\n| **Spindle Servo System** | Provides precise positional control and feedback, ensuring the tool operates at the correct speed and position. | Feedback and Positioning Control |\n| **Rotation Control** | Manages the rotational movement and coordinates the input to the Pulse Power Supply. | Control Management |\n| **Water Pump** | Likely a cooling or auxiliary system controlled by the main power flow. | Auxiliary Operation |\n\n### II. System Flow Diagram (Functional Mapping)\n\nThe diagram illustrates a sequential and feedback-driven operational loop:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[MACHINE TOOL] --> B[RECTIFIER UNIT];\n    B -->|Selection/Manual Adjustment| C{PULSE POWER SUPPLY};\n    C --> D[WATER P	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 03:35:25	2026-04-24 04:57:35
