rt format, designed to diagnose operational issues related to a high-pressure fluid system (likely involving a working fluid, pump, and electric motor).\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, organized by diagnostic phase, using tables and structured text for maximum clarity.\n\n---\n\n## 🔧 Detailed Troubleshooting Summary\n\nThe guide initiates when the primary symptom is observed: **The working fluid is not normally sprayed.**\n\n### I. Initial System Checks (Motor and Pump)\n\nThe guide first verifies the mechanical integrity of the system components.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Rotation** | Is the motor rotating normally? | If NO: 1. The fuse is burnt out. 2. The contactor is broken. 3. The motor is broken. |\n| **Pump Sound** | Does the pump have a rotary sound? | If NO: 1. The shaft connection key is detached or worn seriously. 2. The pump is broken. |\n| **Pump Maintenance** | *(General Advice)* | **⚠️ Important:** Please don't forget to change the pump oil regularly. |\n\n### II. Operational Line Checks (Water and Pressure)\n\nThese steps verify the status of the fluid lines and required pressure levels.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **High Pressure Line** | Is the high pressure water line normal? | If NO: Check the joints for water leakage. |\n| **Pressure Output** | Does the working fluid pressure reach the set pressure? | If NO: 1. Adjust the built-in and external pressure regulator to the set value of **6.0 MPa**. 2. Working fluid with tap water or distilled water. |\n\n### III. Filtration and Fluid Supply Checks\n\nThese sections focus on the blockages and availability of the fluid.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inlet Filter** | Whether the inlet pipe filter element are blocked? | If YES: Clean the filter and filter. |\n| **Working Fluid Level** | Is there enough working fluid? | If NO: 1. Fill the working fluid. 2. Whether the inlet filter sinks to the bottom of the container. |\n\n### IV. Final Component Check (Electrode)\n\nThe final operational check focuses on the primary spraying component.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Hole** | Whether the copper tube electrode hole is blocked? | If YES: Special scissors cut the front of the copper tube electrode. |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Summary Flowchart Diagram (Conceptual)\n\nThis structure represents the troubleshooting logic flow:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Start: Working Fluid is Not Normally Sprayed] --> B{Is the motor rotating normally?};\n    B -- No --> B1(Check: Fuse, Contactor, Motor);\n    B -- Yes --> C{Does the pump have a rotary sound?};\n    C -- No --> C1(Check: Shaft Key, Pump);\n    C -- Yes --> D{Is the high pressure water line normal?};\n    D -- No --> D1(Action: Check joints for leakage);\n    D -- Yes --> E{Does the working fluid pressure reach set pressure?};\n    E -- No --> E1(Action: Adjust regulator to 6.0 MPa);\n    E -- Yes --> F{Are the inlet pipe filter element blocked?};\n    F -- Yes --> F1(Action: Clean filter);\n    F -- No --> G{Is there enough working fluid?};\n    G -- No --> G	unknown_source	[]	2026-04-24 04:57:38	2026-04-24 04:57:38
