te the system.\n\n***\n\n## 🔌 1. Summary of the Electrical Wiring Diagrams\n\n### A. 10. ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM (System Overview)\n\nThis diagram provides a high-level view of the system's power flow, demonstrating how external power sources are routed through protective and switching elements before reaching the operational components and the main output point (Outlet).\n\n**Key Data Flow:**\n1. **Input Power:** The diagram shows the connection of primary AC/DC power sources.\n2. **Protection & Switching:** This power passes through various relays and contactors, suggesting necessary switching points and overload protection.\n3. **Distribution:** Power is distributed to the main control panel (the central junction point) and the final output load (Outlet).\n\n### B. Panel Wiring Diagram (Detailed Internal Control)\n\nThis is the core functional diagram. It maps the signal and power paths from the initial inputs (sensors/transducers) through signal conditioning (Amplifier) to the final control and output mechanisms.\n\n**System Flow Summary:**\n* **Input Stage:** Signals originate from modules like the Transducer and the Vibrating Plate.\n* **Processing Stage:** The raw signals pass through the **Amplifier board** for conditioning and signal strength adjustment.\n* **Control Stage:** The processed signals interact with the main control circuitry (via relays and dedicated wiring terminals).\n* **Output/Power Stage:** The control logic drives the **Transformer** and ultimately the main **Outlet**, completing the circuit.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ 2. Component Breakdown and Interconnections\n\nThe system relies on several distinct modules that manage power, signal, and control.\n\n### Table 1: Key Components and Functions\n\n| Component/Module | Function | Role in System |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Transformer** | Steps down/up AC voltage; provides isolation for sensitive electronics. | Converts and regulates power for control circuitry. |\n| **Transducer Board** | Convert