doc-0f69b918186f85ee57eae1d2c37947bf	default	unknown_source	[Page 1]\nThis document is the cover page for the **Operating Instructions for MISUMI Conveyor Systems**. As a summary of this introductory page, it establishes the scope of the manual, provides necessary safety and revision warnings, and showcases the vast range of products covered.\n\nDue to the nature of the image being a title page, the summary focuses on the structural elements, scope, and critical warnings included.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Detailed Summary of Operating Instructions\n\n### 1. Document Overview\n*   **Title:** Operating Instructions for Conveyor Systems\n*   **Manufacturer/Brand:** MISUMI\n*   **Language:** English\n*   **Date:** December 2022\n*   **Purpose:** To provide comprehensive guidance for the safe and correct operation, installation, and maintenance of various automated conveyor systems.\n\n### 2. Product Scope and Visual Representation (Diagrammatic Summary)\n\nThe cover page features multiple diagrams showcasing the breadth of conveyor types covered by the manual. These visuals illustrate the modular nature of the systems and the variety of applications.\n\n**Key Conveyor System Types Illustrated:**\n\n| Product Type | Description | Application Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mini/Micro Conveyors** | Small, box-like, or slide-based units. | Handling small components, indexing, or assembly lines. |\n| **Belt Conveyors** | Flat, linear systems designed for smooth, continuous movement. | Transporting materials over short to medium distances. |\n| **Sectional Conveyors** | Multiple modular sections that can be combined (shown in varying lengths and heights). | Customizable production lines and sorting mechanisms. |\n| **Sliding/Linear Conveyors** | Units that facilitate unidirectional, straight-line movement. | Guiding components through processing stations. |\n| **Overall Function** | The diagrams collectively demonstrate a comprehensive industrial automation solution for material handling. |\n\n### 3. Key Operational Mandates and Safety Warnings (Data Points)\n\nThe page prominently features several critical warnings and disclaimers, which serve as mandatory guidelines for the user before starting operation.\n\n#### ⚠️ Mandatory Safety and Operational Instructions\n\n| Warning/Instruction | Required Action | Priority Level |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Before First Commissioning** | Carefully read *all* operating instructions. | Critical |\n| **Safety Adherence** | Observe all mandated safety instructions. | Critical |\n| **Information Retention** | Keep the manual in a safe place for future reference. | High |\n\n#### 📜 Revision and Translation Notice (Key Data)\n\n| Category | Detail Provided | Implication for User |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Information Status** | The information is subject to change without notice. | Users must verify the latest version and check for revisions. |\n| **Document Nature** | This document is a **translation** of the original operating instruction. | Users should be aware that minor technical details may reflect the source language. |\n| **Date Stamp** | December 2022 | Establishes the baseline date of the manual's content. |\n\n---\n***In summary, this document serves as a comprehensive, safety-focused guide for integrating and operating MISUMI's diverse range of conveyor systems, emphasizing that rigorous adherence to instructions and safety protocols is mandatory before any operational use.***\n\n[Page 2]\nThis document is a comprehensive legal and operational manual preface, typically found in a technical product guide. Its primary purpose is to establish the legal compliance framework, define the scope of use, assign safety responsibilities to the user, and protect the intellectual property of MISUMI.\n\nGiven that the source material is purely text, diagrams and visuals cannot be provided. However, the summary is organized using tables and bulleted lists to present the key data and legal points in the most structured manner possible.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Detailed Summary of the Operating Manual\n\n### 1. Purpose and Scope of the Manual (Operational Mandate)\n\nThe manual serves as the primary source of technical documentation for the machinery. Its comprehensive reading is mandatory for safe and correct operation.\n\n*   **Comprehensive Understanding:** The user must read and understand the **entire** manual, including all accompanying documents, before operating the machinery.\n*   **Updates:** The user is responsible for ensuring the machine remains safe by updating their knowledge based on the manual’s recommendations and safety alerts.\n*   **Safety Priority:** Operating the equipment requires constant awareness of the safety standards detailed within the manual.\n\n### 2. Compliance and Technical Standards (Key Data)\n\nThe machinery and its documentation are guaranteed to adhere to international standards, ensuring quality and safety.\n\n| Standard Body | Standard Description | Compliance Scope |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **IEC (International Electrotechnical Commission)** | Technical documentation standards. | Core technical aspects of the machine. |\n| **ISO (International Organization for Standardization)** | Quality and operational standards. | Operational best practices and safety protocols. |\n| **IEC 2006/22/C and 2008** | Specific guidelines governing operation and safety. | Ensures compliance with international electrical and mechanical standards. |\n| **Safety Standards** | Manual of Machinery (MISUMI) and IEC standards. | Defines the safety parameters and permissible use environment. |\n\n***Note:*** *The manual confirms compliance with numerous specific standards, ensuring that the product meets global requirements (e.g., related to mechanical, electrical, and operational safety).*\n\n### 3. Legal Disclaimers and Limitations (Critical Warnings)\n\nThese sections contain critical warnings that define the legal relationship between the manufacturer (MISUMI) and the user.\n\n#### A. Liability Disclaimer (No Fault/No Guarantee)\n*   **Assumption of Risk:** By operating the machine, the user assumes full responsibility for the proper handling, usage, and maintenance.\n*   **No Liability Clause:** MISUMI disclaims any and all liability for damages, loss, injury, or death resulting from:\n    1.  Misuse of the machinery.\n    2.  Ignoring the manual's safety instructions.\n    3.  Failure to follow prescribed operating procedures.\n*   **Mandate to Verify:** Users must verify the suitability and integrity of the machinery components and the operating environment before use.\n\n#### B. Intellectual Property and Confidentiality (Copyright)\n*   **Ownership:** All content, including technical information, diagrams, text, and formatting, is protected by copyright and belongs solely to MISUMI.\n*   **Usage Restriction:** The manual cannot be reproduced, distributed, or copied without the express written permission of the copyright holder.\n*   **Legal Status:** This manual is strictly for educational and operational use and should not be viewed as a warranty or binding legal contract beyond the stated terms.\n\n### 4. Summary of Key Responsibilities (Checklist)\n\nThe user's responsibilities are summarized into four non-negotiable actions:\n\n1.  **Read Thoroughly:** Read and understand every section of the manual.\n2.  **Maintain Safety:** Regularly review and implement safety practices to maintain the machine's safe operational status.\n3.  **Adhere to Limits:** Never operate the machinery outside the technical specifications and limitations outlined in the manual.\n4.  **Report Changes:** Ensure that all modifications or operational changes are documented and approved according to the technical standards provided.\n\n---\n### 🔑 Key Data Snapshot\n\n| Element | Detail | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Manufacturer** | MISUMI | The entity responsible for the product and documentation. |\n| **Date of Issue** | December 2022 | Indicates the current revision date and knowledge cutoff for the guidelines. |\n| **Core Mandate** | Full Manual Adherence | Operation is contingent upon the user's demonstrated understanding of the entire document. |\n| **Legal Protection** | Copyrighted Content | Reproduction or unauthorized sharing of the manual is prohibited. |\n| **Liability Stance** | Limited/Disclaimer | Manufacturer is not liable for damages resulting from user error or non-adherence to safety protocols. |\n\n[Page 3]\nThis document provides comprehensive guidelines covering safety hazard communication, operational compliance, and required personnel competence for industrial equipment (likely conveyor systems or related machinery).\n\nHere is a detailed, structured summary of the page:\n\n---\n\n## 📘 I. Safety Hazard Communication (Pictograms & Signal Words)\n\nThe document defines standardized signal words and pictograms used to communicate potential risks.\n\n### A. Signal Word Definitions and Associated Risks\n\n| Signal Word | Pictogram/Symbol | Hazard Level | Explanation (Meaning) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Danger** | ⚠️ (Triangle with exclamation mark) | Highest | Indicates a severe hazard that **must** be avoided. |\n| **Warning** | ⚠️ (Triangle with exclamation mark) | High | Indicates a hazard that could cause injury if ignored. |\n| **Caution** | ⚠️ (Triangle with exclamation mark) | Medium | Indicates a hazard that could cause injury if not acted upon. |\n| **Attention** | ⚠️ (Triangle with exclamation mark) | Low | Indicates a potential hazard requiring awareness. |\n| **Important** | (Various) | General | Provides essential operational or safety information. |\n\n*(**Note:** The document specifies that "The combination of a pictogram and a signal word classifies the respective safety information. The pictogram may vary according to the type of danger depicted.")*\n\n### B. Detailed Signal Word Use\n\n| Signal Word | Visual Cue | Primary Use / Implication |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Danger** | **RED/BLACK** | Used when death or irreversible health injuries can occur (e.g., exposed electrical components, severe mechanical crushing). |\n| **Warning** | **ORANGE/YELLOW** | Used when death or serious injury can occur (e.g., moving parts, hot surfaces). |\n| **Caution** | **YELLOW/AMBER** | Used when injury can occur if the hazard is not recognized or avoided (e.g., slip hazards, minor contact points). |\n| **Attention** | **YELLOW** | Used to draw general notice to a potential hazard or procedure. |\n| **Important** | (N/A) | Used for critical procedural information or mandatory operational steps. |\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ II. Operational Roles and Competence\n\nThe document outlines the specific training, knowledge, and responsibilities required for different personnel groups interacting with the equipment.\n\n### A. Target Personnel Groups\n\nThe equipment manual is addressed to personnel who must be fully competent in the following areas:\n\n*   **Operators:** Individuals who use the equipment daily.\n*   **Specialist Staff:** Personnel with deep technical knowledge (maintenance, repair, complex diagnosis).\n*   **Inspectors:** Staff responsible for routine safety checks and compliance.\n\n### B. Areas of Responsibility (Competence Required)\n\n| Area | Key Responsibilities / Skills | Relevant Personnel |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Transport** | Loading, unloading, maneuvering the equipment safely. | Operators, Riggers |\n| **Assembly / Installation** | Mounting, positioning, and ensuring correct structural stability. | Specialist Staff |\n| **Inspection / Commissioning** | Conducting pre-use safety checks, verifying alignment, and testing functionality. | Inspectors, Specialist Staff |\n| **Operating / Decommissioning** | Safe startup, routine use, and planned shutdown procedures. | Operators |\n| **Maintenance / Repairs** | Diagnosing faults, performing routine servicing, and executing complex repairs. | Specialist Staff |\n| **Troubleshooting** | Identifying the root cause of operational failures and implementing corrective actions. | Specialist Staff |\n\n---\n\n## 📜 III. Regulatory Compliance and Legal Notices\n\nThese sections provide critical warnings regarding the equipment's conformity, use, and disposal.\n\n### A. EU / UK Conformity Declaration (Key Data)\n\n*   **Status:** The equipment is declared compliant with the **EU Declaration of Conformity** and the **UK Declaration of Conformity**.\n*   **System Scope:** The compliance applies to the conveyor system in the specified configuration.\n*   **Critical Warning (Do Not Use):** The declaration *does not* cover or imply conformity for:\n    *   Any modification of the system after initial compliance.\n    *   Use of components or accessories not supplied by the manufacturer.\n    *   Use in circumstances deviating from the original operational context (e.g., extreme environments).\n\n### B. Specific Usage Limitations\n\n*   **MISMUI:** Requires users to ensure that the conveyor system is used **only** in conjunction with the listed CE Marking and EU Declaration of Conformity.\n*   **Mandatory Checks:** Personnel must visually inspect the system and all components before operation.\n\n### C. Archiving and Documentation\n\n*   **Procedure:** The operating manual and technical documentation must be retained.\n*   **Duration:** Retention must be maintained **for at least 10 years** after the equipment is decommissioned or retired.\n*   **Accessibility:** All instructions must always be readily available to the user.\n\n---\n\n## ✨ Summary Key Takeaways (Bullet Points)\n\n*   **Safety First:** All operations must strictly adhere to the safety hierarchy defined by **Danger $\\rightarrow$ Warning $\\rightarrow$ Caution $\\rightarrow$ Attention**.\n*   **Professional Responsibility:** Operation and maintenance require defined competency in specific areas (e.g., assembly, troubleshooting, inspection).\n*   **Legal Compliance:** The system is certified (EU/UK), but user modifications or use outside the specified parameters **void** this compliance.\n*   **Record Keeping:** All manuals and documentation must be kept on file for a minimum of 10 years.\n\n[Page 4]\nBased on the image provided, which is a Table of Contents (ToC), this document is a highly structured, detailed technical manual or operational guide, likely related to industrial machinery, specifically a conveyor system.\n\nSince only the Table of Contents is provided, the summary details the **scope, structure, and key data points** of the manual, rather than the content itself.\n\n---\n\n## 📖 Detailed Summary of the Document Scope\n\nThis manual is comprehensive, covering every stage of the product lifecycle—from basic identification and general specifications through detailed safety protocols, installation, routine operation, and final shutdown. It is organized into seven major sections, demonstrating a logical progression from theory to practical application.\n\n### 📊 Key Data Table (Structure and Page Mapping)\n\n| Section | Title | Sub-Sections Covered | Page Range | Primary Focus |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.** | **Identification** | Model numbers, intended use, general specifications. | 23 – 24 | Defining the scope and identity of the product. |\n| **2.** | **General Information** | Operational parameters, definitions, and technical specifications. | 25 – 28 | Understanding the system's rules, limitations, and basic components. |\n| **3.** | **Safety Instructions** | Electrical, mechanical, and environmental precautions. | 29 – 31 | Critical safety guidelines for handling and maintenance. |\n| **4.** | **Installation and Operation** | Mechanical fitting, electrical connections, and operational theory. | 32 – 41 | Hands-on guidance for setup and day-to-day use. |\n| **5.** | **Troubleshooting** | Identifying and resolving common operational issues. | 42 – 43 | Diagnostic flowcharts and corrective actions. |\n| **6.** | **Operation** | Running procedures, emergency stops, and system shutdowns. | 52 – 53 | Step-by-step guidance for running and securing the system. |\n| **7.** | **Shut-down** | Procedures for controlled power-off and securing the unit. | 53 | Final, controlled decommissioning steps. |\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Thematic Breakdown and Core Content Areas\n\n#### 1. Foundational Understanding (Sections 1 & 2)\nThis initial phase establishes the "what" and "how much" of the system.\n\n*   **Identification (Sec 1):** Provides essential metadata, including Model Number (1.1) and the specific application or Intended Use (1.2), ensuring the user understands the exact piece of equipment they are operating.\n*   **General Information (Sec 2):** Delves into the core rules. It defines the **Objectives of the Operating Manual** (2.1) and lists necessary parameters (2.2, 2.3), setting the stage for safe and effective operation.\n\n#### 2. Safety and Compliance (Section 3)\nThis section is the most critical, dedicating significant space to preventing accidents. It treats safety as a non-negotiable prerequisite for all further procedures.\n\n*   **Key Data Focus:** Detailed protocols are provided for **Electrical Safety** (3.1), **Mechanical Hazards** (3.2), and **Environmental/Physical Safety** (3.3).\n*   **Scope:** Users will learn about specific warnings (e.g., guarding requirements, voltage checks) before touching any components.\n\n#### 3. Installation and Technical Setup (Section 4)\nThis section acts as the technical blueprint, guiding physical assembly and connection.\n\n*   **Flow Diagram/Process:** It starts with **Mechanical Assembly** (4.1, 4.2), detailing procedures like belt alignment and motor mounting.\n*   **Connectivity Focus:** It then moves to **Electrical Integration** (4.3), covering connection methods and wiring best practices.\n*   **Theoretical Knowledge:** Sections like "Motor Manufacturer A..." (4.3.5) suggest the manual covers details specific to the major components used.\n\n#### 4. Operational Procedures and Maintenance (Sections 5 & 6)\nThese sections transition the user from *assembly* to *use*.\n\n*   **Troubleshooting (Sec 5):** This functions as a diagnostic guide, allowing users to follow a structured path (e.g., "Is it running?") to identify faults (5.1, 5.2).\n*   **Operation (Sec 6):** Provides the step-by-step command for running the machine. This includes detailing the **emergency stop procedure** (6.2) and the normal operational cycle.\n\n#### 5. System Shutdown (Section 7)\nThe manual concludes with a detailed **Shut-down** process (7.0), ensuring the equipment is secured, powered down, and properly maintained after use, preventing accidents or damage during inactivity.\n\n***\n### 🔑 Summary of Key Data and Concepts\n\n*   **Primary Subject:** Technical operation and maintenance of industrial conveyor systems.\n*   **Core Principle:** Safety is paramount (Section 3 must be followed before Sections 4 or 6).\n*   **Key Operational Flow:** Identification $\\rightarrow$ Installation $\\rightarrow$ Operation $\\rightarrow$ Troubleshooting $\\rightarrow$ Shutdown.\n*   **Technical Depth:** The manual covers both high-level electrical schematics (wiring, voltage) and low-level mechanical detail (alignment, specific components like "Belt Plate" or "Drive Motor").\n\n[Page 5]\nBased on the image provided, the page is a comprehensive **Table of Contents** for a technical manual or user guide published by MISUMI. Since the image only contains the index and not the actual content, the summary below details the **structure, scope, and key data** of the manual rather than summarizing the text itself.\n\n***\n\n## 📘 Summary of Document Structure (MISUMI Manual)\n\nThis manual is highly organized, covering everything from basic product descriptions and setup to advanced maintenance, troubleshooting, and safety compliance. The content is divided into major thematic sections, suggesting a complete guide for technical professionals or end-users.\n\n### 📊 Key Data Table: Table of Contents Overview\n\nThe following table organizes the chapters, their main topics, and the page ranges they cover:\n\n| Section | Title / Subject Area | Key Topics Covered | Page Range |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **7.0** | **(Components/Setup)** | Components of the system, basic setup, installation steps. | 53–61 |\n| **8.0** | **(Product Specifics)** | Detailed descriptions of various mechanical components (e.g., belt, chains, guide rails, spacers). | 53–70 |\n| **9.0** | **(Operation & Service)** | Operating procedures, maintenance, error handling, and compliance information. | 61–70 |\n| **10.0** | **(Safety & Maintenance)** | Critical safety information, warning details, and specific disposal/installation checks. | 65–70 |\n\n***\n\n### 📜 Detailed Thematic Summary\n\nThe manual can be broken down into five core functional areas:\n\n#### 1. System Setup and Integration (Chapter 7)\nThis section details the foundational aspects of implementing the system.\n*   **Focus:** Mechanical installation and basic operational components.\n*   **Topics:** Includes steps for switching the conveyor system, adjusting the machine (e.g., to a standstill), and the initial disposal/handling of the machine.\n\n#### 2. Mechanical Components and Product Catalog (Chapter 8)\nThis is the most extensive section, functioning as a detailed component guide, breaking down every type of hardware used.\n*   **Focus:** Specific product types and associated hardware specifications.\n*   **Key Component Categories:**\n    *   **Belts/Chains:** Covers belt and chain types (e.g., Belt carriage, Belt guide) and specific applications (e.g., Guide roller, Spacers).\n    *   **Rails/Guides:** Detailed sections for guide rails, guide rollers, and specific rail components.\n    *   **Misc. Hardware:** Includes topics like transfer rollers and plastic covers.\n\n#### 3. Operation, Maintenance, and Troubleshooting (Chapter 9)\nThis section guides the user through routine operations and problem resolution.\n*   **Focus:** Day-to-day running, upkeep, and procedural knowledge.\n*   **Key Areas:**\n    *   **Operation:** How to run the system safely and correctly (e.g., Cleaning of the conveyor system).\n    *   **Maintenance:** General upkeep advice, including general maintenance and component service.\n    *   **Error Handling:** Addresses "Troubleshooting" and provides answers to "Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)."\n\n#### 4. Safety and Compliance (Chapter 10)\nThis crucial section addresses the physical safety and regulatory aspects of the equipment.\n*   **Focus:** Safety precautions and regulatory compliance.\n*   **Key Areas:**\n    *   **Safety:** Details required safety procedures (e.g., General Safety, Functioning in Case of Operational Malfunctions).\n    *   **Compliance:** Contains sections verifying compliance with international standards (EU Declaration of Conformity, UKCA Declaration of Conformity).\n\n#### 5. Supporting Information (Chapter 8.7)\nThis final subsection is reserved for specialized, general components like spare parts, ensuring the user knows where to source replacements.\n\n***\n\n### 🔑 Summary of Key Data Points\n\n*   **Primary Focus:** The guide covers the entire lifecycle of the system: **Setup (7.0) → Installation/Parts (8.0) → Operation (9.0) → Safety/Compliance (10.0).**\n*   **Critical Documentation:** The manual provides verification for international safety standards, specifically noting the **EU Declaration of Conformity** and **UKCA Declaration of Conformity**.\n*   **High Detail Level:** The granular breakdown of components (e.g., 8.2.1 Belt carriage, 8.2.2 Belt guide, 8.3.1 Alignment corrector) indicates a highly specialized, technical audience.\n\n[Page 6]\nThis document provides a highly detailed technical guide for identifying and structuring product codes for Misumi conveyor systems. The core function of the page is to translate an alphanumeric product code into specific technical parameters, such as dimensions, power, and included components.\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, incorporating the key tables and data presented on the page.\n\n***\n\n## 📑 Summary of Misumi Conveyor System Identification\n\n### 1. Product Identification Code Structure (The Key Data)\n\nThe technical data relies on a standardized product code format, which acts as a condensed technical diagram. The general structure is:\n\n$$\\text{XXXX} - \\text{100} - \\text{20} - \\text{25} - \\text{TA230} - \\text{IM} - \\text{25} - \\text{H} - \\text{R}$$\n\nEach segment of this code corresponds to a specific physical or electrical component of the conveyor system:\n\n| Code Segment | Parameter Represented | Description / Range |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **XXXX** | **Type Designation** | Defines the main type of the conveyor system. |\n| **100** | **Width (W)** | Standardized width measurement (in mm). |\n| **20** | **Length (L)** | Standardized length measurement (in mm). |\n| **25** | **Motor Output** | Represents the motor's output capacity (e.g., 25 units). |\n| **TA230** | **Operating Voltage** | Specifies the required voltage for operation. |\n| **IM** | **Motor Power Input** | Indicates the motor's power input capacity. |\n| **25** | **Gear Ratio** | Specifies the gearing ratio of the system. |\n| **H** | **Belt Specification** | Designates the type or specification of the conveyor belt. |\n| **R** | **Motor Manufacturer** | Identifies the manufacturer of the motor used in the unit. |\n\n***\n\n### 2. Classification of Conveyor Systems (Model Tables)\n\nThe page details different configurations by providing distinct product codes for various system types. The codes are consistently used across all tables, but the selected segment values change based on the required functionality (e.g., whether a drive is included, or whether a belt is needed).\n\n#### A. Conveyor System (Complete)\nThis category represents the full, ready-to-use system.\n\n*   **Example Code:** `SVKAE-100-20-50-TA230-IM-25-H-R`\n*   **Key Data:**\n    *   **System Status:** Complete unit (including drive and belt).\n    *   **Components:** Width (100 mm), Length (20 mm), Motor Output (50 units), Operating Voltage (TA230), Gear Ratio (25), Belt Specification (H), Motor Manufacturer (R).\n\n#### B. Conveyor System Without Drive\nThis configuration is used when the main driving mechanism is separate or not included in the primary product code.\n\n*   **Example Code:** `SVKAE-100-50-25-TA230-IM-25-H-B`\n*   **Key Data:**\n    *   **System Status:** Conveyor structure only (No drive included).\n    *   **Components:** Width (100 mm), Length (50 mm), Motor Output (25 units), Operating Voltage (TA230), Gear Ratio (25), Belt Specification (H), Motor Manufacturer (B).\n\n#### C. Conveyor System Without Belt\nThis configuration is for the structural base or frame, where the belt component is omitted.\n\n*   **Example Code:** `SVKAE-100-50-25-TA230-IM-25-H-B`\n*   **Key Data:**\n    *   **System Status:** Structure without the belt element.\n    *   **Components:** Width (100 mm), Length (50 mm), Motor Output (25 units), Operating Voltage (TA230), Gear Ratio (25), Belt Specification (H), Motor Manufacturer (B).\n\n***\n\n### 3. Key Functionality Summary Table\n\nThis table summarizes the changes in the code structure and what they signify in practical terms:\n\n| Feature / Function | Segment Code Affected | Meaning | Implication |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **System Completeness** | `XXXX` (Type Designation) | Differentiates if the system is "Complete," "Without Drive," or "Without Belt." | Crucial for purchasing the correct assembly level. |\n| **Belt Presence** | `H` (Belt Specification) | Specifies if a belt is required or omitted. | Determines the physical components shipped with the system. |\n| **Motor Power** | `25` (Motor Output) | Indicates the motor's power rating. | Directly related to the load capacity of the conveyor. |\n| **Voltage** | `TA230` (Operating Voltage) | Specifies the electrical operating voltage. | Ensures compatibility with local power infrastructure. |\n\n### Summary Conclusion\n\nThe page acts as a comprehensive dictionary for specifying complex industrial machinery. By breaking the product into nine distinct code segments, Misumi allows engineers and purchasers to precisely order a conveyor system, detailing everything from its physical dimensions (Width/Length) and required power (Voltage/Motor Output) to its included components (Belt/Drive status).\n\n[Page 7]\nThis document section details the mandatory safety guidelines, operational parameters, and strict usage limitations for the conveyor system.\n\nSince the source material is purely text-based (procedural manual writing), there are no diagrams or images to include. However, the critical data and instructions have been compiled into structured tables for maximum clarity and retention of the key rules.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Conveyor System Operational Guidelines\n\nThe guidelines are structured into three main areas: mandatory safety rules, permitted operations (Intended Use), and strictly prohibited operations (Improper Use).\n\n### 1. ⚠️ Critical Safety and Identification Requirements (Mandatory Rules)\n\nThe manual emphasizes that operational safety relies heavily on proper identification, training, and adherence to procedures.\n\n| Category | Key Rule / Guideline | Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Identification** | Operators must be trained and qualified. | Only use the system with proper documentation and after following the operating manual. |\n| **Routine Inspection** | Ongoing safety and maintenance checks are mandatory. | Perform routine inspections and strictly follow safety/operational procedures. |\n| **Power Disconnection** | Never service the machine when live. | Always **disconnect the system from power** before carrying out any maintenance or inspection. |\n| **Unauthorized Modifications** | Any change must be documented and authorized. | **Do not** perform modifications or adjustments without consulting and receiving signed documents from MISUMI Europa GmbH. |\n| **Improper Use Warning** | Deviation from guidelines creates risks. | Users must be trained, inspect the machine, and strictly adhere to the manual at all times. |\n\n### 2. ✅ Section 1.2: Intended Use (What the System CAN Do)\n\nThe conveyor system is designed for specialized material handling and transporting various grades of bulk and packaged goods, provided it is used within specified parameters.\n\n#### A. Material Handling Capacities:\n*   **Solid Materials:** Transport of solids in a defined direction.\n*   **Bulk Materials:** Handling of bagged goods, pallets, and various other materials (e.g., specific grades of cement).\n*   **Inspection:** The system is used for transferring materials between defined handling points (e.g., conveyor to storage area).\n\n#### B. Operational Compliance:\n*   The system must be used in accordance with all safety and operating instructions.\n*   Personnel must ensure the machine is operating within its documented service limits.\n\n### 3. 🚫 Section 1.3: Improper Use (What the System CANNOT Do)\n\nViolation of these rules voids safety protections and is strictly prohibited.\n\n#### A. Modifications and Reconstruction:\n*   **Prohibited:** Any self-initiated modification, adjustment, or reconstruction of the conveyor system.\n*   **Requirement:** All modifications must be documented and executed by qualified personnel in conjunction with **MISUMI Europa GmbH**.\n\n#### B. Operational Scope Limitations:\n*   **Forbidden:** Using the system for unintended purposes, such as:\n    *   Transporting people or animals.\n    *   Transporting items other than the specified bulk or bagged goods (e.g., hazardous materials, explosives, radioactive items).\n    *   Operating the system when safety mechanisms are bypassed or disabled.\n\n#### C. Maintenance and Compliance:\n*   **Forbidden:** Operating the system without consulting the official manual or without performing routine inspections.\n\n***\n\n### 📊 Key Data Summary Table: DOs vs. DON'Ts\n\n| Category | DO (Permitted Actions) | DON'T (Prohibited Actions) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Usage** | Transport specified bulk and bagged goods in a defined direction. | Transport people, animals, or explosive/hazardous materials. |\n| **Maintenance** | Perform routine inspections and follow safety procedures. | Bypass safety mechanisms or operate without official training/manual. |\n| **Alterations** | Follow all instructions in the operating manual. | Make any modification or reconstruction without written approval from MISUMI Europa GmbH. |\n| **Power** | (N/A) | Operate the system without ensuring the main power is disconnected during service. |\n\n[Page 8]\nThis document provides a detailed summary of the operational guidelines, safety mandates, and interpretation of warning signals for the conveyor system, as drawn from the sections dedicated to General Information and Safety Symbols.\n\n***\n\n## 📜 Overview of Operational Guidelines and Liability\n\n### 2.1 Guarantee and Liability\nThe manual emphasizes that the safe use and proper operation of the conveyor system are contingent upon the user adhering strictly to the provided guidelines.\n\n**Key Liability Exclusions:**\nLiability and warranty are immediately voided if the system is subjected to:\n1.  Improper use or handling.\n2.  Ignoring warnings and signs.\n3.  Unauthorized modifications, maintenance, or repairs.\n4.  Failure to consult the complete manual prior to operation.\n\n### 2.2 Objectives of the Operating Manual\nThe manual serves as the authoritative resource, providing all necessary information for the safe, reliable, and optimal operation of the conveyor. Users are strictly instructed to consult this manual *before* commencing any operational procedures.\n\n***\n\n## ⚠️ 2.3 Signs and Symbols: Interpreting Safety Mandates\n\nThe core of the manual details the mandatory interpretation of various safety signals. Attention must be paid to the specific type of warning signal to understand the level of danger and the required action.\n\n### A. 2.3.1 Mandatory Safety Signs\nThese signs indicate required actions or protective equipment that must be utilized to ensure safe operation.\n\n| Symbol/Area | Instruction/Requirement | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Prophylactic Clothing** | Requires appropriate protective garments. | Personal protective equipment (PPE). |\n| **Headgear Use** | Mandates the use of a protective head covering. | Protection from falling objects. |\n| **Use Safety Shoes** | Requires the wearing of protective footwear. | Protection for feet/ankles. |\n| **Eye Protection** | Mandates the use of safety eyewear. | Protection from flying debris. |\n| **No Smoking/Open Flame** | Indicates that combustion sources are strictly prohibited. | Fire prevention. |\n\n### B. 2.3.2 Hazard Signs\nThese signs warn of specific physical dangers present in the working environment, requiring immediate caution.\n\n| Symbol/Hazard | Description of Danger | Symbol/Hazard | Description of Danger |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical Voltage** | Danger associated with high-voltage electricity. | **Danger of Falling** | Risk of injury from objects/people falling. |\n| **Danger of Tripping** | Risk of stumbling or tripping over obstacles. | **Danger of Oil/Grease** | Risk of slip or fire due to fluids. |\n| **Danger of Machinery** | Warning regarding moving or operating equipment. | **High Temperature** | Warning of heat exposure or burn risk. |\n| **Danger of Falling Objects** | Warning of falling debris or materials. | **Live Electrical** | Warning regarding active electrical components. |\n\n### C. 2.3.3 Prohibition Safety Signs\nThese signs explicitly forbid certain actions or entries into restricted areas.\n\n| Symbol/Action | Prohibition | Symbol/Action | Prohibition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Unauthorized Access** | Entry is strictly forbidden without permission. | **Smoking** | Open flames and combustion sources are prohibited. |\n| **No Dirt/Oil** | The area must be kept clear of contaminants. | **Dirt/Oil** | Warning against depositing waste materials. |\n| **No Eating** | Consumption of food is forbidden in the area. | **Restricted Area** | Defines an area where public access is barred. |\n\n### D. 2.3.4 Instruction Signs\nThese signs give mandatory directions or alerts regarding the general environment.\n\n| Symbol/Instruction | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Dangerous Environment** | Indicates an area with inherent risks or unstable conditions. |\n| **Attention/Be Careful** | Directs attention to a potential hazard or point of interest. |\n\n***\n\n## 💡 Summary of Safety Signal Categories (Key Data)\n\nThe system utilizes a structured hierarchy of signals, forcing the operator to recognize the severity of the warning:\n\n*   **DANGER:** Imminent danger; immediate hazard exists.\n*   **WARNING:** Potential danger; accident possible if precautions are not taken.\n*   **CAUTION:** Potential injury; hazard requires attention.\n\n**Mandatory Actions:** The safety signs function as critical guides, ensuring the operator understands not just *what* the hazard is (e.g., voltage), but also *how* to mitigate it (e.g., required PPE, prohibited actions).\n\n[Page 9]\nThis document provides a detailed set of safety instructions and operational obligations for the use of conveyor systems. The summary is structured using tables and headings to categorize the critical data and warnings presented on the page.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Safety Instructions: Overview and Scope\n\nThe purpose of this section (3. Safety Instructions) is to establish the mandatory rules, obligations, and necessary precautions to ensure the safe operation of conveyor systems.\n\n### 📋 3.1.1 Obligations (Mandatory Responsibilities)\n\nThe manual explicitly outlines the duties of the user/operator. These obligations are non-negotiable and relate to ensuring the machine is operated only by trained personnel and under controlled conditions.\n\n**Key Obligations for Personnel:**\n\n*   **Operation Scope:** Only trained and authorized personnel must operate the equipment.\n*   **Maintenance/Inspection:** Operators must perform daily checks and report any anomalies immediately.\n*   **Hazard Control:** Users must strictly follow all safety instructions, manuals, and training protocols.\n*   **Procedural Compliance:** All work must be performed in a safe manner, adhering to designated work procedures.\n\n***\n\n## ⚠️ Critical Safety Warnings and Dangers\n\nThe manual uses specific hazard warnings (indicated by yellow triangular symbols) to alert users to potential critical failures and the severe consequences of non-compliance.\n\n### Table 1: Danger Assessment and Consequences\n\n| Hazard Area | Danger Description | Key Consequence / Risk | Mitigation / Required Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **General Operation** | Failure to observe safety instructions. | Injury, damage, or death. | Strict adherence to all procedures and manuals. |\n| **Machine Handling** | Operating the machine when improperly handled or configured. | Severe injury or death. | Only qualified personnel are permitted to operate the machine. |\n| **System Malfunction** | Operating the system while neglecting required maintenance or procedures. | Injury or fatality. | Mandatory pre-operation inspections and adherence to established protocols. |\n\n***\n\n## ⚖️ Detailed Operational Obligations (Action Checklist)\n\nThis section details specific duties that personnel must perform to ensure a safe working environment.\n\n### Table 2: User Duties and Compliance Checklist\n\n| Category | Required Action / Obligation | Details |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Personnel Certification** | Only trained personnel may operate the machine. | Operators must be aware of the latest safety features and protocols. |\n| **Procedural Adherence** | Follow established procedures meticulously. | Never bypass safety mechanisms (e.g., emergency stops, guards). |\n| **Documentation** | Keep records of maintenance and inspection. | All deviations must be logged and reported. |\n| **Emergency Response** | Be prepared for immediate response. | Know the location and use of emergency shutdown procedures. |\n| **Physical Safety** | Maintain clear physical movement around the machine. | Do not place objects or personnel where they interfere with movement. |\n\n***\n\n## ⚡ Potential System Hazards (The Five Main Risks)\n\nThe manual concludes by summarizing the primary physical and mechanical risks associated with the operation of conveyor systems. Understanding these risks is critical for prevention.\n\n### Table 3: Summary of Potential Operational Hazards\n\n| Hazard Category | Detailed Risk Description | Potential Outcome |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Component Failure** | Failure of essential functional parts (e.g., motor, belt, gear). | Loss of power, uncontrolled movement, or mechanical seizure. |\n| **Entanglement** | Entrapment of personnel due to moving parts. | Severe crushing or amputation injuries. |\n| **Electrical Danger** | Contact with live electrical parts or systems. | Electrocution and severe shock hazard. |\n| **Operational Negligence** | Failure to follow mandated maintenance or safety procedures. | System malfunction leading to runaway or uncontrolled movement. |\n| **Physical Interference** | Obstruction or placement of foreign materials (e.g., debris) on the belt/mechanism. | Overheating, system jamming, or sudden stopping. |\n\n[Page 10]\nThis page segment is an extremely critical section of a technical manual, focusing entirely on **Safety Instructions, Operational Warnings, and Hazard Management** for conveyor systems. The content is structured as mandatory warnings that must be strictly followed to prevent severe injury, death, and equipment damage.\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, structured with tables, key data, and diagrams for maximum clarity.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Conveyor System Safety and Operation\n\n### I. 🚨 Core Operational Warnings (The "Mandatory Checks")\n\nThe manual repeatedly stresses that the machine cannot be operated unless all preparatory checks have been completed and safety protocols are followed.\n\n#### Table 1: Critical Operational Risks (Must Be Checked Before Use)\n\n| Warning Type | Condition/Risk | Required Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belting/Components** | If belts are missing or damaged. | **DO NOT OPERATE.** Failure to use belt guard is also warned. |\n| **Machine Integrity** | If there is an improper condition or visible damage to the machinery. | Stop operation and report for immediate maintenance/repair. |\n| **Power Source** | If any power, electrical, or control system component is damaged or if power has not been fully isolated. | **NEVER** operate. Wait for authorized technical staff. |\n| **Starting/Restarting** | Attempting to start the machine when power is not fully disconnected, or if the machine is damaged. | **DO NOT ATTEMPT.** Power must be locked out by authorized personnel. |\n\n### II. 🔌 Safety Instructions and Hazard Management (Section 3.2)\n\nThis section details the multi-faceted risks associated with the equipment and mandates strict protocols for maintenance and shutdown.\n\n#### A. Managing Residual Energy and Hazards\n\nResidual risks are hazards that persist even after the machine has been physically shut down. All residual risks must be managed.\n\n**Key Data:** The following types of energy must be accounted for and neutralized before maintenance.\n\n| Hazard Type | Description | Safety Protocol Requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical Energy** | Stored charge, capacitors, or residual current. | Must be verified as **Zero Voltage** using appropriate testing equipment. |\n| **Mechanical Energy** | Kinetic energy (moving parts, belts, rollers). | Must be physically restrained (locked out) to prevent unexpected motion. |\n| **Thermal Energy** | Hot surfaces, overheated components. | Must be allowed to cool completely before touch. |\n| **Potential/Stored Energy** | Pressure buildup (hydraulics, pneumatics), stored electrical charges, etc. | Must be safely released and discharged according to procedure. |\n\n#### B. De-energization Protocol (Lockout/Tagout)**\n\nThe highest priority safety rule is **total power isolation**.\n\n*   **Source:** All power sources (electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, etc.) must be physically isolated and locked out.\n*   **Procedure:** Ensure that the conveyor system is completely de-energized. Personnel must verify that the power cannot be restored accidentally.\n*   **Mandate:** Only trained and authorized personnel are allowed to perform this lockout/tagout procedure.\n\n### III. 📋 Summary of Key Data and Warnings\n\n#### Table 2: Comprehensive Danger Zones and Precautions\n\n| Danger Point | Hazard/Risk | Consequence of Failure | Mandatory Precaution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mechanical** | Exposed belts, rotating shafts, nip points. | Crushing, entanglement, amputation. | Must be guarded; never reach into operational areas. |\n| **Electrical** | High voltage components, capacitors, exposed wiring. | Electric shock, electrocution, fire. | Must be de-energized (lockout) and tested for zero voltage. |\n| **Residual Energy** | Stored mechanical/electrical pressure (pneumatics, hydraulics). | Unexpected movement, release of force. | Must be bled/released and physically locked out. |\n| **Operation** | Lack of proper training or bypassing safety features. | Severe injury or death. | Adhere strictly to the operation manual and only use trained staff. |\n\n### IV. 🔄 Conceptual Diagram: Maintenance/Repair Sequence\n\nThe safety instructions dictate a mandatory linear process before any work can begin.\n\n$$\\text{Operational State} \\rightarrow \\text{STOP} \\rightarrow \\text{ISOLATE} \\rightarrow \\text{VERIFY} \\rightarrow \\text{MAINTAIN} \\rightarrow \\text{RESUME}$$\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1.  **STOP:** Initiate emergency shutdown.\n2.  **ISOLATE:** Lock out **ALL** power sources (electrical, mechanical, pneumatic).\n3.  **VERIFY (Test):** Confirm that all stored energy (voltage, pressure) is zero.\n4.  **MAINTAIN:** Perform inspection, repair, or adjustment only by authorized personnel.\n5.  **RESUME:** Following successful repair and re-energization, the system can be safely brought back online.\n\n---\n### Key Takeaway Summary\n\nThe entire document emphasizes that operating or maintaining the conveyor system is a **high-risk activity**. Safety protocols are absolute and must follow the "Zero Energy State" principle: **No work begins until all forms of power (electrical, mechanical, thermal, and stored) are physically isolated, locked out, and verified as zero.** Failure to adhere to these procedures results in extreme danger to life.\n\n[Page 11]\nThis page provides comprehensive **Safety Instructions** for operating and maintaining **Conveyor Systems**. The content is highly structured, dividing potential risks into specific hazard categories (Emissions and Mechanical Energy).\n\nBecause the source material is purely text warnings, the summary utilizes detailed tables and bullet points to simulate the organization of diagrams and key data points, ensuring maximum detail.\n\n---\n\n# ⚙️ Conveyor Systems: Safety Instructions (Section 3)\n\n## ⚠️ I. Overview of Hazards\n\nThe manual outlines warnings across three primary hazard types:\n1.  **Emissions Hazards:** Risks related to airborne or emitted substances (heat, noise).\n2.  **Mechanical Energy Hazards:** Risks associated with moving parts and physical forces (crushing, entanglement).\n3.  **Operational/Personnel Hazards:** Risks related to general usage, access, and setup (falling objects, improper handling).\n\n## 📊 II. Hazard-Specific Warnings and Protocols\n\nThe following table summarizes the key warnings, the associated risks, and the mandatory safety protocols.\n\n| Hazard Category | Source Section | Key Risks Identified | Mandatory Safety Protocols / Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Heat & Emissions** | 3.1 | **Hot Surfaces:** Danger of contact burns. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Maintain safe distance. Always wear appropriate protective clothing. |\n| **Noise** | 3.1 | **Noise Exposure:** Risk of hearing injury. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Follow all operational instructions. Must wear specialized hearing protection. |\n| **Mechanical (General)** | 3.2 | **Entanglement/Crushing:** Fingers, clothing, or body parts can be pulled in or crushed. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Keep hands and body parts clear of moving elements. **Wear protective clothing.** |\n| **Material/Load** | 3.2 | **Falling Objects:** Materials or loads can drop unexpectedly. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Do not reach over or under the conveyor. Use proper controls. |\n| **Power/Start-up** | 3.2 | **Operation:** High risk if improperly handled. | 🔑 **Key Personnel:** Only qualified personnel may operate the system. **Wear protective equipment.** |\n| **Falling Debris** | General | **Overhead:** Danger from falling materials or tools. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Prohibit unauthorized access beneath the conveyor. **Wear protective equipment.** |\n| **Access/Trip** | General | **Floor Hazards:** Slips, trips, or obstructions in the work area. | ⚠️ **Warning:** Keep the area clear of clutter. **Wear protective equipment.** |\n| **Operation/Personnel** | General | **Load/Unload:** Injuries during material transfer. | 🔑 **Personnel Requirement:** Only trained and authorized personnel may load or unload. The area must be secured. |\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ III. Key Data and Operational Compliance\n\nThese bullet points summarize the critical rules regarding who can operate the system and the equipment necessary for safety.\n\n### 👷 Personnel Restrictions and Training\n*   **Authorized Personnel:** Operation and maintenance are strictly limited to **qualified and trained personnel**.\n*   **System Access:** Unauthorized individuals are prohibited from operating the machine or accessing restricted areas.\n*   **Supervision:** Work must be carried out under appropriate supervision.\n\n### 🧤 Required Protective Equipment (PPE)\nPPE is required for nearly every type of hazard identified:\n*   **Hearing Protection:** Required due to potential excessive noise levels.\n*   **Eye/Hand Protection:** Required when operating, handling loads, or working near moving machinery.\n*   **Protective Clothing:** Required to prevent entanglement with moving parts.\n\n### 🛑 Critical Operational Directives (The "Do Not" List)\n*   **Do Not Reach Over:** Never reach over, under, or around the conveyor system.\n*   **Do Not Remove Guards:** Never bypass or remove safety guards or safety interlocks.\n*   **Do Not Adjust Without Power:** Do not adjust or repair the system without first following lockout/tagout procedures.\n*   **Do Not Operate Blindly:** Never assume the system is safe if the power is on; always follow established start-up procedures.\n\n---\n\n## 📘 IV. Summary of Safety Principles (The 3 Pillars)\n\nThe entire manual emphasizes three overarching safety principles:\n\n1.  **Awareness:** Users must be aware of all potential hazards (heat, noise, crushing, falling objects).\n2.  **Prevention:** Hazards must be mitigated by maintaining a safe distance, clearing the work area, and following machine guidelines.\n3.  **Protection:** Mandatory use of appropriate Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) is required for all users.\n\n[Page 12]\nBased on the provided page excerpt, here is a detailed summary of the safety instructions regarding Emergency Situations.\n\n---\n\n## 📘 Summary of Safety Instructions: Emergency Situations (Section 3.3)\n\nThis section provides critical, mandatory guidelines for responding to accidents, especially involving fires, emphasizing immediate safety actions and proper firefighting techniques.\n\n### I. ⚠️ General Safety Directives (Important)\n\nThese are overarching rules that must be observed in *all* emergency scenarios:\n\n*   **First Aid:** Individuals must take a first-aid course at regular intervals.\n*   **Compliance:** All operating procedures and instructions issued by the operating company (in the case of fire, accident, etc.) **must** be strictly obeyed.\n*   **Disclaimer:** The recommendations provided are considered **general guidelines** derived from standard instructions only.\n\n***\n\n### II. 🔥 Emergency Firefighting Protocols (DANGER)\n\nThis section outlines critical warnings, prohibitions, and mandatory steps when a fire occurs. These rules are grouped for clarity:\n\n#### 🚨 **Key Safety Hazards & Warnings**\n\n| Symbol/Icon | Hazard/Warning | Mandatory Action/Prohibition |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| ⚡️ | **Electric Shock Risk** | Never use water to fight electrical fires, as this creates a danger of death due to electric shock. |\n| 💧❌ | **Flammable Liquid/Gas Fires** | **DO NOT** use water to extinguish fuel fires (such as oil, gasoline, or solvents) or electrical fires. |\n\n#### 🛠️ **Mandatory Pre-Fire Actions & Equipment**\n\n| Priority | Instruction | Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **Highest** | **Power Shutoff** | If at all possible, the equipment must be turned off (powered down) *before* commencing fire extinguishing efforts. |\n| **Primary Method** | **Recommended Agent** | Fires must be extinguished using **Carbon Dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$)**. |\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Quick Reference Guide (Summary Data Table)\n\n| Action Type | Item | Rule/Guideline |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Water Use** | Electric Fires | **Prohibited.** (Risk of electrocution) |\n| **Water Use** | Fuel/Gas Fires | **Prohibited.** (Ineffective/Dangerous) |\n| **Water Use** | General Firefighting | **Never** use water on oil, gasoline, solvents, or electrical fires. |\n| **Recommended Agent** | Fire Extinguishing | Use $\\text{CO}_2$. |\n| **Preparation** | Pre-Extinguishing Step | **Always** turn off equipment power if safe to do so. |\n| **Personnel Safety** | Training | Take regular first-aid courses. |\n| **Compliance** | Operation | Obey company-issued operating instructions. |\n\n[Page 13]\nThis page provides a highly detailed guide to the installation, operation, and core technical specifications of conveyor systems, specifically focusing on the MiSUMI products.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including all key data, tables, and diagram descriptions.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Summary: Conveyor Systems - Installation and Operation\n\n### 📋 1. Technical Data (Section 4.1)\n\nA detailed table of the operating specifications for the equipment is provided:\n\n| Parameter | Specification |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Rating** | 230 V |\n| **Tolerated Voltage** | ± 10 % |\n| **Frequency** | 50 Hz |\n| **Torque Rating (Low)** | 0W/k A: 15W/0.4A; 25W/0.6A |\n| **Torque Rating (High)** | 40W/1A; 60W/1A; 90W/2A |\n| **Motor Protection** | Built-in |\n| **Type of Power Connection** | Fixed connection |\n| **Airborne Sound** | ≤ 55 dB |\n| **Electromagnetic Compatibility** | According to the EMC Directive and applicable EMC standards for use in industrial area |\n\n### 🔄 2. Conveyor System Types and Components (Section 4.2)\n\nThe system offers flexibility based on the material and application requirements.\n\n#### A. Conveyor System Types\nMiSUMI offers three primary types of conveyor systems:\n1.  **Belt Conveyor:** Using a continuous flexible belt.\n2.  **Chain Conveyor:** Using durable linked chains.\n3.  **Timing Belt Conveyor:** Using a toothed timing belt.\n\n#### B. Mechanical Components (Diagrammatic Listing)\nThe mechanical description details the key components of the system:\n*   **1. Belt:** The primary carrying surface.\n*   **2. Sprocket:** The wheel that engages the belt/chain.\n*   **3. Idler:** Support rollers that guide the belt and maintain tension.\n*   **4. Drive Pulley:** The main pulley attached to the motor/drive unit.\n*   **5. Idler Pulley:** Support pulleys used for alignment or guiding.\n*   **6. Conveyor Belt:** The name referring to the overall belt material.\n\n### 🔩 3. Drive Motor Installation Options (Section 4.2.1)\n\nThere are three methods for installing the drive unit, offering flexibility in layout and maintenance access.\n\n| Option | Description | Diagrammatic Representation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Head Drive** | The motor is positioned at the head (start end) of the conveyor. | (Diagram shows motor connected to the drive pulley at one end) |\n| **Centre Drive** | The motor is mounted in the center section of the conveyor run. | (Diagram shows motor connected to the drive pulley in the middle) |\n| **Integrated Drive** | The motor/drive unit is fully incorporated or integrated into the conveyor structure itself. | (Diagram shows the motor unit forming part of the structure) |\n\n### 🛡️ 4. Safety and Operational Instructions\n\n#### Key Safety Directives:\n*   **Mandatory Reading:** Operators must follow all safety instructions detailed in Chapter 3 ("Safety Instructions").\n*   **Maintenance:** All technical instructions and signs on the conveyor system and in the manufacturer’s documentation must be observed.\n\n#### General Technical Advice:\n*   The system is designed for robust industrial use.\n*   Any modification or unauthorized changes to the system must be avoided.\n\n### ✨ 5. Special Features (Operational Benefits)\n\nThe system is designed with efficiency and maintenance in mind, featuring:\n*   **Single Track:** Supports single-line operations.\n*   **Low Noise Transport:** Ensures quiet operation in industrial environments.\n*   **Transport of Peace Goods:** Suitable for transporting sensitive or valuable items.\n*   **Supplements Rolling Conveyors:** Offers modular expansion options.\n*   **Easy to Combine:** Can be easily paired with other conveyor technologies.\n*   **Fully Integrated:** The belt can be fully integrated into the frame.\n*   **Long Conveyor Models:** Suitable for covering large distances.\n*   **Motor:** Equipped with various technologies.\n\n### 🔧 Summary Table of Key Data Points\n\n| Category | Item | Detail / Specification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage** | Rating | 230 V (Tolerated ± 10%) |\n| **Power/Torque** | Min/Max | 15W – 90W (Torque ranges provided for low/high usage) |\n| **Frequency** | Standard | 50 Hz |\n| **Safety** | Compliance | EMC Directive; Required adherence to Chapter 3 safety rules. |\n| **Installation** | Options | Head Drive, Centre Drive, Integrated Drive. |\n| **Conveyor Types** | Options | Belt, Chain, Timing Belt. |\n| **Sound Level** | Maximum | ≤ 55 dB |\n\n[Page 14]\nThis document provides a detailed technical summary of two primary conveyor systems offered by MISUMI: **Chain Conveyors** and **Timing Belt Conveyors**. It outlines the components, operational features, and various system configurations available.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 1. Chain Conveyor System (4.2.3)\n\nThis system utilizes heavy-duty chain mechanisms, designed for robust transport and stability.\n\n### 📐 Mechanical Structure (Diagram Nomenclature)\n\nThe structure is identified using the following numbered components:\n\n| Component No. | Part Name | Function/Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective Cover | Guards the moving mechanisms. |\n| **2** | Chain | The primary element for product movement. |\n| **3** | Sprockets | Gears used to drive and guide the chain. |\n| **4** | Frame | The supporting structural chassis. |\n| **5** | (Not listed, but implied drive point) | Drive mechanism connection. |\n| **6** | Sprockets | Guiding/driving gears. |\n| **7** | (End Point/Support) | Structural endpoint. |\n\n### ✨ Special Features and Capabilities\n\nThe chain conveyor is highly versatile, offering the following operational benefits:\n\n*   **Two-track:** Capable of handling items on multiple tracks.\n*   **Positive and Slip-free:** Ensures reliable movement without slippage.\n*   **Heavy Load Transport:** Designed specifically for transporting significant weights.\n*   **Guided Chains:** The chains are guided along the length of the conveyor.\n*   **Re-tensioning:** Allows for maintaining optimal tension levels.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 2. Timing Belt Conveyor System (4.2.4)\n\nThis system uses precision timing belts, ideal for synchronized, guided movement of goods.\n\n### 📐 Mechanical Structure (Diagram Nomenclature)\n\nThe structure is identified using the following numbered components:\n\n| Component No. | Part Name | Function/Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective Cover | Guards the belt and mechanical components. |\n| **2** | Belt Pulley | The wheel that drives the belt. |\n| **3** | Timing Belt | The flexible, synchronized belt medium. |\n| **4** | Frame | The supporting structural chassis. |\n| **5** | Drive Unit (Here, center drive) | The motor/gear unit providing power. |\n| **6** | Frame/Roller | Supports the belt path. |\n| **7** | Drive Guard | Safety guard surrounding the drive unit. |\n\n### ✨ Special Features and Capabilities\n\nThe timing belt system is optimized for precision and controlled movement:\n\n*   **Two-track:** Supports simultaneous movement on two lines.\n*   **Positive and Slip-free:** Guarantees stable, non-slipping operation.\n*   **Bulky Goods Transport:** Suitable for larger, irregularly shaped items.\n*   **Guided and Re-tensioned:** The timing belts can be guided and their tension adjusted.\n*   **Synchronous Running:** The belts are driven by a motor via a drive shaft, ensuring perfectly timed movement.\n\n***\n\n## 💡 3. General System Information & Configurations\n\n### 🌐 Conveyor System Configurations (4.3)\n\nMISUMI conveyor systems are highly modular and can be configured based on the necessary drive components and media. Users can select from the following setups:\n\n*   **With drive and belt:** Full motorized operation with belt power.\n*   **With drive without belt:** Motorized setup used perhaps for manual belt attachment or specialized end-effectors.\n*   **Without drive and belt:** Non-powered, purely structural setup (e.g., for fixed transport guides).\n*   **Without drive and without belt:** Pure structural frame setup.\n\n### 📦 Key Operational Notes\n\n*   **Accessories:** A wide range of accessories is available for both conveyor types (details are referenced on Page 60).\n*   **Principle of Operation:** Both systems prioritize reliable motion; the key differentiating factor is the power source and transport medium (chain vs. synchronized belt).\n*   **Installation:** The manual warns about the differing applicability of the components, necessitating careful review of specifications for optimal setup.\n\n[Page 15]\nThis page functions as a detailed **Product Designation Index** for various types of conveyor belts manufactured by MiSUMI. It does not contain diagrams or instructional text, but rather a highly structured catalog listing key product codes, their respective pages in the manual, and their material type.\n\nThe summary below details the contents, structure, and key product categories presented in the table.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Detailed Summary of MiSUMI Product Designation Index\n\n**Source:** MiSUMI Conveyor Systems - Operating Instructions (Page 4)\n**Purpose:** To provide a comprehensive index mapping specific product designations to their physical type of conveyor belt.\n**Data Structure:** The table utilizes three columns: `Page` (The page number where details can be found), `Designation` (The specific product code), and `Type` (The belt material or function).\n\n### 🔍 Key Data Extraction and Categorization\n\nThe index covers product listings from page 12 to page 34. The products are clearly categorized into three primary mechanical types: Flat Belt, Timing Belt, and specialized carriers (Plastic Chains/Stainless Steel).\n\n#### 1. Flat Belt Conveyors (The largest group)\nThis category includes belts suitable for general conveyance and is marked by various alphanumeric codes (starting with SVKA, SVKQ, CVK, etc.).\n\n*   **Pages Covered:** 12 through 20 (with some overlaps).\n*   **Function:** General industrial conveyance.\n*   **Key Examples:**\n    *   SVKAE (Page 12)\n    *   SVKQE (Page 12)\n    *   CVKAE (Page 19)\n\n#### 2. Timing Belt Conveyors (The most diverse group)\nThis category lists belts designed for precise, motorized synchronization and movement. This is the most numerous group in the index.\n\n*   **Pages Covered:** 14 through 33.\n*   **Function:** Synchronized, precision movement (timing).\n*   **Key Examples:**\n    *   CVTGAE (Page 14)\n    *   CVTSE (Page 15)\n    *   CVTQA (Page 22)\n    *   CVTGBE (Page 31)\n\n#### 3. Specialized Carriers\nThe remaining designations cover belts made of specialized materials or designs:\n\n*   **A. Stainless Steel:** For corrosion resistance or demanding industrial environments.\n    *   **Product:** CVSSAE (Page 32)\n    *   **Type:** Stainless steel conveyor belt\n*   **B. Plastic Chain Conveyors:** For applications requiring chain-like movement or durability with plastics.\n    *   **Products:** CVSPA (Page 32), CVSPCE (Page 34)\n    *   **Type:** Plastic chain conveyors\n\n### 📊 Summary Table of Product Types\n\n| Product Type | Typical Designation Prefix | Page Range | Primary Use |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Flat Belt Conveyor** | SVKA, CVK, etc. | 12–20 | General conveying of materials. |\n| **Timing Belt Conveyor** | CVT (most common) | 14–33 | Precision, synchronized movement; motorized systems. |\n| **Plastic Chain Conveyor**| CVSP, CVSPA | 32–34 | Handling of materials via chain linkage. |\n| **Stainless Steel Belt** | CVSSAE | 32 | High durability, resistance to rust/corrosion. |\n\n### 🔑 Key Data Points and Observations\n\n1.  **Density of Information:** The index is extremely dense, listing 30+ unique product designations across 23 pages.\n2.  **Designation Pattern:** The structure suggests that the designation codes relate directly to the product function (e.g., **CVT** strongly implies a **C**onveyor **V**ery **T**iming product).\n3.  **Systematic Indexing:** The list is organized in a clear, systematic manner, transitioning from flatter belts to specialized timing belts, and finally to alternative material conveyors (stainless steel and plastic chains).\n4.  **Scope:** This page serves as the main navigational guide for locating the specific technical details and application parameters for every listed belt type in the comprehensive MiSUMI manual.\n\n[Page 16]\nThis page provides a highly detailed technical summary and performance comparison for two models of conveyor belting systems: **4.3.1 SVKAE** and **4.3.2 SVKBE**. Both models are industrial, single-belt, head-drive conveyor components.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Overview and Products\n\n| Model | Type | Key Features |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **4.3.1 SVKAE** | Industrial Conveyor | Flat belt conveyors, single belt, $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley, Head drive. |\n| **4.3.2 SVKBE** | Industrial Conveyor | Flat belt conveyors, single belt, $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley, Head drive, **PLUS Belt meandering prevention crosspiece.** |\n\n*Note: The difference between the two models is the addition of a **Belt meandering prevention crosspiece** in the SVKBE model.*\n\n## 📋 Technical Specifications (Common to both Models)\n\nThe technical specifications for both the SVKAE and SVKBE units are identical, defining their operational parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Specification Range | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $50 - 400$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Length** | $300 - 3000$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Weight** | $3.7 - 28.8$ | $\\text{kg}$ |\n| **Power Output** | $6 - 25$ | $\\text{W}$ |\n| **Voltage** | $220$ | $\\text{V}$ |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ |\n\n## 📉 Diagram: Conveyor Capacity\n\nBoth models feature a "Conveyor Capacity" diagram illustrating the relationship between operational speed and the necessary belting ratio.\n\n*   **Y-Axis (Vertical):** Torque (N $\\cdot$ m) and speed (m/s)\n*   **X-Axis (Horizontal):** Belting ratio and speed (m/s)\n*   **Function:** The diagram serves as a key performance guide, helping the user determine the maximum achievable capacity (torque/speed) based on the selected operational parameters and belt ratio.\n\n## 📊 Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio\n\nThe product performance is detailed using two tables that show the relationship between the required **Gearhead Reduction Ratio** and the operating frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz). *Note: The data presented in both models is identical.*\n\n### **Table 1: Gearhead Reduction Ratio (50 Hz vs. 60 Hz)**\n\nThis table shows the required reduction ratio for various belt lengths (indicated by the list of values on the left).\n\n| Length/Ratio | 50 Hz | 60 Hz |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(Start)** | $5.7$ | $56.4$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $7.5$ | $37.6$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $12.5$ | $22.6$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $15$ | $18.8$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $25$ | $11.3$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $30$ | $9.4$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $60$ | $5.8$ |\n| **(Next Value)** | $75$ | $4.7$ |\n| **(End)** | $180$ | $1.6$ |\n\n***\n\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data Points\n\n*   **Primary Function:** Providing reliable, low-speed/high-torque movement for industrial material handling (conveying).\n*   **Common Constraints:** Both units operate within a $50-400 \\text{ mm}$ belt width, up to $25 \\text{ W}$ power, and at $220 \\text{ V}$.\n*   **Key Differentiation:** The SVKBE model is superior in complex applications due to its inclusion of the **belt meandering prevention crosspiece**, which stabilizes the belt path.\n*   **Performance Guidance:** The capacity charts and reduction ratio tables are the primary tools for engineers to size the product correctly, ensuring the unit can handle the necessary torque and speed given the selected operational frequency.\n\n[Page 17]\nThis document is a detailed technical sheet from MISUMI concerning **Conveyor Systems** (Models 4.3.3 SVKNE and 4.3.4 SVKRE). It provides comprehensive information on the features, technical specifications, operational performance, and wiring details for these belt conveyor units.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Detailed Summary\n\n### 1. Product Overview and Models\n\nThe page presents two similar conveyor models, differing slightly in anti-vibration/meandering features:\n\n*   **4.3.3 SVKNE:** A general-purpose belt conveyor.\n*   **4.3.4 SVKRE:** An enhanced belt conveyor that includes a **meandering prevention cresspipe**.\n\n**Special Features Common to Both:**\n*   Flat belt, single belt\n*   Pulley $\\emptyset$ 30 mm\n*   Central drive\n\n### 2. Technical Specifications (Key Data)\n\nThe general technical parameters are listed below (Note: These specifications appear identical for both models on the sheet):\n\n| Specification | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50–400 | mm |\n| **Length** | 390–3000 | mm |\n| **Weight (avg.)** | 5–41.5 | kg |\n| **Power output (W)** | 6 | W |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 220 | V |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 3. Operational Diagrams (Performance Curves)\n\nThe sheet includes two graphs for each model (SVKNE and SVKRE), demonstrating operational relationships.\n\n*   **Diagram 1 (Left):** Shows a performance curve (likely relating effort/load to speed or time). The curve is smooth and semi-circular, indicating stable operation within specified load ranges.\n*   **Diagram 2 (Right):** Shows the corresponding performance curve for the other metric (e.g., time vs. load).\n\n*(These diagrams visually convey the operational limits and performance envelope of the conveyor system across various operating loads.)*\n\n### 4. Gearhead Reduction Ratio Performance (Data Tables)\n\nThe most detailed data is presented in two performance tables for both models, illustrating how the gearhead reduction ratio changes based on the operating frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz) and the required belt speed (measured in rpm).\n\n#### ⚙️ Performance Tables for 4.3.3 SVKNE\n\n| Belt Speed (rpm) | 50 Hz (Reduction Ratio) | 60 Hz (Reduction Ratio) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 3.7 | 4.71 |\n| **7.5** | 5.1 | 37.4 |\n| **12** | 8.5 | 28.2 |\n| **15** | 10.8 | 18.8 |\n| **18** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **25** | 22 | 11.3 |\n| **30** | 30 | 9.4 |\n| **35** | 36 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **60** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **75** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| **90** | 2.6 | 3.1 |\n| **100** | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **120** | 2.0 | 2.4 |\n| **150** | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n#### ⚙️ Performance Tables for 4.3.4 SVKRE\n\n*(Note: The data provided in the second set of tables (SVKRE) is identical to the first set (SVKNE), suggesting the reduction ratio performance is consistent regardless of the anti-meandering feature.)*\n\n| Belt Speed (rpm) | 50 Hz (Reduction Ratio) | 60 Hz (Reduction Ratio) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 3.7 | 4.71 |\n| **7.5** | 5.1 | 37.4 |\n| **12** | 8.5 | 28.2 |\n| **15** | 10.8 | 18.8 |\n| **18** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **25** | 22 | 11.3 |\n| **30** | 30 | 9.4 |\n| **35** | 36 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **60** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **75** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| **90** | 2.6 | 3.1 |\n| **100** | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **120** | 2.0 | 2.4 |\n| **150** | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n### 5. Summary of Key Takeaways\n\n1.  **Function:** The product is a central-drive belt conveyor system designed for various industrial applications.\n2.  **Differentiator:** The SVKRE model is preferred if measures to prevent belt meandering are required.\n3.  **Technical Standard:** The system operates at a standard voltage of 220 V and supports both 50 Hz and 60 Hz frequencies.\n4.  **Performance:** The reduction ratio of the gearhead must be carefully selected based on the desired operating belt speed (rpm) and the electrical frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz), as detailed in the tables.\n\n[Page 18]\nThis page provides detailed technical specifications, features, and performance data for two industrial conveyor systems, **4.3.5 GVHAE** and **4.3.6 GVFAE**, manufactured by MISUMI.\n\nThe summary is structured by product, followed by general technical specifications.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Product 1: 4.3.5 GVHAE\n\n**Category:** Conveyor Systems / Installation and Operation\n**Diagram:** A cross-section diagram illustrating a roller or belt conveyor mechanism driven by a head unit.\n\n### Key Features (Special Features)\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt\n*   Pulley $\\phi 30 \\text{ mm}$\n*   Head drive\n\n### Technical Data (Specifications)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $30 - 300$ | $\\text{mm}$ | |\n| **Length** | $300 - 2000$ | $\\text{mm}$ | |\n| **Power Cutoff** | $6 / 25$ | $\\text{kW}$ | (Two listed options) |\n| **Voltage** | $220$ | $\\text{V}$ | |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ | |\n\n### Performance Data (Conveyor Capacity vs. Belt Speed)\nThe graph and associated data tables illustrate the maximum conveyor capacity (mass) achievable at different belt speeds, based on frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n**Summary Data Points (Approximate, using the listed capacity ranges):**\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m/s}$) | Capacity (50 Hz) ($\\text{kg}$) | Capacity (60 Hz) ($\\text{kg}$) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **~0.5** | $\\sim 3.8 - 5.0$ | $\\sim 3.8 - 5.0$ |\n| **~1.0** | $\\sim 4.5 - 5.0$ | $\\sim 4.5 - 5.0$ |\n| **~2.0** | $\\sim 3.0 - 3.8$ | $\\sim 3.0 - 3.8$ |\n\n---\n\n## 🏭 Product 2: 4.3.6 GVFAE\n\n**Category:** Conveyor Systems / Installation and Operation\n**Diagram:** A cross-section diagram illustrating a roller or belt conveyor mechanism, visually similar to the first product.\n\n### Key Features (Special Features)\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt\n*   Pulley $\\phi 30 \\text{ mm}$\n*   Head drive\n*   Full belt\n\n### Technical Data (Specifications)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $50 - 300$ | $\\text{mm}$ | (Slightly wider minimum than Product 1) |\n| **Length** | $300 - 2000$ | $\\text{mm}$ | |\n| **Power Cutoff** | $6 / 25$ | $\\text{kW}$ | (Two listed options) |\n| **Voltage** | $220$ | $\\text{V}$ | |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ | |\n\n### Performance Data (Conveyor Capacity vs. Belt Speed)\nThe performance chart for 4.3.6 GVFAE is structurally identical to 4.3.5 GVHAE, indicating similar operational envelopes for capacity based on belt speed and frequency.\n\n**Summary Data Points (Approximate, mirroring the first product):**\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m/s}$) | Capacity (50 Hz) ($\\text{kg}$) | Capacity (60 Hz) ($\\text{kg}$) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **~0.5** | $\\sim 3.8 - 5.0$ | $\\sim 3.8 - 5.0$ |\n| **~1.0** | $\\sim 4.5 - 5.0$ | $\\sim 4.5 - 5.0$ |\n| **~2.0** | $\\sim 3.0 - 3.8$ | $\\sim 3.0 - 3.8$ |\n\n---\n\n## 📑 General Technical Information & Footer\n\nThe bottom section of the page provides navigational and supporting information regarding MISUMI's product ecosystem.\n\n**Manufacturer:** MISUMI\n\n**Further Information (Links to documentation):**\n*   **Connection:** From page 43\n*   **Belt characteristics:** From page 36\n*   **Accessories:** From page 63\n\n**Summary of Purpose:**\nThe page serves as a detailed technical reference for two modular industrial conveyor systems, allowing users to select components based on required belt width, length, operating voltage, and desired conveyor capacity relative to belt speed.\n\n[Page 19]\nThis page provides a detailed technical summary and guide for two related lines of industrial belt drive components from MISUMI: **4.3.7 GVHNE** and **4.3.8 GVFNE**.\n\nThe summary is structured by product, including visual descriptions, key specifications, and critical performance data presented in tables and graphs.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 General Overview\n\nThe page focuses on belt drive systems, detailing components used for transmitting power via belts and pulleys. The specifications provided cover belt dimensions, material properties, and operational limits based on required reduction ratios and operating frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 1. Model 4.3.7 GVHNE (Installation and Operation)\n\n### 📝 Special Features & Diagram\n*   **Diagram:** Illustrates a belt transmitting power between two pulleys (a belt conveyor system).\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt; single belt.\n    *   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n    *   Central drive.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Specifications)\nThe following parameters define the mechanical boundaries and sizes of the components:\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $30 - 300$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Length** | $300 - 2000$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Weight** | (Variable) | $\\text{kg}$ |\n| **Power (W)** | $25 - 90$ | $\\text{W}$ |\n| **Voltage (V)** | $230$ | $\\text{V}$ |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ |\n\n### 📊 Performance Data (Governor Capacity and Reduction Ratio)\nThe graphs compare the achievable **Governor Capacity** and **Gearhead Reduction Ratio** based on belt width and operating frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n#### **Governor Capacity and Reduction Ratio Table (Example Data Points)**\n\n| Belt Width ($\\text{mm}$) | Governor Capacity (50 Hz) | Governor Capacity (60 Hz) | Reduction Ratio (50 Hz) | Reduction Ratio (60 Hz) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 3.7 | 4.1 | 57.1 | 36.5 |\n| **15** | 12.5 | 18.8 | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **35** | 9.4 | 11.3 | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 | 3.8 | 4.7 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.8 | - | - |\n\n*(Note: The graph displays continuous data, but the table summarizes the key width points provided.)*\n\n***\n\n## 🚀 2. Model 4.3.8 GVFNE (Installation and Operation)\n\n### 📝 Special Features & Diagram\n*   **Diagram:** Similar to 4.3.7, illustrating a belt conveyor system.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt; single belt.\n    *   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n    *   Central drive.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Specifications)\nThe general specifications are highly consistent with the 4.3.7 model, indicating a similar product line intended for interchangeable use or parallel applications.\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $30 - 300$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Length** | $300 - 2000$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Weight** | (Variable) | $\\text{kg}$ |\n| **Power (W)** | $25 - 90$ | $\\text{W}$ |\n| **Voltage (V)** | $230$ | $\\text{V}$ |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ |\n\n### 📊 Performance Data (Governor Capacity and Reduction Ratio)\nThe performance graphs and associated data demonstrate the operational capacity relative to belt width, mirroring the structure of the 4.3.7 model.\n\n#### **Governor Capacity and Reduction Ratio Table (Example Data Points)**\n\n| Belt Width ($\\text{mm}$) | Governor Capacity (50 Hz) | Governor Capacity (60 Hz) | Reduction Ratio (50 Hz) | Reduction Ratio (60 Hz) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 3.7 | 4.1 | 57.1 | 36.5 |\n| **15** | 12.5 | 18.8 | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **35** | 9.4 | 11.3 | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 | 3.8 | 4.7 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.8 | - | - |\n\n***\n\n## 📑 Summary of Key Data and Takeaways\n\n1.  **Application:** Both models are used for transferring power in belt drive conveyor systems.\n2.  **Dimensions:** Both utilize flat belts and pulleys with a nominal diameter of $30 \\text{ mm}$.\n3.  **Technical Similarity:** The operational ranges (Power, Voltage, Frequency, Belt Width) are nearly identical, suggesting they are functionally equivalent alternatives or highly similar variants.\n4.  **Performance Scaling:** The graph data is the most critical feature. It shows a direct relationship between **Belt Width**, **Governor Capacity**, and **Reduction Ratio**. As the belt width increases, the available capacity generally increases (up to a point), allowing for different operational load requirements.\n5.  **Standards:** The system operates across two major industrial frequencies: 50 Hz and 60 Hz.\n\n### 📚 Further Information Links\nThe footer indicates that detailed technical information on the products can be found in the following sections:\n\n*   **Connection:** Page 43\n*   **Belt characteristics:** Page 36\n*   **Accessories:** Page 63\n\n[Page 20]\nThis document is a detailed technical summary for two models of conveyor systems, focusing on their specifications, operational features, and performance curves.\n\nThe summary is divided into two major sections: **4.3.9 GVTSAE** and **4.3.10 GVTSNE**.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Overall Context and Product Overview\n\nThe page provides operating instructions and technical specifications for belt conveyor systems, specifically those utilizing **timing belts** and a **pulley $\\emptyset 30$ mm**.\n\n### 📏 Common Technical Specifications\n\nBoth models share similar fundamental technical data parameters, which are provided in tabular format:\n\n| Parameter | Unit | Range / Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | mm\\* | 10, 20, 30 |\n| **Length** | mm\\* | 200 – 2000 |\n| **Weight** | kg\\* | Variable (indicated by measurement*) |\n| **Power Output** | W\\* | Variable (indicated by measurement*) |\n| **Voltage (V)** | V | 220 - 380 |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | Hz | 50, 60 |\n\n***\n\n## ➡️ Section 1: Model 4.3.9 GVTSAE (Head Drive)\n\nThis section details a conveyor system utilizing a **Head drive** mechanism.\n\n### 📋 Special Features\n*   **Type:** Timing belt conveyor.\n*   **Belt Configuration:** Single belt.\n*   **Pulley:** Diameter $\\emptyset 30$ mm.\n*   **Drive System:** Head drive.\n\n### 📊 Belt Speed vs. Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Diagram & Data)\n\nThe graph illustrates the relationship between **Belt speed (m/min)** and the **Gearhead reduction ratio** for two common frequencies (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n| Reduction Ratio (X-axis) | Belt Speed (50 Hz) (m/min) | Belt Speed (60 Hz) (m/min) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.0** | 10.0 | 10.0 |\n| **1.5** | 12.5 | 12.5 |\n| **2.0** | 15.0 | 15.0 |\n| **2.5** | 17.5 | 17.5 |\n| **3.0** | 20.0 | 20.0 |\n| **4.0** | 25.0 | 25.0 |\n| **6.0** | 35.0 | 35.0 |\n| **10.0** | 55.0 | 55.0 |\n\n***\n\n## ⬅️ Section 2: Model 4.3.10 GVTSNE (Central Drive)\n\nThis section details a conveyor system utilizing a **Central drive** mechanism.\n\n### 📋 Special Features\n*   **Type:** Timing belt conveyor.\n*   **Belt Configuration:** Single belt.\n*   **Pulley:** Diameter $\\emptyset 30$ mm.\n*   **Drive System:** Central drive.\n\n### 📊 Belt Speed vs. Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Diagram & Data)\n\nThe graph illustrates the relationship between **Belt speed (m/min)** and the **Gearhead reduction ratio** for two common frequencies (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n| Reduction Ratio (X-axis) | Belt Speed (50 Hz) (m/min) | Belt Speed (60 Hz) (m/min) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1.0** | 10.0 | 10.0 |\n| **1.5** | 12.5 | 12.5 |\n| **2.0** | 15.0 | 15.0 |\n| **2.5** | 17.5 | 17.5 |\n| **3.0** | 20.0 | 20.0 |\n| **4.0** | 25.0 | 25.0 |\n| **6.0** | 35.0 | 35.0 |\n| **10.0** | 55.0 | 55.0 |\n\n***\n\n## 🔍 Summary of Key Differences\n\nWhile the technical specifications (belt widths, lengths, power ratings) are identical, the primary difference lies in the **Drive System**:\n\n*   **4.3.9 GVTSAE:** Features a **Head drive** mechanism.\n*   **4.3.10 GVTSNE:** Features a **Central drive** mechanism.\n\nBoth models demonstrate identical performance curves regarding the relationship between Gearhead Reduction Ratio and Belt Speed across 50 Hz and 60 Hz operation.\n\n### 💡 Note on Data Points\nThe technical data tables accompanying the graphs (the detailed list of reduction ratio vs. speed) are identical for both models, suggesting that the functional performance characteristics are the same, differing only in the physical location of the motor/drive unit.\n\n[Page 21]\nThis page provides detailed operational and technical specifications for two conveyor systems: the **4.3.11 GVTWAUE** and the **4.3.12 GVTWASE**. The summary is structured by product, incorporating all key data, diagrams, and tables.\n\n***\n\n## 🔍 Overall Overview\n\nThe document section pertains to conveyor systems and their operational instructions. Both models use timing belt technology but differ in their structural capabilities, output power, and weight.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 4.3.11 GVTWAUE (Double Track Conveyor)\n\n**[Diagram: Shows a timing belt conveyor setup.]**\n\n### Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors.\n*   Double track structure (can handle two belts/lines simultaneously).\n*   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Head drive installed **(INSIDE the motor)**.\n\n### Technical Data\n| Specification | Range / Value | Units | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50 – 300 | mm | |\n| **Length** | 250 – 2000 | mm | |\n| **Weight** | 4 – 8.3 | kg* | *Approximate value |\n| **Power output** | 8 | W* | *Approximate value |\n| **Voltage** | 220 | V | |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz | |\n\n### Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio\nThe graphs and tables illustrate the necessary reduction ratio for the gearhead required to maintain specific belt speeds at standard operating frequencies (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n**Graph Interpretation:**\n*   The relationship shows that as the desired belt speed (Horizontal/Vertical) increases, the required gearhead reduction ratio decreases (indicating a faster motor output relative to the belt speed).\n\n**Key Data Table: Gearhead Reduction Ratio (50 Hz and 60 Hz)**\n\n| Belt Speed (m/min) | 50 Hz (Ratio) | 60 Hz (Ratio) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 30 | 36.0 | 36.0 |\n| 20 | 24.0 | 24.0 |\n| 15 | 20.0 | 20.0 |\n| 12.5 | 16.0 | 16.0 |\n| 10 | 12.0 | 12.0 |\n| 8 | 10.0 | 10.0 |\n| 5 | 6.0 | 6.0 |\n| 3 | 4.2 | 4.2 |\n| 2 | 3.0 | 3.0 |\n| 1.5 | 2.0 | 2.0 |\n| 1.0 | 1.8 | 1.8 |\n\n***\n\n## ⚡ 4.3.12 GVTWASE (Single Belt Conveyor)\n\n**[Diagram: Shows a timing belt conveyor setup.]**\n\n### Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors.\n*   Single belt structure (single line capacity).\n*   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Head drive installed **(OUTSIDE the motor)**.\n\n### Technical Data\n| Specification | Range / Value | Units | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50 – 300 | mm | |\n| **Length** | 250 – 2000 | mm | |\n| **Weight** | 4 – 8.3 | kg* | *Approximate value |\n| **Power output** | 2 | W* | *Approximate value |\n| **Voltage** | 220 | V | |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz | |\n\n### Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio\nThe graph and table provide the necessary reduction ratio for the gearhead.\n\n**Key Data Table: Gearhead Reduction Ratio (50 Hz and 60 Hz)**\n\n| Belt Speed (m/min) | 50 Hz (Ratio) | 60 Hz (Ratio) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 30 | 36.0 | 36.0 |\n| 20 | 24.0 | 24.0 |\n| 15 | 20.0 | 20.0 |\n| 12.5 | 16.0 | 16.0 |\n| 10 | 12.0 | 12.0 |\n| 8 | 10.0 | 10.0 |\n| 5 | 6.0 | 6.0 |\n| 3 | 4.2 | 4.2 |\n| 2 | 3.0 | 3.0 |\n| 1.5 | 2.0 | 2.0 |\n| 1.0 | 1.8 | 1.8 |\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Summary Comparison (Key Data)\n\nThe primary differences between the two models are noted in their power output, weight, and how the head drive is mounted.\n\n| Feature | 4.3.11 GVTWAUE | 4.3.12 GVTWASE | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Tracking** | Double Track | Single Belt | Capacity (2 belts vs. 1 belt). |\n| **Power Output** | 8 W* | 2 W* | Higher capacity for GVTWAUE. |\n| **Head Drive Location** | **INSIDE** the motor | **OUTSIDE** the motor | Difference in mechanical setup. |\n| **Technical Data Match** | Belts, Length, Voltage, Frequency are comparable. | | |\n\n***\n\n## ℹ️ Further Information (Navigation)\n\nThe page concludes with navigation links directing the user to comprehensive information on related topics:\n\n*   **Connection:** $\\rightarrow$ page 43\n*   **Belt characteristics:** $\\rightarrow$ page 36\n*   **Accessories:** $\\rightarrow$ page 63\n\n[Page 22]\nThis page provides a detailed technical comparison and operational guide for two similar industrial conveyor pulley systems: **4.3.13 GVTNUE** and **4.3.14 GVTNWSE**. Both systems are designed for timing belt conveyor applications.\n\nThe summary below details the structure, key specifications, and performance data for both models.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Summary of Conveyor Systems Data Sheet\n\n### ⚙️ I. Product Overview and Special Features\n\nThe systems are displayed with diagrams showing the pulley mechanism and roller arrangement.\n\n**General Features Common to Both Models:**\n*   **Conveyor Type:** Timing belt conveyors\n*   **Track Design:** Double track\n*   **Pulley Diameter ($\\emptyset$):** 30 mm\n*   **Function:** Used in conveyor and material handling systems.\n\n**Key Difference (Drive Location):**\n*   **Model 4.3.13 GVTNUE:** Features a **Central drive located INSIDE** the pulley system.\n*   **Model 4.3.14 GVTNWSE:** Features a **Central drive located OUTSIDE** the pulley system.\n\n### 📋 II. Comparative Technical Data\n\nThe two models share identical dimensions and operational specifications, suggesting they are interchangeable based on installation constraints (internal vs. external drive).\n\n| Specification | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 280–2000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 3.5–8.5 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 2–25 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 📈 III. Performance Characteristics and Diagrams\n\nThe sheet includes graphs and tables detailing performance metrics based on operating frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n#### A. Graphs (Diagram Interpretation)\n\nTwo graphs illustrate the system's operational limits:\n\n1.  **Conveyor Capacity:** Shows the maximum theoretical belt capacity (load) achievable at varying belt speeds (m/s).\n2.  **Gearhead Reduction Ratio:** Shows the ratio of speed reduction achieved by the gearhead mechanism at varying belt speeds.\n\n*(Note: Both graphs demonstrate a clear relationship between speed, capacity, and reduction ratio.)*\n\n#### B. Detailed Performance Tables\n\nThe numerical performance is provided in two tables, detailing capacity and reduction ratio for specific operating frequencies and belt speeds.\n\n**1. Conveyor Capacity (Belt/Track Load)**\n*(Measured in kg, relative to Belt Speed (m/s))*\n\n| Belt Speed (m/s) | 50 Hz (kg) | 60 Hz (kg) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **5** | 30.0 | 36.0 |\n| **7.5** | 20.0 | 24.0 |\n| **10** | 16.7 | 20.0 |\n| **12.5** | 14.4 | 18.7 |\n| **15** | 10.0 | 12.0 |\n| **18** | 8.3 | 10.0 |\n| **25** | 6.0 | 8.0 |\n| **30** | 5.0 | 6.0 |\n| **36** | 4.2 | 5.0 |\n\n**2. Gearhead Reduction Ratio**\n*(Measured relative to Belt Speed (m/s))*\n\n| Belt Speed (m/s) | 50 Hz (Ratio) | 60 Hz (Ratio) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **5** | 3.0 | 3.6 |\n| **7.5** | 2.5 | 3.0 |\n| **10** | 2.0 | 2.5 |\n| **12.5** | 1.8 | 2.2 |\n| **15** | 1.5 | 1.8 |\n| **18** | 1.2 | 1.5 |\n| **25** | 1.0 | 1.2 |\n| **30** | 1.0 | 1.2 |\n| **36** | 0.8 | 1.0 |\n\n### 📍 IV. Further Information\n\nThe bottom section provides links to supporting documentation:\n\n*   **Connection:** Page 43\n*   **Belt characteristics:** Page 36\n*   **Accessories:** Page 63\n\n---\n### 🔑 Key Data Summary Table\n\n| Metric | 4.3.13 GVTNUE (Inside Drive) | 4.3.14 GVTNWSE (Outside Drive) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Purpose** | Timing belt conveyor system | Timing belt conveyor system |\n| **Drive Location** | Central Drive: **INSIDE** | Central Drive: **OUTSIDE** |\n| **Voltage** | 230 V | 230 V |\n| **Belt Width Range** | 50–300 mm | 50–300 mm |\n| **Performance Basis** | Load and Ratio data provided for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz operation. |\n| **Primary Difference** | *Installation orientation* | *Installation orientation* |\n\n[Page 23]\nThis page from the MISUMI catalog details two models of conveyor belt systems—the **4.3.15 CVGAE** and the **4.3.16 CVGCE**—providing comprehensive technical specifications, key features, and operational performance data.\n\nThe summary below is organized by product, followed by a consolidation of shared technical data and performance charts.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Overview of Conveyor Systems\n\nBoth models are industrial conveyor systems designed for flat belts, utilizing a single belt setup and a dedicated head drive. The primary differences lie in the pulley size, the range of bolt widths, and the general dimensions.\n\n### 🏷️ Product 1: 4.3.15 CVGAE\n\n*   **Diagram:** Shows the structural unit of the conveyor belt system.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt, single belt setup.\n    *   Pulley $\\text{Ø}$ 30 mm.\n    *   Head drive unit included.\n*   **Technical Data:**\n    *   **Bolt width (mm):** 30–300\n    *   **Length (mm):** 190–2000\n    *   **Weight (kg):** 4.2–17.4\n    *   **Power output (W):** 6–25\n    *   **Voltage (V):** 230\n    *   **Frequency (Hz):** 50\n\n### 🏷️ Product 2: 4.3.16 CVGCE\n\n*   **Diagram:** Shows the structural unit of the conveyor belt system.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt, single belt setup.\n    *   Pulley $\\text{Ø}$ 50 mm.\n    *   Head drive unit included.\n*   **Technical Data:**\n    *   **Bolt width (mm):** 40–300\n    *   **Length (mm):** 240–2000\n    *   **Weight (kg):** 6–25.7\n    *   **Power output (W):** 6–25\n    *   **Voltage (V):** 230\n    *   **Frequency (Hz):** 50\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Key Comparative Data and Performance\n\n### 🔍 Technical Differences Summary\n\n| Feature | 4.3.15 CVGAE | 4.3.16 CVGCE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | 30 mm | 50 mm |\n| **Bolt Width Range** | 30–300 mm | 40–300 mm |\n| **Minimum Length** | 190 mm | 240 mm |\n| **Weight Range** | 4.2–17.4 kg | 6–25.7 kg |\n| **Voltage / Frequency** | 230 V / 50 Hz | 230 V / 50 Hz |\n| **Primary Use** | Conveying, belt systems | Conveying, belt systems |\n\n### 📈 Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Performance Tables)\n\nThe performance tables detail the **Gearhead reduction ratio** based on the required **Belt Speed ($\\text{m/min}$)**. These specifications are provided for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz operation.\n\n#### 1. 4.3.15 CVGAE Reduction Ratio\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m/min}$) | 50 Hz Reduction Ratio | 60 Hz Reduction Ratio |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 5 | 57.4 | 67.7 |\n| 7.5 | 37.6 | 45.1 |\n| 9 | 31.3 | 36 |\n| 12.5 | 22.6 | 27.1 |\n| 15 | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| 18 | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| 25 | 11.3 | 15.7 |\n| 30 | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| 36 | 7.8 | 9.4 |\n| 50 | 5.6 | 8.8 |\n| 60 | 4.7 | 5.6 |\n| 7.5 | 3.8 | 4.5 |\n| 90 | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| 100 | 2.8 | 3.1 |\n| 120 | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| 150 | 1.9 | 2.3 |\n| 180 | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n\n#### 2. 4.3.16 CVGCE Reduction Ratio\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m/min}$) | 50 Hz Reduction Ratio | 60 Hz Reduction Ratio |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 5 | 47.7 | 56.5 |\n| 7.5 | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| 9 | 26.2 | 31.4 |\n| 12.5 | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| 15 | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| 18 | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| 25 | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| 30 | 7.8 | 9.4 |\n| 36 | 6.4 | 8.8 |\n| 50 | 5.0 | 6.0 |\n| 60 | 4.7 | 5.6 |\n| 7.5 | 3.8 | 4.5 |\n| 90 | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| 100 | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| 120 | 2.0 | 2.4 |\n| 150 | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| 180 | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n---\n***Disclaimer:*** *All technical details are drawn from the provided page images. "From page X" indicates where the information is sourced in the catalog.*\n\n[Page 24]\nThis document details specifications and data for two conveyor roller units: the **4.3.17 CVGNE** and the **4.3.18 CVGRE**. Both systems are designed for single-belt, flat-belt conveyance and feature central drives with adjustable belt tension.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ 4.3.17 CVGNE (Conveyor Unit)\n\n### 🖼️ Diagrammatic Overview\nThe diagram illustrates the roller unit design, highlighting the physical components (roller frame, pulley, motor, etc.).\n\n### ✨ Special Features\n*   **Belt Type:** Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   **Pulley Diameter:*