mance curves.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Overview and Model Comparison\n\nThe two models differ primarily in their maximum pulley diameter and corresponding belt width suitability.\n\n| Feature | 4.3.19 CVGBE (Left) | 4.3.20 CVGDE (Right) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Application** | Conveyor system (Head Drive) | Conveyor system (Head Drive) |\n| **Belt Type** | Flat belt, single belt | Flat belt, single belt |\n| **Max Pulley Diameter ($\\text{D}$)** | $\\le 30 \\text{ mm}$ | $\\le 50 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Primary Belt Width Range** | $150 - 300 \\text{ mm}$ | $50 - 300 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Overall Purpose** | Handling medium-sized loads with restricted pulley size. | Handling a wider range of loads with a larger pulley capacity. |\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Technical Specifications (Common)\n\nBoth systems share the following standard operating electrical specifications:\n\n*   **Voltage ($\\text{V}$):** $230 \\text{ V}$\n*   **Frequency ($\\text{Hz}$):** $50 \\text{ Hz}$\n*   **Voltage Range:** $230 \\text{ V}$\n*   **Operation:** Suitable for both $50 \\text{ Hz}$ and $60 \\text{ Hz}$ operation (as indicated by the performance graphs).\n\n### Technical Data Table\n\n| Parameter | 4.3.19 CVGBE | 4.3.20 CVGDE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width ($\\text{mm}$)** | $150 - 300$ | $50 - 300$ |\n| **Length ($\\text{mm}$)** | $190 - 2000$ | $240 - 2000$ |\n| **Weight ($\\text{kg}$)** | $4.2 - 17.4$ | $6 - 25.7$ |\n\n***\n\n## 📈 Performance Data and Diagrams\n\nBoth models feature operational diagrams showing the relationship between load/torque and speed, and the accompanying detailed data tables.\n\n### 1. Torque / Power Graph Analysis\n\n*   **Diagram:** Both models feature a graph illustrating the relationship between $\\text{Torque}/\\text{Power}$ and $\\text{Speed}$ (or $\\text{Speed}$ vs. $\\text{Load}$).\n*   **Interpretation:** The graph demonstrates how the conveyor operates effectively across different speeds and corresponding power output, suitable for both $50 \\text{ Hz}$ and $60 \\text{ Hz}$ environments.\n\n### 2. Key Operating Performance Table (Torque/Power)\n\nThe following tables illustrate the continuous operating performance (Torque/Power) for various speeds (assuming a standard operating condition, likely nominal load).\n\n#### 💽 4.3.19 CVGBE Performance Table\n\n| Speed ($\\text{RPM}$) | $50 \\text{ Hz}$ (Torque Unit) | $60 \\text{ Hz}$ (Torque Unit) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $5$ | $56.4$ | $67.7$ |\n| $7.5$ | $37.6$ | $45.1$ |\n| $11$ | $25.5$ | $37.6$ |\n| $12.5$ | $22.6$ | $27.1$ |\n| $15$ | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| $18$ | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| $22.5$ | $11.3$ | $13.5$ |\n| $30$ | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| $36$ | $7.8$ | $9.4$ |\n| $50$ | $5.6$ | $6.8$ |\n| $60$ | $4.7$ | $5.6$ |\n| $75$ | $3.8$ | $4.5$ |\n| $90$ | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| $100$ | $2.8$ | $3.4$ |\n| $120$ | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| $150$ | $1.9$ | $2.3$ |\n| $180$ | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n\n#### 💽 4.3.20 CVGDE Performance Table\n\n| Speed ($\\text{RPM}$) | $50 \\text{ Hz}$ (Torque Unit) | $60 \\text{ Hz}$ (Torque Unit) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $5$ | $47.1$ | $56.5$ |\n| $7.5$ | $31.4$ | $37.7$ |\n| $11$ | $22.2$ | $31.4$ |\n| $12.5$ | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| $15$ | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| $18$ | $13.1$ | $15.7$ |\n| $22.5$ | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| $30$ | $7.8$ | $9.4$ |\n| $36$ | $6.2$ | $7.8$ |\n| $50$ | $4.7$ | $5.6$ |\n| $60$ | $3.7$ | $4.5$ |\n| $75$ | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| $90$ | $2.6$ | $3.1$ |\n| $100$ | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| $120$ | $2.2$ | $2.4$ |\n| $150$ | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n| $180$ | $1.3$ | $1.6$ |\n\n***\n\n## ℹ️ Summary of Available Information\n\nThe page serves as a comprehensive quick-reference guide, directing users to further specialized information on:\n\n*   **Connection:** (from page 43)\n*   **Belt characteristics:** (from page 36)\n*   **Accessories:** (from page 63)\n\n[Page 26]\nThis page provides a detailed technical summary and specifications for two models of electric conveyor systems: the **4.3.21 CVGPE** and the **4.3.22 CVGWE**. Both systems are designed for flat belt applications and are equipped with central drives and meandering prevention features.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ General System Overview\n\nThe manuals detail overhead conveyor systems used for industrial material transport. Key components include a central drive system and safety features to prevent belt meandering.\n\n### 📑 Technical Data (Applicable to Both Models)\n\n| Specification | Range | Units | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50 - 300 | mm | |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 230 | V | |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 | Hz | |\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 Model 4.3.21 CVGPE (Left Side)\n\nThis model is characterized by a **30 mm pulley diameter**.\n\n### **Features & Functionality**\n\n*   **Application:** Flat belt conveyors.\n*   **Drive Type:** Central drive.\n*   **Pulley:** $\\emptyset$ 30 mm.\n*   **Safety Feature:** Belt with meandering prevention crosspiece.\n\n### **Technical Specifications (CVGPE)**\n\n| Specification | Range | Units | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Length** | 300 - 2000 | mm | |\n| **Weight** | 5.8 - 23.9 | kg | |\n| **Power Output** | 6 - 25 | W | |\n\n### **Performance Data: Conveyor Capacity vs. Speed**\n\nThis table shows the calculated **Conveyor Capacity (Nm)** based on belt speed (rpm) for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz operations.\n\n| Speed (rpm) | Conveyor Capacity @ 50 Hz (Nm) | Conveyor Capacity @ 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 47.1 | 56.5 |\n| **10** | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| **15** | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| **20** | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **25** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **30** | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **35** | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **40** | 6.5 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **60** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **75** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 Model 4.3.22 CVGWE (Right Side)\n\nThis model is characterized by a **50 mm pulley diameter**.\n\n### **Features & Functionality**\n\n*   **Application:** Flat belt conveyors.\n*   **Drive Type:** Central drive.\n*   **Pulley:** $\\emptyset$ 50 mm.\n*   **Safety Feature:** Belt with meandering prevention crosspiece.\n\n### **Technical Specifications (CVGWE)**\n\n| Specification | Range | Units | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Length** | 385 - 2000 | mm | |\n| **Weight** | 7.3 - 30.6 | kg | |\n| **Power Output** | 6 - 25 | W | |\n\n### **Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio vs. Speed**\n\nThis table shows the calculated **Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Nm)** based on belt speed (rpm) for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz operations.\n\n| Speed (rpm) | Gearhead Reduction Ratio @ 50 Hz (Nm) | Gearhead Reduction Ratio @ 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 47.1 | 56.5 |\n| **10** | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| **15** | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| **20** | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **25** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **30** | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **35** | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **40** | 6.5 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **60** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **75** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Differences Summary\n\n| Feature | 4.3.21 CVGPE | 4.3.22 CVGWE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | $\\emptyset$ 30 mm | $\\emptyset$ 50 mm |\n| **Focus of Performance** | Conveyor Capacity (Nm) | Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Nm) |\n| **Length Range** | 300–2000 mm | 385–2000 mm |\n| **Weight Range** | 5.8–23.9 kg | 7.3–30.6 kg |\n\n***\n*Note: The diagrams illustrate the overall configuration of the conveyors, confirming the central drive and belt mechanism.*\n\n[Page 27]\nThis page provides detailed specifications and performance graphs for two models of conveyor systems: **4.3.23 CVSCE** and **4.3.24 CVSFE**. Both systems appear to be high-power, single-belt, flat-belt conveyors designed for various industrial applications.\n\n***\n\n## ⚡️ 1. Model 4.3.23 CVSCE (Standard Conveyor)\n\nThis model features a basic, robust conveyor design.\n\n### 🖼️ Diagrams and Features\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a linear, industrial conveyor mechanism.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt, single belt, high power.\n    *   Pulley $\\emptyset$ 30, 60 mm.\n    *   Head drive.\n    *   Medium conveying length.\n\n### ⚙️ Technical Data (Shared Specs)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bolt Width** | 100 – 500 | mm |\n| **Length** | 440 – 6000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 15.9 – 81.5 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 60, 90 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 220 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity (Graph Data)\nThe graph illustrates the relationship between **Conveyor Capacity (kg)** and the **reduction ratio** for two frequencies (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n**Key Data Trends (Reading the Graph):**\n*   The maximum capacity is achieved at lower reduction ratios (closer to 0).\n*   The graph is segmented for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz, showing the load limit the system can handle as the gear reduction decreases.\n\n***\n\n## 🌟 2. Model 4.3.24 CVSFE (Advanced Conveyor)\n\nThis model is nearly identical to the CVSCE but includes an added component for stability.\n\n### 🖼️ Diagrams and Features\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a linear, industrial conveyor mechanism, similar to the CVSCE.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt, single belt, high power.\n    *   Pulley $\\emptyset$ 30, 60 mm.\n    *   Head drive.\n    *   **Key Difference:** Belt with meandering prevention crosspiece (Adds stability and keeps the belt aligned).\n\n### ⚙️ Technical Data (Shared Specs)\n*The technical specifications for 4.3.24 CVSFE are identical to 4.3.23 CVSCE.*\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bolt Width** | 100 – 500 | mm |\n| **Length** | 440 – 6000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 15.9 – 81.5 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 60, 90 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 220 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity (Graph Data)\nThe capacity graph for the CVSFE follows the same pattern as the CVSCE, confirming the operational limits based on reduction ratio and frequency.\n\n***\n\n## 🎯 Summary of Key Differences\n\nThe primary distinction between the two models lies in the included components, which suggests a difference in intended application stability:\n\n| Feature | Model 4.3.23 CVSCE | Model 4.3.24 CVSFE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Differentiator** | Standard setup. | **Includes Meandering Prevention Crosspiece.** |\n| **Function** | Standard conveyance. | Enhanced belt stability, preventing horizontal movement (meandering). |\n| **Technical Specs** | Identical to CVSFE. | Identical to CVSCE. |\n\n### Summary of Key Data Points\n\n*   **Application:** High-power, industrial, flat-belt conveyance.\n*   **Power Options:** 60 W or 90 W.\n*   **Voltage/Frequency:** 220 V / 50 Hz (Standard configuration displayed).\n*   **Structural Flexibility:** Extremely adjustable, with bolt widths ranging from 100 mm to 500 mm and lengths from 440 mm up to 6000 mm.\n*   **Capacity:** Performance is defined by a dynamic graph showing that capacity (kg) decreases as the gear reduction ratio increases.\n\n[Page 28]\nThis document is a detailed technical specification and performance data sheet for two models of industrial conveyor systems manufactured by Misumi: **4.3.25 CVSXE** and **4.3.26 CVSYE**.\n\nThe page presents a comprehensive overview, including technical specifications, operational features, and detailed performance tables for calculating capacity based on the required belt and reduction ratios.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ I. Product Overview and Technical Specifications\n\nBoth models represent high-power, single-belt flat conveyor systems.\n\n### A. Model 4.3.25 CVSXE (Left Side)\n\n**Diagram:** Displays a standard belt conveyor system configuration.\n**Special Features:**\n*   Flat belt conveyors\n*   Single belt\n*   High power\n**Technical Data:**\n*   **Pulley:** $\\varnothing$ 30 mm\n*   **Drive System:** Central drive\n*   **Operation:** Medium conveying length\n\n### B. Model 4.3.26 CVSYE (Right Side)\n\n**Diagram:** Displays a standard belt conveyor system configuration.\n**Special Features:**\n*   Flat belt conveyors\n*   Single belt\n*   High power\n**Technical Data:**\n*   **Pulley:** $\\varnothing$ 30 mm\n*   **Drive System:** Central drive\n*   **Special Feature:** Equipped with a belt meandering prevention crosspiece (suggesting enhanced stability for long runs).\n\n---\n\n## 📊 II. Key Technical Data (Common Specifications)\n\n| Parameter | Specification | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bolt Width** | 100–500 | mm |\n| **Length** | 480–6000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 20–94 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 60–230 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n---\n\n## 📋 III. Performance and Operational Tables\n\nThe document includes three primary performance tables: Conveyor Capacity, Gearhead Reduction Ratio, and detailed capacity tables that depend on the operating frequency (50 Hz vs. 60 Hz).\n\n### A. Conveyor Capacity (Based on Transmittable Mass)\n\nThis section defines the conveyor’s capacity in terms of the **transmittable mass** (maximum weight that can be safely conveyed). The calculation is related to the ratio between the input and output belt speed.\n\n### B. Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Operational Performance)\n\nThis table shows the achievable reduction ratio based on the operating frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz) and various fixed operating parameters.\n\n| Ratio/Mass | 50 Hz (Example Values) | 60 Hz (Example Values) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Low Range** | 3.5 – 9.4 | 3.5 – 9.4 |\n| **Mid Range** | 11.3 – 15.7 | 11.3 – 15.7 |\n| **High Range** | 16.1 – 24.8 | 16.1 – 24.8 |\n\n### C. Capacity/Reduction Tables (Detailed Data)\n\nThese large tables provide the specific capacity data for the belts, varying based on both the required reduction ratio and the operating frequency.\n\n**1. Belt Reduction Ratio Table (Conceptual Summary):**\nThis table provides specific capacity values across a range of belts and ratios, demonstrating how the required reduction ratio relates to the resulting capacity (Tons/hr, or mass/hour). The data confirms the correlation between frequency (50 Hz vs. 60 Hz) and operational capacity.\n\n**2. Key Capacity Data (Variable Parameters):**\nThese tables illustrate specific operational points:\n\n*   **Belt/Mass:** Capacity is defined for specific belt parameters (e.g., 4.7, 9.4, 11.3, 16.1) at various operational frequencies.\n*   **Structure:** The capacity is clearly dependent on the combination of:\n    *   Belt type/mass (kg)\n    *   Desired reduction ratio\n    *   Operating frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz)\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data Points\n\n| Feature | 4.3.25 CVSXE | 4.3.26 CVSYE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Function** | High-power, single-belt conveyor | High-power, single-belt conveyor |\n| **Pulley Size** | $\\varnothing$ 30 mm | $\\varnothing$ 30 mm |\n| **Drive Type** | Central drive | Central drive |\n| **Unique Feature** | Medium conveying length | Belt meandering prevention crosspiece |\n| **Voltage Range** | 230 V | 230 V |\n| **Operation** | Highly rated for industrial use (50 Hz / 60 Hz) | Highly rated for industrial use (50 Hz / 60 Hz) |\n\n**In essence, the 4.3.26 CVSYE model is an upgraded version of the 4.3.25 CVSXE, adding a specialized anti-meandering crosspiece, while retaining all core technical specifications.**\n\n[Page 29]\nThis page provides detailed technical specifications, operational data, and performance graphs for two types of full-belt conveyors: **Model 4.3.27 CVSFAE** and **Model 4.3.28 CVSFBE**.\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, including the key technical data, diagrams, and performance tables.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Overview of Conveyor Systems (Misumi)\n\nThe page is dedicated to detailing the installation and operation of full-belt conveyor systems, which are designed for various material handling needs.\n\n### 1. Model 4.3.27 CVSFAE (Installation and Operation)\n\nThis model features smaller pulleys compared to the other unit and has its own specific set of technical dimensions.\n\n#### Key Features & Specifications\n*   **Type:** Full-belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   **Pulleys:** Ø 30 mm.\n*   **Special Feature:** Total surface area usable for transport.\n*   **Technical Dimensions:**\n    *   **Belt Width:** 60–300 mm\n    *   **Length:** 280–2000 mm\n    *   **Weight:** 4.3–16.3 kg\n\n#### 📊 Performance Graphs & Diagrams\nTwo graphs are provided, both showing the relationship between **Conveyor Capacity** and **Belt Speed (Hz)**.\n\n*   **Diagram Description:** The graphs illustrate how conveyor capacity changes relative to the operating frequency (speed). The capacity generally increases as speed increases, showing a specific operating range.\n*   **Data Interpretation:** The curves demonstrate maximum achievable capacity at different operating speeds.\n\n#### 📈 Performance Data Tables\n\nThe data provides specific metrics for Conveyor Capacity and Gearhead Reduction Ratio based on two common operating frequencies (50 Hz and 60 Hz) and corresponding belt widths (30 mm, 50 mm).\n\n**A. Conveyor Capacity (kg/min):**\n*(Capacity is measured in kg/min and varies by operating frequency and belt size.)*\n\n| Belt Width (mm) | 50 Hz (kg/min) | 60 Hz (kg/min) |\n| :-------------- | :------------- | :------------- |\n| 12              | 22.6           | 27.1           |\n| 15              | 18.8           | 22.6           |\n| 20              | 15.7           | 18.8           |\n| 25              | 11.3           | 13.5           |\n| 30              | 9.4            | 11.3           |\n| 35              | 7.8            | 9.4            |\n| 40              | 6.6            | 8.6            |\n| 50              | 4.7            | 5.7            |\n| 60              | 3.8            | 5.6            |\n| 75              | 3.8            | 4.5            |\n| 100             | 3.1            | 3.8            |\n| 120             | 2.8            | 3.4            |\n| 150             | 2.4            | 2.8            |\n| 180             | 2.3            | 2.4            |\n\n**B. Gearhead Reduction Ratio (i):**\n*(The ratio relates the gearhead speed to the belt speed.)*\n\n| Belt Width (mm) | 5