d by common technical information.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ 1. 4.3.39 CVGTBE (Conveyor Drive System)\n\nThis unit is designed for conveyor applications and features robust motor and control elements.\n\n### 🏗️ Key Features\n*   **Application:** Conveyors, double track setup.\n*   **Drive Component:** Features a 50 mm pulley ($\\emptyset$ 50 mm).\n*   **Design:** Includes a dedicated head drive mechanism.\n*   **Adaptability:** Allows for the installation of stoppers and sensors within the gap structure.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Dimensions and Power)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bolt Width** | 80–300 | Bolt Mounting Range |\n| **Length** | 265–300 | Unit Length |\n| **Weight** | 9.1–24.3 kg | Weight Range |\n| **Power Output (W)** | 25–40 W | Variable Power Output |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 220 V | Operating Voltage |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 Hz | Standard Frequency |\n\n### 📈 Performance Data (Converter Efficiency)\nThe graph illustrates the converter efficiency (% of power delivered) versus belt speed, demonstrating the performance at 50 Hz and 60 Hz.\n\n*   **Trend:** Efficiency is highest at low belt speeds. As belt speed increases (moving toward 60 Hz), efficiency tends to decrease, though it remains relatively high across the operational range.\n*   **(Note: Specific numerical data points are best understood by referencing the graph, showing a smooth curve decrease from high efficiency at low speed to moderate efficiency at high speed.)**\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ 2. 4.3.40 CVGTNE (Conveyor Drive System)\n\nThis model is similar in function to the CVGTBE but utilizes a smaller pulley and features a central drive mechanism.\n\n### 🏗️ Key Features\n*   **Application:** Conveyors, double track setup.\n*   **Drive Component:** Features a 30 mm pulley ($\\emptyset$ 30 mm).\n*   **Design:** Utilizes a central drive mechanism.\n*   **Adaptability:** Allows for the installation of stoppers and sensors within the gap structure.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Dimensions and Power)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bolt Width** | 80–300 | Bolt Mounting Range |\n| **Length** | 265–300 | Unit Length |\n| **Weight** | 6.6–13.4 kg | Lighter Weight than CVGTBE |\n| **Power Output (W)** | 25–40 W | Variable Power Output |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 220 V | Operating Voltage |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 Hz | Standard Frequency |\n\n### 📈 Performance Data (Converter Efficiency)\nThe efficiency data is provided in a detailed table, showing the percentage efficiency for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz across various belt speeds.\n\n**Efficiency (%) vs. Belt Speed**\n\n| Belt Speed (Hz) | 50 Hz Efficiency (%) | 60 Hz Efficiency (%) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **5** | 48.0 | 57.6 |\n| **15** | 36.0 | 43.7 |\n| **30** | 28.0 | 36.1 |\n| **45** | 21.6 | 28.0 |\n| **60** | 18.0 | 19.2 |\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Summary of Common/General Information\n\n### Operating Instructions\n*   **Classification:** Conveyor systems - Operating instructions.\n*   **Voltage:** 220V\n*   **Frequency:** 50 Hz\n\n### Further Information\nThe page directs users to supplementary material regarding:\n*   **Connection:** Information available from page 43.\n*   **Belt Characteristics:** Detailed specifications available from page 36.\n*   **Accessories:** Additional accessories information available from page 63.\n\n[Page 36]\nThis summary provides a detailed breakdown of the technical specifications, product features, and performance data for two models of conveyor systems: the **CVGTPE** (timing belt) and the **CVSPAE** (plastic chain).\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Product Overview and Technical Specifications\n\nThe page presents data for two conveyor types, both designed for various industrial applications and offering modular specifications.\n\n### 1. 4.3.41 CVGTPE (Timing Belt Conveyor)\n\nThis model utilizes a timing belt system and is suited for applications requiring precise movement and clean operation.\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Timing belt conveyor (Syncros movement).\n*   Double track capability.\n*   Pulley diameter ($\\phi$): $50 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Central drive mechanism.\n*   Integration for shoppers and sensors (can be installed in the gap).\n\n**Technical Data / Specifications:**\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $80-300$ | $\\text{mm}$ | Available belt width. |\n| **Length** | $325-3000$ | $\\text{mm}$ | Available length range. |\n| **Weight** | $9.8-26.4$ | $\\text{kg}$ | Operational weight range. |\n| **Power Output** | $25-40$ | $\\text{W}$ | Power consumption range. |\n| **Voltage** | $220$ | $\\text{V}$ | Operating voltage. |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ | Operating frequency. |\n\n### 2. 4.3.42 CVSPAE (Plastic Chain Conveyor)\n\nThis model utilizes a durable plastic chain system, ideal for robust environments and heavy loads.\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Plastic chain conveyor.\n*   Double track capability.\n*   Sprocket diameter ($\\phi$): $57 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Head drive mechanism.\n*   Integration for shoppers and sensors (can be installed in the gap).\n\n**Technical Data / Specifications:**\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $80-300$ | $\\text{mm}$ | Available belt width. |\n| **Length** | $300-3000$ | $\\text{mm}$ | Available length range. |\n| **Weight** | $9.6-27.7$ | $\\text{kg}$ | Operational weight range. |\n| **Power Output** | $25-40$ | $\\text{W}$ | Power consumption range. |\n| **Voltage** | $220$ | $\\text{V}$ | Operating voltage. |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | $\\text{Hz}$ | Operating frequency. |\n\n---\n\n## 📈 Performance and Capacity Data (Diagrams and Tables)\n\nThe document includes two graphs illustrating performance based on **Belt Speed** ($\\text{m}/\\text{min}$) and operating frequency ($50 \\text{ Hz}$ vs. $60 \\text{ Hz}$).\n\n### 1. Conveyor Capacity vs. Belt Speed\n\nThis graph shows the maximum weight capacity (in $\\text{kg}$) the conveyor can handle based on its speed.\n\n**Key Trend:** Capacity decreases significantly as belt speed increases. The conveyor generally shows a higher capacity rating at $60 \\text{ Hz}$ compared to $50 \\text{ Hz}$ for similar speeds, though the graph highlights both parameters.\n\n**Data Table (Key Capacity Readings):**\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m}/\\text{min}$) | Capacity ($\\text{kg}$) @ 50 Hz | Capacity ($\\text{kg}$) @ 60 Hz |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $5$ | $32.0$ | $57.6$ |\n| $10$ | $26.7$ | $35.8$ |\n| $15$ | $19.2$ | $23.0$ |\n| $20$ | $15.5$ | $19.2$ |\n| $25$ | $13.3$ | $16.0$ |\n| $30$ | $11.5$ | $13.8$ |\n| $35$ | $9.6$ | $11.5$ |\n| $40$ | $8.0$ | $9.6$ |\n| $50$ | $4.8$ | $6.8$ |\n| $60$ | $3.2$ | $4.8$ |\n\n### 2. Gearhead Reduction Ratio vs. Belt Speed\n\nThis graph illustrates how the gearhead reduction ratio changes with increasing belt speed. The data point values for $50 \\text{ Hz}$ and $60 \\text{ Hz}$ are provided across various speed ranges.\n\n**Key Trend:** The reduction ratio remains relatively stable but decreases slightly as the belt speed increases. The detailed table shows multiple data pairs demonstrating this operational parameter across various speed levels.\n\n**Data Summary (Illustrative Range):**\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m}/\\text{min}$) | Reduction Ratio @ 50 Hz | Reduction Ratio @ 60 Hz |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $5$ | $5.3$ | $35.7$ |\n| $10$ | $7.5$ | $32.0$ |\n| $15$ | $12.5$ | $21.5$ |\n| $20$ | $15.0$ | $19.2$ |\n| $25$ | $18.0$ | $14.9$ |\n| $30$ | $20.0$ | $17.9$ |\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Technical Summary (Key Data Points)\n\n*   **Application Focus:** Both models are equipped with advanced features like double tracking, sensor/shopper integration, and robust drive systems (Central vs. Head drive).\n*   **Operational Range:** Both conveyor types are highly customizable, offering belt widths from $80 \\text{ mm}$ to $300 \\text{ mm}$ and lengths from $300 \\text{ mm}$ to $3000 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   **Power & Electrics:** Both units operate on $220 \\text{ V}$ at $50 \\text{ Hz}$, with a power output range of $25 \\text{ W}$ to $40 \\text{ W}$.\n*   **Limitation:** The capacity of the conveyor is highly sensitive to belt speed; lower speeds allow for heavier loads.\n\n[Page 37]\nThis page is a detailed technical datasheet for two specific conveyor and belt systems: the **4.3.43 CVSSAE** and the **4.3.44 CVDSAE**. The content includes product specifications, special features, technical data tables, and performance graphs illustrating operational ratios.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Product Overview\n\n### 1. 4.3.43 CVSSAE\nThis model is designed for general belt conveyor applications.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Stainless steel belt conveyors.\n    *   Single belt system.\n*   **Dimensions/Components:**\n    *   Pulley diameter ($\\text{Ø}$): 50 mm.\n    *   System function: Head drive.\n    *   Properties: High heat resistance and conductivity.\n\n### 2. 4.3.44 CVDSAE\nThis model is designed for specific industrial applications, particularly those involving foodstuffs.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt conveyor with studs.\n    *   Single belt system.\n    *   Suitable for foodstuffs and sloped design.\n*   **Dimensions/Components:**\n    *   Pulley diameter ($\\text{Ø}$): 50 mm.\n    *   System function: Head drive.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Technical Data and Specifications\n\nThe core electrical and physical specifications are highly consistent across both models, indicating they are part of a single product line operated on standard industrial power.\n\n| Parameter | Unit | 4.3.43 CVSSAE (Range) | 4.3.44 CVDSAE (Range) | Common Specifications |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | mm | 40–150 | 50–300 | N/A |\n| **Length** | mm | 250–2000 | 500–3000 | N/A |\n| **Weight** | kg | 6.7–20.4 | 11.4–37.6 | N/A |\n| **Power Output** | W | 6–25 | 6–25 | N/A |\n| **Voltage** | V | 230 | 230 | 230 V |\n| **Frequency** | Hz | 50 | 50 | 50 Hz |\n\n---\n\n## 📈 Performance Graphs and Reaction Ratio Data\n\nThe graphs illustrate the relationship between the operating speed and the various "Reaction Ratios" for both the conveyor and the gearhead.\n\n### 1. Conveyor Reaction Ratio (Ratio vs. Speed)\nThis curve shows the required ratio for the belt conveyor system.\n*   **Curve Shape:** The ratio decreases as the speed increases, indicating a specific operational relationship between the input speed and the belt's effective speed.\n\n**Data Table (Conveyor Reaction Ratio):**\n\n| Belt Width (mm) | Ratio @ 50 Hz | Ratio @ 60 Hz |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **6** | 15.5 | 18.8 |\n| **12** | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **18** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **25** | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **36** | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **50** | 6.7 | 7.9 |\n| **75** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **100** | 2.6 | 3.1 |\n| **120** | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **150** | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n### 2. Gearhead Reaction Ratio (Ratio vs. Speed)\nThis curve shows the required ratio for the gearhead system.\n*   **Curve Shape:** Similar to the conveyor, the ratio decreases as the speed increases.\n\n**Data Table (Gearhead Reaction Ratio):**\n\n| Belt Width (mm) | Ratio @ 50 Hz | Ratio @ 60 Hz |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **6** | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| **12** | 26.2 | 31.4 |\n| **18** | 15.5 | 18.8 |\n| **25** | 13.7 | 15.7 |\n| **36** | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **50** | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **75** | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **100** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| **120** | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **150** | 1.9 | 2.2 |\n| **180** | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n\n---\n\n## ℹ️ Additional Information\n\nThe page concludes with references directing users to supporting information:\n*   **Further Information:** Available for Connection, Belt characteristics, and Accessories.\n*   **Page References:** Detailed information can be found on pages 43, 36, and 63.\n\n[Page 38]\nThis page provides detailed technical specifications and operational data for two models of industrial conveyor systems: **4.3.45 CVDSBE** and **4.3.46 CVSPCE**. The summary is broken down by product for maximum clarity, including diagrams, key features, technical specifications, and operational capacity tables.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 4.3.45 CVDSBE (Belt Conveyor System)\n\nThis model appears to be a system designed for flat, smooth conveyor belt applications.\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram and Features\n*   **Diagram:** Illustrates a standard belt conveyor system with rollers and a drive mechanism.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Flat belt conveyor with studs, single belt.\n    *   Pulley $\\phi$ 50 mm.\n    *   Head drive.\n    *   Suitable for foodstuffs, sloped design, oil resistant.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Specifications)\n| Parameter | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt width** | 50–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 500–3000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 11.4–37.8 | kg |\n| **Power (input)** | 230 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity and Operation\nThe graph shows the relationship between Conveyor Capacity (presumably load or weight) and Belt Speed (in m/min).\n\n**Capacity Table (Load vs. Speed):**\n\n| Belt speed (m/min) | Capacity (kg) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **50** | 7.5 |\n| **60** | 9.1 |\n| **70** | 12.5 |\n| **80** | 15.7 |\n| **90** | 18.8 |\n| **100** | 22.5 |\n| **110** | 25.8 |\n| **120** | 30.7 |\n| **130** | 33.5 |\n| **140** | 37.7 |\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ 4.3.46 CVSPCE (Chain/Belt Conveyor System)\n\nThis model appears to be a more robust system utilizing a plastic chain or belt structure.\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram and Features\n*   **Diagram:** Illustrates a conveyor system utilizing a chain belt roller and drive mechanism.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Plastic chain conveyors, single belt.\n    *   Chain-wheel $\\phi$ 57 mm.\n    *   Head drive.\n    *   Prevents scratches, easy to maintain.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Specifications)\n| Parameter | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt width** | 50–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 500–3000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 5.3–12.9 | kg |\n| **Power (input)** | 230 | W |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity and Operation\nThe graph shows the relationship between Conveyor Capacity and Belt Speed.\n\n**Capacity Table (Load vs. Speed):**\n\n| Belt speed (m/min) | Capacity (kg) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **50** | 7.5 |\n| **60** | 9.1 |\n| **70** | 12.5 |\n| **80** | 15.7 |\n| **90** | 18.8 |\n| **100** | 22.5 |\n| **110** | 25.8 |\n| **120** | 30.7 |\n| **130** | 33.5 |\n| **140** | 37.7 |\n\n***\n\n## 💡 Key Comparison and Data Summary\n\n| Feature | 4.3.45 CVDSBE | 4.3.46 CVSPCE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Type** | Flat belt (studs) | Plastic chain |\n| **Pulley/Wheel Diameter** | $\\phi$ 50 mm | $\\phi$ 57 mm |\n| **Weight (Range)** | 11.4–37.8 kg | 5.3–12.9 kg |\n| **Purpose/Material** | Foodstuffs, oil resistant | Prevents scratches, easy to maintain |\n| **Power/Voltage** | 230 W / 230 V | 230 W / 230 V |\n| **Max Capacity (Theoretical)** | 37.7 kg @ 140 m/min | 37.7 kg @ 140 m/min |\n\n**Summary Insight:** Both models share identical electrical specifications and maximum listed operational capacities. The key difference lies in the physical structure and application: CVDSBE uses a flat, studded belt suitable for demanding/food contexts, while CVSPCE uses a plastic chain structure designed for cleaner, scratch-preventing operation.\n\n[Page 39]\nThis page provides a detailed guide to conveyor systems, focusing on the selection, application, and technical data for various types of belts and plastic chains.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including tables and key data:\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Conveyor Systems Overview\n\nThe document explains that conveyor systems are designed with different belts depending on the required application, speed, and conditions. The belts are available depending on the specific application.\n\n### 1. Belts (General Guide)\n\nThe guide illustrates several types of belts, detailing their materials and typical applications:\n\n| Belt Type | Illustration | Application | Key Function/Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt** | (Image of flat/woven belt) | All-purpose, Slidding | General conveyor belt usage. |\n| **Flats belts with guide** | (Image of guide belt) | N/A | Used in conjunction with guides. |\n| **Stainless steel belt** | (Image of stainless steel belt) | All-purpose, Slidding | Recommended for food-grade applications. |\n| **Timing belts** | (Image of timing belt) | N/A | Used for power transmission and synchronized movement. |\n| **Plastic chain** | (Image of plastic chain) | Transport of work-pieces | Used for moving objects/workpieces. |\n| **Flat belt conveyor with studs** | (Image of studded belt) | Transport of foodstuffs | Used specifically for food transport. |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Installation and Operation (Cautionary Notes)\n\nThe section under **"CAUTION"** provides critical operational advice:\n\n*   **Damages:** Belt failure can be caused by severe bending or bending radius issues.\n*   **Operation:** Always use specified pulleys and guidance rollers.\n*   **Avoidance:** Do not force the belt to operate; always check the machine before operating it.\n*   **Speed:** Never exceed the belt speed rating.\n*   **Safety:** Use appropriate gloves and guidance rollers!\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Technical Data and Specifications\n\nThe page includes specific technical data tables for belts and chains.\n\n### 💡 Belt Characteristics – Timing Belts\n\nThis table lists technical specifications for timing belts:\n\n| Model | Material | Allowable Tension (N) | Continuous Working Temperature (°C) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **LIBR** | Polyurethane | 120 | -20 to 70 |\n| **LTBRA** | Polyurethane | 150 | 0 to 80 |\n| **LTBJA** | Polyurethane | 150 | 0 to 80 |\n\n### 💡 Belting Characteristics – Plastic Chain\n\nThis table provides data for the plastic chain:\n\n| Model | Material | Allowable Tension (N) | Continuous Working Temperature (°C) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **CHEED** | Polysacetal | 45 | -5 to 85 |\n\n### 💡 General Belting Data\n\n| Parameter | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Permissible chain speed** | 60 | m/min |\n| **Sliding friction coefficient** | 0.32 | - |\n\n---\n***Source:** Misure Conveyor Systems - Operating Instructions*\n\n[Page 40]\nThis page is a detailed technical specification sheet titled **"Belt characteristics - flat belts,"** provided by MISUMI. It functions as a comprehensive product catalog segment, allowing users to select the appropriate flat belt based on its intended **Application** and required **Performance Parameters** (material, elongation, friction, etc.).\n\nSince the provided image is a structured data table and does not contain any conceptual diagrams, the summary will focus on detailing the table structure and transcribing the key technical data.\n\n---\n\n### ⚙️ Summary of Belt Specifications\n\n**Page Context:**\n*   **Company:** MISUMI\n*   **Title:** Belt characteristics - flat belts\n*   **Chapter:** Installation and Operation\n*   **Function:** To provide specific, measurable data points for various flat belt models (MISUMI part numbers) to ensure proper belt selection for a given industrial application.\n\n**Key Technical Parameters Included:**\nThe table is designed around selecting a belt by specifying performance across multiple axes:\n\n1.  **Material Composition:** Both the front side and back side materials are listed (e.g., Polyurethane, Rubber, etc.).\n2.  **Dimensional/Physical Properties:** Thickness/Colour, Minimum Elongation (%), and Curvature Radius (R).\n3.  **Performance Metrics:** Friction coefficient values are given for both the front and back sides, critical for determining slip and grip in operation.\n\n---\n\n### 📑 Comprehensive Data Table\n\nThe page contains a highly dense, tabular dataset. Below is a representative structure and an excerpt of the data, detailing the belts and their specific properties.\n\n**(Note: Due to the length and sheer volume of the data, the following section recreates the structure and key entries. The full table is too extensive for a narrative summary, but the data structure is preserved.)**\n\n| MISUMI Part Numbers | Application | Material (Front side) | Material (Back side) | Thickness/Colours | Min. elongation (%) | Curv. radius (R) | Friction Coefficient (Front side) | Friction Coefficient (Back side) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **HBLT** | - | | | | | | | |\n| **HBLTWH** | - | white | | | 0.8 | 0.9 | 4 | 25 | R3 | 0.80 | 0.3 | 0.1 |\n| **HBLTFG** | All-purpose | green | | | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.8 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTLT** | Operation | Polyurethane | | | 1.7 | 1.9 | 8 | 50 | | | |\n| **HBLTGN** | - | | | | | | | | |\n| **HBLTG** | - | black | | | 1.4 | 1.4 | 8 | 25 | R3 | 0.10 | 0.6 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTGW** | - | Polyurethane | | | 0.5 | 0.4 | 25 | 4 | R3 | 0.1 | 0.6 | 0.1 |\n| **HBLTLWTWH** | Flexible | Polyurethane | | | 0.5 | 1.6 | 4 | 30 | | | |\n| **HBLTWH** | Forciled transport | | | white | | 0.9 | 1 | 35 | R3 | -10.0 | 0.7 | 0.1 |\n| **HBLG** | - | Polyurethane | | | 0.9 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.7 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTLG** | Operation | Polyurethane | | | 1.4 | 0.7 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.8 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTGN** | - | green | | | 1.4 | 0.7 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.8 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTWH** | - | Polyurethane | | | 1.4 | 0.7 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.8 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTWH** | - | white | | | 1.4 | 0.7 | 4 | 15 | R3 | -30.0 | 0.8 | 0.2 |\n| **HBLTFG** | - | - | - | - | - | - | - | - |\n\n---\n\n### 🔑 Key Takeaways & Summary of Functions\n\nThe page serves as a lookup tool for industrial clients. The key data points allow users to perform the following comparisons:\n\n1.  **Material Selection:** Choosing between Polyurethane (PU) or various rubbers, depending on the wear and tear expected.\n2.  **Performance Grading (Friction):** The friction coefficient determines if the belt will grip well (higher coefficient) or if it will slip (lower coefficient) under load.\n3.  **Operational Limits (Elongation/Radius):** The "Min. elongation" and "Curv. radius" parameters specify the operational flexibility and minimum bending size the belt can handle.\n4.  **Application Matching:** By listing specific belts under different names (e.g., HBLTWH vs. HBLTFG), the catalogue directs the user to the precisely engineered belt required for applications like "Flexible transport" or "All-purpose" use.\n\n**In essence, this page is a critical engineering specification sheet detailing the compatibility, material science, and operational limits of MISUMI's flat belt product line.**\n\n[Page 41]\nThis page is a detailed technical specification sheet from MISUMI, titled "Installation and Operation." It provides comprehensive data for various types of conveyor belts, pulleys, and specialized components, categorized by their application, material composition, and mechanical specifications.\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Overall Summary and Scope\n\nThe document is organized into two main sections:\n1.  **Standard Belts and Pulleys:** Components for general transport, handling, and specific industrial applications.\n2.  **Belts with Guides:** Specialized components designed for controlled movement and superior operational characteristics.\n\nThe data provided is highly granular, detailing dimensions, materials (e.g., Polyurethane, Electrically conductive), and critical performance data like the **Friction Coefficient ($\\mu$)**.\n\n*(Note: No diagrams were provided in the source material; the summary relies entirely on structured tables and key data points.)*\n\n***\n\n### ⚙️ Section 1: Standard Belts and Pulleys Data\n\nThis section details general-purpose belts for various functions, from transport to packaging.\n\n#### 📑 Key Components and Applications\n\n| MISUMI Code | Application / Function | Material Type | Belt Side Material | Belt Colour | Use/Feature |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **FHBLT** | General Handling | Polyurethane | N/A | White | General belt use. |\n| **KBLWT** | Transport (General) | N/A | N/A | White | General conveyor belt. |\n| **KBLWN** | Transport (For Food) | N/A | N/A | White | Specialized for food processing. |\n| **KBLWLDN** | Transport (For Food) | N/A | N/A | Polyurethane | Food-grade belt option. |\n| **PHBLT** | General Handling | Polyurethane | N/A | White | General purpose belt. |\n| **PHBLWN** | General Handling | N/A | N/A | White | General purpose belt. |\n| **DHBLT** | Conveyors for electronic components | Polyurethane | N/A | Black | Specialized for sensitive electronic use. |\n| **DHBLTDS** | Conveyors for electronic components | Polyurethane | N/A | Black | Same as DHBLT, emphasizes electronic use. |\n\n#### 📊 Technical Specifications and Performance (Data Extract)\n\nThe tables provide precise specifications for dimensions (thickness, width) and performance coefficients.\n\n| Code | Belt Material | Thickness (mm) | Width (mm) | Belt/Pulley Size (mm) | Friction ($\\mu$) (Concrete) | Friction ($\\mu$) (Steel) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **FHBLT** | Polyurethane | 0.8 | 9 | 3.5 | 20 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWT** | N/A | 0.8 | 7 | 4 | 15 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWN** | N/A | 0.8 | 7 | 4 | 15 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWLDN** | Polyurethane | 1.4 | 5 | 8 | 25(1) | 0.2 |\n| **PHBLT** | Polyurethane | 1.7 | 9 | 5 | 20 | 0.2 |\n| **PHBLWN** | N/A | 1.7 | 6 | 3 | 30 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLT** | Polyurethane | 1.7 | 6 | 3 | 30 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLTDS** | Polyurethane | 0.8 | 7 | 4 | 15 | 0.2 |\n\n***\n\n### ✨ Section 2: Characteristics Belt - Belts with Guides\n\nThis second section details specialized, guided belts, which typically offer improved stability and control.\n\n#### 📑 Key Components and Applications\n\n| MISUMI Code | Application / Function | Material Type | Belt Side Material | Belt Colour | Use/Feature |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **FHBLTDSG** | General Handling | Polyurethane | N/A | Green | Versatile, guided belt. |\n| **KBLHTDSG** | General Handling | N/A |