the compliance process can be summarized conceptually as follows:\n\n**[Inputs/Requirements]** $\\longrightarrow$ **[Testing & Certification]** $\\longrightarrow$ **[Output/Declaration]**\n\n1.  **Inputs (Regulatory Requirements):**\n    *   Supply of Machinery (Safety) Regulations 2008 (UK SI 2008 No. 1597).\n    *   EN 620:2021.\n2.  **Process (Testing & Review):**\n    *   MISUMI Corporation ensures compliance with all required safety elements.\n3.  **Output (Declaration):**\n    *   **UKCA Declaration of Incorporation** is issued by MISUMI, confirming that the machinery models (e.g., CVKSE, CVKSE, etc.) are safe and legally operable in the UK market, with specific exclusions noted (the drive requirement).	\N	2026-04-24 11:23:08	2026-04-24 11:23:08
doc-2feeaec019b1678f1cca564ab3aa98b3	default	unknown_source	[Page 1]\nBased on the image provided, which is the cover page of a user manual, the page contains only textual identification details. Therefore, there are **no tables or diagrams** to summarize.\n\nThe detailed summary below focuses on structuring the available key data and information in a highly organized format, simulating the extraction of data points from the document.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Document Summary: Product Manual Cover Page\n\nThis page serves as the title and identification page for a User Manual, outlining the specific machine model, the type of equipment, and the manufacturing details.\n\n### 🔍 I. Document Identification\n\n| Field | Detail | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Document Type** | User's Manual | Indicates that the document provides operational instructions, usage guidelines, and technical specifications for the machine. |\n| **Product Model** | DK-908 | The specific model number of the machinery. |\n| **Product Name** | DESKTOP EDM HOLE DRILLING MACHINE | Defines the machine's function and primary technology (Electrical Discharge Machining - EDM) used for drilling holes, suitable for desktop use. |\n\n### ⚙️ II. Key Product Data (Specifications Summary)\n\nThis section summarizes the core equipment details.\n\n**Product:** DK-908 Desktop EDM Hole Drilling Machine\n**Primary Function:** Hole Drilling (using EDM technology)\n**Operational Style:** Desktop (implies compact, stable, and self-contained operation)\n**Technology:** EDM (Electrical Discharge Machining)\n\n### 🏭 III. Manufacturer and Publisher Information\n\nThis details the company responsible for manufacturing the equipment and the manual.\n\n| Category | Detail | Location |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Company Name** | Luoyang Xincheng Precision Machinery Co., Ltd. | N/A |\n| **Headquarters/Origin** | Luoyang | China |\n| **Industry Focus** | Precision Machinery (Implies expertise in high-precision, specialized industrial equipment) | N/A |\n\n***\n\n### 📊 Summary of Absence of Data Types\n\nThe page is purely textual and does not contain the following types of visual or structured data:\n\n*   **Tables:** No technical specifications tables (e.g., voltage ranges, material capacity, cycle times) are present.\n*   **Diagrams:** No diagrams, flow charts, or component illustrations are visible.\n*   **Key Data (beyond identity):** No operational warnings, safety guidelines, or model serial number placeholders are visible.\n\n[Page 2]\nThis document provides comprehensive safety, installation, and operational guidelines for the use of industrial equipment (likely a machine involving electrodes and machining).\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, structured using tables and flow diagrams to ensure clarity and rapid reference for all critical operational requirements.\n\n---\n\n# 🛠️ Detailed Equipment Operational Guide Summary\n\n## 📋 I. Usage Prerequisites (Mandatory Start-Up)\n\nBefore operating the machine, the user must adhere to strict guidelines to ensure longevity and safety.\n\n| Guideline Area | Requirement | Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Manual Proficiency** | **READ Thoroughly** | The operator must fully understand the machine's operation methods before initiating work. |\n| **Maintenance Focus** | **Condition Monitoring** | Operate the machine in a manner that maintains the best possible operational condition over time. |\n| **General Rule** | **Observation** | Strictly observe all listed safety and operational items (detailed below). |\n\n## ⚠️ II. Safety Concerns (Immediate Safety Protocols)\n\nSafety is paramount. These points must be checked *before* every operation.\n\n| Item No. | Concern | Required Action / Warning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | **Grounding** | **MANDATORY:** Confirm that the ground wire is properly connected. |\n| **2** | **Contact Hazard** | **DO NOT TOUCH:** Never manually touch the electrodes or the workpieces. |\n| **3** | **Enclosure Security** | **ALWAYS CLOSE:** The lower part of the machine and the door of the operation box must be fully closed during use. |\n\n## 🏡 III. Installation and Environmental Controls\n\nThese guidelines dictate where and how the machine should be positioned to prevent damage and ensure safe operation.\n\n### 📍 Installation Location Checklist\n\n| Prohibition | Reason / Concern | Correct Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Temperature Fluctuation** | Direct sunlight exposure can cause drastic temperature changes. | Locate the machine in a stable, shaded environment. |\n| **Vibration/Impact** | High vibrations (e.g., those from punching) can damage the unit. | Install the machine away from high-vibration areas. |\n| **Contamination** | Dust, trash, or debris accumulation. | Ensure the area is clean and free of accumulating contaminants. |\n| **Worksurface Cleanliness** | Prevents accurate operation and material movement. | Keep the cabinet and operation box clear of items. Only the workpiece should be placed on the workbench. |\n\n### 📐 Placement Summary\n\n*   **Optimal Position:** Must be installed correctly and horizontally.\n*   **DO NOT:** Place items on the cabinet or operation box.\n*   **Workbench Rule:** Only the workpiece is permitted on the workbench surface.\n\n## 🛠️ IV. Operational Procedures & Maintenance\n\nThese points cover preparing for, conducting, and shutting down the process.\n\n| Phase | Requirement | Action Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pre-Process** | **Preparation** | Clean the work area after the completion of the previous job to prepare for the next. |\n| **Maintenance** | **Long-Term Care** | Various scheduled maintenance routines must be implemented regularly. |\n| **System Check** | **Operational Flow** | Ensure all connections and components are secure before starting. |\n\n### 🛑 Critical Shutdown Protocol (Key Data Alert)\n\nThe sequence of turning off the machine at the end of the process is extremely important to prevent electrical malfunction.\n\n**PROCEDURE FLOW:**\n\n1. **End Process:** The machining process concludes.\n2. **Separation:** The electrode copper tube **MUST** be physically separated from the work piece.\n3. **Power Down:** The machine power must be turned off.\n\n**⛔ CRITICAL WARNING (Failure to Follow):**\nIf the power is turned back on while the electrode copper tube is still in contact with the work piece, it will result in **MALFUNCTION**.\n\n---\n***In summary, the operational process requires thorough preparation, strict adherence to grounding and enclosure safety, environmental stability, and a mandatory, careful sequence during machine shutdown.***\n\n[Page 3]\nBased on the image provided, which is a Table of Contents, the summary details the structure and content flow of a comprehensive technical manual, likely for a complex machine or industrial process.\n\nSince the page is only a Contents page, there are no actual diagrams or unique data points to include other than the section titles and page numbers. However, the structure below organizes this key data into a detailed, professional summary format.\n\n***\n\n## 📑 Detailed Summary of Contents Page\n\nThis document outlines the contents of a comprehensive technical manual, designed to guide the reader through every stage of working with a specific machine or process. The manual follows a logical progression, moving from initial setup and understanding (**Sections 1–5**) to operational procedures (**Sections 6–7**), and finally to maintenance, troubleshooting, and compliance (**Sections 8–12**).\n\n### I. Overview of Manual Structure\n\nThe manual is divided into three major operational phases:\n\n1.  **Setup & Theory (Pages 3–9):** Covering basic theory, identification, and physical installation.\n2.  **Operation & Problem Solving (Pages 17–32):** Detailing the "how-to" guides—how to use the machine, what to do when it fails, and how it works mechanically/electrically.\n3.  **Support & Compliance (Pages 36–39):** Addressing long-term support, electrical schematics, compliance standards, and required parts.\n\n***\n\n### II. Key Data Table: Table of Contents Breakdown\n\nThe following table summarizes the major sections, their primary function, and their starting page number.\n\n| Section No. | Section Title | Key Focus / Purpose | Starting Page |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: |\n| **1** | **SUMMARY** | Provides a high-level overview and purpose statement for the entire machine or manual. | 3 |\n| **2** | **MACHINE COMPOSITION** | Detailed breakdown of the machine's physical parts and overall structure. | 4 |\n| **3** | **MACHINE PARAMETER** | Specifies the technical specifications, dimensions, and operational limits (e.g., power, speed, capacity). | 8 |\n| **4** | **INSTALLATION** | Step-by-step guide for physically setting up the machine in its working environment. | 9 |\n| **5** | **PARTS NAME AND FUNCTIONS** | A glossary or detailed listing of specific components and what each part does. | 17 |\n| **6** | **PROCESSING ORDER** | The standard, step-by-step procedure for operating the machine correctly. | 22 |\n| **7** | **ABNORMAL PROCESSING CONCERNS** | Troubleshooting initial operational errors, warnings, and abnormal outputs during processing. | 27 |\n| **8** | **MAINTENANCE AND INSPECTION** | Procedures for routine upkeep, preventative maintenance, and safety checks. | 30 |\n| **9** | **TROUBLESHOOTING PROCESS** | Advanced guide for diagnosing and resolving mechanical or operational failures. | 32 |\n| **10** | **ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM** | Technical schematic detailing the electrical connections and components. | 36 |\n| **11** | **CERTIFICATE OF COMPLIANCE** | Documentation proving that the machine meets necessary industry and safety standards. | 38 |\n| **12** | **SPARE PARTS PACKING LIST** | A complete manifest of necessary replacement parts and their quantities/identifiers. | 39 |\n\n***\n\n### III. Summary by Operational Phase\n\nTo provide a deeper understanding of the manual's flow, the sections can be grouped into three logical operational phases:\n\n#### 💡 Phase 1: Initial Setup and Identification (Sections 1–5)\nThis phase is for the technician or user who is first engaging with the equipment. It establishes foundational knowledge:\n*   **SUMMARY (3):** Provides the purpose.\n*   **MACHINE COMPOSITION (4):** Shows the anatomy.\n*   **MACHINE PARAMETER (8):** Defines the limits and specs.\n*   **INSTALLATION (9):** Details how to put it in place.\n*   **PARTS NAME AND FUNCTIONS (17):** Provides the dictionary of parts.\n\n#### ⚙️ Phase 2: Operation and Maintenance (Sections 6–9)\nThis is the core operational guide, detailing usage and problem-solving.\n*   **PROCESSING ORDER (22):** The "how-to" guide for daily use.\n*   **ABNORMAL PROCESSING CONCERNS (27):** What to do when minor operational issues occur.\n*   **MAINTENANCE AND INSPECTION (30):** How to keep it running smoothly.\n*   **TROUBLESHOOTING PROCESS (32):** Advanced solutions for major failures.\n\n#### 🔩 Phase 3: Technical Support and Compliance (Sections 10–12)\nThis phase is crucial for advanced technical staff, compliance officers, and supply chain managers.\n*   **ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM (36):** Essential for electrical repair and diagnosis.\n*   **CERTIFICATE OF COMPLIANCE (38):** Documentation for legal and safety verification.\n*   **SPARE PARTS PACKING LIST (39):** Inventory management and procurement information.\n\n[Page 4]\nThis document provides a detailed technical summary of the DK-908 Electro Discharge Machining (EDM) Hole Drilling machine. Since the source material consists of minimal text and a large technical diagram, the summary is structured to incorporate the text summary, a technical data table, and a detailed description of the visual apparatus (diagram).\n\n***\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary of the DK-908 EDM Hole Drilling Machine\n\n### I. General Overview and Purpose (Section 1. SUMMARY)\n\nThe DK-908 machine is a specialized piece of industrial equipment designed for **EDM Hole Drilling**.\n\n**Core Function:** The primary purpose of the machine is to create precise holes by utilizing the principles of electrical discharge (spark erosion).\n\n**Working Principle:** The process involves using a **thin metal copper tube**, which serves as the electrode. This copper electrode is driven in a controlled **vertical up and down motion** against the target metal workpiece. The drilling action is achieved through the controlled passage of electrical sparks (spark discharge), which rapidly erodes the metal material to form a hole.\n\n### II. Key Data and Technical Specifications (Table Format)\n\n| Attribute | Detail | Description / Function |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Model** | DK-908 | The specific designation for the EDM Hole Drilling apparatus. |\n| **Operation Type** | EDM (Electro Discharge Machining) | Non-contact material removal process using electric sparks. |\n| **Process Goal** | Hole Drilling | Creating precise bore holes in metal materials. |\n| **Electrode Material** | Copper Tube (Thin Metal) | The consumable electrode used for machining. |\n| **Movement Pattern** | Vertical Up and Down | Defines the controlled feed and retraction motion of the electrode. |\n| **Energy Source** | Spark Discharge | The mechanism by which material removal (erosion) occurs. |\n| **Scope of Manual** | Entire Process & Maintenance | Indicates that the accompanying manual covers setup, operation, and upkeep. |\n\n### III. Detailed Diagrammatic Analysis (Diagram Inclusion)\n\nThe page features one large, highly technical **line diagram** that illustrates the physical setup and operational components of the DK-908 machine. This diagram is the core visual representation and shows the integration of multiple systems.\n\n**Structure Description:**\n\n1.  **The Main Processing Unit:** This is the largest component, housing the working mechanism and the electrodes. It features the primary drilling head assembly.\n2.  **The Control System:** Located on the right side, the control panel is visible, indicating the integration of controls for power, feed rate, voltage, and process parameters.\n3.  **Power and Connection Points:** The diagram shows various input ports, cables, and connection points, confirming that the machine is electrically powered and requires complex peripheral connections for operation.\n4.  **The Working Mechanism:** The central vertical assembly depicts the physical trajectory of the electrode (the copper tube) as it moves up and down to perform the drilling action.\n\n**Interpretation:** The diagram confirms that the DK-908 is a sophisticated, integrated system requiring careful mechanical and electrical setup, supporting the manual's claim that it covers the "Entire Process."\n\n### IV. Summary Synthesis (Conclusion)\n\nIn summary, the DK-908 is a dedicated industrial tool utilizing the electro-discharge principle for drilling precision holes. Its efficiency relies on the controlled vertical movement of a copper electrode which achieves material removal through intense electrical sparking. The accompanying documentation (implied by the footer) is comprehensive, guiding the operator through the entire lifecycle of the machine—from initial setup and operation to routine maintenance.\n\n[Page 5]\nThis page, titled **"2. MACHINE COMPOSITION"** and **"MACHINE EXTERNAL IDENTIFICATION DIAGRAM,"** provides a detailed technical breakdown and schematic view of a complex industrial machine. The document’s primary purpose is to identify and list all the structural and functional components that make up the complete operational unit.\n\nSince the content is highly illustrative, the summary is structured using a **System Overview, Component Table,** and a **Diagrammatic Description** to capture all the required detail.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Detailed Summary: Machine Composition\n\n### I. System Overview and Introduction\n\nThe document establishes that the machine is a composite system, meaning it is assembled from several major subsystems. According to the introductory text, the machine is mainly composed of:\n\n1.  **Machine Head:** The primary operational component.\n2.  **Box Part:** The main structural housing/base.\n3.  **Working Fluid Supply Unit:** Component responsible for providing necessary fluids/pressure.\n4.  **Processing Power Supply Unit:** Provides the required electrical power.\n5.  **Processing Control Device:** Manages and regulates the entire operational sequence.\n\n### II. Key Data: Component Breakdown (Table)\n\nThe following table lists all identifiable components shown in the diagram, representing the key data of the machine's structure:\n\n| Component Name | Function/Purpose | Location (Relative) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Head** | The central operational unit, responsible for the primary processing task. | Upper/Central Assembly |\n| **Working Lamp** | A component associated with illumination or a specific processing function. | Integrated near the Machine Head |\n| **Processing Control Device** | The "brain" of the system; manages the operation and regulates machine functions. | Upper/Side Unit |\n| **Processing Power Supply Unit** | Provides the necessary electrical power input to run the machine. | Lower/Front Section |\n| **Box Part** | The main base structure, providing stability and housing other components. | Bottom/Base |\n| **Pressure Regulating Valve** | Controls and stabilizes the fluid pressure within the system. | Integrated into the upper mechanics |\n| **Working Bench Department** | A designated support or work area within the assembly. | Upper/Flanking the central unit |\n| **Hand Pump** | A manual mechanism used for localized operation or priming. | Upper/Mid-section |\n| **Power Load Switch** | Safety and operational switch to control electrical load. | Integrated into the lower assembly |\n| **Processing Industry Supply Device** | A secondary unit providing industrial fluid or power supply. | Left side, near the base |\n\n### III. Diagrammatic Analysis (Schematic Representation)\n\nThe core of the page is a large, cross-sectional schematic diagram that visually represents the *Machine External Identification Diagram*.\n\n#### A. Structural Layout Description\n\nThe machine is depicted as a modular assembly, built upon a foundational base (the **Box Part**). The major functional units are stacked and integrated around this base:\n\n1.  **The Base Layer:** The **Box Part** forms the foundational structure. The **Processing Power Supply Unit** and associated controls like the **Power Load Switch** are mounted directly onto or into this base.\n2.  **The Mechanical Core:** Above the base, the **Processing Industry Supply Device** is positioned, along with mechanical elements like the **Pressure Regulating Valve** and the **Hand Pump**.\n3.  **The Operational Head:** The **Machine Head** is the uppermost and most complex unit. It is connected to the **Working Lamp** and is centrally located.\n4.  **Control and Integration:** The **Processing Control Device** is mounted adjacent to the Machine Head. The entire system is supported and stabilized by the **Working Bench Department**.\n\n#### B. Key Operational Flow (Inferred)\n\nThe diagram suggests a clear flow of power and control:\n\n*   **Power:** Electricity flows into the **Processing Power Supply Unit** and is regulated by the **Power Load Switch**.\n*   **Control:** The **Processing Control Device** takes input power and manages the functions of the **Machine Head** and the auxiliary components (e.g., the working fluid pressure regulated by the **Pressure Regulating Valve**).\n*   **Operation:** The combined action of the Machine Head, working fluid, and electrical power allows the machine to perform its intended industrial process.\n\n***\n\n### Summary Conclusion\n\nIn essence, the page provides a comprehensive engineering map of a specialized industrial machine. It systematically identifies all major components—ranging from the structural housing (Box Part) to the primary operational components (Machine Head) and regulatory systems (Processing Control Device)—demonstrating how they are physically integrated into a single, functioning unit.\n\n[Page 6]\nThis page provides a detailed technical overview and assembly diagram of a **Machine Head**, which is a critical component used in machining operations.\n\nHere is a detailed, structured summary, including the functions, component breakdowns, and a key data table.\n\n***\n\n## 📄 Summary: Machine Head Internal Assembly Diagram\n\n### 💡 General Overview and Purpose\n\nThe Machine Head is a complex assembly mounted on a column structure. Its primary function is to facilitate precision machining, utilizing sophisticated movements such as **servo feed** (automatic, controlled feeding) and **W-axis movement** (counter shaft movement). The central mechanism is the spindle system, which drives the cutting or working process.\n\n---\n\n### 📜 Key Textual Components and Functionality\n\nThe text identifies the primary functional units of the machine head:\n\n1.  **Spindle System:** This is highlighted as the *main unit* responsible for executing the servo feed necessary for machining tasks. It provides the motive power and controlled motion for the working parts.\n2.  **Counter Shaft (W-axis system):** This system is integral to the head and manages movements along the W-axis, providing secondary positioning capabilities.\n3.  **Rotating Head:** This unit is attached to the structure and is the primary point of action, rotating to engage with the workpiece.\n\n### 🔩 Diagrammatic Component Breakdown\n\nThe accompanying diagram illustrates the physical structure and labels the individual components of the assembly.\n\n| Component Name | Location/Relation | Implied Function |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Column** | Main structural support | Provides the fixed mounting base for the entire assembly. |\n| **Pressure Gauge Seat / Manual Lifting** | Mounted on the column | Suggests an integrated feature for monitoring pressure or manually adjusting the height/positioning of the head. |\n| **Z-Axis Motor** | Actuator within the assembly | Provides the motive force for movement along the Z-axis (depth/feed axis). |\n| **Z-Axis Screw** | Transmits motor force | Converts the rotational movement of the motor into precise linear motion along the Z-axis. |\n| **Rotating Head** | The working end of the machine | The component that physically rotates and performs the cutting/feeding action. |\n| **Linear Guides** | Structural supports | Ensures smooth, controlled, and precise linear movement of the head assembly. |\n| **Guiding Device** | Structural mechanism | Assists in maintaining the proper alignment and guided motion of the working parts. |\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Key Data Summary Table\n\nThis table consolidates the components, their purpose, and the overall system function.\n\n| System Element | Sub-Components / Actuators | Core Function | Importance / Role |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Movement System** | Z-Axis Motor, Z-Axis Screw, Linear Guides | Controls linear and depth movement (Z-axis) of the working head. | Ensures controlled, precise feed for machining. |\n| **Rotation System** | Rotating Head, Spindle System | Provides rotary motion for the working end of the machine. | Executes the primary cutting or processing action. |\n| **Axis Movement** | Counter Shaft (W-axis system) | Provides movement along a secondary axis (W-axis). | Adds dimensional flexibility to the machining process. |\n| **Structure & Support** | Column, Linear Guides, Guiding Device | Forms the physical structure and guarantees stable alignment. | Maintains precision and structural integrity. |\n\n***\n*In essence, the Machine Head is a highly integrated electromechanical system designed to deliver controlled, multi-axis motion (Z, W, and Rotation) for high-precision industrial machining.*\n\n[Page 7]\nThis page provides a detailed description and a visual blueprint of the **Box Internal Assembly**, which serves as the core functional unit of the device.\n\nBelow is a comprehensive, detailed summary, including structural tables and a description of the diagram.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: 2. Box Internal Assembly\n\n### 🔍 I. Overview and Function\n\nThe "Box" is the central unit responsible for operational fluid supply and electrical processing power supply.\n\n**Primary Function:**\n1. **Fluid Management:** Providing a working fluid supply (via the Water Tank and Pump).\n2. **Power Processing:** Supplying electrical power (via the Transformer and Electrical Installation Board).\n3. **Thermal Management:** Ensuring optimal cooling and ventilation for components.\n\n**Structural Advantages:**\nThe internal structure is designed for maximum efficiency, offering multiple benefits beyond simple operation:\n*   **Maintenance and Installation:** It is organized into two distinct, separated spaces.\n*   **Cooling Efficiency:** It provides better overall ventilation and cooling effects.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 II. Structural Composition\n\nThe Box is structurally divided into two main operational zones:\n\n| Zone | Function | Components Associated |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Working Fluid Supply Device** | Manages the physical fluid necessary for the process. | Water Tank, Water Pump, Motor, Air Inlet/Outlet. |\n| **Processing Power Supply Unit** | Converts, controls, and distributes the electrical energy. | Transformer, Drive Resistance, High Frequency Resistance, Electrical Installation Board. |\n\n---\n\n### ⚙️ III. Key Components and Systems (Table Format)\n\nThe following table lists the major components identified in the "Box Internal Assembly Diagram" and their functional roles.\n\n| Component Name | Type / System | Primary Function | Notes / Role |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Water Tank** | Fluid Storage | Stores the working fluid necessary for the process. | Reservoir for the fluid cycle. |\n| **Water Pump** | Fluid Mover | Drives the working fluid through the system. | Maintains necessary fluid circulation. |\n| **Motor** | Power Source | Provides mechanical power to the pump. | Drives the Water Pump. |\n| **Air Inlet / Air Outlet** | Ventilation | Controls the exchange of air within the unit. | Facilitates overall ventilation and cooling. |\n| **Fan** | Ventilation | Moves air through the unit. | Contributes to improved cooling efficiency. |\n| **Transformer** | Electrical Power | Steps down/up the electrical voltage. | Processes incoming electrical power. |\n| **High Frequency Resistance** | Electrical Load/Component | Handles high-frequency electrical demands. | Part of the power processing stage. |\n| **Drive Resistance** | Electrical Load/Component | Handles resistance related to motor or drive action. | Part of the power processing stage. |\n| **Electrical Installation Board** | Control/Wiring | Houses the central wiring and control systems. | Manages the operational flow of power. |\n\n---\n\n### 📐 IV. Diagrammatic Representation (Assembly Flow)\n\nThe diagram illustrates a defined operational flow, moving from power input, through processing, and finally to physical output.\n\n**A. Power Flow (Top Section):**\n*   Power enters the system and passes through the **Electrical Installation Board**.\n*   The power is managed by the **Transformer**, which then feeds into the **High Frequency Resistance** and **Drive Resistance**.\n\n**B. Fluid Flow (Bottom Section):**\n*   The fluid cycle starts with the **Water Tank** and is driven by the **Motor** and **Water Pump**.\n\n**C. Combined Operational Flow (Cooling/Exhaust):**\n*   The entire system is enclosed and relies on **Air Inlet** and **Air Outlet**.\n*   The **Fan** assists in drawing or expelling air, ensuring that the dual structure maintains optimal cooling and ventilation.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Summary of Key Data Points (Structured Checklist)\n\n| Feature | Detail | Importance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Structure** | Dual-spaced design (Fluid Supply / Power Supply). | Enhances maintenance access and efficiency. |\n| **Power Components** | Transformer, Electrical Installation Board, Drive Resistance. | Ensures regulated and controlled power output. |\n| **Fluid Components** | Water Tank, Water Pump, Motor. | Guarantees constant working fluid circulation. |\n| **Thermal Control** | Fan, Air Inlet, Air Outlet. | Key for optimal ventilation and cooling performance. |\n\n[Page 8]\nThis page provides a highly detailed engineering schematic and technical specification for the **Workbench** component within a larger machine system, titled **"Machine Tool Transmission Pattern."**\n\nThe summary is divided into three main sections: the structural components, the dimensional data, and the operational coordinate system.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 1. Workbench Component Diagram (Structural Overview)\n\nThe workbench is shown in a detailed vertical cross-section, illustrating its layered, multi-stage construction from top to bottom.\n\n### A. Components List and Assembly Order\nThe workbench is built from several distinct parts, stacked sequentially:\n\n1.  **COLUMN:** The uppermost structural element.\n2.  **MARBLE:** A smooth, solid surface situated below the column.\n3.  **WATER TRAY:** Located beneath the marble, designated for potential fluid management (cooling or lubrication).\n4.  **UPPER BOARD:** The first main structural board.\n5.  **MIDDLE BOARD:** A board situated in the center of the stack.\n6.  **LOWER BOARD:** The bottom primary support board.\n7.  **PALLET BASE:** The foundational base upon which the entire structure rests.\n\n### B. Dimensional Data (Key Measurements)\nThe diagram provides three primary dimensional measurements for the entire unit:\n\n| Dimension | Value (Units) | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Overall Height** | 970 | The total vertical height of the workbench structure. |\n| **Width (X-Axis)** | 480 | The horizontal dimension across the front/rear face. |\n| **Depth (Y-Axis)** | 710 | The horizontal dimension from side to side (or front to back, depending on orientation). |\n\n***\n\n## 📐 2. Work Bench Diagram (Schematic Diagram)\n\nThe diagram itself serves as the primary technical reference, establishing the physical relationship between the components and the measurements:\n\n*   **Vertical Flow:** The overall height of 970 spans the distance from the Column down to the Pallet Base.\n*   **Horizontal Footprint:** The base is defined by the measurements of 480 and 710.\n*   **Layering:** The diagram visually confirms the stacked nature of the components (Upper Board $\\rightarrow$ Middle Board $\\rightarrow$ Lower Board $\\rightarrow$ Pallet Base).\n\n***\n\n## 🌐 3. Operational Coordinates (Coordinate System)\n\nThe second diagram establishes the machine's spatial working volume using a standard three-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system. This defines how tools and parts will interact with the workbench.\n\n### A. Axes Definition\nThe machine utilizes three primary, perpendicular axes:\n\n*   **Z-Axis (Vertical):** Represents the vertical dimension (Upward is the positive direction, Z+). This is typically the primary motion axis for vertical movement.\n*   **X-Axis (Horizontal/Front):** Represents the dimension pointing forward (Forward is the positive direction, X+).\n*   **Y-Axis (Horizontal/Side):** Represents the dimension pointing sideways (Right is the positive direction, Y+).\n\n### B. Interpretation\nThe coordinate system defines the *working volume* for the machine tool system, providing a reference frame for programming and assembly.\n\n***\n\n## 📋 Summary Table: Key Data Extraction\n\n| Feature | Component/Axis | Measurement/Direction | Technical Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Structural Height** | Workbench (Total) | 970 | Overall vertical clearance/size. |\n| **Horizontal Dimension 1** | 