ance of the spindle in the Z-direction. |\n| **Maximum Distance Between Guide And Workbench** | (mm) | 320 | Max operational spacing. |\n\n### 🚀 3.3 Main Technical Parameters\n\nThis section covers the performance capabilities, power requirements, and operational systems of the machine.\n\n| Parameter | Specification/Unit | Value | Details |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Diameter Range** | (mm) | $\\Phi$ 0.3 ~ $\\Phi$ 3.0 | Range of electrode sizes supported. |\n| **Spindle (Z-Axis) Maximum Feeding Speed** | (mm/min) | 750 | Maximum feed rate capability. |\n| **Rotary Head (C Axis) Speed** | (r/min) | 0 ~ 500 | Speed range of the rotary head. |\n| **Input Voltage** | (V/HZ) | 3 Phase * AC240V 50HZ | Electrical power requirement. |\n| **Maximum Input Power** | (KVA) | 3.3 | Maximum power draw. |\n| **Maximum Machining Current** | (A) | 30 | Maximum operating current. |\n| **Machine Head (X Axis) Secondary Lifting Form** | N/A | Manual | Operational control method. |\n| **Digital Display** | N/A | TWO AXIS | Type of primary user display. |\n| **Working Fluid Supply Device** | N/A | Electric Piston Pump | Mechanism for fluid circulation. |\n| **Maximum Working Fluid Pressure** | (Mpa) | 7 | Maximum operational pressure. |\n\n***\n\n### 🔬 Key Data Takeaways\n\n*   **Machine Identity:** Item No. DK-908.\n*   **Dimensions:** The machine is substantial, measuring $1000 \\text{ mm} \\times 1000 \\text{ mm} \\times 1900 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   **Core Capacity:** It supports a maximum working load of **300 KG**.\n*   **Power:** It requires a 3-Phase, AC240V, 50HZ input, with a maximum input power of **3.3 KVA**.\n*   **Precision:** The machine operates with a fine minimum reading unit of **0.005 mm**.\n*   **Movement:** Key motion controls include a **380 mm** servo stroke on the Z-axis (Spindle) and a maximum X-axis travel of **300 mm** and Y-axis of **400 mm**.\n*   **Fluid System:** It operates under a working fluid pressure of **7 Mpa**, supplied by an Electric Piston Pump.\n\n[Page 10]\nThis page provides detailed instructions and precautions for the safe **Installation and Transport** of the machinery, specifically focusing on the transport procedures.\n\nGiven the technical nature of the content, the summary is structured using procedural steps, key warnings, and a description of the included diagram to provide maximum detail.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Detailed Summary: Machine Installation & Transport\n\n### ⚙️ 4.1 Transport Procedures (Key Data & Steps)\n\nThe document outlines specific methods for lifting and moving the machine to ensure structural integrity and operator safety.\n\n#### **1. Lifting Method**\n*   **Required Point:** Use the eyebolts located on the side of the machine's pallet base.\n*   **Action:** Lift the machine solely using these designated points.\n\n#### **2. Transporting/Moving the Machine**\n*   **Support:** When transporting, a **skid** must be used.\n*   **Purpose:** The skid is used specifically to support the bottom cover of the machine head.\n\n#### **3. General Handling Precautions (Safety & Operational Warnings)**\nThese are critical guidelines that must be adhered to prevent damage to the machine components:\n\n| Component/Area | Action Required | Warning/Prohibition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Stainless Steel Guide Plate** | N/A | **Do not** touch this plate during transport. |\n| **External Parts/Structure** | N/A | Do not use any parts related to the machine (e.g., spindles, guides, workbenches, sheet metal, etc.) as a fulcrum or leverage point. |\n| **Overall Handling** | Ensure careful maneuvering. | Avoid unnecessary stress or contact with vulnerable mechanical components. |\n\n### 📐 Diagram Summary: Rope Loop Transport\n\nA diagram titled "Rope Loop Transport Diagram" is provided.\n\n*   **Method Illustrated:** This diagram demonstrates the correct method for hoisting and lifting the machine using a rope loop system.\n*   **Principle:** The lifting mechanism must distribute force evenly and utilize the designated lifting points (eyebolts) to ensure the machine is raised safely and stably.\n\n### 💡 Summary Table of Best Practices\n\n| Process | Tool/Location Used | Critical Action/Precaution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Lifting** | Eyebolts (on pallet base) | Use only designated lifting points. |\n| **Movement/Support** | Skid | Support the bottom cover of the machine head. |\n| **Safety** | All Parts | **NEVER** use external parts as fulcrums. |\n| **Safety** | Stainless Steel Guide Plate | **DO NOT** touch the guide plate. |\n\n[Page 11]\nThis page provides detailed technical instructions and guidelines for the proper setup, installation, and environmental placement of a large industrial machine (likely an automated loading/unloading or press machine).\n\nThe summary is divided into three main sections: the technical diagram, the physical setup procedure, and the critical guidelines for placement and environment.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Machine Installation and Placement\n\n### 1. Diagrammatic Representation\n**Title:** (5) Forklift Loading And Unloading Diagram\n**Key Data:** The diagram visually represents the structural components of the machine, including the frame, operational area, and most importantly, the **adjustable machine feet** and the structure for handling forklift loading/unloading. This diagram is crucial for understanding the required structural support and the adjustment points.\n\n### 2. Installation Procedure (Section 6)\nThis section details the initial steps required to prepare the machine for operation, focusing on foundation adjustment.\n\n**Procedure Steps:**\n1. **Preparation:** Remove fixed wooden strips and the stow-wood feet from the machine base.\n2. **Adjustment:** Locate the adjustable machine feet.\n3. **Calibration:** Adjust the nut of the adjustable machine feet until the machine is perfectly level and stable.\n4. **Final Check:** Ensure the feet are set at the correct height to ensure proper function and support.\n\n### 3. Placement Environment and Adjustment (Section 4.2)\nThis section provides critical checks that must be completed *before* the machine is put into service. The focus is on ensuring the facility and environment can support the machine's operation and stability.\n\n#### 🌎 Key Environmental and Structural Requirements\n\nA structured check list summarizes the required conditions:\n\n| Component | Requirement/Guideline | Critical Action/Measure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(1) Placement Ground** | Must be structurally robust. | The foundation must be capable of withstanding the entire weight of the machine. |\n| **(2) Vibration Countermeasure** | Must ensure stability during operation. | Place the machine on a stable ground that is free of vibrations. If adjacent machines cause vibration, install a **rubber buffer** system. |\n| **(3) Environment** | Must be stable and protected. | The ideal location requires: <ul><li>Minimal temperature fluctuation.</li><li>Low levels of dust or debris (garbage).</li><li>Protection from direct sunlight.</li></ul> |\n| **(4) Level Adjustment** | Must ensure optimal operational alignment. | Use the adjustable feet system (as described in Section 6) to guarantee the machine is perfectly level. |\n\n***\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaways (Summary Points)\n\n*   **Stability is Paramount:** The most emphasized safety and operational concern is maintaining a stable foundation, free from vibration and able to handle the machine's full weight.\n*   **Accessibility:** The design incorporates dedicated points for **forklift loading and unloading**, making logistics integral to the setup.\n*   **Precision Setup:** The machine cannot be operated until the adjustable feet have been properly set to achieve perfect level and alignment.\n*   **Proactive Mitigation:** Users are instructed to proactively mitigate environmental risks (temperature swings, dust, external vibrations) to prevent operational failure and maintain accuracy.\n\n[Page 12]\nThis page provides detailed operational guidelines, technical specifications, and schematic diagrams for setting up and running a machine, focusing specifically on the Water Circulation and Water Pump systems.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including key data, diagrams, and technical tables.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Operational Guidelines and Setup\n\n### A. Initial Adjustment Procedure\nWhen performing the horizontal adjustment, the following steps are mandatory:\n1.  **Level Adjustment:** Adjust the level at the center of the workbench.\n2.  **Fine-Tuning:** Adjust the level of the machine's four feet.\n3.  **Specification:** Ensure the adjustment is done within **$0.4 \\text{mm}/M$**.\n4.  **Completion:** After adjustment, tighten the nuts on the machine's feet screw and confirm that the machine's feet are in full contact with the ground.\n\n### B. Environmental and Electrical Requirements\n*   **Room Temperature Management:** Users must be advised that changes in ambient temperature and time zone can affect the process and result in a lesser quality outcome.\n*   **Voltage (Power Supply):** The power supply voltage must be **AC 240V 50HZ**.\n*   **Grounding:** The grounding wire must be connected securely.\n\n---\n\n## 💧 1. Water Circulation Device (Diagram and Components)\n\nThe page includes a schematic diagram illustrating the complete water circulation setup.\n\n### Diagram: Electric Pump Water Circulation System\n**(A diagram showing the flow path of water, powered by a motor, and regulated by various components.)**\n\n### Key Components Identified:\nThe system comprises several interconnected parts:\n*   **Motor:** Provides the power source.\n*   **High Pressure Water Pipe:** The conduit for the pressurized water.\n*   **External Pressure Regulator:** Controls the output pressure of the water.\n*   **Water Tank:** The reservoir for the circulating water.\n*   **Float:** Used to monitor water levels in the tank.\n*   **Water Inlet Switch:** Controls the water flow into the system.\n*   **Universal Connector:** Connects the power source to the system.\n*   **High Pressure Gauge:** Measures the system pressure.\n*   **Inlet Pipe:** Entry point for the water.\n\n---\n\n## 🌊 2. Water Pump Specifications\n\nThe pump system used is described as a light-small three-cylinder ceramic plunger pump, designed for high-pressure and high-speed operation.\n\n### Operational Specifications\n*   **Operating Temperature Range:** The water temperature should be maintained between **$0$ to $40 \\text{°C}$** at room temperature.\n\n### Technical Parameters (Table)\nThe pump's technical capabilities are summarized in a detailed table:\n\n| Parameter | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Plunger Diameter x Stroke** | $\\phi 18 \\times 4.5$ | $\\text{mm}$ |\n| **Rated Speed** | $1390$ | $\\text{r/min}$ |\n\n[Page 13]\nThis is a detailed technical summary of the provided specifications and operational guidelines for a pump system.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Technical Summary\n\n### I. Key Specifications and Data (Technical Table)\n\nThe following table compiles the core performance metrics and physical dimensions of the unit.\n\n| Feature | Specification | Unit/Value |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Theoretical Flow** | 4.88 | L/min |\n| **Rated Pressure** | 7 | Mpa |\n| **Matching Motor** | N/A | 750W |\n| **Crankshaft Lubricant** | N68-N150 Machinery Oil Gb3142-82 | N/A |\n| **Plunger Material** | Ceramics | N/A |\n| **Overall Dimension** | 200 x 190 x 115 | mm |\n| **Base Size** | 80 x 80-M6 Screw | N/A |\n| **Weight** | 4.8 | Kg |\n| **Connector** | PC10-G3/8" | N/A |\n| **Outlet Joint Thread** | M12 x 1.25 | N/A |\n| **Suction Pipe Inner Diameter** | $\\phi 10$ | mm |\n| **Outlet Pipe Inner Diameter** | $\\phi 6$ | mm |\n\n***\n\n### II. Operational Guidelines and Warnings\n\nThe operational section provides critical warnings regarding proper use, maintenance, and performance limitations.\n\n**💧 Water and Performance:**\n*   The pump requires a **clean water supply**.\n*   Users must ensure that the water level remains **above the suction hole** at all times.\n*   If the pump is operating under low water pressure, it is recommended that the lowest level of the water tank is maintained.\n*   The **Water Pressure Gauge** must be monitored. The pump must operate while maintaining both sufficient pressure and a steady water flow.\n*   The maximum continuous running time limit is **2 minutes**.\n\n**⚠️ Cautionary Notes:**\n*   **Damping:** The pump pressure is highly dependent on the damping of the pump outlet. If the damping amount is larger, the pressure will be lower.\n*   **Sealing:** Regular checks are required for any signs of dripping or leakage during operation.\n*   **Suction/Outlet:** The pump operates by drawing water from the suction pipe through the plunger, and discharging it through the outlet.\n\n### III. Maintenance and Service Procedures\n\n**🔩 Break-In and Initial Use (New Pump):**\n*   A new pump must undergo a break-in period for **20 hours**.\n*   Oil change must be performed **after** the 20-hour break-in period.\n\n**📅 Service Intervals:**\n*   **Oil Change:** The lubricant must be changed every **100 hours**.\n\n**🛠️ Oil Change Method (Detailed Procedure):**\n1.  Open the fuel cap and locate the oil drain bolt.\n2.  Drain the old oil completely.\n3.  Screw in the oil drain bolt and inject the specified new lubricating oil.\n\n***\n\n### 📊 Summary of Key Data Points\n\n| Parameter | Value/Requirement | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Max Pressure** | 7 Mpa | Rated operational pressure. |\n| **Flow Rate** | 4.88 L/min | Theoretical maximum flow. |\n| **Service Interval** | 100 Hours | Mandatory oil replacement frequency. |\n| **Break-In Period** | 20 Hours | Time required for new unit before first oil change. |\n| **Engine Power** | 750 W | Motor specification. |\n| **Operating Fluid** | Clean Water | Critical for proper function. |\n| **Maximum Runtime** | 2 Minutes | Safety/Operational limitation. |\n| **Lubricant** | N68-N150 Machinery Oil | Specific oil type required. |\n\n[Page 14]\nThis text appears to be an excerpt from a highly technical operational and maintenance manual for a fluid or hydraulic pumping system. The instructions cover everything from safety warnings and routine checks to specific component procedures for adjusting pressure and cleaning filters.\n\nSince I cannot generate physical diagrams or functional tables, I have structured the summary using technical formats, bullet points, and tables to ensure all key data and procedures are included in a detailed, easy-to-reference format.\n\n***\n\n# ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Pumping System Operation and Maintenance Manual\n\n**System Scope:** Routine operation, pressure regulation, and detailed maintenance procedures for a pump unit (motor, pump, and associated fluid components).\n\n## ⚠️ I. Critical Safety Warnings and Pre-Operation Checks\n\n**❗ Key Warning:** Do **NOT** disassemble the cylinder head or open the crankcase yourself. For any major issues or required repairs, contact the company and use only original parts.\n\n**✅ Pre-Operation Checklist (Key Data):**\n\n| Component | Check/Action Required | Frequency/Limit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Oil Level** | Ensure the oil level is at half of the oil mark. | Before startup |\n| **Fluid Status** | Check for leaks (oil, water, leakage). | Before startup |\n| **Run Time (Inspection)** | Comprehensive inspection required. | Approximately 500 hours |\n| **Parts** | Replace any suspected defective parts. | As needed |\n\n## 🛠️ II. Component Function and Maintenance Procedures\n\n### A. Motor and Pump Unit\n*   **Motor Connection:** The motor and water pump are assembled in a direct connection manner.\n*   **Motor Function:** The motor's sole purpose is to ensure the continuous and reliable performance of the water pump.\n\n### B. Pressure Regulation System (Process Flow Diagram)\nThe system relies on specific components for controlled pressure output:\n\n**Pressure Output Flow:**\n$$\n\\text{PUMP $\\rightarrow$ PRESSURE GAUGE $\\rightarrow$ REGULATING VALVE $\\rightarrow$ FLUID OUT}\n$$\n\n1.  **Pressure Gauge:** Used to display the actual working fluid pressure.\n2.  **Pressure Regulating Valve (PRV):**\n    *   **Function:** Controls and limits the maximum pressure output.\n    *   **Adjustment:** The PRV can be adjusted via a handwheel located at the front door of the machine cabinet.\n    *   **Key Data:** The PRV must be adjusted to achieve the desired pressure output, which can range up to **8 MPa**.\n3.  **Operational Adjustment:**\n    *   To **increase** pressure, turn the regulating valve to the **right**.\n    *   To **decrease** pressure, turn the regulating valve to the **left**.\n\n### C. Filter Element Cleaning Procedure (Step-by-Step Diagram)\nThe filter element is designed to prevent foreign materials from entering the system.\n\n**Steps for Cleaning:**\n1.  **Preparation:** Locate the filter and ensure the element is fully accessible.\n2.  **Cleaning Method:** Use a little force to rotate the inner wall of the filter.\n3.  **Cleaning Agent:** Use **diesel** to clean the filter.\n4.  **Final Rinse:** Dry the filter using an **air gun**.\n5.  **Inspection:** The filter must be immersed below the water surface during processing (to inspect for blockages).\n\n## 📝 III. Operational Guidelines and Troubleshooting\n\n### A. General Operation\n*   **Fluid Cap:** When the pump is running, the oil level must be checked.\n*   **System Check:** Periodically inspect the pump for any signs of wear, leakage, or damage.\n\n### B. Key Procedure Highlights\n*   **Purpose of Valve/Gauge:** The system's purpose is to safely display and maintain the correct working pressure.\n*   **Pressure Loss/Failure:** If the pressure gauge reads zero, or if there is no fluid output, check the fluid supply and the operating valves.\n\n***\n***Disclaimer:*** *This summary synthesizes information from provided fragments and is intended for educational/informational use only. Always refer to the original manufacturer’s manual for critical operational data.*\n\n[Page 15]\nThis document provides a highly detailed summary of the technical procedures related to **Filter Maintenance (Section 7)** and **Power Supply Testing and Inspection (Sections 4.4 & 2)**.\n\nDue to the procedural nature of the text, the summary is presented using structured tables, checklists, and highlighted key data points to ensure clarity.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Technical Procedures\n\n### I. (7) Filter Maintenance\n\nThis section outlines the handling and replacement of the filter element.\n\n| Procedure Step | Key Action / Requirement | Warning / Critical Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Replacement** | Replace the filter after its designated period of use. | Blockage due to dirt or impurities is common. |\n| **Cleaning** | If cleaning is necessary, use caution. Do not force the cleaning process. | **CRITICAL:** Do not force cleaning, as this can damage the element or cause blockages. |\n| **Installation** | 1. Use a **special wrench** for unscrewing the filter element. 2. Ensure the element is clean and properly tightened. | The filter element must be secured and tightened correctly to ensure proper function. |\n\n### II. (4.4) Test Power Supply\n\nThis section details the steps for connecting and verifying the electrical power supply.\n\n#### 🔌 (1) Connect The Power Supply\n\nThe primary focus is verifying the correct voltage and phasing before connecting the main power.\n\n*   **Testing Instrument:** Multimeter\n*   **Target Voltage:** $750\\text{V}$\n*   **Phasing Check:** Verify the correct phasing and sequence of the supply.\n*   **Safety Requirement:** Users must ensure they have the **owner's consent** and follow all **isolation procedures** before connecting the power supply.\n\n#### 📝 (2) Line Routine Inspection (Pre-Connection Checklist)\n\nThis is a critical visual and instrumental inspection performed on the entire power line and equipment.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Visual & Physical Inspection Checklist\n\n| Item | Action Required | Key Inspection Points |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A. Visual Inspection** | Check for signs of physical damage. | 1. Look for cracks or signs of wear on cables and equipment. 2. Check for damage sustained during transportation (handling stress). |\n| **B. Instrument Check (Wiring)** | Verify the condition and specifications of the power cables. | **Minimum Wire Gauge (Copper):** $\\ge 2.5\\text{mm}^2$ (multi-core copper wire) **Connection Check:** Ensure all screws, lugs, and terminals are clean and tightly secured. |\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Electrical Testing & Measurement\n\n| Test Parameter | Measurement Method / Device | Specification / Condition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Measurement** | Multimeter | Verify $750\\text{V}$ output at the source side. |\n| **Phasing Check** | Multimeter | Verify the correct phase sequence (U, V, W) for the motor supply. |\n| **Circuit Integrity** | Visual/Physical Check | Check the main circuit and surrounding components for damage or disconnections. |\n| **System Check** | Multimeter | Verify the proper voltage and functionality of the three-phase resistors and the secondary stroke motor. |\n\n---\n\n### 🔑 Key Data and Specifications Summary\n\n| Element | Specification / Value | Purpose / Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Power Voltage** | $750\\text{V}$ | Standard voltage to be measured and verified. |\n| **Cable Minimum Size** | $\\ge 2.5\\text{mm}^2$ | Minimum cross-sectional area for multi-core copper wiring. |\n| **Tooling (Filter)** | Special Wrench | Required for safe and proper unscrewing of the filter element. |\n| **Motor Phases** | U, V, W | The sequence of phases to be checked for correct rotation and function. |\n| **Safety Protocol** | Owner Consent / Isolation | Must be followed before performing any connection or testing on the power supply. |\n\n[Page 16]\nBased on the provided text, this document appears to be a highly detailed technical checklist and operational guide for the **Commissioning, Troubleshooting, and Startup of industrial machinery**.\n\nBecause the source material is a continuous stream of fragmented technical instructions, the summary is structured into distinct phases: **Pre-Operation Checks, Power-Up Sequence, and Functional Verification.**\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary of Machine Commissioning Procedure\n\n### 💡 Overall Purpose\nThe primary goal of this procedure is to ensure the machine is electrically safe, all components are functioning within operational parameters, and the correct power phase sequence is established *before* running the machine.\n\n### 📋 Key Tools and Equipment\n| Tool | Purpose | Checks Performed |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ohmmeter** | Measuring electrical resistance and continuity. | Spindle motor, rotary motor, grounding resistance, short circuits (AC/DC circuits). |\n| **Voltmeter** | Measuring electrical potential (voltage). | Main power switch voltage, overall machine power supply voltage. |\n| **Manual/Checklist** | Following the documented safety and sequential steps. | Verifying system requirements and confirming operational permits. |\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Phase 1: Electrical Safety and Continuity Checks (Pre-Startup)\n\nThis phase focuses on ensuring all electrical connections are sound, grounded, and resistant measurements are within spec.\n\n#### 1. Motor and Circuit Integrity Checks\n(Action: Use the Ohmmeter)\n*   **Spindle Motor:** Check resistance.\n*   **Rotary Motor:** Check resistance.\n*   **Grounding:** Check grounding resistance for the machine and components.\n*   **Short Circuits:** Perform checks for short circuits on the following pathways:\n    *   AC circuit to the DC circuit.\n    *   Machine power supply circuits (checking continuity).\n\n#### 2. System Power and Connection Checks\n*   **Grounding:** Verify the grounding in *each phase* of the motor unit.\n*   **Connection Verification:** Confirm the physical link and electrical integrity of the power lines:\n    *   Check the connection between the AC and DC circuits.\n    *   Check the connection between the main power supplier's cabinet and the machine.\n\n### 🚦 Phase 2: Power-Up Sequence (Startup Checklist)\n\nThis outlines the mandatory, sequential steps for safely applying power.\n\n| Step | Action | Tool/Verification | Key Objective |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Main Power Check (a)** | Use the ohmmeter to verify the voltage at the main power switch. | **Ohmmeter / Voltmeter** | Ensure incoming power voltage is correct. |\n| **2. Power Source Verification (b)** | Confirm the owner's power distribution cabinet is ready (checking required permissions/permits). | **Inspection / Documents** | Ensure operational clearance is granted. |\n| **3. System Voltage Check (c)** | Use the voltmeter to ensure the voltage meets all machine system requirements. | **Voltmeter** | Prevent system damage due to low/high voltage. |\n| **4. Activation (d)** | Release the emergency stop switch. | **Physical Action** | Energize the machine safely after all checks are complete. |\n\n### 🟢 Phase 3: Operational and Phase Sequencing Checks\n\nOnce power is applied, functional checks must confirm correct movement and power distribution.\n\n#### 1. Phase Sequence and Direction Check\nThis is a critical step to ensure the machine operates correctly and safely.\n\n*   **Procedure:** Before the machine is fully energized, verify the rotational direction of the secondary stroke (W-axis).\n*   **Test:** The technician must click the **UP button** and physically move the **W axis up**.\n*   **Goal:** Confirm the movement direction and operational range.\n\n#### 2. Power Phase Sequence Confirmation\nThe machine must be confirmed to run using the correct electrical phase sequence.\n\n*   **Verification:** The proper **three-phase power supply** must be confirmed (JOG the DW button and move the W axis down).\n*   **Outcome:** Upon successful completion of all steps, the machine display will show the initial coordinates and operation panel indicators.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Simulated Flow Diagram: Startup Process\n\nThis diagram summarizes the sequence, ensuring that electrical checks precede physical activation.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Start: Initial Inspection] --> B{Electrical Checks};\n    B --> B1[Check Resistance (Ohmmeter): Motors, Grounds, Short Circuits];\n    B --> B2[Check Voltage (Voltmeter): Mains Switch, System Requirements];\n    B2 --> C[Confirm Power Supply Cabinet Readiness];\n    C --> D[Release E-Stop Switch];\n    D --> E{Functional Checks};\n    E --> E1[Verify Phase Sequence: 3-Phase Power Check (W-Axis Down)];\n    E --> E2[Verify Direction: UP button / W-Axis Up];\n    E2 --> F[System Ready: Display Initial Coordinates];\n```\n\n[Page 17]\nThis document appears to be an excerpt from a technical troubleshooting and maintenance manual, providing instructions for resolving operational issues related to machinery axes (specifically the W-axis and U/V axes) and proper power sequence checking.\n\nSince the source material is text-only, the summary will use highly structured formatting (tables, detailed lists, and bullet points) to simulate diagrams and organize the key technical data.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary and Analysis of Technical Procedures\n\n### I. 🚨 Troubleshooting Guide (Power and Movement Issues)\n\nThe text addresses two main operational failures: the inability to move the UP button and the requirement to check the power phase sequence.\n\n#### 1. Why Can't I Press the UP Button from the Beginning?\n\n| Cause | Description | Solution/Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **W-axis Support Blockage** | The W-axis may have a support pad located in the lower part of the W-axis. | **Check/Clear:** Ensure the W-axis support pad is functioning correctly. |\n| **Functionality Issue** | The system requires the three-phase power supply to be connected, and the limit switch must be activated/working. | **Power & Safety Check:** Verify the three-phase power and confirm the status of the limit switches. |\n\n#### 2. Why Check the Power Phase Sequence?\n\n| Condition/Failure | Importance | Root Cause Check |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Three-Phase Power Supply** | Improper sequence can damage components and cause failure. | **Check Sequence:** Verify the phase sequence of the three-phase power supply. |\n| **Operation (U/V Axis)** | Proper sequencing is critical for smooth and safe movement. | **Calibration:** If the W-axis needs to move correctly, the power sequence must be adjusted/re-calibrated. |\n| **Observed Failure** | If the axis moves but the movement is incorrect or fails to stop, the power sequence is the most likely culprit. | **Action:** Adjust or confirm the three-phase power sequence. |\n\n---\n\n### II. ⚙️ Detailed Maintenance Procedure: Disassembling the Locking Device\n\n**(Reference: (4) Disassemble locking device)** This section provides a precise, sequential guide for disassembling and reassembling the locking mechanisms.\n\n**Objective:** To ensure the proper functionality of the W-axis and related components.\n\n#### A. Initial Steps (Locking Plate Removal)\n\n1.  **Release Power:** Ensure the machine is properly de-energized (power off).\n2.  **Remove Components:**\n    *   Locate the **Allen key** in the toolbox.\n    *   Use the Allen key to remove the **X-axis left-hand pallet sheet metal fixing screw**.\n    *   The base and the X-axis carriage have the **locking plate fixed connection**.\n    *   **Action:** Loosen the locking plate.\n    *   **Removal:** The locking plate can be removed and then dragged.\n    *   **Re-installation:** The sheet metal must be reinstalled and fixed.\n\n#### B. Final Steps (Reassembly and Testing)\n\n1.  **Adjustments:** Check for any loose connections or required power sequence adjustments.\n2.  **Re-locking:**\n    *   Use the Allen key to **re-fix the X-axis left-hand pallet sheet metal fixing screw**.\n3.  **Testing the Function:**\n    *   **Goal:** To confirm proper operation after reassembly.\n    *   **Action:** After the phase sequence adjustment of the three-phase power supply, **please debug the limit switch** again to work normally.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Key Data and Summary Table\n\n| Component/System | Function/Role | Critical Check Point | Failure Implication |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Three-Phase Power** | Provides necessary power for the W-axis and related movements. | **Phase Sequence** (Crucial) | Incorrect sequence leads to erratic or failed movement. |\n| **W-axis Support Pad** | Supports the W-axis in its lower part. | **Clearance/Physical Integrity** | Prevents the UP button from being activated/moving. |\n| **Limit Switch** | Safety and positional feedback mechanism. | **Functionality/Debugging** | Must be confirmed working after any major maintenance/power adjustment. |\n| **Locking Plate/Screw** | Secures the X-axis carriage base to the machine structure. | **Tightness/Fixing** | Must be properly reinstalled and secured to ensure stability. |\n\n### ⭐ Overall Summary Takeaways\n\n1.  **Prioritize Power:** All movement issues are fundamentally linked to the **three-phase power supply** and its **phase sequence**. This must be checked first.\n2.  **Sequential Process:** Maintenance (like removing/replacing the locking plate) must follow a strict sequence (Power Off $\\rightarrow$ Unlock $\\rightarrow$ Remove $\\rightarrow$ Adjust $\\rightarrow$ Re-lock $\\rightarrow$ Test).\n3.  **Troubleshooting Flow:** If the axis is non-responsive, check for physical blockage (support pads, switches). If the axis moves incorrectly, check the electrical input (power sequence).\n\n[Page 18]\nThis page describes the structure and function of the **Electrical Control System** used in machinery, detailing two primary subsystems: the Pulse Power Supply and the Spindle Servo System.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including structured components, a functional diagram, and key data points.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Summary: Electrical Control System\n\n**System Overview:** The electrical control system is the operational brain of the machine. It is responsible for controlling the movement, power distribution, and performance of the main components, including the rotary head device, rectifier unit, and the spindle.\n\n### I. Key System Components (The Parts)\n\n| Component | Function | Primary Role |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rectifier Unit** | Converts incoming electrical power to usable forms for the system. | Power Conditioning |\n| **Pulse Power Supply** | Generates and manages precise electrical pulses (pulses and pulse channels) necessary for operation. | Pulse Generation / Actuation |\n| **Machine Tool / Spindle** | The mechanical end effector that performs the work. | Work Execution |\n| **Spindle Servo System** | Provides precise positional control and feedback, ensuring the tool operates at the correct speed and position. | Feedback and Positioning Control |\n| **Rotation Control** | Manages the rotational movement and coordinates the input to the Pulse Power Supply. | Control Management |\n| **Water Pump** | Likely a cooling or auxiliary system controlled by the main power flow. | Auxiliary Operation |\n\n### II. System Flow Diagram (Functional Mapping)\n\nThe diagram illustrates a sequential and feedback-driven operational loop:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[MACHINE TOOL] --> B[RECTIFIER UNIT];\n    B -->|Selection/Manual Adjustment| C{PULSE POWER SUPPLY};\n    C --> D[WATER PUMP];\n    C --> E[MACHINE TOOL SPINDLE];\n\n    E --> F(SPINDLE SERVO);\n    F --> G[ROTATION CONTROL];\n    G --> C;\n\n    style A fill:#DDF,stroke:#333\n    style B fill:#FFC,stroke:#333\n    style C fill:#CCF,stroke:#333\n    style F fill:#CCF,stroke:#333\n```\n\n***(Flow Interpretation:*** *Power flows from the Rectifier $\\rightarrow$ Pulse Supply. The resulting power drives the Spindle/Water Pump. The Spindle's movement is monitored by the Servo System, which sends feedback to the Rotation Control, adjusting the Pulse Supply.*)\n\n### III. Detailed System Functionality\n\n#### 1. Pulse Power Supply (1)\n\n**Purpose:** To generate precise pulses and manage power flow based on operational selections.\n\n**Key Data & Process:**\n*   **Selection:** Operation depends on the selection of the "selection switch" and "power amplifier tube."\n*   **Action:** The system generates the corresponding necessary **pulses and pulses**.\n*   **Control:** It simultaneously turns on the corresponding power tube channels.\n*   **Operation:** The overall system operation (the program) is executed by managing the power and processing tubes.\n\n#### 2. Spindle Servo System (2)\n\n**Purpose:** To maintain precise positional control and monitor the actual performance of the machine tool.\n\n**Key Data & Process:**\n*   **Components:**\n    1.  Control Power Driving Unit\n    2.  Feedback Signal Processing Unit\n    3.  Stepping Motor\n*   **Feedback Loop:** The core function is feedback-driven. The control unit continuously adjusts the pulse signal based on the **difference** between:\n    *   The **given value** (the desired setting).\n    *   The **actual running value** (reported by the feedback signal).\n*   **Power Delivery:** The power driving unit acts as the main loop of the system.\n*   **Output Control:** The pulse signal is converted into the corresponding **angular displacement** on the stepper motor.\n*   **Adjustability:** The speed of the stepper motor can be adjusted dynamically.\n\n---\n### 💡 Key Takeaways (Summary Checklist)\n\n*   **Input Power:** Managed by the **Rectifier Unit**.\n*   **Power Control:** **Pulse Power Supply** governs the distribution of electrical pulses.\n*   **Motor Control:** The **Spindle Servo System** provides closed-loop feedback, ensuring accuracy.\n*   **Core Principle:** The servo system constantly minimizes the error by comparing the *desired* state vs. the *actual* state.\n*   **Output Mechanism:** The final command is converted by the system into angular displacement via the **Stepper Motor**.\n\n[Page 19]\nThis page provides a technical description and diagram of a highly specialized device called the **Rotating Head**, detailing its function, operational mechanisms, and technical advantages.\n\nBelow is a detailed summary organized by function, technical specifications, and component breakdown.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Summary: Rotating Head (Section 5.2)\n\n### I. Operational Overview and Function\nThe Rotating Head is an integrated design mounted on a spindle slider. Its primary purpose is to facilitate the **sealing introduction of high-pressure working fluid** during various electro-chemical processes.\n\n**Core Functions:**\n*   **Process Application:** It is actively used during clamping, rotation, conduction, and the rotation of the copper tube electrode.\n*   **Mechanism:** The rotation of the copper tube is directly driven by the motor.\n*   **Fluid Management:** High-pressure working fluid is injected into the central hole of the rotor.\n\n### II. Key Technical Features and Advantages (Key Data)\n\nThe design emphasizes reliability, efficiency, and adjustability.\n\n| Feature | Specification / Advantage | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fluid Introduction** | High-pressure working fluid injection into the central hole of the rotor. | Ensures effective material interaction and sealing. |\n| **Design Reliability** | Excellent water leakage prevention performance and a more reasonable structure for the sealed water inlet device. | Enhances durability and operational safety. |\n| **Motor Drive** | Rotation is directly driven by the motor (Synchronous gear reduction drive is omitted in the middle). | Provides efficient and direct torque transmission. |\n| **Performance Metrics** | **Wide** voltage adjustment range; **Large** torque; **Low** failure rate; Adjustable rotation speed. | Provides maximum operational flexibility and reliability. |\n\n### III. Component Diagram Breakdown\n\nThe accompanying diagram illustrates the physical structure of the rotating head, detailing key components involved in the fluid introduction and rotation process.\n\n#### 📐 Diagram Components (Labeled Key Data)\n\n*   **Rotary Motor:** The primary power source for rotation.\n*   **High Pressure Inlet Pipe:** The conduit through which the high-pressure working fluid is introduced into the system.\n*   **Return Pipe:** Manages the path of the fluid after its use within the rotor chamber.\n*   **Sealed Waterproof Device:** A critical component ensuring that the system maintains an airtight seal while operating under high pressure.\n*   **Carbon Brush:** A component utilized in the motor system for electrical conductivity.\n*   **Water Spray Outlet:** The point where the processed fluid or working fluid exits the device.\n\n### IV. Legal and Intellectual Property Status\n\n*   **Patent Status:** This product is explicitly identified as a **patented product** belonging to the State Intellectual Property Office.\n*   **Warning:** The document includes a stern warning that the product is **forbidden to be counterfeited**.\n\n[Page 20]\nThis page provides detailed instructions for two distinct, but related, mechanical and electrical processes: **(1) Securing the Motor Shaft/Chuck**, and **(2) Operating the Control Panel.**\n\nThe summary is broken down into three parts: the operational procedure, the component breakdown, and the overall schematic summary.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ I. Operational Procedure: Motor Shaft Locking\n\nThis section details the steps required to secure and tighten the electrical chuck attached to the motor shaft.\n\n### 1. Key Action\nThe process involves securing the motor shaft lock cap and tightening the chuck.\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Instructions\nThe operation requires three sequential actions:\n1. **Rotate the head cover:** (Implied preliminary step).\n2. **Lock the device:** Rotate the motor shaft to lock the internal screw device.\n3. **Secure the Chuck:** Press the screw cap, which simultaneously locks and tightens the copper tube electric chuck.\n\n### 3. Diagrammatic Element (Motor Shaft Lock Cap)\n*   **Component:** MOTOR SHAFT LOCK CAP\n*   **Function:** Used to secure the chuck and stabilize the motor shaft connection.\n*   **Mechanism:** Requires rotation for locking/tightening.\n\n***\n\n## 💻 II. Component Analysis: Control Panel (5.3)\n\nThis section details the layout and components of the machine's main electrical control panel. The panel features numerous switches, indicator lights, and control points, labeled both numerically and physically.\n\n### 1. Panel Layout Overview\nThe diagram illustrates a structured electrical control panel containing multiple input switches and output indicators.\n\n### 2. Key Data: Component Mapping (Diagram Interpretation)\n\n| Number Range | Component Type (Inferred) | Function/Description | Location on Diagram |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1, 2, 3** | **Main Panel Groups** | Identifiers for specific sections (e.g., 1: Primary Controls, 2: Indicators/Switches, 3: Power/Circuitry). | Left Side |\n| **4–10** | **Input/Switch Controls** | Primary control points, likely for specific machine functions, modes, or power relays. | Bottom Row (Labeled 4 through 10) |\n| **11–15** | **Output/Indicators** | Indicator lights or secondary control functions (e.g., operational status, fault alerts). | Right Side |\n\n### 3. Diagrammatic Element (The Control Panel Schematic)\nThe diagram uses a grid and wiring layout to visualize the electrical connections. Key features include:\n\n*   **Switches/Dials:** Numerous switches (likely rocker or rotary type) are visible within the main circuit section.\n*   **Indicator Lights:** Several circular elements are positioned for visual status reporting.\n*   **Circuitry:** The layout suggests multiple separate circuits managed by the labeled controls.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 III. Detailed Summary Tables\n\n### Table 1: Operational Summary\n\n| Process | Component | Action | Result/Function |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Chuck Securing** | Motor Shaft Lock Cap | Rotate & Press | Locks and tightens the copper tube electric chuck to the motor shaft. |\n| **Control Input** | Switches (4–10) | Actuate | Initiates or modifies specific machine functions. |\n| **System Status** | Indicators (11–15) | Illuminated | Provides visual feedback on system status or errors. |\n\n### Table 2: Component Identification\n\n| Label/Group | Physical Location | Description | Function Type |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Shaft Lock Cap** | Top Diagram | Mechanical securing cap. | Mechanical Lock |\n| **Panel Group 1, 2, 3** | Left Side | Categorization of control sections. | Structural/Organizational |\n| **Control Points (4–10)** | Bottom Panel | Primary user interface switches/buttons. | Input/Control |\n| **Indicator Points (11–15)** | Right Panel | Status lights or secondary control selectors. | Output/Feedback |\n\n***\n\n## 📌 Overall Summary\n\nThe page serves as a detailed technical manual segment, focusing on the **physical setup and electrical control** of a machine. The user must first follow a **mechanical procedure** (locking the motor shaft chuck) and then interact with the **Control Panel**, which features 15+ distinct monitored or controlled points (labeled 1 through 15) to operate the machine.\n\n[Page 21]\nThis document provides a comprehensive summary of the control indicators, switches, and operational procedures detailed in the provided text. This equipment appears to be a sophisticated industrial machine, likely involving precision machining or electric discharge machining (EDM), given the focus on voltage, current, and specialized power switches.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed System Summary: Controls and Monitoring\n\n### 📝 Overview\n\nThe system is controlled through several specialized switches and utilizes multiple indicators (Voltmeter, Ammeter, Indicator Light) to monitor electrical status, operational parameters, and machining progress. Operation requires strict adherence to specific startup sequences, particularly concerning water supply and power synchronization.\n\n### 📊 I. Component Function Tables\n\nThe functions are categorized into **Monitoring Indicators**, **Control Switches**, and **Operational Safety Checks**.\n\n#### A. Monitoring Indicators (The "What is happening?")\n\n| Indicator | Function | Key Data Monitored | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Indicator Light** | Status Check | Power On/Off | Verifies the main power status of the unit. |\n| **Voltmeter** | Voltage Measurement | Machining Gap Voltage / Pulse Processing | Displays the electrical voltage applied during machining. |\n| **Ammeter** | Current Measurement | Machining Current | Displays the electrical current draw during machining. |\n\n#### B. Primary Control Switches (The "How to operate?")\n\n| Switch | Primary Function | Control Mechanism / Action | Key Safety Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Switch** | Pressure/Voltage Mode Selection | **High Voltage $\\to$ Low Pressure** **Low Voltage $\\to$ High Pressure** | Controls the machine's operational pressure and voltage profiles. |\n| **Tool Switch** | Machining Initiation & Control | Starts buzer, starts machine and spindle. Allows flexible operation. | **CRITICAL:** Cannot be opened during normal machining operations. |\n| **Rotary Head Switch** | Motor Rotation Direction | Activates the motor rotation to "stun." | The rotation direction is factory-set (Clockwise view when viewed from above). |\n| **High Frequency Switch** | Power System Activation | Turns on the high frequency power and pulse power. | Initializes the main energy supply for the process. |\n\n#### C. System Preparation & Utility Switches (The "Prerequisite Checks")\n\n| Switch | Function | Essential Procedural Check | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Water Pump Switch** | Water Supply Management | 1. Turn on the pump. 2. Inspect the copper electrode (must be out of water). 3. Verify the copper pipe water supply before processing. | Ensures continuous, clean coolant supply necessary for machining. |\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ II. Operational Data and Key Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Value / Condition | Function/Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **High Voltage Mode** | Low Pressure | Achieved by pressing the switch. |\n| **Low Voltage Mode** | High Pressure | Achieved by pressing the switch. |\n| **Pulse Processing** | Monitored by Voltmeter | Indication that the machine is actively running the electrical discharge process. |\n| **Startup Check** | Water Flow & Electrode Status | Mandatory check before any processing begins. |\n\n### ⚙️ III. Conceptual Operational Flow (Diagrammatic Sequence)\n\nThe process can be broken down into four main phases: Preparation $\\to$ Initialization $\\to$ Machining $\\to$ Monitoring.\n\n#### **Phase 1: Preparation (Pre-Run Check)**\n1. **Water Check:** Activate the **Water Pump Switch**.\n2. **System Check:** Confirm the copper electrode is correctly positioned (out of water).\n3. **Supply Check:** Verify the entire copper pipe water supply is adequate.\n\n$$\\downarrow$$\n\n#### **Phase 2: Initialization (Powering Up)**\n1. **Power Status:** Confirm the **Indicator Light** is ON.\n2. **Power Sequence:** Activate the **High Frequency Switch** and the main pulse power.\n3. **System Status:** The **Voltmeter** begins displaying readings, confirming pulse processing capability.\n\n$$\\downarrow$$\n\n#### **Phase 3: Operational Setup (Ready to Work)**\n1. **Voltage Setup:** Use the **Voltage Switch** to select the desired operational mode (High Voltage/Low Pressure or Low Voltage/High Pressure).\n2. **Head Setup:** Use the **Rotary Head Switch** to set the desired rotation direction (factory-set, clockwise viewed from above).\n3. **Tool Setup:** Position the tooling accurately and ensure readiness.\n\n$$\\downarrow$$\n\n#### **Phase 4: Machining & Monitoring (Run Cycle)**\n1. **Start:** Engage the **Tool Switch**. (Buzer sounds, machine starts, spindle starts).\n2. **Adjustment:** Use the potentiometers to fine-tune the work piece position (vertical and horizontal adjustments).\n3. **Monitoring:** Continuously monitor the **Voltmeter** and **Ammeter** for accurate gap voltage and current draw.\n4. **Safety Warning:** *Crucially, do not operate the Tool Switch or open the machine during normal machining.*\n\n[Page 22]\nThis summary synthesizes the provided text, which details the operational procedures and key components of specialized machining equipment. Given that no diagrams or external images were provided, a detailed description of the operational flows is used in place of actual visual aids.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of Machining Equipment Operation\n\nThe text outlines a structured set of procedures covering setup, adjustments, safety controls, and general operational principles for a machine that processes copper pipes, materials, and utilizes voltage and cooling systems.\n\n### I. Operational Principles and Process Flow\n\nThe machining process requires meticulous setup involving multiple parameters:\n\n*   **Power Requirement:** Proper voltage is necessary for the copper tube and the workpiece to be charged and the machining process to occur.\n*   **Cooling:** Cooling systems (like water pumps) are integral to the operation.\n*   **Setup Focus:** The operator must ensure proper setup before activation, paying attention to the gap voltage and the workpiece/pipe geometry.\n\n### II. Key Control Components and Functions\n\nThe machine features several specialized switches and knobs used for fine-tuning and safety.\n\n#### 🔩 1. Parameter Adjustment Knob (Item 12)\nThis knob is used to select optimal machining parameters based on the specific materials being processed.\n*   **Function:** Allows selection for different materials, processing voltages, and types of copper pipes.\n*   **Key Rule:** The physical size of the copper pipe correlates to the position of the gear selected (larger diameter pipe $\\rightarrow$ higher corresponding gear position).\n\n#### 🔌 2. Potentiometer (Item 11)\nThe potentiometer is a critical control element for regulating voltage and current.\n*   **Controlled Parameters:** Adjusts both the gap voltage and the machining current.\n*   **Operational Adjustment:**\n    *   **Clockwise Rotation:** Increases the gap voltage.\n    *   **Counter-Clockwise Rotation:** Decreases the gap voltage.\n*   **Key Data:** The optimal processing voltage is generally stable within **20 to 25V**.\n\n#### 🛡️ 3. Amplifier Switch (Item 13)\nThis switch controls the electrical flow and output capacity of the machine, operating across four distinct points/channels.\n\n| Amplifier Identification | I | II | III | IV |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **Purpose** | (Controls current) | (Controls current) | (Controls current) | (Controls current) |\n\n*   **Function:** The switch dictates the current flow and output state.\n*   **Observation:** The more power amplifier tubes are opened, the larger the machining current is, and the loss is also increased.\n\n#### ⚠️ 4. Emergency Stop Switch (Item 14)\nThis is the primary safety mechanism.\n*   **Action:** To restore the machine’s energy and power, the operator must **release the knob**.\n\n### III. Operational Procedures (Sequencing)\n\nThe following steps define the structured procedures for starting, operating, and shutting down the equipment:\n\n| Item | Component | Procedure / Action | Operational Goal |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **9** | **Processing Switch** | 1. Turn off the high frequency switch. 2. Turn off the water pump. 3. Rotate the head. | Structured shutdown sequence after machining. |\n| **10** | **Manual Switch** | *(No specific operational instructions provided)* | Manual control activation. |\n| **11** | **Potentiometer** | Adjust to set machining parameters (Clockwise $\\uparrow$, Counter-Clockwise $\\downarrow$). | Maintain voltage stability (20–25V) and regulate machining current. |\n| **12** | **Parameter Knob** | Select position based on material type and pipe size. | Fine-tune the process for optimal cutting efficiency. |\n| **13** | **Amplifier Switch** | (Controls flow based on needed current). | Manage power distribution across the four channels. |\n\n---\n### 🖼️ Conceptual Diagram Flow (Operational Sequence)\n\nSince no physical diagram is provided, the critical operational sequence (Startup/Setup) should follow a strict order:\n\n1.  **Safety Check:** Ensure the machine is de-energized and the Emergency Stop is monitored.\n2.  **Setup/Tuning:** Adjust the **Parameter Adjustment Knob (12)** based on material type.\n3.  **Voltage/Current:** Use the **Potentiometer (11)** to stabilize the gap voltage (20–25V) and set the necessary machining current.\n4.  **Power Activation:** Engage the **Amplifier Switch (13)** to supply appropriate power.\n5.  **Operation:** Follow the specific Start procedures (implied activation of water pumps and high frequency systems) until the job is complete.\n6.  **Shutdown:** Execute the sequence defined by the **Processing Switch (9)**.\n\n[Page 23]\nThis document outlines the operational features, display functions, measurement systems, and the step-by-step procedure for setting up and conducting a machining process.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ I. Operational Controls and Display Areas\n\n### 🔴 Safety Feature\nIn the event of an **unexpected situation**, the user must press the knob to immediately cut off the power.\n\n### 📏 Key Display/Function Areas\n\n| Label | Area/Function | Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **[15]** | **Light Ruler Function Area** | Indicates a specific function area related to measurement or guidance. |\n| **[16]** | **XY Axis Coordinate Display Area** | Displays the current coordinate readings for the X and Y axes. |\n\n### 📊 Gap Capacitance Reading System\n\nThis system measures gap capacitance using three distinct reading positions (C1, C2, C3).\n\n**Diagrammatic Representation:**\nThe diagram shows three sensor points arranged in a circle, labeled C1, C2, and C3.\n\n**Key Data:**\n*   **C1, C2, C3:** Represent three different, measurable capacitance values.\n*   **X:** Indicates the **neutral** position or reading.\n*   **Operational Constraint:** The button switch cannot be placed in the neutral position while the processing is underway.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ II. Process Setup and Order (6. PROCESSING ORDER)\n\nThe following steps must be followed sequentially to prepare the machine for machining.\n\n### 🎯 6.1 Pre-Processing Checklist (Before Starts)\n\n**1. Placement of the Work-piece**\n*   Place the work-piece securely on the designated table balance bar.\n\n**2. Electrode Installation**\n*   The copper tube electrode must be correctly mounted in the chuck.\n*   Subsequently, the electrode is mounted onto the machined rotary head shaft.\n\n**3. Confirmation of Working Fluid**\n*   Turn on the pump to verify that the working fluid can be ejected normally from the front end of the electrode.\n\n**4. Adjustment of Machining Position**\nThis step involves moving and setting the machine axes to the precise operational coordinates:\n*   **X and Y Axes:** Move both axes to the desired machining position using the hand wheel.\n*   **W Axis:** Adjust the position of the W axis carefully. The lower end of the guide must maintain a precise distance of **3 to 5 mm** from the work-piece machining surface.\n\n**5. Processing Condition Setting**\n*   Set all necessary machining parameters.\n*   Turn the potentiometer to the **far left** to finalize the setup.\n\n[Page 24]\nThis page provides detailed, step-by-step instructions for setting up and installing copper tube electrodes within a processing machine, emphasizing necessary quality checks and maintenance.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Electrode Installation Procedure\n\n**Context:** The procedure begins when processing starts (Section 6). The focus is on preparing the guide system and ensuring the copper tube electrode is properly inspected and installed.\n\n### I. Key Objectives & Data\n\n| Component | Function | Key Specification / Data |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Guide** | Holds the electrode tube. | Must be suitable for the specific copper tube diameter. |\n| **Guide Plate** | Mounting surface for the guide. | The guide must be installed firmly in the guide plate hole. |\n| **Copper Tube** | Working electrode. | Inspection is critical, especially for tubes $\\text{below } \\emptyset 0.5 \\text{ mm}$. |\n| **Measurement** | Length of cut. | Cut the end off by **a few millimeters**. |\n\n### II. Procedure Summary Table\n\n| Section | Step | Action Required | Critical Details / Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **6.2 Setting Electrode Guide** | **Preparation** | Install the guide. | Ensure the guide is firmly installed into the hole of the guide plate. |\n| **6.3 (1) Inspection** | **Electrode Selection** | Select the copper tube electrode. | Must be visually inspected for clogging. |\n| **6.3 (1) Inspection** | **Inspection Method** | Check for clogs (especially $\\text{below } \\emptyset 0.5 \\text{ mm}$). | Place one end in the mouth and the other in the water. Blow through the tube to check for blockages. |\n| **6.3 (1) Inspection** | **Action on Blisters** | If blisters are found, assess the quantity. | If the number is appropriate, no action is needed. |\n| **6.3 (1) Installation** | **Cleaning/Cutting** | If the tube is suspected of clogging, clean the front end. | 1. Turn the tube. 2. Cut the tube and the front end by a few millimeters while rotating the tube. |\n| **6.3 (1) Installation (Alternative)** | **Alternative Setup** | If possible, install the tube on the machine. | 1. Open the water pump. 2. Use special scissors to cut off the end of the copper tube by a few millimeters. |\n\n### III. Diagram Reference\n\nThe provided diagram illustrates the setup and components used during the installation process (Section 6.3).\n\n**(Diagram Description based on the image provided in the source text):**\n\nThe diagram depicts a cross-section of the electrode setup, showing the following labeled parts:\n\n*   **Guide Plate:** The main platform component.\n*   **Guide Lock Screw:** Secures the guide assembly.\n*   **Electrode Director:** A component guiding the electrode flow.\n*   **Copper Tube Electrode:** The core working element.\n\n### IV. Key Procedural Highlights\n\n*   **Clogging Prevention:** The primary technical challenge addressed is the potential clogging of the copper tube electrode, particularly those with smaller diameters ($\\text{below } \\emptyset 0.5 \\text{ mm}$).\n*   **Finalizing the Electrode:** Regardless of the method used (manual cutting or machine installation), the final action is to trim the electrode end using special scissors by **a few millimeters**.\n*   **Safety Check:** When cutting, care must be taken to prevent bending or damage to the copper tube.\n\n[Page 25]\nThis page details the precise, step-by-step procedure for the **Electrode Mounting Method** (Method 2). It describes the assembly of the electrode components, listing specific parts, required fitments, and critical measurements.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including tables and descriptions of the diagrams.\n\n---\n\n## 🔌 Detailed Summary: Electrode Mounting Method\n\nThe procedure involves assembling an electrode holder structure onto a copper tube electrode, which is designed to connect to a motor shaft.\n\n### 📋 Component List (Key Parts)\n\nThe following components are utilized in the mounting assembly:\n\n| Component Name | Function / Role |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Copper Tube** | The main structural material; the electrode itself. |\n| **Sealing Ring** | Provides a secure seal or connection point. |\n| **Three-Sided Claw** | A specialized component shown at the end of the assembly. |\n| **Electrode Chuck** | The housing or mechanism that holds the electrode assembly. |\n| **Knurled Nut** | Used to secure or tighten components within the assembly. |\n| **Concentric Axis** | A structural rod ensuring alignment within the chuck. |\n\n### 🛠️ Assembly Procedure (Step-by-Step)\n\nThe process involves careful installation of the sealing ring and subsequent passing of the electrode holder components:\n\n1.  **Sealing Ring Installation:** Install the sealing ring onto the copper tube electrode.\n2.  **Assembly Insertion:** Pass the electrode holder components (starting from top to bottom) through the center hole of the chuck.\n3.  **Material Check:** Use a sealing ring that is specifically suitable for the diameter of the copper tube electrode.\n4.  **Motor Shaft Connection:** The tapered head of the copper tube seal must fit upwards with the inner tapered surface of the motor shaft.\n\n### 💡 Key Data and Specifications\n\nThese are the critical measurements, warnings, and fitment requirements provided on the page:\n\n| Specification/Warning | Detail | Importance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Upper End Length** | The upper end of the copper tube must exceed **10 mm** of the sealing ring. | Critical for proper sealing and fit. |\n| **Fitment Requirement** | The tapered head of the copper tube seal must fit upwards into the inner tapered surface of the motor shaft. | Ensures structural connection and sealing. |\n| **Operational Caution** | Be careful not to bend the copper tube electrode during operation. | Prevents damage to the electrode and compromises function. |\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram Descriptions (Visual Components)\n\nThe page utilizes two main diagrams to illustrate the components and their overall structure:\n\n#### 1. Main Assembly Diagram (Cross-Section View)\nThis diagram illustrates the vertical stacking of the main parts:\n*   **Top Components:** Sealed Ring $\\rightarrow$ Concentric Axis $\\rightarrow$ Knurled Nut $\\rightarrow$ Electrode Chuck.\n*   **Connection:** The electrode components are attached to the Electrode Copper Tube.\n\n#### 2. End Cap/Electrode Detail Diagram\nThis diagram shows the terminal structure of the electrode assembly:\n*   **Structure:** It depicts the diameter of the copper tube electrode and the terminal point, which incorporates the **Three-Sided Claw**.\n*   **Measurement Detail:** It highlights the crucial **10 mm or more** dimension required for the sealing ring relative to the copper tube electrode.\n\n[Page 26]\nThis document appears to be an excerpt from a technical manual detailing the procedures for operating a specific piece of machinery, likely involving electroding or machining processes (indicated by terms like "electrodes," "work-piece," "chuck," and "machining").\n\nHere is a detailed summary, structured with headings, key data, and procedural steps.\n\n***\n\n## Detailed Summary of Technical Procedures\n\n### ⚙️ Operational Checks and Warnings\n\n**Warning (Electrodes):**\n*   When using copper tubes, ensure the **electrode holder is a three-sided claw**.\n*   **Danger/Precaution:** Do not allow the copper tube to be bent or spot-weld due to force, especially when operating with the electrode chuck.\n\n**Safety/Preparation:**\n*   Ensure the machine is prepared for operation, including proper electrode attachment and power readiness.\n\n### 🛠️ Section 6.3: Electrode Chuck Installation\n\nThis section details the specific steps for setting up and attaching the electrode chuck.\n\n**Procedure Steps:**\n1.  **Preparation:** Open the machining switch and adjust the potentiometer to raise the spindle to the fastest speed.\n2.  **Electrode Setup:**\n    *   Hold the electrode chuckknut on the left hand and align it with the lower end of the rotating motor shaft (outer taper hole external thread).\n    *   Turn the rotary motor switch on the right hand.\n    *   Knuckle nut must be automatically locked upwards (when the knurled nut and the motor shaft rotate at the same time).\n3.  **Functionality:** Copper tube electrodes must be sized $\\text{Ø} 1$MM.\n4.  **Installation:** The electrode holder must be fixed to the machine according to the procedure.\n\n### 🔩 Section 6.4: Work-Piece Installation\n\nThis section outlines the preparation and securing of the material being worked upon.\n\n**Procedure Steps:**\n1.  **Placement:** Place the work-piece on the stainless steel balance bar.\n2.  **Securing:** Fix the work-piece with a clamp.\n3.  **Alignment:** The magnetic table of the dial gauge is to be attracted to the lower end and cover of the spindle to position the work-piece.\n4.  **Final Check:** After positioning, secure the work-piece with the clamp.\n\n### 📐 Section 6.5: Method Of Determining Processing Position\n\nThis section describes the alignment and positioning of the workpiece relative to the electrode.\n\n**Procedure Steps:**\n1.  **Initial Setup:** Open the tool setting switch, the rotary head switch, and the processing switch so that the copper tube electrode exceeds the lower end and surface of the electrode guide by **20 to 30 mm**.\n2.  **Indexing:** Slowly move the XY-axis carriage toward the end face of the workpiece so that the side of the copper tube electrode is in contact with the end surface of the workpiece.\n3.  **Surface Preparation:** *(If using oil, water, or carbage)* Ensure the material is wiped clean if it affects the accuracy of the machining position.\n4.  **Final Positioning:** Once the side of the electrode comes into contact with the end face of the workpiece, the contact buzzer will activate.\n\n### ⚠️ Key Data and Parameters (Summary Table)\n\n| Component/Action | Detail/Specification | Critical Requirement | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Type** | Copper tube; must use a **three-sided claw** electrode holder. | Prevent bending or spot-welding of the tube. | Machining/Processing. |\n| **Electrode Size** | $\\text{Ø} 1$MM | N/A | Specified copper tube size. |\n| **Work-Piece Mounting** | On a stainless steel balance bar. | Securely clamped. | Stability for machining. |\n| **Initial Clearance** | Electrode must exceed the lower end/surface of the electrode guide by **20 to 30 mm**. | Proper alignment is crucial for setup. | Allows movement and accurate positioning. |\n| **Cleaning** | If using liquid media (oil, water, carbage). | The workpiece must be **wiped clean**. | Maintains machining accuracy. |\n\n***\n*(Note: Since no actual diagrams or complex tables were provided in the source text, the "diagram" representation is described via clear procedural flow and the "tables" summarize key parameters and warnings.)*\n\n[Page 27]\nThis page provides detailed instructions regarding the operation, movement, and technical setup parameters for a piece of industrial machinery, likely involving electroplating or electrode machining, focusing particularly on the movement of the XY axis and precise processing settings.\n\n***\n\n## 📜 Detailed Summary\n\n### I. Operational Guidance and Axis Movement\n\nThe initial text describes the mechanics and movement control for the operational process:\n\n*   **XY Axis Movement:** When the copper tube strikes the end surface of the workpiece, the XY axis must move.\n*   **Copper Tube Guidance:** The copper tube is guided by the workpiece, which dictates the direction of movement.\n*   **Tracking and Control:** When the XY axis moves to bore the board, the copper tube follows the workpiece. The operator must continuously observe the digital display and use it to guide the movement.\n*   **Adjustment:** The amount of movement of the XY axis must be precisely considered.\n\n### II. Processing Condition Setting (Section 6.6)\n\nThis section focuses on the critical initial setup and parameter control required for the process.\n\n**Key Principle:** The processor must set various processing parameters, including:\n*   The diameter of the electrode.\n*   The material of the workpiece.\n*   The roughness of the machined surface.\n*   The desired processing efficiency.\n\n### III. Processing Parameter Selection Table (Key Data)\n\nThe core data is presented in the **Processing Parameter Selection Table**, which dictates the optimal machine settings based on the required electrode diameter ($\\phi$).\n\n**(Note: This table includes all parameters and ranges provided on the source page.)**\n\n| Electrode Diameter ($\\phi$ mm) | Speed | Gear position | Power tube | Processing gap current (A) | Gap capacitance | Pressure |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **$\\phi$ 0.3** | Low | 3 | 2 | 40 | 2 | C1 | High and low |\n| **$\\phi$ 0.3** | Medium | 4 | 2 | 30 | 3 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 0.5** | High | 4 | 3 | 25 | 4 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 0.5** | Low | 3 | 3 | 25 | 5 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 0.7** | Medium | 3 | 4 | 15 | 8 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 1.0** | Medium | 5 | 4 | 15 | 14 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 1.5** | High | 7 | 5 | 10 | 15 | C1C3 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 2.0** | High | 8 | 6 | 10 | 20 | C1C3 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 2.5** | High | 9 | 7 | 10 | 25 | C2C3 | High Pressure |\n| **$\\phi$ 3.0** | High | 9 | 8 | 10 | 30 | C2C3 | High Pressure |\n\n***\n\n### Summary of Key Parameters and Trends\n\n*   **Relationship between Diameter and Gap Current:** As the electrode diameter ($\\phi$) increases from 0.3 mm to 3.0 mm, the required **Processing gap current (A)** generally remains stable or slightly increases, while the required **Power tube** and **Gear position** parameters increase.\n*   **Pressure Settings:** For larger electrodes ($\\phi$ 1.5 mm and above), the recommended setting is consistently **High Pressure**.\n*   **Speed and Positioning:** The optimal **Speed** and **Gear position** are highly specific to the diameter and process stage (e.g., $\\phi$ 0.3 mm requires different settings for 'Low' and 'Medium' speeds).\n*   **Control Focus:** The overall process emphasizes continuous observation of the digital display and highly controlled, guided movement of the XY axis.\n\n[Page 28]\nThis document provides detailed, sequential instructions for the machining process (Section 6.7) and critical guidelines for identifying and resolving abnormal processing issues (Section 7).\n\n***\n\n### 🛠️ Procedural Guide: Machining Process (6.7)\n\nThe machining process requires careful calibration and sequence of operation to ensure optimal performance.\n\n#### A. Initialization and Calibration Steps\n| Step | Action | Focus/Adjustment | Key Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | **Set Parameters** | Calibrate the machine's settings. | Adjust the potentiometer and confirm the draw switch is engaged. |\n| **2** | **Activate Systems** | Turn on all necessary components. | Activate the rotary head switch, the water pump switch, and the high frequency switch. |\n| **3** | **Verification** | Confirm system readiness. | Adjust the potentiometer and verify that the discharge machine is stable. |\n| **4** | **Warning Alert** | Prepare for operation. | Caution is needed when a spark discharges from the lower part of the workpiece; the working fluid may be ejected. |\n\n#### B. Pre-Machining Preparation & Setup\n*   **Pre-Cleaning:** If the workpiece requires preparation, it should first be fitted with a rubber band or pad to guide the area needing a "beautiful penetration hole."\n*   **Goal:** The objective is to allow for a smooth, optimal flow of the workpiece material through the guide.\n\n***\n\n### ⚠️ Troubleshooting: Abnormal Processing Concerns (7)\n\nThis section details potential issues that can disrupt the machining process and provides solutions for electrode system integrity.\n\n#### 7.1 Electrode Director Inspection and Maintenance\n\n| Concern Area | Inspection/Check | Required Action / Fix | Critical Safety Point |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Guide Integrity** | Check the guide nozzle and the electrode guide for physical defects (cracks, notches, etc.). | **If debris/dirt is found:** Use fine sandpaper to gently remove foreign matter. | Ensure the guide area is thoroughly cleaned before operation. |\n| **Electrode Guide Fixing** | Inspect the guide mechanism's attachment point. | **Check the fit:** The guide should pass smoothly through the electrode guide without binding. | The guide is secured by stainless steel screws. Excessive force or loose screws can compromise accuracy. |\n\n#### 7.2 Performance Monitoring & Safety Checks\n\n*   **Material Handling:** Before and after machining, the workpiece must be accurately moved through the guide.\n*   **Safety Protocol:** Operators must remain cognizant of the potential for sparks and ejected working fluids.\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Summary of Key Operating Principles\n\n*   **Process Goal:** To achieve smooth and stable machining by ensuring all system parameters are calibrated and the electrode guides are flawlessly maintained.\n*   **Critical Warning:** The machine must be operated with extreme caution due to potential spark discharge and the risk of ejected working fluid.\n*   **Guide Maintenance Principle:** The accuracy and stability of the process directly depend on the pristine condition and secure fastening of the electrode guide and guide nozzle.\n\n[Page 29]\nThis page summarizes detailed procedures for machine setup, focusing specifically on the **Perpendicularity Adjustment of the Electrode Guide** and crucial pre-operational checks for both general processing and specialized bending tasks.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ I. Perpendicularity Adjustment Method of Electrode Guide (Section 3)\n\nThe process involves physically adjusting the guide mechanism to ensure precise perpendicular alignment of the electrode.\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram Key Elements\nThe diagram illustrates a mechanical fixture setup featuring a **VERTICAL ADJUSTMENT SCREW**, indicating a manual, adjustable mechanism used to fine-tune the guide's alignment.\n\n### 📋 Adjustment Procedure (Key Data)\n| Step | Purpose / Action | Key Specifications / Measurements |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(a) Initial Setup** | Position the copper tube electrode. | Electrode diameter $\\geq 1.5 \\text{ mm}$. Electrode must be fit at the electrode guide. |\n| **(b) Alignment Check** | Use specialized tools (square/right-angled block) to measure and verify the gap distance. | Measure the distance between the square block and the copper tube electrode. |\n| **(c) Finalization** | Secure the mechanism. | Tighten **all** vertical adjustment screws thoroughly. |\n\n---\n\n## ✅ II. Pre-Operational Machine Checks\n\nThe document outlines critical checks divided into general processing stability (7.2) and specific tasks like bending (7.3).\n\n### 🔍 7.2 Unstable Processing Check List\nBefore initiating the process, the following seven areas must be thoroughly inspected to prevent instability or malfunction:\n\n| Check Point | Condition to Verify | Purpose / Warning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(1) Processing Conditions** | Confirm all specified operating parameters are correct. | General system readiness. |\n| **(2) Fluid Ejection** | Check for proper fluid ejection. | Ensure the cooling/process fluid exits cleanly from the front end of the copper pipe. |\n| **(3) Electrode Integrity** | Confirm the copper tube electrode is not bent or eccentric. | Ensures stable contact and reliable processing. |\n| **(4) Workpiece Fixture** | Check the fixture holding the workpiece. | Verifies that the work piece is securely and stably mounted. |\n| **(5) Installation** | Confirm electrode holder installation. | Ensure the electrode holder is properly and non-abnormally installed. |\n| **(6) Electrode Consumption** | Confirm the electrode is not worn out. | Prevents premature failure and poor quality output. |\n| **(7) Electrode Verticality** | Check the perpendicular alignment of the electrode. | Crucial for achieving accurate dimensional output. |\n\n### 🔩 7.3 Copper Tube Electrode Bending Check List\nIf the process involves bending the copper tube electrode, these specific checks are mandatory:\n\n| Check Point | Condition to Verify | Importance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(1) Processing Conditions** | Confirm all specified processing parameters. | Standard operational requirement. |\n| **(2) Insulation** | Ensure no electrical insulation exists. | Prevents short circuits between the electrode and the workpiece. |\n| **(3) Wiring Connection** | Verify all necessary output wires are connected. | Confirms electrical continuity between the workpiece and electrode. |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Summary of Key Data and Best Practices\n\n| Component | Key Parameter / Measurement | Recommended Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Diameter** | $\\geq 1.5 \\text{ mm}$ | Essential for correct setup and processing. |\n| **Adjustment** | Vertical Adjustment Screw | Tighten all screws after verifying perpendicularity. |\n| **Alignment** | Using a right-angled block / square | Standard method for measuring and correcting guide alignment. |\n| **System Integrity** | Fluid Ejection & Electrode Wear | Continuous monitoring is required to prevent failure and instability. |\n| **Safety Check** | No Insulation | Must be verified before running the process to prevent electrical faults. |\n\n[Page 30]\nThis document provides a detailed summary of operational checks, troubleshooting protocols, maintenance procedures, and key technical recommendations related to the use of a single-hole copper tube electrode in a machining process.\n\n***\n\n## 💡 Summary Overview: Copper Tube Electrode Operation\n\nThe primary focus of this page is ensuring optimal machine operation by identifying potential issues related to electrode placement, wear, and operational parameters (discharge, sharpening, speed).\n\n### 📋 Key Operational & Setup Checks\n\nBefore beginning or continuing the process, the following fundamental checks must be performed:\n\n1. **Electrode Spacing:** Ensure there is no excessive space between the copper tube electrode and the workpiece.\n2. **Blockage Check:** Confirm that the copper tube electrode is not blocked (clogged).\n\n### 🛠️ Troubleshooting and Warning Protocols\n\nThe page details three specific operational warnings (7.4, 7.5, 7.6), requiring immediate attention upon detection of poor performance:\n\n| Section | Condition Detected | Required Action / Confirmation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **7.4 Abnormal Discharge** | Processing failure / Poor output | Confirm all standard processing conditions are met. |\n| **7.5 Raise The Knife Sharply** | Electrod electrode degradation / Performance issue | 1. Confirm processing conditions. 2. Confirm the copper tube electrode is not bent. 3. **Maintenance:** Use special scissors to cut and remove any blackened or deformed section at the front end. |\n| **7.6 Low Processing Speed** | Slow material removal rate | 1. Confirm processing conditions. 2. Verify the working fluid is spraying smoothly and adequately. 3. Ensure the copper tube electrode is not extending out of the electric chuck too long. |\n\n***\n\n## 🔬 Detailed Maintenance and Technical Procedures\n\n### 1. Electrode Preparation (7.5: Raise The Knife Sharply)\n\nWhen the electrode shows signs of wear, specific maintenance procedures are mandatory:\n\n*   **Blackening/Deformation Check:** If a black spot is found on the front end of the copper tube, this material must be removed.\n*   **Cleaning Procedure:** Use **special scissors** to cut and remove the blackened or deformed part at the front end.\n\n### 2. Technical Recommendation & Key Data\n\nA crucial technical recommendation is provided concerning the electrode type and diameter:\n\n*   **Diameter Specification:** When using a single-hole type copper tube electrode with a diameter of **$\\Phi$1.0mm or more**, it is strongly recommended to switch to a **hollow type copper tube electrode**.\n*   **Reason:** This recommendation is made due to factors like **machining residue** and **unstable processing speed**.\n\n***\n\n## 🖼️ Diagrammatic Summary\n\nThe accompanying diagram illustrates the physical components and the residue generated during the process:\n\n*   **Components Shown:**\n    *   **Workpiece:** The item being machined.\n    *   **Single Hole Copper Tube Electrode:** The tool used for material removal.\n    *   **Processing Residue:** The material and contaminants left behind during the machining process.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Key Data and Action Items Summary\n\n| Parameter | Requirement / Specification | Recommended Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Diameter** | $\\Phi$1.0mm or more (Single-hole type) | **Switch to Hollow Type Electrode** |\n| **Blackened Section Length** | Generally $<10$ mm (If spotted) | Use special scissors for removal. |\n| **Fluid Flow** | Must be smooth and adequate. | Check for blockages and proper pressure. |\n| **Electrode Positioning** | Must be within the electric chuck limit. | Adjust the electrode if it extends too far. |\n| **Spacing** | Minimal gap between electrode and workpiece. | Ensure proper setup alignment. |\n\n[Page 31]\nThis document appears to be an excerpt from a highly technical industrial or machinery maintenance manual. It details specific operational checks, lubrication procedures, and inspection points necessary for keeping the machinery functional and safe.\n\nSince no diagrams were provided, the summary relies heavily on structured text, tables, and bullet points to convey the detailed information.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary of Machinery Inspection and Maintenance Procedures\n\n### I. Pre-Checks and Observations\n\nThe manual starts with general observational checkpoints regarding machine components:\n\n*   **(4) Copper Tube and Electrode Guide:** Must be checked to ensure the material is **not identical** (i.e., ensuring appropriate material selection or condition).\n*   **(5) Electrode Guide:** Must be checked to ensure it is **not perpendicular** (suggesting a potential alignment or wear issue).\n\n### II. W Axis Operational Checks (Section 7.7)\n\nThis section outlines five critical steps for inspecting and maintaining the W Axis function:\n\n1.  **Three-Phase Power:** Confirm that the three-phase electrical power source is out of phase (ensuring correct electrical setup).\n2.  **Three-Phase Electrical Sequence:** Confirm the proper sequence of the three-phase electrical connections.\n3.  **Pneumatic Connections:** Check that the manifold and down buttons are in good contact and function correctly.\n4.  **Limit Switch:** Confirm that the limit switch is damaged and that the machine is stuck (identifying failure points).\n5.  **Intermediate Relay:** Please confirm whether the intermediate relay works normally or if the conduction is not good.\n\n### III. Lubrication Points and Procedures (Section 8.1)\n\nThis is a highly structured section detailing the necessary lubricants, quantities, and procedural descriptions for various machine positions.\n\n#### Key Data Table: Lubrication Schedule\n\n| Lubrication Position | Lubricating Oil | Quantity | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Worldbench Carriage Guide Plane** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Drag Plate Screw And Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Z-Axis Linear Guide, Lead Screw and Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Machine Head Secondary Screw Rod and Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n\n### IV. General Maintenance and Inspection (Sections 8.2 & Subsequent)\n\nThis section provides a comprehensive checklist for general machine integrity, covering power systems, electronics, and fluid mechanics.\n\n#### A. Power Distribution Connector Checklist\n*   **Function:** Check the connection of the three-phase power cable to the grid and the machine.\n\n#### B. Electrical Installation Checklist\n*   **Power Draw:** Check the power distribution unit.\n*   **Electrical Device:** Inspect the electrical device for proper function.\n*   **Water Circulation Device:** Inspect the water circulation system.\n*   **Circuit Board Connector:** Regularly check the connection points.\n*   **Internal Wiring:** Check whether the contact wiring is loose.\n*   **Components:** Check for signs of high-frequency capacitors, shunt, work-pieces, electrode wires, etc.\n*   **High-Current Components:** Ensure all high-current components are properly connected and functional.\n*   **Switch Operation:** Ensure the switch is often used for processing on the panel.\n*   **Plug-in:** If the plug-in is loose, inspect and adjust it immediately.\n\n#### C. Water Circulation Device Checklist\n*   **Condition:** Since the machining fluid has been operating under high pressure conditions, the requirements for the entire water circulation system are relatively high.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** The joint of the water circulation system must be checked for any looseness during inspection.\n\n***\n\n### 🔑 Summary of Key Data & Procedures\n\n*   **Primary Lubricant:** Rail Oil\n*   **Core Action (8.1):** All specified components require "Hand pump automatic injection" at intervals of **1 time / 100 hours**.\n*   **Power:** The system must handle proper three-phase power and verify sequence integrity.\n*   **System Integrity Focus:** High emphasis is placed on checking **connections** (power distribution, circuit boards, contacts) and **fluid systems** (water circulation/machining fluid) due to high-pressure usage.\n\n[Page 32]\nThis page is an excerpt from a technical manual detailing routine maintenance, inspection procedures, and best practices for operating and cleaning machinery. The information is highly structured, focusing on ensuring equipment longevity and proper functionality of core systems like ventilation, fluid handling, and electrical components.\n\nSince the provided text is purely descriptive and lacks actual visual graphics, the summary below presents the required "diagrams" and "tables" by interpreting the technical instructions into structured data and procedural flows.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Detailed Summary: Maintenance and Operating Procedures\n\n### 1. General Principles (Circuit & Safety)\n\n**Critical Action:** Before any maintenance, fluid handling, or detailed cleaning, the machine must be powered down and isolated.\n\n*   **Circuit Board Connection:** Regular inspection is necessary. The circuit board must be examined for proper function.\n*   **Safety Protocol:** The main control power must be **turned off** before performing any cleaning or maintenance activities.\n\n### 2. Component-Specific Maintenance Procedures\n\n#### 🌬️ 8.3 Machine Ventilation Inspection\nThis section outlines the required checks for the cooling system to prevent overheating and ensure efficient operation.\n\n| Component | Inspection Task | Recommended Frequency | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cooling Fan** | Check for proper operation and any signs of blockage/damage. | Regular Inspection | Ensures efficient heat dissipation. |\n| **Air Inlets** | Check for dust accumulation or blockages. | Every **1 to 3 months** | Prevents restricted airflow and machine failure. |\n\n#### 🧪 8.4 Working Fluid Management\nThis section provides key data regarding the preparation, mixing, and replenishment of the machining fluid.\n\n**Source Requirements:**\n1.  Tap water or distilled water.\n2.  Special machining fluid (approved type).\n\n**Key Data Point (Dilution Ratio):**\n*   **Ratio:** 20:1 (20 parts fluid to 1 part water/diluent).\n*   **Use:** The fluid should be used continuously during processing.\n*   **Replenishment:** Fluid levels must be checked regularly and replenished immediately to maintain optimal working conditions.\n\n### 3. Cleaning and Component Care (8.5)\n\nThe general rule for cleaning is **daily cleaning** combined with thorough periodic servicing.\n\n| Component / Area | Cleaning Concern | Action Required | Frequency |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base/Foundation** | Accumulated residue, overflow from filler screw, plane surface. | Thorough cleaning, scrubbing, and removal of all debris. | As needed (due to overflow/residue). |\n| **Fluid Containment** | Residue, contamination, or accumulated particulates. | Flushing and removal of debris. | Daily/As required. |\n| **Fluid Handling** | Potential sources of leaks or improper runoff. | Ensure runoff is directed only to appropriate effluent/collection points. | Ongoing Inspection. |\n\n### 🌐 Maintenance Workflow Diagram\n\n**(Note: As no diagram was provided, this structured flowchart represents the required safety and maintenance sequence.)**\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Identify Need for Maintenance/Cleaning] --> B{Safety Check};\n    B --> C[POWER OFF: Turn off Main Control Power];\n    C --> D[Disassemble/Prepare Component];\n    D --> E[Inspection/Cleaning Process];\n    E --> F{Address Specific Component Needs};\n    F --> G[Reassemble Component];\n    G --> H[Test System Operation];\n    H --> I[Maintenance Complete];\n```\n\n### 4. Key Takeaways and Critical Warnings\n\n*   **Handling Fluid:** While the working fluid itself is not poisonous, **discharged fluid** containing machining chips and waste must be handled carefully. It should never be allowed to flow directly into the sewer or natural outdoor drain.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** Inspection should be comprehensive, covering not just the primary moving parts but also the *support structure* (the base and carrier) for accumulated grime.\n*   **Proactive Cleaning:** Dirt accumulation on the base or carrier can restrict movement and impede clean operation. Daily cleaning is mandatory.\n\n[Page 33]\nThis page is an excerpt from a technical manual detailing the essential **Maintenance Procedures** and a step-by-step **Troubleshooting Process** for a specific piece of machinery (likely industrial or mechanical).\n\nHere is a detailed summary, incorporating tables, a flow diagram, and key data points.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Summary: Machinery Maintenance & Troubleshooting\n\n### I. Key Operational Principles\n\n*   **Goal:** To ensure the continuous, efficient, and safe operation of the machine.\n*   **Warning:** The process can be affected by poor conductivity or sparking. Regular and meticulous cleaning is paramount.\n\n### II. Routine Maintenance and Cleaning Procedures (Action Items)\n\nThese steps detail areas that require regular cleaning and lubrication to prevent malfunction.\n\n| Step | Area to Clean/Maintain | Required Action / Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Waterproof Plate** | The base of the machine. | Must be cleaned carefully. |\n| **Head Storage Tube** | Component for storage. | Needs cleaning. |\n| **Lubrication** | Screw, rotating parts. | Must be maintained with oil to ensure proper lubrication. |\n| **Rotating Head & Guide Plate** | Moving components. | Requires thorough cleaning. |\n| **General** | Machine body/components. | Must be clean to prevent operational failures caused by poor conductivity or sparking. |\n\n### III. Troubleshooting Process (Flow Diagram Summary)\n\nThe page provides a structured diagnostic flow (Section 9) designed to identify the root cause when the "Rotating head does not work." This process is sequential, meaning one check must be passed before proceeding to the next.\n\n#### ➡️ Troubleshooting Flowchart\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[START: Rotating head does not work] --> B{Is the connector in good contact?};\n    B -- No --> C[Action: Check and fix the motor, driver, and pulse power board connectors.];\n    B -- Yes --> D{Is the indicator light normal?};\n    D -- No --> E[Action: Check if proper power and pulse power board light is on.];\n    D -- Yes --> F{Is the rotary switch on and off normally?};\n    F -- No --> G[Action: Replace the rotary switch.];\n    F -- Yes --> H{Does the motor have a sound?};\n    H -- No --> I[Action: Replace the motor.];\n    H -- Yes --> J{Motor speed is abnormal or stuck?};\n    J -- No --> K[Potential Issues: 1. Connector not in good contact. 2. Motor bearing wear.];\n    J -- Yes --> Z[Resolution: Continue basic inspection/maintenance];\n```\n\n### IV. Key Data and Action Items Table\n\nThis table summarizes the immediate corrective actions taken at each stage of the troubleshooting process.\n\n| Fault Diagnosed | Condition Checked | Root Cause / Necessary Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Connector Contact | Check/fix motor, driver, and pulse power board connectors. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Indicator Light | Confirm proper power and pulse power board illumination. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Rotary Switch Operation | Replace the rotary switch. |\n| **Inoperative Head** | Motor Sound/Function | Replace the motor. |\n| **Abnormal Speed/Stuck** | Motor Speed | 1. Check connector contact. **OR** 2. Check motor bearing wear. |\n\n***\n*Note: The instruction for the troubleshooting process explicitly states: "Make sure that the rotary head is clockwise during machining (view from above)."*\n\n[Page 34]\nThis document is a detailed **Diagnostic Flowchart/Troubleshooting Guide** used to diagnose the root cause when a machine spindle cannot move up and down.\n\nThe summary is presented below using a structured flow, a detailed table, and key data points for quick reference.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Spindle Movement Diagnostic Checklist\n\n**Overall Objective:** To systematically troubleshoot mechanical or electrical failures preventing the vertical movement (up and down) of the machine spindle.\n\n### 📊 Diagnostic Flow Diagram (Conceptual)\n\nThe process follows a linear, step-by-step path, starting with the initial symptom and branching based on the answers (Yes/No) to critical checks.\n\n**START** $\\rightarrow$ **[Symptom]:** Spindle cannot move up and down\n$\\downarrow$\n**[Check 1]:** Is the connector loose?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[STOP]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** $\\downarrow$\n**[Check 2]:** Is the servo motor voltage abnormal?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[FIX VOLTAGE]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** $\\downarrow$\n**[Check 3]:** Is the limit switch damaged?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[REPLACE/FIX LIMIT SWITCH]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** $\\downarrow$\n**[Check 4]:** Is the servo board processing abnormal?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[CHECK CONNECTIONS]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** $\\downarrow$\n**[Check 5]:** Is the motor hot?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[SEVERE FAILURE]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** $\\downarrow$\n**[Check 6]:** Are there interferences in each part of main shaft?\n$\\rightarrow$ **(Yes):** (Action) $\\rightarrow$ **[CLEAN/LOOSEN]**\n$\\rightarrow$ **(No):** **[Diagnosis Complete / System Operational]**\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Troubleshooting Table (Q&A Format)\n\nThis table summarizes the entire diagnostic pathway, presenting the question, the implications of a "Yes" answer, and the actions required.\n\n| Step | Diagnostic Question | Yes (Fault Detected) | No (System Passed) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Is the connector loose? | (Action Required) Check and secure the connection. | Proceed to Step 2. |\n| **2** | Is the servo motor voltage abnormal? | Investigate and correct the voltage issue. | Proceed to Step 3. |\n| **3** | Is the limit switch damaged? | Repair or replace the limit switch. | Proceed to Step 4. |\n| **4** | Is the servo board processing abnormal? | **Critical Check:** Check motor/servo board connectors, fix the soldering, and ensure proper electrical grounding. | Proceed to Step 5. |\n| **5** | Is the motor hot? | **Motor Failure:** The motor is faulty ("bad") OR there is motor leakage. | Proceed to Step 6. |\n| **6** | Are there interferences in each part of main shaft? | **Mechanical Fault:** 1. Coupling is loose. 2. Clear the interference blockage. | **System OK:** If all checks are passed, the issue may be complex or non-obvious. |\n\n***\n\n### 💡 Key Data and Action Points\n\n#### 1. Electrical & Component Checks (Focus on Steps 2, 4, 5)\n\n*   **Voltage Checks (Servo Motor):** The diagnostic must verify that the voltage provided to the servo motor is within the acceptable operational range.\n*   **Servo Board Check:** If processing is abnormal, the diagnostic requires a thorough check of:\n    *   The motor connector.\n    *   The servo board connector.\n    *   The status of the soldering (must be fixed/secure).\n    *   *Note:* The system indicates that if the servo board detects an issue, the **speeed adjustment button** should not be used for diagnosing, as this may be unrelated to the primary failure.\n*   **Motor Health:** If the motor is hot, it suggests immediate failure: either the motor itself is damaged, or there is a fluid/electrical leakage.\n\n#### 2. Mechanical & Structural Checks (Focus on Steps 1, 3, 6)\n\n*   **Connectors:** The initial check focuses on simple physical connectivity (loose connectors).\n*   **Limit Switch:** If the switch is damaged, it must be repaired or replaced to ensure proper positional feedback to the system.\n*   **Shaft Interferences:** The final mechanical check involves ensuring that the main shaft and all parts surrounding it are free of obstructions or physical binding.\n    *   **Common Causes:** Loose couplings or foreign objects causing friction.\n\n#### 3. Advanced Electrical Troubleshooting (Implied Checks/Data)\n\nWhile not always asked in the flow, the diagram contains specific technical advice that implies necessary knowledge of the system components:\n\n*   **Voltage Specifics:** If the servo board is found to be faulty, the following components and voltages must be checked:\n    *   **DC Voltage:** 32V\n    *   **Components:** Bridge stack, filter capacitor, and voltage transformer.\n    *   **Secondary Check:** The AC output must be checked for proper function.\n\n#### Summary Conclusion\n\nThe diagnostic process is comprehensive, moving logically from simple fixes (loose cables, loose couplings) to complex electrical failures (faulty servo boards, motor leakage). The severity of the required intervention increases as the diagnostic proceeds down the flowchart.\n\n[Page 35]\nThis page is a highly detailed **Troubleshooting and Maintenance Flowchart** for a complex industrial fluid or electro-chemical spray system (likely involving a high-pressure pump and electrode mechanism).\n\nThe purpose of the document is to guide a technician through sequential checks to identify the root cause of system failure, ranging from low fluid levels to component blockages.\n\nSince the source is a flowchart, the summary is presented using a structured table format to replicate the decision-making logic, alongside key warnings and data points.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary: System Troubleshooting Guide\n\n### I. General Operational Warnings and Key Data\n\nThe document emphasizes several critical maintenance actions that must be performed regardless of the specific failure point:\n\n| Area | Action Required | Specification/Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Oil Change** | Pump Oil | Must be changed regularly. |\n| **Pressure Regulator** | Adjustment | Must be adjusted to a set value of **6 MPa**. |\n| **Leaks** | Joints Check | Check all joints for potential water leakage. |\n| **Fluid Addition** | Working Fluid | Use either **tap water** or **distilled water**. |\n\n### II. Troubleshooting Flowchart (System Check Points)\n\nThe flowchart follows a logical path, where failure at one step requires subsequent checks. The table below outlines the decision points and corresponding actions.\n\n#### 1. Initial Failure Condition\n*   **Symptom:** The working fluid is not normally sprayed.\n*   *(The flow chart progresses only if the initial system status is questioned/checked.)*\n\n#### 2. Mechanical System Integrity Checks (The Motor & Pump)\n| Checkpoint | Question | Pass (Y) Action | Fail (N) Action / Cause |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Rotation** | Is the motor rotating normally? | Continue check. | 1. The fuse is burnt out. <br> 2. The contactor is broken. <br> 3. The motor is broken. |\n| **Pump Sound** | Does the pump have a rotary sound? | Continue check. | 1. The shaft connection key is detached or worn seriously. <br> 2. The pump is broken. |\n| **High Pressure Line** | Is the high pressure water line normal? | Continue check. | *Self-Correction:* (Requires no specific action, usually indicates a leak or blockage.) |\n\n#### 3. Operational Performance Checks (The Fluid & Power)\n| Checkpoint | Question | Action / Correction | Failure/Issue Detected |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fluid Pressure** | Does the working fluid pressure reach the set pressure? | *(System is functioning normally.)* | *If failed:* Indicates a potential blockage or low input pressure. |\n| **Filter Blockage** | Whether the inlet pipe filters element are blocked? | **Action:** Clean the filter and filter. | Blockage is the primary cause. |\n| **Fluid Volume** | Is there enough working fluid? | **Action:** Fill the working fluid. <br> **Check:** Ensure the inlet filter sinks to the bottom of the container. | Insufficient fluid volume. |\n| **Electrode Blockage** | Whether the copper tube electrode hole is blocked? | **Action:** Use special scissors to cut the front of the copper tube electrode. | Electrode tip blockage. |\n\n---\n\n### III. Summary Flow Diagram (Conceptual)\n\nThe entire guide can be visualized as a checklist, moving from electrical/mechanical failures (Motor/Pump) to fluid/system failures (Pressure/Filter/Level).\n\n**START $\\rightarrow$** *Is Fluid Spraying Normally?*\n$\\downarrow$\n**[Electrical/Mechanical Check]** (Motor $\\rightarrow$ Pump $\\rightarrow$ Water Line)\n$\\downarrow$\n**[Performance Check]** (Pressure $\\rightarrow$ Filters $\\rightarrow$ Fluid Level $\\rightarrow$ Electrode)\n$\\downarrow$\n**END / CORRECTIVE ACTION**\n\n### IV. Key Data Table (At-a-Glance Checklist)\n\n| Component | Failure Symptom | Diagnosis / Cause | Recommended Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor** | Failure to run | Blown fuse, broken contactor, broken motor. | Inspect/Replace electrical components. |\n| **Pump** | No rotary sound | Detached key, worn shaft, broken pump. | Check physical connections and mechanical integrity. |\n| **Filter Element** | Low pressure | Blocked filter. | Clean the filter element. |\n| **Working Fluid** | Poor performance | Low fluid level. | Fill the working fluid (use tap or distilled water). |\n| **Electrode Tip** | Poor spray pattern | Blocked electrode hole. | Use special scissors to cut the front of the copper tube electrode. |\n| **System Integrity** | Leakage/Failure | Loose joints or incorrect pressure setting. | Check all joints for leaks. Adjust regulator to **6 MPa**. |\n\n[Page 36]\nThis page details a comprehensive **Troubleshooting Flowchart** used to diagnose the root causes of **Insufficient Processing Performance** in a machining or cutting process. The information is structured as a sequential decision tree, guiding the user through a series of checks until the problem source is identified.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Diagnostic Flowchart: Insufficient Processing Performance\n\nThe diagram outlines eight key sequential checks, starting from the initial observation of poor performance and narrowing down the potential failure points (Workpiece, Fluid, Tooling, Machinery).\n\n### 🔍 Step 1: Initial Checks (Conditions & Workpiece)\n\n| Diagnostic Check | Condition | Action / Conclusion |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Processing Conditions** | $\\text{No}$ (Not Normal) | View the machining parameters to determine the set value. |\n| **Workpiece Appropriateness** | $\\text{Yes}$ (Appropriate) | Proceed to material checks. |\n| **Workpiece Material** | Super Hard | (Implies specific material requirements/wear considerations). |\n\n### 🛠️ Step 2: Operational Components Check (Fluid, Electrode, Discharge)\n\nThis section focuses on the consumables and immediate physical environment.\n\n#### A. Working Fluid Pressure\n*   **Check:** Is the working fluid pressure normal?\n*   **If No (Abnormal):**\n    *   **Action:** Adjust the fluid pressure to the set value.\n    *   **Note:** Potential causes include using tap water or distilled water (indicating fluid quality issues).\n*   **If Yes (Normal):** Proceed.\n\n#### B. Electrode Status\n*   **Check:** Is the electrode abnormal?\n*   **If Yes (Abnormal):**\n    *   **Cause 1:** The electrode is bent or deflected too much.\n    *   **Cause 2:** The electrode quality is poor.\n*   **If No (Normal):** Proceed.\n\n#### C. Workpiece Discharge\n*   **Check:** Is workpiece discharge abnormal?\n*   **If Yes (Abnormal):**\n    *   **Solution/Action:** Verify the following mechanical requirements:\n        1. The electrode wire and the workpiece wire must be firmly fixed.\n        2. The workpiece must be in full contact with the balance bar and clamped.\n*   **If No (Normal):** Proceed.\n\n### 🌡️ Step 3: Machinery and Process Checks (Conduction & Spindle)\n\nThe final stages investigate heat transfer and the main mechanical components.\n\n#### A. Rotating Head Conduction\n*   **Check:** Is rotating head conduction abnormal?\n*   *(No specific action is listed in the 'Yes' branch, implying a review of heat transfer parameters is needed.)*\n\n#### B. Spindle Functionality\n*   **Check:** Is there a spindle abnormality?\n*   **If Yes (Abnormal):** (Indicates mechanical or electronic failure)\n    *   **Cause 1:** Servo board aging.\n    *   **Cause 2:** Mechanical failure.\n*   **If No (Normal):** The process is confirmed stable (based on the preceding checks).\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Summary Table of Key Findings and Troubleshooting Paths\n\n| Area Checked | Abnormal Sign / Failure Mode | Potential Cause(s) / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Processing Parameters** | Insufficient Performance | Review and adjust machining parameters to the set value. |\n| **Working Fluid** | Low/Abnormal Pressure | 1. Adjust pressure to set value. 2. Check for incorrect fluid type (e.g., tap/distilled water). |\n| **Electrode** | Abnormal | 1. Excessive bending or deflection. 2. Poor electrode quality. |\n| **Workpiece Discharge** | Abnormal | 1. Ensure strong fixing of both electrode and workpiece wires. 2. Confirm full contact and clamping of the workpiece to the balance bar. |\n| **Spindle** | Abnormal | 1. Servo board aging. 2. Mechanical failure. |\n| **General** | N/A | If all checks pass (fluid, electrode, discharge, and spindle are normal), the machine is functioning correctly relative to the set parameters. |\n\n***\n\n### 💡 Key Takeaways\n\n*   **Systematic Diagnosis:** The flowchart enforces a methodical, top-to-bottom approach to troubleshooting, ensuring no major component failure (from fluid to servo board) is overlooked.\n*   **Focus on Fundamentals:** The most common issues are related to **setup/parameters** (processing conditions), **consumables** (electrode/workpiece), and **basic maintenance** (fluid pressure and mechanical fixation).\n*   **Critical Components:** The system highlights the importance of the **servo board** (for electronic faults) and the physical **electrode/workpiece fixation** (for mechanical reliability) when diagnosing severe performance drops.\n\n[Page 37]\nThis page presents a comprehensive set of schematics detailing the electrical wiring and assembly of a complex system, likely an experimental or industrial machine. The documentation is divided into three main sections: the main electrical wiring diagram, the panel wiring diagram, and the final electrical assembly drawing.\n\nSince the provided content is highly technical schematics, the summary will focus on describing the **structure, flow, components, and connections** shown in the diagrams, using tables to organize the key data.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Overall Summary and Function\n\nThe diagrams illustrate the complete electrical architecture, showing how primary power is input, controlled, and distributed to various output mechanisms (vibrating plates, amplifiers, resistance loads). The system utilizes a modular approach, managed by a central control panel.\n\n**Key Data Flow:**\n1. **Power Input:** Incoming power connects through circuit breakers.\n2. **Control & Safety:** Power passes through control relays, timers, and a transformer for specialized low-voltage control signals.\n3. **Distribution:** Controlled signals are routed through terminals and wiring harnesses.\n4. **Output:** Power is supplied to peripheral loads, including motors/plates and variable resistors.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 I. 10. ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM (Main Schematic)\n\nThis diagram establishes the overall power hierarchy and main components of the system.\n\n### A. Key Components and Functions\n| Component | Function/Role | Key Connections Shown |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Circuit Breakers** | Safety and Overcurrent Protection. | Primary incoming power lines. |\n| **Timers/Relays** | Control Logic; manages the timing and sequencing of operations. | Receive control power; activate output circuits. |\n| **Transformer** | Converts high voltage (HV) to low voltage (LV) for precise control circuits. | Connects between main power and control logic boards. |\n| **Output Terminal Blocks** | Safe distribution point for controlled power to external loads. | Feeds power to the main panel wiring area. |\n| **Contactors/Switches** | Main switches to activate or deactivate high-power circuits. | Linked to the control logic board (low voltage side). |\n\n### B. Signal Flow (Main Power to Control)\nThe diagram shows a progression: **AC Input $\\rightarrow$ Protection (Breakers) $\\rightarrow$ Control (Timers/Transformer) $\\rightarrow$ Output Activation (Contactors).**\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ II. PANEL WIRING DIAGRAM (Control Panel Detail)\n\nThis section details the physical arrangement and interconnection of the control components within the electrical panel.\n\n### A. Panel Layout and Components\nThe panel houses multiple control units, including:\n\n*   **Timers and Relays:** Multiple modular units are shown, suggesting different, synchronized operational stages (e.g., Timer 1, Timer 2).\n*   **Control Power Sources:** Dedicated terminal blocks and wiring indicate structured power distribution.\n*   **Terminal Management:** Extensive use of terminal blocks ensures that every wire connection is organized and traceable.\n\n### B. Wiring Interconnections\nThe diagram demonstrates two types of wiring connections:\n1.  **Control Wiring (Low Voltage):** Used to send signals (e.g., "Start," "Stop," "Signal Confirmed") from timers to contactors.\n2.  **Power Wiring (High Current):** Used to supply the main operational power from the input side to the output motor/load connections.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡️ III. ELECTRICAL ASSEMBLY DRAWING (Peripheral Loads)\n\nThis final section illustrates how the main panel connects to the specific functional output components of the system.\n\n### A. Connection Summary Table\n| Periphery Component | Function | Power Connection Point | Control Linkage |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Vibration Plate** | High-power output; generates physical vibration. | Connected to the main power lines from the panel. | Receives activation signal from the main control panel. |\n| **Amplifier Board** | Provides controlled power amplification for specific signals or motors. | Connects to the central panel's high-power terminals. | Controlled via the main timing/relay sequence. |\n| **Resistance Box** | Provides variable resistive loads (dump loads). | Connected to the main power supply lines (current draw). | Used to dissipate energy or regulate system draw based on control signals. |\n\n### B. Conceptual Diagram Flow\nThe flow here is straightforward:\n**Panel Output Terminals $\\rightarrow$ Wiring Harness $\\rightarrow$ Peripheral Load (Plate, Amplifier, Resistance)**\n\n*   **Vibration Plate:** The wiring shows a direct, robust connection for physical movement/power.\n*   **Amplifier Board & Resistance Box:** These are shown to draw power from the panel and function as test or load mechanisms, demonstrating the system's capability to precisely manage energy consumption.\n\n---\n\n## 📈 Conclusion: Key Takeaways\n\nThe entire page constitutes a complete **wiring schematic package**.\n\n*   **Purpose:** To provide a precise, technical guide for installing, troubleshooting, and maintaining the electrical infrastructure of a complex electromechanical device.\n*   **Key Principles Illustrated:** Safety (Circuit Breakers), Control (Timers/Transformer), and Modular Expansion (Peripheral Load Drawings).\n*   **Complexity Level:** High. The diagram requires specialized knowledge in electrical engineering, circuit analysis, and industrial control systems.\n\n[Page 38]\nThis page is an extremely complex **Electrical Schematic** (a detailed wiring diagram) for a sophisticated electronic control unit, likely associated with an industrial automation system, a power management system, or a specialized motor control application.\n\nDue to the high density and complexity of the circuit, the summary cannot provide a simple textual description of every wire. Instead, I will provide a detailed functional analysis, breaking the schematic down into its core subsystems, identifying the major data flows, and summarizing the key components and voltage levels.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Comprehensive Schematic Summary\n\n### 1. ⚙️ Overall Function and Purpose\nThe schematic illustrates the internal workings of a central control module. Its primary function is to **receive multiple power inputs**, condition these inputs into various required DC voltages, process control signals via microcontrollers/logic ICs, and then actuate physical outputs (like relays or load drivers) to control external equipment.\n\n### 2. 🏗️ Functional Block Diagram (Conceptual Diagram)\nThe schematic is organized into four distinct, sequential blocks:\n\n| Block | Function | Primary Components | Key Process |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **I. Power Input & Rectification** | Manages incoming AC and DC power sources. | Transformers, Rectifier Bridges, Large Capacitors. | Converts variable input power into stable, separated DC voltage rails. |\n| **II. Power Conditioning & Supply** | Provides stable, regulated voltages for the logic and components. | Voltage Regulators, Filter Capacitors, Power Supplies (labeled DC/AC). | Generates dedicated power rails (e.g., 5V, 12V, 24V) for the control logic. |\n| **III. Control & Logic Processing** | Reads inputs, processes data, and determines output states. | Integrated Circuits (ICs, e.g., U labels), Logic Gates, Signal Conditioners. | The "brain" of the system; manages state transitions and signal routing. |\n| **IV. Output Stage & Actuation** | Interfaces the low-power logic signals with the high-power loads. | Relays, Solid State Switches, Output Contact Blocks. | Physically switches high-voltage/high-current lines based on control signals. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. ⚡ Key Data Analysis: Power Distribution\n\nThe unit is designed to handle multiple voltage levels, indicating integration with various industrial standards.\n\n| Power Rail/Voltage | Purpose | Function/Role | Location in Schematic |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **AC Input** | Primary source power. | Feeds the initial rectification stage. | Top left section (AC connections). |\n| **DC Power Supply** | Main operating power. | Supplies the fundamental high-power rails (e.g., main motor voltage). | Central, large transformer/rectifier section. |\n| **24V DC** | Standard industrial control voltage. | Used for most sensor inputs and low-power control logic. | Several regulators and input points. |\n| **12V DC** | Auxiliary power. | Used for auxiliary loads or specific sensor types. | Dedicated regulator paths. |\n| **5V DC** | Digital logic power. | Dedicated power for microcontrollers and ICs. | Multiple point regulators (critical for logic function). |\n| **GND (Ground)** | Reference point. | Common ground for all signals and power. | Throughout the diagram. |\n\n### 4. 💡 Key Data Analysis: Signal Flow and Control\n\nThe flow of control signals (digital and analog) moves from the inputs, through the central ICs, and out to the relays.\n\n#### A. Input Signals (Sensors/Measurements)\n*   **Type:** Low-voltage, conditioned signals (often 4-20mA current loops or discrete digital signals).\n*   **Role:** Reading the status of the environment or connected machinery (e.g., limit switches, sensor feedback).\n*   **Processing:** These signals are conditioned in the Logic Processing block (IC circuits) to be compatible with the core control logic.\n\n#### B. Control Logic (The "Brain")\n*   **Components:** Clusters of ICs and associated passive components (resistors, capacitors).\n*   **Function:** Executes the programmed logic. It takes the sensed inputs and determines the necessary actions.\n*   **Key Data:** The output of this stage is not power, but a clean, low-current signal (e.g., 5V HIGH/LOW) that activates the output relays.\n\n#### C. Output Actuation (The "Muscle")\n*   **Components:** Multiple relays and switch contacts.\n*   **Function:** This is the high-power switching interface. The low-power signal from the Control Logic activates the relay coil, causing the physical relay contacts to switch a high-power line (e.g., switching 220V AC or 24V DC to a motor).\n\n### 5. 🖼️ Summary Table: Component Roles\n\n| Component Type | Typical Designation | Role in System | Impact of Failure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rectifier Bridges** | (Large component on power lines) | Convert AC power into pulsed DC power. | Total loss of primary power input. |\n| **Voltage Regulators** | (Regulator ICs) | Ensure that specific ICs (like the microcontrollers) receive a perfectly stable voltage (e.g., 5V). | Logic failure; inability to process signals even if high voltage is present. |\n| **IC Clusters** | (ICs, marked U) | Execute the core control program and decision-making algorithms. | Control failure; the system cannot react to inputs. |\n| **Relays / Contact Blocks** | (Switching mechanism) | Interface the low-voltage control signal to the high-current load. | Output failure; the system cannot physically turn on or off external devices. |\n\n### 6. 📝 Conclusion\n\nIn summary, this schematic describes a fully integrated, robust **Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) or industrial controller**. It is designed to be self-sufficient, handling power from multiple sources and managing the complex flow from electrical input $\\rightarrow$ low-voltage digital processing $\\rightarrow$ high-voltage physical output. Proper understanding of this schematic is critical for troubleshooting power issues, signal integrity problems, or control failures.\n\n[Page 39]\nThis document is a formal **Certificate of Compliance** issued by a manufacturing company, certifying that a specific piece of machinery meets quality standards and is approved for sale.\n\nSince the source material is a text-based form and not an illustrative diagram, the summary utilizes highly structured tables and detailed section breakdowns to convey all key information.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: Certificate of Compliance\n\n### I. Document Overview\n\n*   **Document Type:** Certificate of Compliance (Section 11).\n*   **Purpose:** To formally certify that the manufactured machine has been inspected, qualified, and approved by the company for delivery.\n*   **Status:** The machine is stated to be **"Qualified and Approved for Delivery."**\n\n### II. Key Technical Data (Key Data Table)\n\nThe certificate specifies the unique identifying and physical dimensions of the machinery.\n\n| Specification Field | Value | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Model** | DK-908 | The specific model identifier. |\n| **Work Travel** | 400 X 300 mm | The operational dimensions of the machine (Length x Width). |\n| **Serial Number** | (Blank Line) | Space reserved for the unique unit identifier. |\n\n### III. Compliance and Approval Process\n\nThe document mandates three key actions for the compliance confirmation, requiring signatures and dates:\n\n1.  **Production Department:** Required signature (Indicates manufacturing sign-off).\n2.  **Quality Inspection Department:** Required signature (Indicates final quality assurance sign-off).\n3.  **Date of Manufacture:** Required date (Records the date the unit was completed/certified).\n\n**Certification Statement:**\n> *“The Machine is Qualified and Approved for Delivery.”*\n\n### IV. Company and Contact Information\n\nThe footer provides complete corporate identity and contact details for the issuing authority.\n\n#### 🏢 Company Details\n\n| Field | Information |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Company Name** | LUOYANG XINCHENG PRECISION MACHINERY CO.,LTD |\n| **Address** | NO.256 TANGGONG ROAD, LUOYANG, HENAN, CHINA 471000 |\n| **Telephone (TEL)** | 0379-63512276 |\n| **Fax Machine (FAX)** | 0379-69960770 |\n\n---\n***Disclaimer:** The page number displayed is 38.*\n\n[Page 40]\nThis page details the **Spare Parts and Accessories Packing List** for a machine tool. The document serves as an inventory guide, listing various components, their model specifications, and required quantities for maintenance and operation.\n\nSince the source material is purely text and a table, no diagrams are included in the summary.\n\n