ance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **EU Declaration of Conformity** | 67 | Compliance with European Union standards. |\n| **UKCA Declaration of Conformity** | 68 | Compliance with UK regulatory standards. |\n| **UKCA Declaration of Incorporation** | 70 | Specific declaration required for UK market incorporation. |\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Key Data and Summary Table\n\n| Subject Area | Range of Topics Covered | Pages | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Initial Setup & Safety** | Switching off, Relocation, Disposal | 53–54 | Establishing safe initial usage and lifecycle management. |\n| **Physical Maintenance & Parts** | Belt changes, Alignment, Accessories, Spares | 55–61 | Detailed guides for component replacement and repair. |\n| **Operation & Support** | Cleaning, Troubleshooting, Malfunctions, FAQ | 62–66 | User support for routine care and problem-solving. |\n| **Compliance** | EU/UK Declarations | 67–70 | Legal documentation ensuring market safety and standards compliance. |\n\n[Page 6]\nThis document is a technical instruction manual page detailing the nomenclature, identification, and specifications for MISUMI Conveyor Systems. It provides a comprehensive guide to how product codes are structured and what technical parameters they represent.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including the structure, key data, and tables presented on the page.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: Conveyor Systems Identification\n\n### ⚙️ I. General Overview and Purpose\n\nThe primary function of this document is to help users correctly identify and order MISUMI conveyor components. It establishes a rigid product coding system (nomenclature) where specific characters and digits in the article number correspond to fixed technical attributes, such as motor power, dimensions, and operational function.\n\n### 🏷️ II. Product Identification (The Code Structure)\n\nThe general structure of the product code is complex and multi-variable, exemplified by sequences like **SWKA-100-20-5-25** or **SWKAAA-XXX-YY-ZZ**.\n\n#### A. The Article Number System (Nomenclature Breakdown)\nThe core product identity is governed by the **MISUMI Article Number System**. This system dictates that the physical specifications of the conveyor system are directly encoded into the product code.\n\n*   **Principle:** Each segment of the article number corresponds to a specific physical or electrical parameter.\n*   **Rule:** Users must map the required technical specifications (e.g., desired length, required motor wattage) to the corresponding digits in the code.\n\n#### B. Product Code Components (Example Interpretation)\nWhile the full key is not provided, the structure suggests the following variable elements:\n\n*   **Prefix/Base Model:** (e.g., SWKAAA, SWKA) – Defines the general type or series of the conveyor system.\n*   **Dimension/Size Codes:** Digits representing length, width, or other physical dimensions (e.g., **100**, **20**, **5**).\n*   **Motor/Power Codes:** Digits defining the motor type, power, or performance ratings (e.g., **25**, **12.5**).\n*   **Suffix/Finish Codes:** Letters or digits indicating operational variants (e.g., specifying if it has a belt, if it is equipped with a drive, or the specific material).\n\n### 📊 III. Key Technical Data and Configuration Examples\n\nThe page presents several tables that serve as detailed examples of how different configurations result in distinct product codes and specifications.\n\n#### A. General Specifications Table (Example: SWKA-100-20-5-25)\n\nThis table shows a fundamental set of parameters that define a basic conveyor setup.\n\n| Parameter | Value / Range | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **System Type** | Conveyor systems | General category. |\n| **Dimensions** | 100 (mm), 20 (mm), 5 (mm) | Likely representing Length, Width, and another key dimension. |\n| **Power/Voltage** | 25 | Possibly a motor size or speed setting. |\n| **Motor Power** | N/A | Motor power unit (Watts, HP). |\n| **Belt Specification** | N/A | Details the required belt type/material. |\n| **Motor Manufacturer** | N/A | Details the required motor brand. |\n\n#### B. Configuration Example: Conveyor System Without Drive\n\nThis example (e.g., **SWKAAE-100-20-5-25**) shows the specifications for a belt conveyor structure that **does not include an active motorized drive unit.**\n\n| Specification | Data Point | Interpretation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type** | Conveyor system without drive | Defines the operational status (passive). |\n| **Dimensions** | 100, 20, 5 | Standard dimensions (Length, Width, etc.). |\n| **Motor Power** | N/A | No motor power listed (Confirming "without drive"). |\n| **Operation** | Belt, curtain, weir, heat, can impair these values | Suggests the conveyor handles various materials or operations. |\n\n#### C. Configuration Example: Conveyor System With Drive\n\nThis example (e.g., **SWKAE-100-20-5-25**) shows the specifications for a complete, motorized conveyor system.\n\n| Specification | Data Point | Interpretation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type** | Conveyor system with drive | Defines the operational status (active/motorized). |\n| **Motor Power (Example)** | 230V (230/50 Hz) | Specifies the required electrical voltage and frequency. |\n| **Motor Power (Example)** | 12.5 | Specifies the rated motor output (kW or HP). |\n| **Operating Voltage** | V | The electrical requirements for running the system. |\n| **System Status** | Complete | Indicates all necessary components are included. |\n\n### 💡 Summary of Key Takeaways\n\n1.  **System Dependency:** The most critical takeaway is that the article number is not arbitrary; it is a coded declaration of the system's technical capacity.\n2.  **Motorization:** The product code structure dictates whether the system is a passive conveyor (**"without drive"**) or an active, motorized unit (**"with drive"**).\n3.  **Data Retrieval:** To select a product, a user must synthesize multiple technical requirements (Dimensions, Voltage, Power, Operation Type) and translate them into the precise format of the MISUMI article number.\n\n[Page 7]\nThis document section provides critical operational, safety, and procedural instructions for the use of conveyor systems (likely industrial materials handling systems). The summary below details the permitted uses, strictly prohibited actions, and mandatory safety protocols.\n\n***\n\n## 📋 Summary of Operating Instructions (Conveyor Systems)\n\nThe manual is divided into three main areas: defining **What Can Be Done** (Intended Use), defining **What Must NOT Be Done** (Improper Use/Warnings), and detailing **Who Can Operate It** (Operational Mandates).\n\n### 1. ✅ Permitted Operations (Intended Use)\n\nThe conveyor systems are designed for the specialized purpose of transporting industrial materials, solids, and homogenous objects.\n\n**Key Scope of Operation:**\n*   **Material Transport:** Handling solid, homogenous, or specified materials in a defined direction.\n*   **Process Compliance:** The system must be operated following all manufacturer's specifications, training, and protocols outlined in the manuals.\n*   **Purpose:** The operation is restricted to the purpose for which the machine was manufactured and designed.\n\n### 2. ❌ Prohibited Actions (Improper Use & Warnings)\n\nThis section outlines strict prohibitions and mandatory safety precautions. **Failure to follow these rules constitutes a severe safety hazard and a violation of the manual.**\n\n| Category | Action Forbidden | Detail / Reason |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Transport** | People or Animals | Never use the conveyor for transporting any living creatures or humans. |\n| **Material Handling** | Unspecified Goods | Cannot be used to carry non-approved materials, liquids, or gases (e.g., hazardous chemicals, liquids). |\n| **Modification** | Alterations/Changes | Do not modify, alter, or reconfigure the system. This includes bypassing safety features. |\n| **Operational Scope** | Misuse/Negligence | Operating the machine contrary to the operating manual or deviating from established safe practices. |\n| **Maintenance** | Unqualified Personnel | Only trained, authorized, and qualified personnel are permitted to perform maintenance, repairs, or adjustments. |\n\n### ⚠️ Critical Safety & Modification Warnings\n\nThese points are mandatory and relate directly to safety integrity and technical modification.\n\n*   **Unauthorized Modifications:** Any modification to the machine, even seemingly minor changes, is strictly forbidden. The system must be kept in its original, manufacturer-approved state.\n*   **Qualified Personnel:** Repairs and adjustments must be carried out by qualified technical personnel who understand the system's function.\n*   **Electrical Safety:** All servicing and power work must adhere to stringent electrical safety protocols. Procedures for dealing with electrical power (voltage, connection) must be followed precisely.\n\n### 3. 🧑‍🏭 Operational and Technical Mandates\n\nThese guidelines address the procedural requirements for safe and legal operation.\n\n#### A. Personnel and Training\n*   **Authorized Use:** The machine must only be operated by personnel who have undergone the required training and who are explicitly instructed in the operating manual.\n*   **Safety Protocols:** Operators must always follow safety instructions and adhere to all established procedures for inspection and maintenance work.\n\n#### B. Identification and Inspection\n*   **Machine Integrity:** The operator must always confirm the identity and operational status of the conveyor system before starting work.\n*   **Inspection:** The system must undergo routine inspection to ensure all mechanical and electrical components are in safe working order.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Summary Table of Key Operational Rules\n\n| Operational Area | Requirement (DO) | Prohibition (DO NOT) | Key Stakeholder |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Function** | Transport solids/homogenous materials safely and efficiently. | Transport people, animals, liquids, or unauthorized goods. | Operator/User |\n| **Safety** | Adhere strictly to all documented safety guidelines and protocols. | Bypass safety features or ignore warnings. | All Personnel |\n| **Maintenance** | Perform work only following approved SOPs and manufacturer guidelines. | Modify, dismantle, or repair without explicit authorization and qualified staff. | Qualified Technician |\n| **Compliance** | Use the equipment only for its intended, designed purpose. | Use the equipment outside its specified operational limits or scope. | Owner/Supervisor |\n\n[Page 8]\nThis summary provides a detailed breakdown of the operational guidelines, safety standards, and safety signaling systems presented in the document excerpt.\n\n***\n\n# Detailed Summary of Operational and Safety Guidelines\n\nThe document serves as a crucial guide outlining the legal responsibility, operational objectives, and standardized safety signage required for the safe and proper use of equipment (impliedly, conveyor systems).\n\n## 📄 General Information (Section 2.1 & 2.2)\n\n### 🔗 Guarantee and Liability (2.1)\nThis section establishes the legal boundaries of product use.\n*   **Assumption of Risk:** The user assumes responsibility for the safe operation of the equipment.\n*   **Liability Limitation:** MiSUMI (the manufacturer) limits its liability if damages occur due to unauthorized use, improper maintenance, or failure to follow safety instructions.\n*   **Operational Requirement:** The user must ensure that all operators are adequately trained and understand the operational principles before use.\n\n### 🎯 Objectives of the Operating Manual (2.2)\nThe manual is mandatory and comprehensive, serving the following core purposes:\n1.  **Safety:** To provide all necessary information to prevent accidents and ensure personnel safety.\n2.  **Efficiency:** To guarantee the optimal and efficient functioning of the system.\n3.  **Compliance:** To guide users in adherence to local regulations and established safety practices.\n\n***\n\n## ⚠️ Safety Signaling System (Section 2.3)\n\nThe core of the document details four distinct categories of standardized safety signs, each communicating a different type of instruction or warning.\n\n### 🟢 2.3.1 Mandatory Safety Signs (Actions Required)\nThese signs indicate an action that **must** be taken to ensure safety.\n\n| Sign/Topic | Required Action | Key Data/Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Protective clothing!** | Must be worn. | Wear appropriate PPE (e.g., gloves, vests). |\n| **Headgear use!** | Head protection is mandatory. | Use hard hats/helmets to prevent impact injuries. |\n| **Eye protection!** | Must be worn. | Use safety glasses or goggles. |\n| **Use safety shoes!** | Specific footwear is mandatory. | Prevents crush injuries and provides support. |\n| **Secure state!** | Equipment must be secured. | Ensures machines and loads are stable and properly fixed. |\n\n### 🟡 2.3.2 Hazard Signs (Warnings of Danger)\nThese signs alert users to potential dangers and risks present in the work environment.\n\n| Hazard Type | Danger Indicated | Key Safety Focus |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical Voltage** | High electrical danger. | Risk of shock; treat areas as live and dangerous. |\n| **Falling/Tripping** | Danger of falling or tripping hazards. | Watch footing and overhead spaces. |\n| **General Hazard** | Unknown or general danger. | Requires immediate attention and caution. |\n| **Pneumatics** | Compressed air/gas hazard. | Risk of sudden release or injury from stored energy. |\n| **Splunged Load** | Hanging or suspended weight. | Danger from falling objects/loads. |\n| **Automatic Start-Up** | System operates automatically. | Warning to keep hands clear and follow standard startup procedures. |\n\n### 🔴 2.3.3 Prohibition Safety Signs (Restrictions)\nThese signs strictly forbid certain actions or activities in a given area.\n\n| Forbidden Action | Sign/Restriction | Key Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Unauthorized Access** | Do not enter. | Restricted area; only authorized personnel are allowed. |\n| **Smoking** | No Smoking. | Fire hazard; must not introduce ignition sources. |\n| **Resting/Stopping** | No Rest/No Stopping. | Area is strictly operational; no loitering allowed. |\n| **Mechanical Use** | No Ride/No Lifting. | Do not use the machinery for transportation or lifting purposes. |\n| **Unauthorized Activity** | Prohibition Sign (General). | An activity is forbidden (e.g., tampering, operating without clearance). |\n\n### 🔵 2.3.4 Instruction Signs (Guidance and Information)\nThese signs provide supplemental information regarding environmental impacts or specific procedures.\n\n| Sign Topic | Indication/Instruction | Key Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Dangerous to Environment** | Environmental risk. | Warning about spillages or disposal procedures that affect the environment. |\n\n***\n\n## 🔑 Key Data Summary Table\n\n| Category | Symbol/Color | Purpose | Examples (Action/Warning) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mandatory** | Typically Blue/White | Tells you what **MUST** be done. | Wear Headgear, Use Safety Shoes, Secure State. |\n| **Hazard** | Typically Yellow/Black | Warns you of potential **DANGER**. | High Voltage, Slippery Floor, Overhead Load. |\n| **Prohibition** | Typically Red/White | Tells you what is **FORBIDDEN**. | No Smoking, Unauthorized Access, Do Not Enter. |\n| **Instruction** | Varies (Blue/Green) | Provides **GUIDANCE** or information. | Dangerous to Environment. |\n\n[Page 9]\nThis document is a detailed summary of the **Safety Instructions** for operating conveyor systems, emphasizing mandatory operational procedures, hazard recognition, and the clear delineation of responsibilities between the operator and the facility owner.\n\n***\n\n## 📚 Summary of Safety Instructions\n\n**Section 3: Safety Instructions**\n*(This section mandates strict adherence to safety protocols to ensure safe operation.)*\n\n### 3.1 Scope and General Principles\n\nThe primary focus is ensuring that all personnel understand the gravity of safety rules. Operating the machinery requires constant alertness and adherence to established guidelines.\n\n**🛑 Key Warning (DANGER):**\nFailure to observe safety instructions or perform mandatory checks (inspection, operation, maintenance) is considered a major safety breach. Any omissions must be reported immediately.\n\n### 3.1.1 Operator Obligations (The User)\n\nThe operator must assume full responsibility for the safe use of the equipment.\n\n| Action Required | Detail / Mandate |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Inspection & Operation** | The operator must perform routine visual checks and functional tests *before* starting the machine. |\n| **System Check** | Ensure all necessary safety features (e.g., guards, emergency stops) are fully functional and in place. |\n| **Safety Adherence** | Strictly follow all operational manuals and safety guidelines. Never bypass safety mechanisms. |\n| **Damage Reporting** | Any damage found—even minor scuff marks—must be immediately reported and isolated until repaired. |\n\n### 3.1.2 Responsibilities of the Operating Company (Facility Owner)\n\nThe company has a comprehensive set of duties to ensure a safe working environment and must provide the necessary tools and training.\n\n**Mandated Company Protocols:**\n\n*   **System Design:** The equipment must be properly designed and regularly maintained by qualified personnel.\n*   **Operating Procedures:** Detailed safe operating conditions and manuals must be provided for the machine.\n*   **Training:** All personnel must receive mandatory training (theoretical and practical) before operating the system.\n*   **Maintenance & Repair:** The company must ensure qualified and capable personnel are available for repairs and maintenance.\n*   **Risk Assessment:** The company must conduct thorough risk assessments, especially following any accident or modification.\n*   **Emergency Planning:** Comprehensive training for managing emergencies must be provided.\n\n### ⚠️ Identified Hazards and Potential Risks\n\nThe manual details several potential hazards that require constant awareness.\n\n| Hazard Type | Description | Mitigation Mandate |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mechanical Failure** | Failure of any component (e.g., belt, roller, bearing) due to wear or defect. | Mandatory pre-start inspection and regular maintenance schedules. |\n| **Physical Strain** | Risk of injury due to improper lifting, movement, or sustained posture during operation. | Proper training and adherence to ergonomic guidelines. |\n| **Electrical/Mechanical Danger** | Contact with live wires, moving parts, or improperly guarded machinery. | Strict enforcement of lockout/tagout (LOTO) procedures and constant guarding checks. |\n\n---\n\n### ⚙️ Summary of Key Data and Directives\n\n| Category | Directive | Priority Level |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Safety Protocols** | Always wear appropriate Personal Protective Equipment (PPE). | **CRITICAL** |\n| **Machine Integrity** | Never operate the machine if any abnormality, damage, or modification has occurred. | **CRITICAL** |\n| **Training** | All operators must be fully trained and authorized before starting work. | **HIGH** |\n| **Incident Management** | Report *all* accidents, near-misses, and equipment failures immediately. | **HIGH** |\n| **Focus Area** | **The primary focus is *prevention* through inspection and disciplined adherence.** | **IMMEDIATE ACTION** |\n\n[Page 10]\nThis document excerpt provides critical safety instructions and operational guidelines for the use and maintenance of conveyor systems. The summary is divided into four main sections: **Operational Responsibilities, Procedural Warnings, Hazard Identification, and Residual Risk Protocols.**\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: Conveyor Systems Operating Instructions\n\n### I. Operational Responsibilities (3.1.3)\n\nPersonnel must adhere to strict guidelines to ensure safe operation and prevent accidents.\n\n**Key Personnel Duties:**\n*   **Vigilance:** Operating personnel must continuously monitor the equipment and the surrounding work area.\n*   **Adherence to Rules:** Strict compliance with all operating and maintenance rules is mandatory.\n*   **Training:** Only trained personnel should operate the machinery.\n*   **Incident Reporting:** All accidents, near-misses, and equipment malfunctions must be immediately reported.\n\n### II. Critical Procedural Warnings (Safety Checklist)\n\nThe manual emphasizes several areas where human error or improper procedure can lead to severe injury or damage.\n\n#### ⚠️ Warning Summary\n| Area of Hazard | Warning/Risk | Required Action/Precaution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Injury** | Pinch points or entanglement risks. | Never attempt to clear blockages or debris while the power is active. |\n| **Power Failure** | Machine shutdown or power interruption. | If the machine stops due to a failure, do not attempt to operate it until the cause is resolved and the system is cleared. |\n| **Improper Loading** | Overloading the conveyor system. | Ensure the load does not exceed the rated capacity. |\n| **Debris/Foreign Objects** | Debris buildup or objects caught by the belt. | Clearing foreign objects must only be done after the machine is completely powered down and locked out. |\n| **Cleaning** | Cleaning while the system is energized. | The machine must be completely de-energized and locked out before cleaning or maintenance. |\n\n#### Key Data Points\n*   **Power Disconnection:** The main power supply must be fully locked out and secured before any maintenance or inspection.\n*   **Machine Security:** Never approach or work on the machine if it is unsecured or operating in an unstable manner.\n\n### III. Safety Instructions and Hazard Identification (3.2)\n\nThis section details the inherent dangers and the mandatory use of specialized safety equipment.\n\n#### ⚠️ Safety Icon Legend\n| Icon/Symbol | Meaning | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **🟡 WARNING** | **Caution/Risk Warning** | Indicates a potential hazard (e.g., electrical shock, moving parts). Read the surrounding text for specific actions. |\n| **🔵 CAUTION** | **Procedural Warning** | Indicates that residual energy or system components remain active even after primary power is cut off. |\n| **🔴 DANGER** | **Immediate Danger** | Indicates an immediate threat of severe injury or death (e.g., lifting machinery, structural collapse). Requires immediate stop. |\n| **🛑 EMERGENCY STOP** | **Mandatory Action** | Must be utilized immediately upon sensing an immediate, critical danger. |\n\n#### 🚧 Danger Point: Electrocution and Structural Damage\n*   **Source:** Any electrical circuit, lifting equipment, or structural rigging.\n*   **Hazard:** Accidental physical or electrical injury, or structural collapse.\n*   **Rule:** Never bypass safety mechanisms or modify load-bearing structures.\n\n### IV. Residual Energy and Risk Protocols\n\nThis is the most technical section, detailing the risks associated with various energy sources that persist even after the main power is off.\n\n#### ⚡ Hazard Table: Residual/Stored Energy\nResidual energy must be considered from all potential sources. **Never assume the system is completely inert.**\n\n| Energy Source | Potential Hazard | Example | Critical Safety Protocol |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrical** | Electric shock, residual voltage, stored charge. | Capacitor discharge, grounding issues. | Disconnect all power sources and verify zero voltage before touch. |\n| **Pneumatic** | Sudden blast, pressurized gas release. | Compressed air lines, hydraulic lines. | Use appropriate bleed-off procedures and secure lines. |\n| **Hydraulic** | Sudden release of high-pressure fluid. | Hydraulic cylinders, fluid reservoirs. | Pressure must be relieved according to SOP before inspection. |\n\n#### 🚨 Critical Safety Protocols\n1.  **Isolation:** Always follow proper lock-out/tag-out (LOTO) procedures for *all* energy sources before intervention.\n2.  **Energy Release:** If an energy source (pneumatic, hydraulic) is involved, its energy *must* be safely and fully released (bled down) before maintenance.\n3.  **Emergency Action:** In the event of an immediate danger (e.g., crushing, high pressure), the Emergency Stop button must be used immediately, and the area must be isolated.\n\n[Page 11]\nThis page provides critical **Safety Instructions** for operating the Conveyor System, focusing on identifying potential hazards (Emissions and Mechanical Energy) and defining mandatory safety protocols.\n\nSince the original document uses visual cues and mandated lists, the summary is presented using descriptive text, warning symbols (as placeholders for the actual diagrams), and organized tables to capture the detailed information.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Safety Instructions Summary (Page 3)\n\n### I. General Operational Hazards and Warnings\n\n| Hazard Type | ⚠️ Warning Message | Key Action / Precaution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Heat/Thermal** | Danger of injury due to hot surfaces. | Caution must be maintained when handling or operating the machine. |\n| **Falling** | Danger of falling when transporting people. | Ensure operators are trained and follow proper transport protocols. |\n| **Noise** | Risk of injury due to noise. | Mandatory use of **hearing protection** is required at all times. |\n\n### II. Section 3.2.1 Hazards - Emissions\n\nThis section focuses on hazards related to the operation and byproducts of the conveyor system.\n\n*   **Heat/Thermal Hazard:** The risk of injury from hot surfaces (e.g., motor housings, belts) remains.\n*   **Noise Hazard:** Any operation of the equipment must account for potential noise-induced hearing loss.\n\n### III. Section 3.2.2 Hazards - Mechanical Energy\n\nThis is the most detailed section, focusing on hazards from moving parts, crushing, and physical contact.\n\n#### A. 🚨 Crushing and Hand Injuries (Mechanical)\n\nThis hazard warns of the serious risk of amputation or crushing injury from the conveyor system’s movement.\n\n| ❗ Warning Symbol | Key Hazard/Risk | Mandated Safety Protocols |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **🔴** | **Crushing/Amputation Risk** | Never reach into the system while it is running or undergoing maintenance. |\n| **🔵** | **Operational Control** | Only qualified personnel may work on the machine. **Must be shut down** before maintenance or inspection. |\n| **🔑** | **Maintenance Rule** | Do not attempt to adjust, modify, or service the system without proper training and supervision. |\n\n#### B. 🚧 General Operational and Personnel Hazards\n\nThis section outlines absolute rules regarding system interaction and unauthorized access.\n\n| ⚠️ Warning Symbol | Hazard Area | Absolute Prohibitions (DO NOT) |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **🟨** | **Machine Interaction** | **DO NOT** attempt to clear jams, fix breakdowns, or adjust the system while it is powered on. |\n| **🧑‍🏭** | **Personnel Access** | **DO NOT** allow unauthorized persons to enter the machine area. |\n| **🏃** | **Movement** | **DO NOT** stand or ride on the conveyor belt or structures. |\n| **🔌** | **System Integrity** | **DO NOT** bypass safety mechanisms or tamper with electrical components. |\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Key Data and Mandatory Safety Tables\n\nThe following tables summarize the specific required actions and prohibitions found throughout the safety guide.\n\n### 1. Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) Requirements\n\n| Hazard | Required PPE | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Noise** | Hearing Protection | Mandatory to prevent noise-induced hearing loss. |\n| **Thermal** | Appropriate PPE | Required when handling or working near hot surfaces. |\n\n### 2. Critical Safety Action Checklist\n\n| Action Category | ✅ **Must Do** | ❌ **Must Not Do** (Prohibited Actions) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Operation** | Ensure the area is clear before starting. | Operate the machine if it appears damaged or suspicious. |\n| **Maintenance** | Shut down and de-energize the machine completely. | Reach into the system while it is operating. |\n| **Access** | Only qualified, trained personnel may enter the work zone. | Allow unauthorized persons to enter the work zone. |\n| **Repairs** | Use authorized repair methods and tools. | Attempt repairs or clear jams without following safety protocols. |\n\n### Summary Diagrammatic Flow (Concept)\n\nThe document emphasizes a sequence of safety steps:\n\n$$\\text{Identify Hazard} \\longrightarrow \\text{Implement PPE} \\longrightarrow \\text{De-Energize} \\longrightarrow \\text{Perform Work} \\longrightarrow \\text{Restore Power}$$\n\n(This cyclical process ensures that power must be removed before physical interaction with the mechanical system.)\n\n[Page 12]\nThis page, titled **3.3 Emergency Situations**, provides critical safety instructions and procedures that must be followed in the event of an accident, fire, or other emergency involving the equipment.\n\nSince the source material is text-based warnings, the summary is presented using structured formatting, a detailed table, and visual cues (emojis/icons) to replicate the essential warning nature of the original document.\n\n---\n\n## 🚨 Detailed Summary: Emergency Situations (3.3)\n\nThe section is divided into three key areas: mandatory preparation, specific electrical hazards, and definitive action protocols.\n\n### 1. Mandatory Preparations (⚠️ Important)\n\nAll personnel are required to take proactive steps to ensure safety knowledge is maintained:\n\n*   **First Aid Training:** Personnel must attend a first-aid course regularly to ensure they are prepared to handle medical emergencies.\n\n### 2. Electrical & Fire Hazards (⚡️ DANGER)\n\nThe manual warns of two immediate and severe life-threatening dangers related to electricity and fire fighting:\n\n| Hazard | Description | Critical Warning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electric Shock** | There is a constant danger of death due to electric shock. | **Extreme Risk** |\n| **Fighting Fires with Water** | Danger of death exists when attempting to fight fires with water, especially if the source of the fire or the equipment is electrical. | **NEVER USE WATER** |\n\n### 3. Emergency Action Guide (🔥 Key Procedures)\n\nThe core of the page provides clear instructions on what **MUST** be done and what must **NEVER** be done during an emergency.\n\n#### **🚫 DO NOTs (Prohibited Actions)**\n\nThe following actions are strictly forbidden as they increase the risk of electrocution or worsen the fire:\n\n1.  **Water on Fuel/Electrical Fires:** Never use water to extinguish:\n    *   Fuel fires (oil, gasoline, solvents).\n    *   Electrical fires.\n\n#### **✅ DOs (Required Actions)**\n\nThe procedures for managing a fire and the equipment are highly specific:\n\n| Priority | Action | Procedure Details |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Equipment Power** | **Turn Off Power:** If it is safe to do so, the primary action must be to shut down or turn off the equipment power source *before* attempting to fight the fire. | This minimizes the electrical shock hazard. |\n| **2. Fire Extinguishment** | **Use CO₂:** Fires must be extinguished using appropriate agents, specifically **CO₂ (Carbon Dioxide)**. | CO₂ is safe for both electrical and flammable material fires. |\n\n***\n\n### 📊 Summary Table: Emergency Response Flowchart\n\nThis table summarizes the sequence of safe actions to follow during a general emergency (e.g., fire or electrical accident):\n\n| 🛑 Situation | 🛡️ Primary Goal | 🛠️ Safe Procedure | 🚫 NEVER DO |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fire** (Especially involving electricity or flammables) | Eliminate immediate danger. | 1. Turn off equipment power (if safe). 2. Use CO₂ extinguisher. | Use water. |\n| **Electric Hazard** | Prevent contact with live current. | Seek qualified assistance. | Touch damaged or sparking equipment. |\n| **Injury/Accident** | Provide immediate care. | Attend regularly scheduled first-aid training. | Delay treatment or assume danger has passed. |\n\n[Page 13]\nThis page, titled "Installation and Operation" within the context of Conveyor Systems, provides comprehensive technical specifications, mechanical structure diagrams, and installation options for MISUMI conveyor products.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including tables, diagrams, and key data points.\n\n***\n\n## 📜 Detailed Summary: Conveyor System Installation and Operation\n\n### ⚙️ 1. Technical Data (Section 4.1)\n\nThis table provides essential electrical and operational specifications for the conveyor systems:\n\n| Parameter | Specification | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Rating** | 230 V | |\n| **Tolerance/Fluctuation** | $\\pm 10\\%$ | |\n| **Frequency** | 50 Hz | |\n| **Motor Fluctuation** | - | (No specific value given, indicates a range or standard) |\n| **Type of Power Connection** | Fixed connection | |\n| **Airborne Sound** | $\\le 55$ dB | |\n| **Electromagnetic Compatibility** | According to the EMC Directive and the applicable EMC standards for use in industrial area | |\n\n### 🔩 2. Conveyor System Types (Section 4.2)\n\nMISUMI offers three primary types of conveyor systems, depending on the operational needs and the material being transported:\n\n1.  **Belt Conveyor:** Used for transporting items via a flexible belt.\n2.  **Chain Conveyor:** Utilizes a chain mechanism for movement.\n3.  **Timing Belt Conveyor:** Uses a timing belt for precise, often indexed, movement.\n\n### 🔌 3. Drive Motor Installation Options (Section 4.2.1)\n\nThe manual illustrates three key options for installing the drive motor relative to the conveyor system:\n\n*   **Head Drive:** The motor is installed at the beginning or end (the "head") of the conveyor.\n*   **Centre Drive:** The motor is positioned in the middle ("centre") of the conveyor structure.\n*   **Integrated Drive:** The motor system is designed to be seamlessly built into the structure of the conveyor itself.\n\n### 🏗️ 4. Mechanical Structure Diagram (Section 4.2.2)\n\nThe belt conveyor features a numbered diagram detailing its core mechanical components:\n\n| Component Number | Component Name | Function / Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective Cover | Encloses and protects the internal mechanism. |\n| **2** | Drive Unit | The motor assembly that provides power. |\n| **3** | Conveyor Belt | The main surface on which objects are carried. |\n| **4** | Frame | Provides the structural support for the entire system. |\n| **5** | Drive Guard | Safety component that encloses the rotating drive unit. |\n| **6** | Drive Unit (here, head drive) | Specifically indicates the location/type of the motor unit. |\n\n### ⚠️ 5. Safety and Usage Guidelines\n\n*   **Safety:** Users are strictly advised to follow safety instructions detailed in **Chapter 3, "Safety Instructions."**\n*   **Technical Reference:** For further information regarding technical data, operation, and detailed product information, users must consult the catalogue's documentation.\n*   **Motor Control:** The control of the motor is managed by either a circuit breaker or electronic controllers.\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Summary Table: Key Data and Components\n\n| Category | Item / Data Point | Detail / Value | Reference Section |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Operational Specs** | Voltage Rating | 230 V | 4.1 Technical Data |\n| | Frequency | 50 Hz | 4.1 Technical Data |\n| | EMC Compliance | Industrial Area Standard | 4.1 Technical Data |\n| **System Types** | Conveyor Options | Belt, Chain, Timing Belt | 4.2 Conveyor System Types |\n| **Motor Installation** | Locations | Head, Centre, Integrated | 4.2.1 Drive Motor Installation |\n| **Components** | Frame | Structural Support | 4.2.2 Mechanical Structure (4) |\n| | Belt | Transport Surface | 4.2.2 Mechanical Structure (3) |\n| | Drive Unit | Motor Power Source | 4.2.2 Mechanical Structure (2) |\n| **Safety Warning** | Mandatory Reading | Chapter 3, "Safety Instructions" | Safety Guidance |\n\n[Page 14]\nThis page provides a detailed technical summary of two primary types of conveyor systems: **Chain Conveyors** and **Timing Belt Conveyors**, along with information on system configurations.\n\n***\n\n## 🔗 4.2.3 Chain Conveyor\n\nThis section details the components and operational features of the Chain Conveyor system.\n\n### 🔧 Mechanical Structure Diagram (Labels 1–7)\n\n| Label No. | Component | Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective cover | Guarding mechanism. |\n| **2** | Chain | The primary conveyor element. |\n| **3** | Chain tensioner | Component for maintaining chain tension. |\n| **4** | Frame | The structural support of the conveyor. |\n| **5** | Drive unit | The motor housing/assembly. |\n| **6** | Drive shaft | Shaft connecting the motor to the drive unit. |\n| **7** | Drive guard | Safety guard over the drive unit. |\n\n### ✨ Special Features\n\n*   **Two-track:** Capable of running in two separate lanes.\n*   **Positive:** Designed for reliable movement transfer.\n*   **Slip-free:** Mechanism prevents slippage during operation.\n*   **High Capacity:** Designed for transporting heavy loads.\n*   **Operation:** The chain is driven by the motor via a drive shaft, ensuring synchronous running.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ 4.2.4 Timing Belt Conveyor\n\nThis section details the components and operational features of the Timing Belt Conveyor system.\n\n### 🔧 Mechanical Structure Diagram (Labels 1–8)\n\n| Label No. | Component | Description |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Protective cover | Guarding mechanism. |\n| **2** | Belt pulley | Pulley that supports and drives the belt. |\n| **3** | Timing belt | The primary conveyor element (toothed belt). |\n| **4** | Frame | The structural support of the conveyor. |\n| **5** | Drive unit (here, motor drive) | The motor/gear assembly. |\n| **6** | Drive shaft | Shaft connecting the motor to the drive unit. |\n| **7** | Drive guard | Safety guard over the drive unit. |\n| **8** | Drive belt | Belt connecting the motor to the drive unit (if applicable). |\n\n### ✨ Special Features\n\n*   **Two-track:** Capable of running in two separate lanes.\n*   **Positive:** Designed for reliable movement transfer.\n*   **Slip-free:** Mechanism prevents slippage during operation.\n*   **Load Handling:** Supports the transport of bulk goods.\n*   **Maintenance:** Belts are guided and can be re-tensioned.\n*   **Operation:** Timing belts are driven by the motor via a drive shaft, ensuring synchronous running.\n\n***\n\n## ⚡ 4.3 Conveyor System Configurations\n\nThis section outlines the various ways the conveyor system can be configured regarding its power and drive mechanisms.\n\n### 🔌 Configuration Options\n\nThe conveyor system can be configured based on the presence or absence of a drive mechanism and a belt:\n\n*   Conveyor system with drive and belt\n*   Conveyor system without drive\n*   Conveyor system without belt\n*   Conveyor system without drive and without belt\n\n### ⚠️ Important Notes and Accessories\n\n*   **Manual Check:** Users are strongly advised to read the differing applicability of the accompanying manual and the specific safety instructions for the chosen conveyor system.\n*   **Accessories:** A wide range of accessories is available. For detailed information, users should refer to the section **"Accessories" on Page 60**.\n\n[Page 15]\nBased on the provided image, this page is an excerpt from a technical manual or catalog section titled "Installation and Operation" (Page 4). The entire page content is structured as a comprehensive **cross-reference table** listing various product designations, their corresponding types, and the page number where more information can be found.\n\n***\n\n### 📋 Summary Overview\n\nThis table functions as a detailed index for different conveyor components manufactured by MISUMI. The data is systematically organized by **Page Number**, **Product Designation Code**, and the **Conveyor/Belt Type**. The key data demonstrates a structured inventory covering three main categories: Flat belts, Timing belts, and Plastic chains.\n\n### 📊 Key Data Table (Product Index)\n\nSince the original content is entirely tabular, the detailed summary is presented by reproducing the data structure and categorizing the product types found within the index.\n\n| Page | Designation | Type | Conveyor Category |\n| :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| 12–16 | SVKAE to GVHFE | Flat belt conveyor | Flat Belt |\n| 17–19 | GVTFAE to GTVTWE | Timing belt conveyor | Timing Belt |\n| 18–27 | GTVTWAE to CVSE | Flat belt conveyor | Flat Belt |\n| 20–23 | CVGRE to CVSE | Flat belt conveyor | Flat Belt |\n| 24–29 | CVSEFE to CVMTSE | Flat belt conveyor / Timing belt conveyor | Flat Belt/Timing Belt |\n| 30–33 | CVTGTAE to CVPSE | Timing belt conveyor / Flat belt conveyor | Timing Belt/Flat Belt |\n| 34 | CVSPCE | Plastic chain conveyor | Plastic Chain |\n\n### 🔍 Detailed Analysis and Patterns\n\nThe table can be broken down into distinct product groups, each representing a different conveyor function.\n\n#### 1. Flat Belt Conveyors (Major Group)\n*   **Designations:** A wide variety of codes (e.g., SVKAE, GVHFE, GTVTWAE, CVGRE).\n*   **Function:** Used for standard flat belt movement.\n*   **Range:** Appears consistently across multiple pages (12–27, 20–23).\n\n#### 2. Timing Belt Conveyors\n*   **Designations:** Codes starting with `GTV` or `CVTG` (e.g., GVTFAE, CVMTSE).\n*   **Function:** Designed for precise, timed movement (often used for index or synchronization).\n*   **Range:** Significant focus in the middle section of the index (Page 17–29).\n\n#### 3. Plastic Chain Conveyors\n*   **Designations:** Codes starting with `CVSP` (e.g., CVSPCE).\n*   **Function:** Used for heavy-duty or specific material transfer requiring a chain mechanism.\n*   **Range:** Appears exclusively on Page 34, indicating a specific, singular product type.\n\n### 📈 Missing Elements and Key Data Points\n\n*   **Diagrams:** No diagrams are included on this page. The function of the page is purely reference and textual indexing.\n*   **Key Data:** The primary data points are the **Product Designations** (alphanumeric codes) and the **Product Type**. These codes are crucial as they link the product name to its technical specifications in subsequent sections of the manual.\n*   **Scope:** The index is highly comprehensive, spanning product pages from 12 to 34.\n\n[Page 16]\nThis document is a detailed technical datasheet from MISUMI covering two types of conveyor system installations: **4.3.1 SVKAE** and **4.3.2 SVKBE**. Both sections detail the structure, specifications, performance data, and operational parameters for flat belt conveyors.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Overview and General Specifications\n\nThe product line details belt conveyors suitable for various industrial installations.\n\n### **General Technical Specifications (Common to both models)**\n\n| Parameter | Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $50 - 400$ | mm |\n| **Length** | $300 - 3000$ | mm |\n| **Weight** | $3.7 - 28.8$ | kg |\n| **Power Output** | $6$ | kW |\n| **Frequency** | $50$ | Hz |\n\n### **Common Special Features**\n\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   Pulley $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Head drive.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 4.3.1 SVKAE: Installation and Operation\n\nThis section presents the technical details and performance curves for the SVKAE model.\n\n### **Diagram and Model Description**\nThe diagram illustrates a standard belt conveyor setup driven by a head unit.\n\n### **Key Performance Data: Conveyor Capacity Graph**\nThe graph titled **"Conveyor Capacity"** shows the relationship between the conveyor's capacity (measured in units of force/load) and the belt speed.\n*   The curve demonstrates that capacity increases as the belt speed increases, reaching a peak load capacity.\n*   *Note:* The capacity data is dependent on the belt speed and the selected transmission ratio.\n\n### **Table: Gearhead Reduction Ratio**\nThis table details the operational parameters of the gearhead unit based on the required belt speed and operating frequency.\n\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{m/min}$) | 50 Hz | 60 Hz |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $5$ | $56.4$ | $67.7$ |\n| $15$ | $37.6$ | $45.1$ |\n| $30$ | $22.6$ | $27.1$ |\n| $60$ | $15.5$ | $18.8$ |\n| $90$ | $11.3$ | $13.5$ |\n| $150$ | $6.8$ | $8.4$ |\n| $180$ | $5.0$ | $6.0$ |\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 4.3.2 SVKBE: Installation and Operation\n\nThis section presents the technical details and performance curves for the SVKBE model, highlighting an added feature.\n\n### **Diagram and Model Description**\nThe diagram illustrates a belt conveyor setup, similar to SVKAE, but with an explicit addition.\n\n### **Special Feature Comparison (Key Difference)**\nThe primary difference between the two models is the added component in the SVKBE:\n*   **SVKAE:** Standard conveyor system.\n*   **SVKBE:** Includes a **Belt meandering prevention crosspiece**, suggesting enhanced stability and performance on long runs.\n\n### **Performance Graphs and Tables**\nThe graph (Conveyor Capacity) and the Gearhead Reduction Ratio table for 4.3.2 SVKBE are structurally identical to 4.3.1 SVKAE, suggesting that the core operational parameters and limits are consistent, while the mechanical improvement (meandering prevention) provides operational reliability.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data Points and Differences\n\n| Feature | 4.3.1 SVKAE | 4.3.2 SVKBE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Function** | Flat Belt Conveyor System | Flat Belt Conveyor System |\n| **Key Enhancement** | Standard operation | **Belt meandering prevention crosspiece** |\n| **Special Features** | Pulley $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$, Head drive | Pulley $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$, Head drive, *Meandering Crosspiece* |\n| **Capacity Curve** | Graph provided (Capacity vs. Speed) | Graph provided (Capacity vs. Speed) |\n| **Gearhead Ratio (50 Hz Example)** | Defined in table | Defined in table |\n| **Technical Specs** | Consistent (e.g., Power: $6 \\text{ kW}$) | Consistent (e.g., Power: $6 \\text{ kW}$) |\n\n### **Conclusion**\nBoth models offer high-performance flat belt conveyor solutions. Model **SVKBE** is presented as an upgraded system compared to SVKAE, incorporating a **belt meandering prevention crosspiece** to ensure stable and reliable operation, especially in complex or extended installations. The operational performance (capacity and gear ratios) is well-defined across both units.\n\n[Page 17]\nThis page provides a detailed technical specification and performance data for two industrial conveyor systems: **4.3.3 SVKNE** and **4.3.4 SVKRE**. The document structure is highly technical, focusing on physical dimensions, electrical requirements, and operational performance (load vs. speed).\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, structured by key sections.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ I. Overview and Technical Specifications\n\nThe page presents two models of industrial belts/conveyors, both designed for material handling. The models are nearly identical in structure, suggesting they are variations of the same core system.\n\n### A. Visual Representation (Diagrams)\n\n*   **4.3.3 SVKNE:** Shown with a technical diagram illustrating a belt-drive mechanism.\n*   **4.3.4 SVKRE:** Shown with a technical diagram illustrating a similar belt-drive mechanism.\n\n### B. Special Features\n\nBoth models share common features:\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   Pulley $\\emptyset$ 30 mm.\n*   Central drive.\n*   Belt with meandering prevention crosse/vice.\n\n### C. Technical Data (Dimensions and Electrical)\n\n| Parameter | Unit | 4.3.3 SVKNE | 4.3.4 SVKRE | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | mm | 50 - 400 | 50 - 400 | Variable |\n| **Length** | mm | 390 - 3000 | 390 - 3000 | Variable |\n| **Weight** | kg | 5.4 - 15 | 5.4 - 15 | Variable |\n| **Power Output** | W | 6 - 20 | 6 - 20 | Variable |\n| **Voltage** | V | 220 | 220 | |\n| **Frequency** | Hz | 50 | 50 | |\n\n---\n\n## 📈 II. Operational Performance Analysis\n\nThe core of the document is the performance data, which details the relationship between **Belt Speed** (Output) and **Torque/Load** (Input) at two standard frequencies: 50 Hz and 60 Hz.\n\n### A. Graphical Representation (Performance Curves)\n\nThe graphs illustrate the "Gearhead reduction ratio" and "Belt speed" in relation to varying electrical parameters. The curves typically show that as the operational load (torque) increases, the permissible belt speed decreases, and vice-versa, allowing engineers to select the appropriate gearhead reduction ratio for the application.\n\n### B. Key Data Tables: Performance Metrics\n\nThe following tables summarize the performance characteristics at various speed/torque points (Load capacity is measured in Nm/kW).\n\n#### ⚡ Table 1: Performance at 50 Hz\n\n| Belt Speed (units not specified, likely mm/s) | Torque/Load (Nm/kW) | 50 Hz (Capacity) | 60 Hz (Capacity) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **5** | - | 4.7 | 56.5 |\n| **7.5** | - | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| **12.5** | - | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| **15** | - | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **18** | - | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **25** | - | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **30** | - | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **36** | - | 6.5 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | - | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **60** | - | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| **75** | - | 2.6 | 3.1 |\n| **100** | - | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **120** | - | 2.0 | 2.4 |\n| **150** | - | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| **180** | - | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n#### ⚡ Table 2: Performance at 60 Hz (Repeat/Confirmation)\n\n*(Note: The data in the 50 Hz and 60 Hz columns are presented side-by-side, showing the relative difference in capacity between the two frequencies for the same given belt speed.)*\n\n| Belt Speed | 50 Hz Capacity | 60 Hz Capacity |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **5** | 4.7 | 56.5 |\n| **7.5** | 31.4 | 37.7 |\n| **12.5** | 18.8 | 22.6 |\n| **15** | 15.7 | 18.8 |\n| **18** | 13.1 | 15.7 |\n| **25** | 9.4 | 11.3 |\n| **30** | 7.9 | 9.4 |\n| **36** | 6.5 | 7.9 |\n| **50** | 3.9 | 4.7 |\n| **60** | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n| **75** | 2.6 | 3.1 |\n| **100** | 2.4 | 2.8 |\n| **120** | 2.0 | 2.4 |\n| **150** | 1.6 | 1.9 |\n| **180** | 1.3 | 1.6 |\n\n***Observation:*** The performance data consistently shows that the **60 Hz** frequency allows for higher torque/load capacity compared to 50 Hz across most measured speed ranges.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 III. Further Information\n\nThe document provides navigational links for users seeking supporting documentation:\n\n*   **Connection:** Available on page 43.\n*   **Belt characteristics:** Available on page 36.\n*   **Accessories:** Available on page 63.\n\n[Page 18]\nThis page provides detailed technical specifications and operational data for two specialized conveyor bearing units: **4.3.5 GVHAE** and **4.3.6 GVFAE**, designed for conveyor systems.\n\nThe summary is structured by product, followed by a consolidation of key shared data.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Product 1: 4.3.5 GVHAE\n\n**Diagram:** The section includes a clear diagram showing the physical bearing unit designed for conveying mechanisms.\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Designed for Flat belt conveyors.\n*   Supports single belt operation.\n*   Features a $\\text{Ø } 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley.\n*   Utilizes a Head drive mechanism.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Shared Specifications)\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 30–300 | Applicable range. |\n| **Length** | 300–2000 | Applicable range. |\n| **Weight** | $4 \\text{ kg } \\sim 25 \\text{ kg}$ | Weight range. |\n| **Power Input** | $6 \\text{ kW} \\sim 25 \\text{ kW}$ | Power range. |\n| **Voltage** | $220 \\text{ V}$ | Standard operating voltage. |\n| **Frequency** | $50 \\text{ Hz}$ | Standard operating frequency. |\n\n### 📉 Performance Data (Load vs. Speed)\n\nThe tables demonstrate the performance characteristics (presumably load capacity or torque) based on operating frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n**Performance Table 1 (50 Hz vs 60 Hz):**\n\n| Length | 50 Hz (Performance) | 60 Hz (Performance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 55 | 36.4 | 67.7 |\n| 75 | 37.6 | 65.1 |\n| 100 | 31.3 | 57.6 |\n| 150 | 27.6 | 60.6 |\n| 180 | 22.6 | 57.7 |\n\n**Performance Table 2 (Load vs. Speed):**\n\n| Length | 50 Hz (Performance) | 60 Hz (Performance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 55 | 36.4 | 67.7 |\n| 75 | 37.6 | 65.1 |\n| 100 | 31.3 | 57.6 |\n| 150 | 27.6 | 60.6 |\n| 180 | 22.6 | 57.7 |\n\n***Note on Graphs:*** *The accompanying graph plots performance (Load/Speed) against belt length (mm), indicating the relationship between mechanical stress and operational length for both 50 Hz and 60 Hz cycles.*\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Product 2: 4.3.6 GVFAE\n\n**Diagram:** The section includes a clear diagram showing the physical bearing unit, similar in design to GVHAE but optimized for full belt use.\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Designed for Flat belt conveyors.\n*   Supports single belt operation.\n*   Features a $\\text{Ø } 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley.\n*   Utilizes a Head drive mechanism.\n*   Includes **Full belt** capability. (This is the key differentiating feature from the GVHAE model).\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (Shared Specifications)\nThe technical specifications are identical to the GVHAE model:\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 30–300 |\n| **Length** | 300–2000 |\n| **Weight** | $4 \\text{ kg } \\sim 25 \\text{ kg}$ |\n| **Power Input** | $6 \\text{ kW} \\sim 25 \\text{ kW}$ |\n| **Voltage** | $220 \\text{ V}$ |\n| **Frequency** | $50 \\text{ Hz}$ |\n\n### 📉 Performance Data (Load vs. Speed)\nThe performance tables for GVFAE are identical in structure and values to the GVHAE model, confirming consistent performance metrics regardless of the "Full belt" feature.\n\n**Performance Table 1 (50 Hz vs 60 Hz):**\n(Values are identical to 4.3.5 GVHAE)\n\n| Length | 50 Hz (Performance) | 60 Hz (Performance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 55 | 36.4 | 67.7 |\n| 75 | 37.6 | 65.1 |\n| 100 | 31.3 | 57.6 |\n| 150 | 27.6 | 60.6 |\n| 180 | 22.6 | 57.7 |\n\n**Performance Table 2 (Load vs. Speed):**\n(Values are identical to 4.3.5 GVHAE)\n\n| Length | 50 Hz (Performance) | 60 Hz (Performance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 55 | 36.4 | 67.7 |\n| 75 | 37.6 | 65.1 |\n| 100 | 31.3 | 57.6 |\n| 150 | 27.6 | 60.6 |\n| 180 | 22.6 | 57.7 |\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data and Differences\n\n**Primary Function:** Both components are specialized conveyor bearing units used in industrial conveying systems.\n\n**Key Shared Technical Specifications:**\n*   **Voltage:** $220 \\text{ V}$\n*   **Frequency:** $50 \\text{ Hz}$ (Standard)\n*   **Power Range:** $6 \\text{ kW} \\sim 25 \\text{ kW}$\n*   **Drive Type:** Head drive\n\n**Key Distinction Between Models:**\n| Model | Primary Feature | Application Focus |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **4.3.5 GVHAE** | Head drive, $\\text{Ø } 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley, Single belt. | Conveyor systems using single belt operation. |\n| **4.3.6 GVFAE** | Head drive, $\\text{Ø } 30 \\text{ mm}$ pulley, **Full belt**. | Conveyor systems requiring full belt support (more robust). |\n\n**Operational Highlight:**\nThe performance tables show that, across all specified lengths, operating the system at **60 Hz** results in significantly higher performance values compared to operating at **50 Hz**, indicating superior operational capacity at the higher frequency.\n\n***\n*Further technical details regarding connection, belt characteristics, and accessories are available via the footer links.*\n\n[Page 19]\nThis document details two specialized models of conveyor systems manufactured by Misumi: **4.3.7 GVHNE** and **4.3.8 GVFNE**. Both models are used for flat belt material transport and share similar technical specifications, with the primary differences lying in their specific operational setup (drive mechanism and belt type).\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ General Overview and Specifications\n\n| Feature | 4.3.7 GVHNE | 4.3.8 GVFNE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Function** | Flat belt conveyor (Single belt) | Flat belt conveyor (Single belt) |\n| **Drive System** | Central drive | Central drive |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$ | $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Key Difference** | Specified as having a Central Drive. | Includes "Full belt" in addition to central drive. |\n\n### 🛠️ Technical Data (Both Models)\n\n| Parameter | Range / Value | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 30–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 300–2000 | mm |\n| **Power (Output)** | 7.8–24 | kW |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Detailed Analysis: 4.3.7 GVHNE (Left Side)\n\nThe 4.3.7 GVHNE is a conveyor system designed for material handling using a single flat belt and central drive mechanism.\n\n### Special Features\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   Pulley $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Central drive.\n\n### Operational Tables & Data\n\n#### 1. Conveyor Capacity\nThis chart relates belt speed (units not specified, but likely RPM or m/s) to conveyor capacity, showing a linear relationship with material loss.\n\n| Belt Speed | 10 | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Capacity (Value)** | 10 | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 |\n\n#### 2. Gearhead Reduction Ratio\nThis table guides the selection of the gearbox ratio based on required belt speed (RPM) and operating frequency (Hz).\n\n| Belt Speed (RPM) | 50 Hz | 60 Hz |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **15** | 5.7 | 41.1 |\n| **20** | 8.5 | 31.4 |\n| **25** | 11.5 | 28.2 |\n| **30** | 15.8 | 18.8 |\n| **35** | 18.7 | 15.8 |\n| **40** | 22.2 | 13.1 |\n| **50** | 31.4 | 9.4 |\n| **60** | 39.7 | 6.5 |\n\n*(Note: The table uses two columns, 50 Hz and 60 Hz, providing reduction ratios for various RPM levels.)*\n\n#### 3. Operating Speed/Ratio Chart (The Operational Grid)\nThis grid maps the relationship between the input speed (RPM) and the achievable gearhead reduction ratio at different frequencies.\n\n| Speed (RPM) | 10 | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ratio (50 Hz)** | 1.5 | 2.6 | 3.1 | 3.8 | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **Ratio (60 Hz)** | 1.0 | 1.8 | 2.4 | 2.8 | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Detailed Analysis: 4.3.8 GVFNE (Right Side)\n\nThe 4.3.8 GVFNE is similar to the 4.3.7 but explicitly includes a "Full belt" and differs slightly in its listed components, suggesting a potentially different operational configuration.\n\n### Special Features\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   Pulley $\\emptyset 30 \\text{ mm}$.\n*   Central drive.\n*   Full belt.\n\n### Operational Tables & Data\n\nThe operational data (Capacity, Reduction Ratio, and the Operational Grid) for the 4.3.8 GVFNE is identical to the 4.3.7 GVHNE model, ensuring consistent performance across the specified operating parameters.\n\n#### 1. Conveyor Capacity\n(Identical structure to 4.3.7 GVHNE)\n\n#### 2. Gearhead Reduction Ratio\n(Identical table structure and values to 4.3.7 GVHNE)\n\n#### 3. Operating Speed/Ratio Chart (The Operational Grid)\n(Identical structure and values to 4.3.7 GVHNE)\n\n| Speed (RPM) | 10 | 20 | 30 | 40 | 50 | 60 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ratio (50 Hz)** | 1.5 | 2.6 | 3.1 | 3.8 | 4.7 | 5.7 |\n| **Ratio (60 Hz)** | 1.0 | 1.8 | 2.4 | 2.8 | 3.1 | 3.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 📘 Summary of Key Differences and Similarities\n\n| Feature | 4.3.7 GVHNE | 4.3.8 GVFNE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Overall Function** | Conveyor System | Conveyor System |\n| **Primary Distinction** | Listed as having a Central Drive. | Listed as having a Central Drive **and** a Full belt. |\n| **Shared Data** | All technical parameters, capacity charts, and operational ratio tables are identical. | |\n\n**In essence, both models are highly standardized conveyor units, with the 4.3.8 GVFNE being the model highlighted to include a "Full belt" configuration.**\n\n[Page 20]\nThis document page is a highly detailed technical specification sheet for two models of Misumi timing belt conveyor systems: **4.3.9 GVTSAE** and **4.3.10 GVTSNE**. The summary below details the structure, features, technical data, and performance curves for both systems.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Overview: Misumi Conveyor Systems\nThe page provides technical and operational data for industrial conveyor systems that utilize timing belts, offering specifications for belt dimensions, motor power, and performance characteristics based on frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n---\n\n## ➡️ Model 1: 4.3.9 GVTSAE\n*(Configuration: Head Drive)*\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram and Special Features\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a motor/gearhead assembly positioned at the head of the conveyor structure.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Timing belt conveyors\n    *   Single belt\n    *   Pulley $\\emptyset 30$ mm\n    *   **Head drive** (Indicates the motor is located at the start/end of the system).\n\n### 📊 Technical Data\nThe technical specifications apply to a range of parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Value Range | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 10 – 150 | mm | |\n| **Length** | 200 – 2000 | mm | |\n| **Width** | 2.2 – 5.2 | mm | |\n| **Power (Input)** | 6 – 25 | kW | |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V | |\n| **Frequency** | 50 / 60 | Hz | |\n\n### 📈 Performance Graphs (Capacity & Reduction Ratio)\n\n**1. Conveyor Capacity (Torque/Power)**\n*   **Curve Shape:** Capacity starts at a low level, rapidly increases, reaches a peak (near the middle of the scale), and then gradually declines.\n*   **Frequency Difference:** The graph shows slightly different capacity curves for 50 Hz and 60 Hz, indicating performance adjustments based on the operational frequency.\n\n**2. Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Speed/Torque)**\n*   **Curve Shape:** The reduction ratio curve is generally parabolic, rising steeply, peaking at a specific operational range, and then falling.\n*   **Frequency Difference:** Similar to capacity, the curve reflects performance differences between 50 Hz and 60 Hz operations.\n\n---\n\n## ⬅️ Model 2: 4.3.10 GVTSNE\n*(Configuration: Central Drive)*\n\n### 🖼️ Diagram and Special Features\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a motor/gearhead assembly, similar in style to Model 1.\n*   **Special Features:**\n    *   Timing belt conveyors\n    *   Single belt\n    *   Pulley $\\emptyset 30$ mm\n    *   **Central drive** (Indicates the motor location is central to the conveyor length, offering different mounting flexibility).\n\n### 📊 Technical Data\nThe technical specifications are very similar to Model 1:\n\n| Parameter | Value Range | Unit | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 10 – 150 | mm | |\n| **Length** | 200 – 2000 | mm | |\n| **Width** | 2.3 – 5.3 | mm | |\n| **Power (Input)** | 6 – 25 | kW | |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V | |\n| **Frequency** | 50 / 60 | Hz | |\n\n### 📈 Performance Graphs (Capacity & Reduction Ratio)\nThe performance characteristics for Model 2 mirror Model 1, displaying the key trade-offs between belt speed/capacity and the reduction ratio at both 50 Hz and 60 Hz.\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Key Data and Differences Summary\n\n| Feature | 4.3.9 GVTSAE | 4.3.10 GVTSNE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Drive Location** | Head drive | Central drive |\n| **Belt Type** | Timing belt conveyors (Single belt) | Timing belt conveyors (Single belt) |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | $\\emptyset 30$ mm | $\\emptyset 30$ mm |\n| **Technical Specs** | Identical range of operational parameters (Width: 10–150 mm, Length: 200–2000 mm, Power: 6–25 kW, etc.) | Identical range of operational parameters |\n| **Application Difference**| Best suited when the motor is positioned at an end-point. | Best suited when the motor is positioned centrally, allowing for better weight distribution or access. |\n\n### 📍 Further Information\nThe page notes that additional detailed information regarding the products, characteristics, and accessories can be found on pages 43, 36, and 63 of the full manual.\n\n[Page 21]\nThis page provides a detailed technical summary for two models of timing belt conveyors: **4.3.11 GTVUAE** and **4.3.12 GTVASE**. Both are automated conveying systems utilizing timing belts, but they feature different configurations (double track vs. single belt).\n\nBelow is a detailed, section-by-section summary, including key data and replicated tables.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ General Overview and Function\n\nBoth products are timing belt conveyors designed for automated material handling. They utilize a robust motor and gearhead system to ensure controlled belt movement and transport.\n\n### 📋 Technical Specifications (Common to both models)\n\n| Parameter | Specification |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 230 |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 |\n| **Belt Width (mm)** | 50 – 300 (GTVUAE) / 50 – 2300 (GTVASE) |\n| **Length (mm)** | 250 – 2000 |\n| **Weight (kg)** | 2 – 10 |\n| **Power Output (W)** | 6 – 25 |\n\n---\n\n## 🔷 Product 1: 4.3.11 GTVUAE (Double Track)\n\nThis model is designed for transporting items on two parallel tracks, offering higher capacity for spread materials or multiple items.\n\n### ✨ Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors, **double track**.\n*   Pulley Diameter ($\\text{\\O}$): 30 mm\n*   Head drive: **INSIDE** the motor\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity (Tension Curve)\nThe chart illustrates the conveyor's capability regarding belt tension versus other operational factors (though the axes are unlabeled in the provided snippet, the curve itself is a key data point showing the system's operational limits).\n\n### 🔩 Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Speed Data)\nThis data specifies the relationship between the reduction ratio and the resulting belt speed, depending on the operating frequency (50 Hz or 60 Hz).\n\n**A. 50 Hz Data (GTVUAE)**\n| Reduction Ratio | Belt Speed (m/min) |\n| :---: | :---: |\n| 5 | 30.0 |\n| 7.5 | 20.0 |\n| 10 | 15.0 |\n| 12.5 | 14.4 |\n| 15 | 12.0 |\n| 18 | 10.0 |\n| 20 | 8.0 |\n| 25 | 6.0 |\n| 30 | 5.0 |\n| 36 | 4.2 |\n| 50 | 3.0 |\n\n**B. 60 Hz Data (GTVUAE)**\n| Reduction Ratio | Belt Speed (m/min) |\n| :---: | :---: |\n| 5 | 30.0 |\n| 7.5 | 20.0 |\n| 10 | 15.0 |\n| 12.5 | 14.4 |\n| 15 | 12.0 |\n| 18 | 10.0 |\n| 20 | 8.0 |\n| 25 | 6.0 |\n| 30 | 5.0 |\n| 36 | 4.2 |\n| 50 | 3.0 |\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Product 2: 4.3.12 GTVASE (Single Belt)\n\nThis model is configured with a single belt and is best suited for general single-stream material transport.\n\n### ✨ Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors, **single belt**.\n*   Pulley Diameter ($\\text{\\O}$): 30 mm\n*   Head drive: **OUTSIDE** the motor\n\n### 📈 Conveyor Capacity (Tension Curve)\n(The curve chart is identical to the GTVUAE, indicating a similar mechanical capacity profile.)\n\n### 🔩 Gearhead Reduction Ratio (Speed Data)\nThe speed data for the GTVASE model is identical to the GTVUAE model, suggesting consistent performance characteristics despite the difference in belt configuration.\n\n**A. 50 Hz Data (GTVASE)**\n| Reduction Ratio | Belt Speed (m/min) |\n| :---: | :---: |\n| 5 | 30.0 |\n| 7.5 | 20.0 |\n| 10 | 15.0 |\n| 12.5 | 14.4 |\n| 15 | 12.0 |\n| 18 | 10.0 |\n| 20 | 8.0 |\n| 25 | 6.0 |\n| 30 | 5.0 |\n| 36 | 4.2 |\n| 50 | 3.0 |\n\n**B. 60 Hz Data (GTVASE)**\n| Reduction Ratio | Belt Speed (m/min) |\n| :---: | :---: |\n| 5 | 30.0 |\n| 7.5 | 20.0 |\n| 10 | 15.0 |\n| 12.5 | 14.4 |\n| 15 | 12.0 |\n| 18 | 10.0 |\n| 20 | 8.0 |\n| 25 | 6.0 |\n| 30 | 5.0 |\n| 36 | 4.2 |\n| 50 | 3.0 |\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Footer Information (Navigation)\n\nThe bottom section directs users to further technical information regarding the product line:\n\n*   **Connection:** $\\rightarrow$ from page 43\n*   **Belt characteristics:** $\\rightarrow$ from page 36\n*   **Accessories:** $\\rightarrow$ from page 63\n\n[Page 22]\nThis page provides detailed technical specifications, installation guidance, and operational data for two related Misumi electric conveyor systems: the **4.3.13 GVTNUE** and the **4.3.14 GVTWNSE**. Both models are timing belt conveyors designed for industrial applications.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ Product Overview and Specifications\n\n**General Function:** Both units are specialized timing belt conveyors featuring a double track system and a pulley diameter of $30 \\text{ mm}$. They are designed for reliable material handling in industrial settings.\n\n### 🔗 Key Difference Between Models\n\nWhile the technical specifications are highly similar, the primary physical difference is the location of the central drive:\n\n*   **4.3.13 GVTNUE:** Central drive is located **INSIDE** the motor housing.\n*   **4.3.14 GVTWNSE:** Central drive is located **OUTSIDE** the motor housing.\n\n### 📈 Shared Technical Data\n\n| Parameter | Specification |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | $50–300 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Length** | $280–2000 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $3.5–8.5 \\text{ kg}$ |\n| **Power Input (W)** | 220 (W) |\n| **Voltage (V)** | 230 (V) |\n| **Frequency (Hz)** | 50 (Hz) |\n\n***\n\n## 🔍 Model 4.3.13 GVTNUE (Central Drive Inside)\n\n### Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors, double track\n*   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$\n*   Central drive (INSIDE the motor)\n\n### Diagrammatic Representation (Load Performance)\nThe page includes a diagram illustrating the operational performance (Speed vs. Power/Torque). The curve represents the relationship between the required operating speed (x-axis) and the maximum available power or torque (y-axis) for the unit under varying load conditions (indicated by different colored lines).\n\n### ⚙️ Performance Tables (Speed vs. Torque/Ratio)\n\nThe following tables detail the specific load performance of the conveyor in terms of belt speed and required gearhead reduction ratio at various operating conditions.\n\n#### A. Belt Speed (Belt speed / $\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$)\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$) | $\\text{50 Hz}$ | $\\text{60 Hz}$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $5$ | $30$ | $30$ |\n| $7.5$ | $20.0$ | $24.0$ |\n| $10$ | $16.7$ | $20.0$ |\n| $12.5$ | $14.4$ | $18.0$ |\n| $15$ | $10.0$ | $12.0$ |\n| $18$ | $8.3$ | $10.0$ |\n| $25$ | $6.0$ | $8.0$ |\n| $30$ | $5.0$ | $6.0$ |\n| $36$ | $4.2$ | $5.0$ |\n| $50$ | $3.0$ | $3.6$ |\n| $60$ | $2.5$ | $3.0$ |\n| $75$ | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| $90$ | $1.7$ | $2.0$ |\n| $100$ | $1.5$ | $1.8$ |\n| $120$ | $1.2$ | $1.5$ |\n| $150$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ |\n| $180$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |\n\n#### B. Gearhead Reduction Ratio\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$) | $\\text{50 Hz}$ | $\\text{60 Hz}$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $5$ | $30$ | $30$ |\n| $7.5$ | $20.0$ | $24.0$ |\n| $10$ | $16.7$ | $20.0$ |\n| $12.5$ | $14.4$ | $18.0$ |\n| $15$ | $10.0$ | $12.0$ |\n| $18$ | $8.3$ | $10.0$ |\n| $25$ | $6.0$ | $8.0$ |\n| $30$ | $5.0$ | $6.0$ |\n| $36$ | $4.2$ | $5.0$ |\n| $50$ | $3.0$ | $3.6$ |\n| $60$ | $2.5$ | $3.0$ |\n| $75$ | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| $90$ | $1.7$ | $2.0$ |\n| $100$ | $1.5$ | $1.8$ |\n| $120$ | $1.2$ | $1.5$ |\n| $150$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ |\n| $180$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |\n\n***\n\n## 🚀 Model 4.3.14 GVTWNSE (Central Drive Outside)\n\n### Special Features\n*   Timing belt conveyors, double track\n*   Pulley $\\text{\\O} 30 \\text{ mm}$\n*   Central drive (OUTSIDE the motor)\n\n### Diagrammatic Representation (Load Performance)\nThe diagram mirrors the performance curve shown for the 4.3.13 model, illustrating the relationship between required speed and available power/torque across varying load conditions.\n\n### ⚙️ Performance Tables (Speed vs. Torque/Ratio)\n\nThe performance tables for the 4.3.14 model are identical to those of the 4.3.13 model, detailing the belt speed and gearhead reduction ratio capabilities.\n\n#### A. Belt Speed (Belt speed / $\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$)\n*(Values are identical to 4.3.13 GVTNUE)*\n| Belt Speed ($\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$) | $\\text{50 Hz}$ | $\\text{60 Hz}$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $5$ | $30$ | $30$ |\n| $7.5$ | $20.0$ | $24.0$ |\n| $\\do