tion between the two models is the pulley size, which impacts the dimensions, weight, and the final reduction ratios:\n\n| Feature | 4.3.15 CVGAE | 4.3.16 CVGCE |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | $\\text{\\O}$ 30 mm | $\\text{\\O}$ 50 mm |\n| **Minimum Length** | 190 mm | 240 mm |\n| **Weight Range** | 4.2 – 17.4 kg | 6 – 25.7 kg |\n| **Reduction Ratio Impact** | Higher/Faster speeds relative to $\\text{\\O}$ 30 mm. | Lower/Slower speeds relative to $\\text{\\O}$ 50 mm. |\n\n## ℹ️ Further Information (Navigation Links)\n\nThe bottom of the page provides links to supplementary technical documentation:\n\n*   **Connection:** (View details on page 43)\n*   **Belt characteristics:** (View details on page 36)\n*   **Accessories:** (View details on page 63)\n\n[Page 24]\nThis page provides a detailed technical comparison and performance data for two models of conveyor systems: **4.3.17 CVGNE** and **4.3.18 CVGRE**.\n\nThe summary below is organized by model and functional area, ensuring all tables, diagrams, and key data points are included.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Product Overview\n\nThe page showcases two models of belts/conveyors manufactured by Misumi. Both systems are designed for belt conveyance applications and share similar technical specifications, while the model numbers distinguish them (4.3.17 vs. 4.3.18).\n\n### 🟢 4.3.17 CVGNE (Left Side)\n*   **Installation and Operation:** Dedicated technical section for this model.\n\n### 🔵 4.3.18 CVGRE (Right Side)\n*   **Installation and Operation:** Dedicated technical section for this model.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Technical Specifications (Common to Both Models)\n\nThe following data points represent the physical and structural features of both conveyor models:\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Flatt belt conveyors, single belt\n*   Pulley $\\text{\\O } 30 \\text{ mm}$\n*   Central drive\n*   Adjustable belt tension\n\n**Technical Data:**\n*   **Belt Width (mm):** $300$\n*   **Length (mm):** $355-2000$\n*   **Weight ($\\text{kg}$):** $5.8-23.9$\n*   **Power output ($\\text{W}$):** $6 - 25$\n*   **Voltage ($\\text{V}$):** $220$\n*   **Frequency ($\\text{Hz}$):** $50$\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Performance Data and Diagrams\n\nBoth models include two performance diagrams:\n1.  **Conveyor Capacity Diagram:** Shows "Conveyor Capacity" (Torque) vs. "Belt speed" (or frequency).\n2.  **Gearhead Reduction Ratio Diagram:** Shows "Gearhead reduction ratio" (Torque) vs. "Belt speed" (or frequency).\n\nThe performance characteristics are presented in detailed tables for both $50 \\text{ Hz}$ and $60 \\text{ Hz}$ operating conditions.\n\n### 1. ➡️ 4.3.17 CVGNE Performance Tables\n\nThis table details the maximum achievable torque (Conveyor Capacity) and the corresponding gearhead reduction ratio at various load levels (represented by weights in pounds).\n\n#### **A. Conveyor Capacity (Torque)**\n\n| Weight (lbs) | 50 Hz (Nm) | 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :----------- | :---------- | :---------- |\n| 5 | $47.1$ | $56.5$ |\n| 7.5 | $31.4$ | $37.7$ |\n| 9 | $26.2$ | $31.4$ |\n| 12.5 | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| 15 | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| 18 | $13.1$ | $15.7$ |\n| 25 | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| 30 | $7.9$ | $9.4$ |\n| 35 | $6.5$ | $7.9$ |\n| 50 | $4.7$ | $5.7$ |\n| 60 | $3.9$ | $4.7$ |\n| 75 | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| 90 | $2.6$ | $3.1$ |\n| 100 | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| 120 | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| 150 | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n| 180 | $1.3$ | $1.6$ |\n\n#### **B. Gearhead Reduction Ratio**\n\n| Weight (lbs) | 50 Hz (Nm) | 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :----------- | :---------- | :---------- |\n| 5 | $47.1$ | $56.5$ |\n| 7.5 | $31.4$ | $37.7$ |\n| 9 | $26.2$ | $31.4$ |\n| 12.5 | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| 15 | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| 18 | $13.1$ | $15.7$ |\n| 25 | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| 30 | $7.9$ | $9.4$ |\n| 35 | $6.5$ | $7.9$ |\n| 50 | $4.7$ | $5.7$ |\n| 60 | $3.9$ | $4.7$ |\n| 75 | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| 90 | $2.6$ | $3.1$ |\n| 100 | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| 120 | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| 150 | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n| 180 | $1.3$ | $1.6$ |\n\n---\n\n### 2. ➡️ 4.3.18 CVGRE Performance Tables\n\nThis table provides the performance data for the 4.3.18 CVGRE model, mirroring the structure of the CVGNE model.\n\n#### **A. Conveyor Capacity (Torque)**\n\n| Weight (lbs) | 50 Hz (Nm) | 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :----------- | :---------- | :---------- |\n| 5 | $47.1$ | $56.5$ |\n| 7.5 | $31.4$ | $37.7$ |\n| 9 | $26.2$ | $31.4$ |\n| 12.5 | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| 15 | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| 18 | $13.1$ | $15.7$ |\n| 25 | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| 30 | $7.9$ | $9.4$ |\n| 35 | $6.5$ | $7.9$ |\n| 50 | $4.7$ | $5.7$ |\n| 60 | $3.9$ | $4.7$ |\n| 75 | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| 90 | $2.6$ | $3.1$ |\n| 100 | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| 120 | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| 150 | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n| 180 | $1.3$ | $1.6$ |\n\n#### **B. Gearhead Reduction Ratio**\n\n| Weight (lbs) | 50 Hz (Nm) | 60 Hz (Nm) |\n| :----------- | :---------- | :---------- |\n| 5 | $47.1$ | $56.5$ |\n| 7.5 | $31.4$ | $37.7$ |\n| 9 | $26.2$ | $31.4$ |\n| 12.5 | $18.8$ | $22.6$ |\n| 15 | $15.7$ | $18.8$ |\n| 18 | $13.1$ | $15.7$ |\n| 25 | $9.4$ | $11.3$ |\n| 30 | $7.9$ | $9.4$ |\n| 35 | $6.5$ | $7.9$ |\n| 50 | $4.7$ | $5.7$ |\n| 60 | $3.9$ | $4.7$ |\n| 75 | $3.1$ | $3.8$ |\n| 90 | $2.6$ | $3.1$ |\n| 100 | $2.4$ | $2.8$ |\n| 120 | $2.0$ | $2.4$ |\n| 150 | $1.6$ | $1.9$ |\n| 180 | $1.3$ | $1.6$ |\n\n---\n\n## 🏷️ Key Takeaways\n\n1.  **Model Consistency:** The performance data for 4.3.17 CVGNE and 4.3.18 CVGRE are virtually identical in this presentation, suggesting they operate under the same mechanical principles and capacity limits.\n2.  **Operating Range:** Both systems are suitable for belt conveyors with adjustable belt tension and pulleys of $30 \\text{ mm}$.\n3.  **Power Output:** They are designed to handle a wide range of power outputs (from $6 \\text{ W}$ to $25 \\text{ W}$) across a highly flexible length range ($355 \\text{ mm}$ to $2000 \\text{ mm}$).\n4.  **Frequency Dependence:** The conveyor capacity and reduction ratio are consistently higher when operating at $60 \\text{ Hz}$ compared to $50 \\text{ Hz}$ across all weight loads.\n\n[Page 25]\nThis page is a detailed technical catalog sheet from MISUMI, focused on **Conveyor Systems** and specific models of belt and drive assemblies. It provides comprehensive specifications, operating conditions, and performance data for two similar, yet distinct, models (CVGBE and CVGDE).\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including key data, diagrams, and tables.\n\n---\n\n## 📘 Overview: Conveyor Systems - Operating Instructions\n\nThe page presents two motor-driven conveyor belt assemblies, both designed for single, flat belts, utilizing a head drive mechanism. The primary difference between the two models lies in the size of the pulley and associated physical dimensions.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Model 1: 4.3.19 CVGBE\n\n### 🔍 Special Features and Diagram\n*   **System:** Conveyor belt.\n*   **Belt Type:** Flat belt, single belt.\n*   **Pulley Size:** $\\emptyset 30$ mm.\n*   **Drive:** Head drive.\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a roller and belt mechanism with the head drive attached.\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (CVGBE)\n| Specification | Value / Range | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 190–2000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 4.2–17.4 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 6–25 | kW |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### ⚡ Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio\nThe chart data provides the relationship between the required belt speed and the motor speed based on the operating frequency (50 Hz and 60 Hz).\n\n#### **Table Summary (Example Data Points):**\n*   **If the required Belt Speed is 30 mm/s (50 Hz):** The corresponding motor speed (rpm) is listed (e.g., 52.4 rpm at 50 Hz).\n*   **Graph:** Illustrates that the reduction ratio required (Y-axis) changes depending on the desired belt speed (X-axis).\n\n---\n\n## 🔩 Model 2: 4.3.20 CVGDE\n\n### 🔍 Special Features and Diagram\n*   **System:** Conveyor belt.\n*   **Belt Type:** Flat belt, single belt.\n*   **Pulley Size:** $\\emptyset 50$ mm.\n*   **Drive:** Head drive.\n*   **Diagram:** Shows a roller and belt mechanism with the head drive attached (similar design to CVGBE).\n\n### 📊 Technical Data (CVGDE)\n| Specification | Value / Range | Unit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Belt Width** | 50–300 | mm |\n| **Length** | 240–2000 | mm |\n| **Weight** | 6–25.7 | kg |\n| **Power Output** | 6–25 | kW |\n| **Voltage** | 230 | V |\n| **Frequency** | 50 | Hz |\n\n### ⚡ Performance Data: Gearhead Reduction Ratio\nThis section mirrors the CVGBE data, providing operational curves and tables for belt speed vs. motor speed under 50 Hz and 60 Hz operation.\n\n#### **Table Summary (Example Data Points):**\n*   **If the required Belt Speed is 30 mm/s (50 Hz):** The corresponding motor speed (rpm) is listed (e.g., 52.4 rpm at 50 Hz).\n*   **Graph:** Shows the relationship between the reduction ratio and motor speed, indicating operational limits and optimal performance zones.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Summary of Diagrams and Visuals\n\n1.  **Comparison of Components:** Both models utilize a standard single belt, flat belt design mounted on rollers and driven by a centralized **Head Drive**.\n2.  **Reduction Ratio Graph:** The graph in both sections visually confirms that the **Gearhead Reduction Ratio** is a function of both the desired **Belt Speed (mm/s)** and the operational **Motor Speed (rpm)**.\n3.  **Operational Ranges:** The graphs define the acceptable operating range for the system, connecting the required output speed to the input motor parameters.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Data Synthesis and Comparison\n\n| Feature | 4.3.19 CVGBE | 4.3.20 CVGDE | Comparison Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pulley Diameter** | $\\emptyset 30$ mm | $\\emptyset 50$ mm | **Major difference.** CVGDE has a significantly larger pulley. |\n| **Typical Length (Min)**| 190 mm | 240 mm | CVGDE generally starts with a longer minimum length. |\n| **Weight Range (Max)**| 17.4 kg | 25.7 kg | CVGDE has a higher maximum weight specification. |\n| **Operating Voltage** | 230 V | 230 V | Identical. |\n| **Operation** | Head Drive | Head Drive | Identical. |\n| **Belt Type** | Flat, Single | Flat, Single | Identical. |\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Further Information (Links)\n\nThe page provides links for more detailed technical documentation regarding the equipment:\n*   Connection\n*   Belt characteristics\n*   Accessories\n\n[Page 26]\nThis page appears to be a detailed technical datasheet from MISUMI, focusing on two models of belt-driven conveyor systems: the **4.3.21 CVGPE** and the **4.3.22 CVGWE**.\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, including descriptions of diagrams, tables, and key technical data.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Overview of Technical Documentation\n\nThe page provides specifications, operational ranges, and performance curves for two conveyor models designed for belt movement (likely indexing or continuous conveying).\n\n### ⚙️ Product 1: 4.3.21 CVGPE (Conveyor Model)\n\n**Diagram:** A detailed diagram illustrates the conveyor structure, showing the belt path and general dimensions.\n\n**Special Features:**\n*   Flat belt conveyors, single belt.\n*   Pulley Ø 30 mm.\n*   Central drive.\n*   Belt with meandering prevention crosspiece.\n\n**Technical Data (Dimensions and Performance):**\n*   **Belt Width (mm):** 50–300\n*   **Length (mm):** 300–2000\n*   **Weight (kg):** 5.8–23.9\n*   **Power Output (W):** 6–25\n*   **Voltage (V):** 220\n*   **Frequency (Hz):** 50\n\n**Performance Chart (Meander/Load):**\nA graph titled "Conveyor Capacity" is provided, showing the performance metrics related to belt tension and speed.\n\n*   **X-axis:** Belt speed (mm/s)\n*   **Y-axis:** Belting/Tension/Load (kPa)\n*   **Graph Data Points (Example Readings):** The chart plots the relationship between belt tension and speed for various load/capacity levels (indicated by different colored lines).\n\n**Operating/Load Table (Conversion Capacity):**\nA detailed table provides specific capacity metrics, likely relating to the motor/drive system's ability to handle different load