ifferent belt types designed for varied industrial applications.\n\n| Belt Type | Application | Illustration / Function |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Flat belts with guide** | General transport, material handling. | Designed with different belts depending on the configuration, operating conditions, etc. |\n| **Timing belts** | *Sliding* / Transport of work-pieces. | Provide synchronized motion and grip. |\n| **Stainless steel belts** | Transport of work-pieces. | Durable for environments requiring resistance to rust/corrosion. |\n| **Plastic chains** | Transport of work-pieces / Transport of foodstuffs. | Used for reliable, non-abrasive material movement. |\n\n***\n\n## ⚠️ Installation and Operation Warnings (Safety)**\n\nThe document includes critical safety warnings regarding belt handling and installation:\n\n*   **Caution:** Exercise caution when adjusting or handling the belt.\n*   **Damage Warning:** Do not attempt to adjust the conveyor belt unless qualified personnel are available. Improper adjustment can cause damage to the belt or system.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Detailed Technical Data Tables\n\nThe page provides specific performance data for two major belt types: Timing Belts and Plastic Chains.\n\n### 1. Timing Belts - Key Data\n\nThis table outlines the specifications for MISUMI Polyurethane Timing Belts, showing multiple grade options.\n\n| Product | Material | Allowable Tension (N) | Continuous Temperature (°C) | Weight (g/m) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **MISUMI** (Grade 1) | Polyurethane | 120 | -20 to 70 | 32.5 |\n| **LTBRA** (Grade 2) | Polyurethane | 150 | -20 to 80 | 20.8 |\n| **LTBJA** (Grade 3) | Polyurethane | 150 | -20 to 80 | 20.8 |\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n*   **Material:** Polyurethane\n*   **Tension Range:** From 120 N to 150 N.\n*   **Temperature Range:** Effective up to 70°C or 80°C depending on the grade.\n\n### 2. Plastic Chain - Key Data\n\nThis table outlines the specifications for MISUMI Polyacetal Plastic Chains.\n\n| Product | Material | Allowable Tension (N) | Continuous Temperature (°C) | Sliding Friction Coefficient ($\\mu$) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **MISUMI** | Polyacetal | 45 | -5 to 85 | 0.32 |\n\n**Key Data Points:**\n*   **Material:** Polyacetal\n*   **Tension:** 45 N\n*   **Temperature Range:** Robust operation from -5°C to 85°C.\n*   **Coefficient ($\\mu$):** 0.32 (This value is critical for friction-related calculations).\n\n***\n\n## 📝 Ordering and Procurement Information\n\n### Belt Ordering - Additional Information\n\n*   **Specifications:** For detailed technical specifications, belts are available in various configurations.\n*   **Action Required:** Customers are advised to consult the full catalogue or the MISUMI website for comprehensive information and ordering details.\n\n[Page 40]\nThis page is a highly detailed technical catalog sheet from **MİSUMİ**, titled "Belt characteristics - flat belts." It serves as a reference guide providing extensive technical specifications, materials, and application guidelines for various models of flat conveyor belts.\n\nSince the content is entirely tabular data, the summary focuses on structuring the data, identifying the key variables, and outlining the purpose of each specification column.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of Belt Specifications\n\n### I. Overview and Purpose\nThe document provides a comprehensive listing of belt models (identified by MSUMI part numbers) tailored for specific industrial applications. The key focus is on relating the belt's material composition (e.g., Polyurethane, PVC) and physical properties (e.g., thickness, tensile strength) to optimal performance in different operational environments.\n\n### II. Structure of the Data Table\nThe core of the page is a large table containing 14 columns of highly technical data. These columns define the belt's specifications:\n\n#### A. Identification and Usage\n*   **MSUMI part numbers:** Unique identifier for the specific belt model.\n*   **Application:** Defines the intended operational use (e.g., Pulp impregnation, Polyurethane, Flexible transport, Oil-Resistant).\n*   **Material:** The core composite material (e.g., Polyurethane, flexible, PVC).\n*   **Material front side:** Indicates the visible finish or color of the front surface.\n\n#### B. Physical and Chemical Specifications (The Measurements)\n*   **Thick-ness:** The physical thickness of the belt (measured numerically, likely in mm).\n*   **Colour:** The overall belt color (e.g., white, green, black).\n*   **Permissi-ble extension:** Specifies the operational stretch allowance.\n*   **Min. tensile strength / Tensile strength:** Critical engineering metrics detailing the belt's minimum and maximum load-bearing capacity (often noted with units and specific notes like $R3$ or $R5$).\n\n#### C. Performance Metrics\n*   **Friction coefficient (Front side / Back side):** Specifies the belt's coefficient of friction for both the front and back running surfaces (measured numerically, typically a decimal value between 0 and 1).\n\n***\n\n### III. Key Data Groupings and Belt Applications\n\nThe belts are grouped by their function, allowing users to select a belt optimized for a specific industrial process:\n\n| Application Category | Example Part Numbers | Key Material & Color | Core Specification / Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Polyurethane Belt** | HBLT/W, HBLT/G | Polyurethane / White, Green | Used for general, durable conveyance applications (e.g., HBLT/W: White, $1.5 \\text{ mm}$ thickness). |\n| **Polyurethane (Oil/Chemical)** | HBLT/POLT, HBLT/POLG | Polyurethane / White, Green | Specifically designed for environments requiring resistance to oils or chemicals (e.g., Oil-Resistant). |\n| **Pulp Impregnation** | HBLT/PI | Polyurethane / White | Optimized for processing materials that require impregnation processes. |\n| **Flexible Transport** | HBLT/F | Flexible / White | Designed for movement in contexts requiring high flexibility and resilience (e.g., conveyor belts). |\n| **Specialized/Unknown** | HBLT/G, HBLT/GBLG | Polyurethane / Various | Covers general-purpose polyurethane belts with varying thickness and tensile strength requirements. |\n\n### IV. Summary of Technical Ranges\n\nThe catalog demonstrates a wide range of operational parameters:\n\n| Specification | Typical Range/Observation | Implications |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Thickness** | Ranges from $0.7 \\text{ mm}$ to $1.5 \\text{ mm}$. | Allows customization for various load levels and conveyor widths. |\n| **Color** | Includes White, Green, and Black. | Used for material identification and aesthetic fit within the conveyor system. |\n| **Tensile Strength** | Varies greatly, with notes like $R3$ (3mm) or $R5$ (5mm) frequently used. | This is the critical load-bearing metric, dictating the belt’s maximum operational load. |\n| **Friction Coefficient** | Ranges from $\\approx 0.7$ to $\\approx 1.0$. | Indicates the grip and traction provided by the belt surface. Low values suggest low grip (e.g., moving painted surfaces), while high values indicate strong grip. |\n\n***\n\n**Conclusion:** This page is an indispensable technical datasheet for engineers and maintenance personnel, enabling the selection of the precise belt model that meets the unique demands of material handling, chemical resistance, and load capacity within an industrial conveyor system.\n\n[Page 41]\nThis document is a highly technical product catalog page from MISUMI, detailing the specifications and usage characteristics of various belts, specifically those designed for conveyor systems and handling electronic components.\n\nSince the provided source material is entirely tabular data, the summary focuses on structuring this raw data into detailed, categorized tables and providing a functional overview of the product lines.\n\n***\n\n## 🏭 Comprehensive Summary: MISUMI Belt Systems\n\n### I. Overview and Purpose\n\nThis page serves as an "Installation and Operation" guide, providing detailed engineering specifications for belts used in automated transport and handling equipment (conveyor systems). The product lines are diverse, catering to different applications, materials (e.g., polyurethanes, natural rubber), and operating environments.\n\n### II. Key Product Categories and Applications\n\nThe belts can be generally grouped by their intended use and construction:\n\n1.  **Conveying/Transport Belts:** (e.g., KBLWT, FHBLT) Used for general material movement.\n2.  **Electronic Component Belts:** (e.g., DHBLTS, DHBLTDS) Designed for handling sensitive or delicate items, often requiring specific anti-static or non-abrasive materials.\n3.  **General Purpose Belts:** (e.g., KBLWT, SHBLTDS) Versatile belts suitable for general mechanical applications.\n\n### III. Key Data Summary Tables\n\nThe original data is presented in two main sections: **General Belt Specifications** and **Belt Characteristics (with Guides)**.\n\n#### 📊 Table 1: General Belt Specifications and Performance\n\nThis table summarizes the critical mechanical and material properties for key conveyor belts.\n\n| MISUMI Code | Application | Front Material | Reverse Material | Key Feature / Purpose | Thickness (mm) | Weight (kg/m) | Permissible Pulling Force (N) | Friction Coeff. (Dry) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **FHBLT** | Conveyor | White | N/A | General Conveying | 0.8 | 0.9 | 3.5 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWT** | Conveyor | White | N/A | General Conveying | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWTN** | Transport | Polyurethane | Polyurethane | Transport/Handling | 1.4 | 1.5 | 8 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLTDS** | Electronics | Black | Electrically conductive polyurethane | Handling components | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLTS** | Electronics | Black | N/A | Handling components | 1.7 | 1.6 | 3 | 0.2 |\n| **SHBLTDS** | Electronics | Black | Electrically conductive polyurethane | Handling components | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **FHBLTDS** | Electronics | Black | Electrically conductive polyurethane | Handling components | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n\n*Note: The 'Friction Coefficient' values are consistently low, indicating smooth, efficient movement across various conditions.*\n\n#### 📊 Table 2: Belt Characteristics (Belts with Guides)\n\nThis table focuses on belts optimized for guiding and general applications, showing consistent material usage and stable performance parameters.\n\n| MISUMI Code | Application | Front Material | Reverse Material | Thickness (mm) | Weight (kg/m) | Permissible Pulling Force (N) | Friction Coeff. (Dry) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **FHBLTDSG** | General/Guiding | Green | N/A | 0.8 | 0.9 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **SHBLTDSG** | General/Guiding | Green | N/A | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWTDSG** | General/Guiding | Green | N/A | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **KBLWTDS** | General/Guiding | N/A | N/A | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLTDSG** | General/Guiding | Green | N/A | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n| **DHBLTDS** | General/Guiding | Black | N/A | 0.8 | 0.7 | 4 | 0.2 |\n\n### IV. Comparative Analysis and Key Data Points\n\n**1. Material Specialization:**\n*   **Polyurethane:** Used extensively for handling and transport (e.g., KBLWTN), offering durable, flexible properties.\n*   **Electrically Conductive Polyurethane:** Designated for handling sensitive electronic components (e.g., DHBLTDS, FHBLTDS), suggesting an anti-static or specific electrical requirement for the workflow.\n*   **General/Guide Use:** Often utilizes a Green material coloration (e.g., FHBLTDSG), likely indicating a distinct construction or standard guide system.\n\n**2. Performance Consistency (Key Data):**\n*   **Permissible Pulling Force:** For belts operating under standard conveyor conditions (0.8mm thickness), the maximum specified pulling force is consistently **4 Newtons (N)**.\n*   **Friction Coefficient:** The belts maintain a highly stable and low coefficient of friction in both dry and wet conditions, listed as **0.2** across nearly all models.\n\n**3. Dimensional Specifications:**\n*   **Typical Thickness:** The most common thickness for belts is **0.8 mm**.\n*   **Minimum Pulley Diameter:** The minimum recommended pulley diameter for the standard belts is **15 mm**.\n*   **Cutting Edge Diameter:** The specified cutting edge diameter is **30 mm** (for most models).\n\n### V. Summary Table of Critical Specifications\n\n| Specification Type | Standard Range / Value | Unit | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Optimal Thickness** | 0.8 mm | mm | Standard size for most models. |\n| **Standard Pulling Force** | 4 | N | General operational limit for recommended load. |\n| **Standard Friction Coeff.** | 0.2 | Unitless | Indicates minimal friction and efficient power transfer. |\n| **Min. Pulley Diameter** | 15 | mm | Minimum dimension required for mounting. |\n| **Special Material** | Electrically Conductive Polyurethane | N/A | Used exclusively for electronics handling. |\n\n[Page 42]\nThis document provides detailed technical specifications and operational guidelines for selecting and installing various conveyor belts and chains, focusing specifically on belts with studs and stainless steel options.\n\n---\n\n### 📜 Summary Overview\n\nThe document is structured into three main sections:\n1. **Flat Belts with Studs (Polyurethane):** Specifications for general use studs belts.\n2. **Stainless Steel Belts:** Specifications for corrosion-resistant belts.\n3. **Installation Guidelines:** Critical notes on pulley sizing, length calculation, and conveying direction.\n\n***\n\n### 🚧 1. Characteristics: Flat Belts with Studs\n\nThis section details standard polyurethane belts designed for flat conveyor applications.\n\n#### A. Belt Data (Polyurethane Stud Belts)\n\n| MISUMI Part Numbers | Application | Material | Colour | Thick (mm) | Permissible width (mm) | Min. Pulley diameter | Tensile strength (MPa) | Belt/Stud type |\n| :--- | :--- | :---