he user for failure by covering **Abnormal Processing Concerns** (p. 27) and providing a dedicated, structured **Troubleshooting Process** (p. 32). This phase also integrates proactive care through **Maintenance and Inspection** (p. 30).\n\n#### ⚡ Phase 3: Technical Compliance & Support (Sections 10–12)\n*   **Goal:** To provide the necessary technical, legal, and logistical resources for long-term ownership.\n*   **Content Covered:** This is the most technical phase, featuring the **Electrical Wiring Diagram** (p. 36), which is crucial for specialized technical personnel. It includes the **Certificate of Compliance** (p. 38), which is essential for legal and safety sign-off. Finally, the manual concludes with the **Spare Parts Packing List** (p. 39), ensuring the user knows exactly what parts are available for replacement.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Conclusion\n\nThis manual is not a simple user guide; it is a **comprehensive technical package** designed to support the entire lifecycle of the equipment, from initial unboxing and installation through daily operation, failure analysis, scheduled maintenance, and final regulatory compliance. Its sequential structure ensures that the user is equipped with everything needed, whether they are a machine operator, a technician, or a quality assurance inspector.\n\n[Page 4]\nThis page provides a comprehensive summary and introduction to the operation and principles of the **DK-908 type EDM Hole Drilling Machine**.\n\nSince the input is a single page containing descriptive text and a technical drawing, the summary is organized into an overview, a technical data table, a description of the working principle, and a detailed analysis of the provided diagram.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of the DK-908 EDM Machine\n\n### 📝 1. Overview and Purpose\n\nThe document confirms that the manual covers the **entire process and maintenance** of the DK-908 EDM setup, from initial preparation to full operation.\n\n*   **Machine Type:** EDM (Electric Discharge Machining) Hole Drilling Machine.\n*   **Model:** DK-908.\n*   **Primary Function:** To drill holes in metal by using the highly controlled erosion effect created by electrical sparks (EDM).\n\n### ⚡ 2. Key Technical Data (Table Format)\n\n| Parameter | Detail | Unit/Range |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Model** | DK-908 | N/A |\n| **Electrode Material** | Copper Tube (Copper Wire) | N/A |\n| **Electrode Size Range** | $\\Phi 0.3 \\sim 3.0$ | millimeters (mm) |\n| **Operating Principle** | Electric Discharge Machining (EDM) | Spark Discharge |\n| **Movement** | Vertical (Up and Down) | Directional |\n\n### ⚙️ 3. Working Principle (How it Works)\n\nThe DK-908 machine utilizes the principle of **Electro-Discharge Erosion (EDM)**.\n\n1.  **Electrode Use:** The core tool is a thin copper metal tube, which serves as the electrode wire.\n2.  **Operation:** This copper electrode is moved **vertically** (up and down) into the metal workpiece.\n3.  **Machining Action:** The process works by forcing a controlled electrical current between the electrode and the workpiece. This highly concentrated electrical current creates intense heat, causing repeated, controlled sparks (spark discharge).\n4.  **Result:** The heat and plasma generated by these sparks gradually erode (drill away) the material, creating a precise hole in the metal.\n\n### 🖼️ 4. Diagram Analysis and Components\n\nThe large diagram illustrates the physical setup of the DK-908 EDM machine. It shows a vertical, heavy-duty industrial setup designed for precision machining.\n\n**Diagrammatic Description:**\n\n*   **Structure:** The machine features a robust, vertical frame structure.\n*   **Work Area:** The central lower section is the work area where the electrode interacts with the workpiece.\n*   **Electrode Feed System:** An apparatus is visible at the top center, designed to guide and feed the thin copper electrodes into the drilling zone.\n*   **Control Panel:** Located on the right side of the machine, this panel houses the electrical controls, power supplies, and monitoring indicators necessary to regulate the spark discharge parameters (voltage, current, pulse duration, etc.).\n*   **Operation Focus:** The diagram emphasizes the contained nature of the machining process, ensuring that the electrodes can move reliably and repeatedly in a controlled vertical axis.\n\n***\n\n### 💡 Summary Synthesis\n\nIn essence, the DK-908 is a highly specialized, electrically controlled drilling machine. It functions by mechanically guiding a precise copper electrode ($\\Phi 0.3 \\text{ to } 3.0 \\text{ mm}$) into a workpiece and then initiating a controlled electrical discharge (sparking). This controlled sparking melts and removes metal material, achieving accurate hole drilling without requiring physical physical tools that might wear down, making it ideal for intricate and delicate metal parts.\n\n[Page 5]\nThis page provides a detailed technical overview and visual identification of the physical components that make up a complex industrial machine, titled **"2. MACHINE COMPOSITION."**\n\nThe summary below captures the structure, function, and components shown in the "Machine External Identification Diagram."\n\n***\n\n## 🔩 Summary: Machine Composition and External Identification\n\n### 1. Overview and General Composition\nThe machine is an integrated system designed for specific industrial processing tasks. The overall architecture is hierarchical, involving core mechanical parts, specialized power/fluid handling units, and dedicated control mechanisms.\n\nThe machine is fundamentally composed of five major functional groups:\n\n1.  **Machine Head:** The primary working mechanism.\n2.  **Box Part:** The main structural chassis or housing.\n3.  **Working Fluid Supply Unit:** Components managing necessary fluids/pressure.\n4.  **Processing Power Supply Unit:** Providing electrical and mechanical power.\n5.  **Processing Control Device:** The intelligence unit managing operation.\n\n### 2. Component Breakdown (Key Data Table)\n\n| Component Name | Type / Role | Function / Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Head** | Core Mechanism | The primary, movable unit responsible for the physical processing action. |\n| **Working Lamp** | Auxiliary/Tooling | An attached component, likely providing focused light or operational energy to the working area. |\n| **Processing Control Device** | Control Unit (PLC/HMI) | The brain of the system; it receives input and coordinates the operation of all other devices. |\n| **Processing Power Supply Unit** | Power Source | Provides the main electrical power required to run the processing functions. |\n| **Box Part** | Structure / Housing | The foundation or main chassis that holds and supports all internal and external components. |\n| **Pressure Regulating Valve** | Fluid Management | Controls and stabilizes the pressure of working fluids (pneumatic or hydraulic). |\n| **Processing Industry Device** | Input/Utility | Likely a source or management unit for industrial utilities (e.g., compressed air, cooling). |\n| **Hand Pump** | Manual Utility | Provides manual pressure input, suggesting pneumatic or hydraulic backup/adjustment. |\n| **Power Load Switch** | Electrical Component | A manual or electrical switch used to manage and switch the main power load to the machine. |\n| **Workbench Department** | Operational Area | Defines the working surface or localized operational area for the machine. |\n\n### 3. Architectural Diagrammatic Description\n\nThe machine components are organized in a linear, stacked manner, from the core processing unit to the structural base:\n\n*   **Upper Section (Working Area):** The **Machine Head** is mounted on the upper frame, and the **Working Lamp** is attached directly to it.\n*   **Mid-Section (Utility and Control):** Below the head, essential fluid and utility management occurs. This includes the **Pressure Regulating Valve** and the **Processing Industry Device**. On the upper/side level, the **Processing Control Device** and **Hand Pump** are positioned, indicating where manual oversight and system command take place.\n*   **Lower Section (Power and Structure):** The main structural base is the **Box Part**. This lower level houses the main utilities, specifically the **Processing Power Supply Unit** (positioned atop the box) and the **Power Load Switch**.\n*   **Operational Zone:** The entire assembly rests within the **Workbench Department**.\n\n### 4. Operational Flow (Interconnectivity)\n\nThe system operates through the following logical flow:\n\n1.  **Power Input:** Power is supplied via the **Processing Power Supply Unit**, which is managed by the **Power Load Switch**.\n2.  **Control:** The **Processing Control Device** receives commands and coordinates the entire process.\n3.  **Actuation:** The control signal directs the **Machine Head** to perform the work, utilizing power delivered through the system.\n4.  **Support:** **Working Fluid** is regulated by the **Pressure Regulating Valve** and supplemented by the **Hand Pump** and **Processing Industry Device** to ensure stable operational conditions.\n5.  **Output:** The result of the process occurs on the **Workbench Department**.\n\n### 📌 Key Takeaways\n\n*   **Highly Integrated System:** The machine is not a single component but a modular assembly designed to manage multiple utilities (electrical, pneumatic, fluid).\n*   **Control Focus:** The **Processing Control Device** is central, indicating that operation is highly automated and managed by programmed inputs.\n*   **Multi-Source Power:** Power is addressed at multiple levels (mechanical head, electrical units, and fluid regulation), demonstrating robust design requirements.\n\n[Page 6]\nThis page provides a detailed technical overview of the **Machine Head** assembly used in machining processes. The summary below breaks down the function, core components, and internal systems of this specialized equipment.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Detailed Summary: Machine Head Assembly\n\nThe Machine Head is the primary component responsible for executing the machining action. It is mounted onto the main column and incorporates multiple specialized systems that work together to ensure precise, controlled movement and feeding.\n\n### ⚙️ Key Function and Overview\n\n**Primary Function:** To execute controlled movement and material feed (servo feed) during machining operations.\n\n**Composition:** The Machine Head is comprised of three main interacting systems:\n1.  **Spindle System:** The central operational unit.\n2.  **Counter Shaft (W-axis) System:** Provides additional movement axes.\n3.  **Rotating Head:** The component that performs the actual machining work.\n\n**Key Operational Detail:** The **spindle system** is highlighted as the main unit responsible for completing the **servo feed** mechanism, ensuring precise control during machining.\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Component Analysis (Table)\n\nThe following table details the major parts and sub-assemblies visible in the internal diagram.\n\n| Component Name | System/Function | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Column** | Structural Base | Provides the mounting and structural support for the entire head assembly. |\n| **Z-Axis Motor** | Actuator System | Drives the linear movement along the Z-axis (often a primary feed axis). |\n| **Z-Axis Screw** | Transmission Component | Converts the rotational movement of the motor into precise linear motion. |\n| **Rotating Head** | Operational Unit | The primary working part of the machine head. |\n| **Spindle System** | Motion/Processing Core | The central unit responsible for driving and completing servo feed. |\n| **Counter Shaft (W-axis)** | Auxiliary Motion System | Provides specialized motion capability (W-axis movement). |\n| **Linear Guides** | Guidance System | Ensures smooth, precise, and stable movement along the axis paths. |\n| **Guiding Device** | Alignment/Support | Provides overall mechanical support and precise path definition for the assembly. |\n| **Pressure Gauge Seat** | Measurement Interface | Point where monitoring pressure devices are mounted. |\n| **Manual Lifting** | Maintenance/Operation | Mechanism used for physical adjustment or raising/lowering of the head. |\n\n***\n\n### 📐 Internal Assembly Diagram\n\n*(Based on the visual representation provided)*\n\n**Diagram Title:** Machine Head Internal Assembly Diagram\n\n**Visualization:** The diagram illustrates a cross-section view, showing the linear arrangement of components mounted on the **Column**.\n\n**Flow of Action (Conceptual):**\n1.  The **Z-Axis Motor** drives the **Z-Axis Screw**.\n2.  This movement is channeled through the **Linear Guides** and the **Guiding Device**.\n3.  The **Rotating Head** is the final working component, receiving motion input to perform the machining task.\n\n***\n\n### 🎯 Key Data and Technical Takeaways\n\n| System | Role | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Servo Feed** | Motion Control | Mechanism ensuring controlled, precise input movement; managed primarily by the spindle system. |\n| **W-axis (Counter Shaft)** | Axial Movement | Provides secondary, specialized movement capabilities beyond the main Z-axis. |\n| **Linear Guides** | Precision & Stability | Critical for maintaining dimensional accuracy and minimizing vibration during high-speed operation. |\n| **Mounting Point** | Column | Acts as the foundational structural element, defining the operational envelope. |\n\n[Page 7]\nThis page provides a detailed description and exploded view of the internal components of a unit labeled "The Box." The summary below details the function, structure, and key components of this assembly.\n\n***\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: Box Internal Assembly\n\n### I. General Overview and Purpose\n\nThe unit, referred to as **"The Box,"** is a complex assembly designed to house and manage two primary functional systems:\n1. **Working Fluid Supply Device:** This system handles the mechanical movement and storage of liquid (water/fluid).\n2. **Processing Power Supply Unit:** This system manages the electrical power required for the operation.\n\n**Key Design Principle:** The structure is not merely for component containment. Its design is highly optimized to ensure:\n* **Convenience:** Facilitating easier maintenance and installation.\n* **Efficiency:** Providing superior overall ventilation and active cooling effects.\n\n### II. Component Breakdown (Key Data Table)\n\nThe box relies on the integration of mechanical, electrical, and thermal management components.\n\n| Component | Function | System Type | Role in Assembly |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Water Tank** | Stores the working fluid (e.g., cooling water). | Mechanical/Fluid | Supply source for the cooling cycle. |\n| **Water Pump** | Propels the working fluid from the tank through the system. | Mechanical/Fluid | Drives the circulating fluid. |\n| **Motor** | Provides the mechanical power to operate the pump and potentially the fan. | Mechanical/Power | Primary power source for fluid movement. |\n| **Transformer** | Converts incoming electrical power to the necessary voltage for other components. | Electrical/Power | Provides regulated power to the unit. |\n| **Drive Resistance** | Regulates and controls the electrical current/power supplied to the motor or other devices. | Electrical/Power | Manages motor speed and operational load. |\n| **Electrical Inst. Board** | Serves as the central control hub; manages wiring, switching, and system logic. | Electrical/Control | Directs power flow and monitors system status. |\n| **Fan** | Moves ambient air across the components and cooling elements. | Thermal/Airflow | Enhances ventilation and cooling. |\n| **High Frequency Resistance** | Acts as a heat dissipation element and potentially a tuning circuit. | Thermal/Electrical | Dissipates heat and manages electrical load. |\n| **Air Inlet** | The primary point of air intake into the assembly. | Thermal/Airflow | Draws ambient air into the unit. |\n| **Air Outlet** | The exit point for heated or processed air from the assembly. | Thermal/Airflow | Releases the cooled or ventilated air. |\n\n### III. Structural and Flow Diagram (Conceptual Layout)\n\nThe assembly is structured linearly, facilitating the flow of power, fluid, and air.\n\n**A. Flow of Fluid (Working Fluid Supply)**\n*   **Path:** Fluid is drawn from the **Water Tank** $\\rightarrow$ pushed by the **Water Pump** $\\rightarrow$ circulated through the cooling loop (implied) $\\rightarrow$ potentially cools the **High Frequency Resistance** and electrical components.\n*   **Power Source:** The **Motor** powers the **Water Pump**.\n\n**B. Flow of Power (Processing Power Supply)**\n*   **Source:** External Power $\\rightarrow$ **Transformer** (Voltage Conversion) $\\rightarrow$ **Electrical Installation Board** (Control/Switching) $\\rightarrow$ **Drive Resistance** (Power Modulation) $\\rightarrow$ **Motor**, **Fan**, and other electrical loads.\n\n**C. Flow of Heat/Air (Ventilation and Cooling)**\n*   **Input:** Ambient air enters through the **Air Inlet**.\n*   **Process:** Air is drawn over the heat-generating components (**High Frequency Resistance**, motor, transformer, etc.) and utilized by the **Fan**.\n*   **Output:** The cooled or ventilated air exits through the **Air Outlet**.\n\n### IV. Summary of Core Functions\n\nIn essence, the "Box" operates as an integrated cooling and power management station:\n\n1.  **Cooling:** The **Water Pump/Motor** maintain the fluid cooling circuit, while the **Fan/Air Inlet/Air Outlet** ensure convective airflow cooling across all electronics.\n2.  **Power Management:** The **Transformer** and **Drive Resistance** condition the electrical power, ensuring the other components receive stable and appropriate energy levels.\n3.  **Structure:** The defined physical separation of the working fluid and power systems enhances operational safety, minimizes heat buildup, and streamlines maintenance processes.\n\n[Page 8]\nThis page provides technical diagrams and dimensional data for two primary aspects of a machine tool: the physical structure of the **Workbench** and the operational reference frame of the **Machine Tool Transmission Pattern**.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including diagrams and key data points.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 I. 3. Workbench Diagram (Physical Structure)\n\nThis section details a cross-sectional view of the machine's workbench, providing the physical layout and component stacking order.\n\n### A. Key Data and Dimensions (Measurements)\n\nThe diagram includes several critical dimensions used for assembly and sizing:\n\n*   **Total Height/Vertical Dimension:** 970 (The overall vertical dimension of the upper components).\n*   **Vertical Dimension:** 480 (Indicates a specific height or segment dimension).\n*   **Horizontal Dimension:** 710 (Indicates the overall depth or width dimension).\n\n### B. Component Stack Diagram (Bottom to Top)\n\nThe workbench is constructed from several distinct, stacked components, designed to provide rigidity and functionality.\n\n| Component Name | Position / Function | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **PALLET BASE** | Bottommost structural component. | Forms the foundation of the machine work area. |\n| **WATER TRAY** | Second component. | Likely used for coolant collection and management. |\n| **LOWER BOARD** | Support structure. | Provides lower-level support and platforming. |\n| **MIDDLE BOARD** | Intermediate support structure. | Divides the unit and provides stability. |\n| **MARBLE** | Upper support/working surface. | Likely refers to a heavy, smooth working surface material. |\n| **COLUMN** | Uppermost structural element. | Supports the operational components above the marble surface. |\n\n### C. Diagrammatic Representation\n\n**(Descriptive Diagram based on the image):**\nThe diagram shows a vertical stack (total height 970) built upon a base. The dimensions 710 and 480 define the horizontal bounds of the assembly, illustrating the depth and width relative to the central stack.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ II. Machine Tool Transmission Pattern\n\nThis section establishes the coordinate system, which is fundamental for programming, motion control, and understanding the spatial axes of the machine tool.\n\n### A. Coordinate System Diagram (3D Axes)\n\nThe diagram displays a standard Cartesian coordinate system ($\\text{X}$, $\\text{Y}$, $\\text{Z}$), viewed from the **FRONT**.\n\n*   **X-Axis:** Represents one horizontal axis of movement (typically depth or width).\n*   **Y-Axis:** Represents the other horizontal axis of movement (typically width or depth).\n*   **Z-Axis:** Represents the vertical axis of movement (often related to the up/down movement or tool feed).\n\n### B. Operational Context\n\nThe purpose of this pattern is to define the workspace volume and the direction of motion. The tool's position is defined by its coordinates $(X, Y, Z)$ relative to the origin $(0, 0, 0)$.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Summary Synthesis\n\nThe page comprehensively details the mechanical and operational framework of a machine tool.\n\n1.  **Physical Foundation:** The **Workbench Diagram** provides detailed dimensional and component data, specifying a rigid, stacked assembly (PALLET BASE $\\rightarrow$ WATER TRAY $\\rightarrow$ boards $\\rightarrow$ MARBLE $\\rightarrow$ COLUMN) necessary for stability and work functionality.\n2.  **Operational Framework:** The **Transmission Pattern** defines the spatial reference system using $\\text{X}$, $\\text{Y}$, and $\\text{Z}$ axes, which are crucial for defining the tool's reach, movement axes, and programmable path within the machine's physical boundaries.\n\n[Page 9]\nThis document provides a detailed technical summary and specifications for a machine, likely a machining or tool-making apparatus, identified by the item number **DK-908**.\n\nThe summary is divided into three main sections: **Machine Parameters**, **Machine Dimensions**, and **Main Technical Parameters**.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ 3. MACHINE PARAMETER\n\n### 3.1 Item No.\n*   **DK-908**\n\n### 3.2 Machine Specification (Dimensions & Capacity)\n\nThis section details the physical size and working ranges of the machine.\n\n| Parameter | Unit | Measurement / Value |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Dimensions** (Length \\* Width \\* Height) | (mm) | 1000 \\* 1000 \\* 1900 |\n| **Weight** | (KG) | 645 |\n| **Workbench Dimension** | (mm) | 440\\*320 |\n| **Minimum Reading Unit** | (mm) | 0.005 |\n| **Workbench Left & Right Moving Distance (Y Axis)** | (mm) | 400 |\n| **Workbench Back & Forth Moving Distance (X Axis)** | (mm) | 300 |\n| **Maximum Loading Weight** | (KG) | 300 |\n| **Head Stroke** | (mm) | 280 |\n| **Spindle (Z-Axis) Servo Stroke** | (mm) | 380 |\n| **Maximum Distance Between Guide And Workbench** | (mm) | 320 |\n\n---\n\n## 📐 3.3 Main Technical Parameters\n\nThis section outlines the operational capacities, motor details, and fluid supply systems.\n\n### 1. Machine Movement & Speed\n*   **Spindle (Z-Axis) Maximum Feeding Speed:** 750 (r/min)\n*   **Rotary Head (C Axis) Speed:** 0 ~ 500 (r/min)\n\n### 2. Electrical & Power Specifications\n*   **Maximum Input Power:** 3.3 (KVA)\n*   **Input Voltage:** 3 Phase \\* AC 240V 50HZ\n*   **Maximum Machining Current:** 30 (A)\n\n### 3. Operating Functions & Systems\n*   **Electrode Diameter Range:** $\\Phi$ 0.3 ~ $\\Phi$ 3.0 (mm)\n*   **Machine Head (W Axis) Secondary Lifting Form:** Manual\n*   **Digital Display:** TWO AXIS\n*   **Working Fluid Supply Device:** Electric Piston Pump\n*   **Maximum Working Fluid Pressure:** 7 (Mpa)\n\n---\n***Summary Key Takeaways:***\n\n*   **Model:** DK-908\n*   **Capacity:** Suitable for handling a maximum loading weight of 300 KG.\n*   **Dimensions:** Large industrial footprint (1000\\*1000\\*1900 mm) with a total weight of 645 KG.\n*   **Functionality:** Features precise servo movements (Z-Axis stroke of 380 mm) and high-speed rotational capabilities (Spindle feeding up to 750 r/min).\n*   **Power:** Operates on 3 Phase, 240V, 50HZ with a maximum input power of 3.3 KVA.\n*   **Fluid System:** Uses an Electric Piston Pump for fluid supply, capable of reaching 7 Mpa pressure.\n\n[Page 10]\nThis page details the essential procedures and safety precautions for the **Installation** and **Transport** of the machinery.\n\nDue to the nature of the source material (a technical manual segment), the summary is highly structured using lists, tables, and detailed descriptions of the visual elements to ensure all key data and procedural instructions are captured.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Summary: 4.1 Transport\n\nThis section outlines the specific methods for lifting, positioning, and safely moving the machine.\n\n### 1. Lifting Procedures (Lifting the Machine)\n\n| Component | Action | Warning/Caution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Lifting Points** | Use the **eyebolts** located on the side of the machine pallet base. | N/A |\n| **Sling Contact** | If the lifting sling is expected to come into contact with the machine, **use a pad or lining cloth** to prevent damage. | **Crucial:** Prevent direct contact between the sling and the machine surface. |\n\n### 2. Transportation Procedures (Moving the Machine)\n\nThe machine must be maneuvered into a stable, low-profile position before being fully transported.\n\n**Key Steps:**\n\n1. **Lowering the Head:** Lower the machine head to the lower part, positioning it near the **lower limit position**.\n2. **Support:** Utilize the **skid** to support the bottom cover of the machine head.\n3. **Critical Warning:** **DO NOT** touch the stainless steel guide plate during this process.\n\n### 3. Essential Safety and Handling Guidelines\n\nSpecial care must be taken at all times during transport to prevent damage to sensitive components.\n\n| Area of Concern | Special Care Required | Prohibited Actions (Key Warnings) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Components** | The spindle head, guides, workbench, sheet metal, etc., require careful handling. | Do not allow these external parts to be damaged. |\n| **Physical Movement** | Maintain a safe distance between the machine and external objects. | **NEVER** use parts related to the machine as a fulcrum (pivot point). |\n\n### 4. Diagrammatic Representation\n\nThe page includes a diagram illustrating the method of lifting and transport.\n\n**Diagram Title:** Rope Loop Transport Diagram\n\n**Diagram Description:**\nThe diagram visually depicts the machine structure suspended and lifted by ropes and a hoisting mechanism (implied overhead crane/lifting gear). The lifting method utilizes a centralized lifting path, distributing the weight across the designated eyebolts.\n\n**Function:** This diagram serves as a visual guide for the correct, safe, and structurally sound method of hoisting the heavy machinery.\n\n---\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data Points\n\n**Installation/Transport Summary Checklist:**\n\n*   **Primary Lifting Points:** Side eyebolts on the pallet base.\n*   **Protection Requirement:** Use pads/lining cloth when slings make contact.\n*   **Target Position:** Lower limit position (assisted by the skid).\n*   **Critical Component to Avoid:** The stainless steel guide plate.\n*   **Absolute Safety Rule:** Never use any machine component as a fulcrum.\n*   **Reference Method:** Use the method demonstrated in the **Rope Loop Transport Diagram**.\n\n[Page 11]\nThis page provides detailed instructions regarding the mechanical installation, loading, and the optimal environmental placement of a specialized machine (likely industrial wood processing or machinery).\n\nThe content is divided into two main parts: **Mechanical Loading/Setup** (Diagram 5) and **Operational Placement Guidelines** (Section 4.2).\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Machine Setup and Placement Guidelines\n\n### 1. Mechanical Loading and Unloading (Diagram 5)\n\nThis section describes the procedural steps for preparing the machine's base and ensuring proper loading/unloading mechanisms are functional.\n\n**Key Element:** Forklift Loading And Unloading Diagram (Referenced by **(5)**)\n\n**Procedure Highlights:**\n\n*   **Base Preparation:** After the machine is placed, the user must remove any existing "stow-wood feet."\n*   **Assembly:** Stow-wood feet must be correctly attached, connecting them to the feet of the box (the main machine body).\n*   **Adjustment:** The procedure involves adjusting nuts and confirming that the adjustable machine feet are securely fixed to the base at the correct height.\n\n***\n\n### 2. Optimal Placement Environment and Adjustment (Section 4.2)\n\nThis section provides critical guidelines for ensuring the machine operates safely, efficiently, and with maximum accuracy. These guidelines address the physical foundation, surroundings, and general environment.\n\n#### 📏 Environmental Requirements (The "Where")\n\n| Requirement | Guideline | Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Placement Ground** | **Level Surface** | The machine must be placed on a level surface that is capable of withstanding its entire weight. |\n| **Vibration Countermeasure** | **Stable, Non-Vibrating Ground** | The machine must be situated on a stable ground that does not vibrate. If the adjacent machine generates vibration, rubber pads must be used for shock proofing. |\n| **Environment (Air Quality)** | **Stable Climate/Cleanliness** | The operating environment must have little temperature change, minimal "garbage" (debris), and must not be exposed to direct sunlight. |\n\n#### 🛠️ Key Data & Adjustment Points (The "How")\n\nThe placement strategy requires careful consideration of several physical factors:\n\n1.  **Level Adjustment:** Ensures the machine is perfectly horizontal for accurate processing.\n2.  **Stability:** Requires a solid foundation to prevent wobble or misalignment during operation.\n3.  **Temperature Control:** Stable ambient temperatures are necessary for maintaining optimal processing accuracy.\n4.  **Debris Mitigation:** The area must be kept clean and free of accumulating waste products.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Summary Table of Key Actions\n\n| Area | Action/Component | Goal/Purpose | Critical Condition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Foundation** | Placement Ground | Support machine weight and maintain level alignment. | Must be **level** and capable of bearing weight. |\n| **Stability** | Vibration Countermeasure | Prevent operation disruption and mechanical damage. | Must be **stable**, non-vibrating; use rubber if necessary. |\n| **Setup/Loading** | Adjustable Machine Feet | Secure the machine to the ground and adjust height. | Must be correctly **fixed** and properly **adjusted**. |\n| **Environment** | Climate & Cleanliness | Ensure smooth, continuous, and accurate operation. | Must have **stable temperature**, low debris, and no direct sunlight. |\n\n[Page 12]\nThis page provides detailed operational instructions, electrical specifications, and technical data for two core systems: the **Water Circulation Device (4.3)** and the **Water Pump (2)**.\n\n## 📝 Detailed Summary\n\n### I. Initial Setup and Adjustment Procedure\n\nThe unit requires careful alignment and electrical grounding before operation.\n\n*   **Alignment:**\n    *   Perform horizontal adjustment and place the level at the center of the workbench.\n    *   Adjust the unit with the adjustment nut of the lower machine's four feet.\n    *   **Accuracy:** The adjustment must be completed and the machine confirmed to be within **$0.04 \\text{ mm/M}$** of the optimal level, and it must maintain contact with the ground.\n\n### II. Operational & Electrical Parameters\n\n*   **Room Temperature Management (5):** For optimal efficiency, it is suggested to process the area with changes in both time and zone.\n*   **Voltage (6):** The specified power supply voltage is **AC240V/M**.\n*   **Grounding (7):** The ground wire must be connected securely.\n\n***\n\n### 💧 4.3 Water Circulation Device\n\nThis section outlines the components and function of the water circulation system.\n\n#### **A. Diagram Description**\nThe diagram illustrates a closed-loop water circulation mechanism powered by a motor and incorporating various regulating elements.\n\n*   **Components Flow:** Water enters the system via the **Inlet Pipe** and passes through the **Water Tank**.\n*   **Circulation Path:** From the tank, water moves toward the **Rotating Head** and then through the **High Pressure Water Pipe**.\n*   **Regulation:** The flow is managed by a **Pressure Gauge** and passes through a **High Pressure Water Regulator**.\n*   **Power and Return:** The **Motor** drives the circulation, and the flow exits through the **Outlet Pipe**. Components like the **Water Inlet Switch** and **Float** are integrated to manage the system's level and operation.\n\n***\n\n### ⚙️ (2) Water Pump\n\nThe pump is a high-performance unit designed for intensive fluid handling.\n\n*   **Type and Function:** It is a little-small three-cylinder ceramic ceramic plunger pump. It is designed to handle **high-pressure** and **high-speed** applications.\n*   **Operating Temperature:** The water temperature must be maintained between **$0 \\text{ to } 40 \\text{ °C}$** at room temperature.\n\n#### **Main Technical Parameters (Table)**\n\n| Parameter | Value |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Plunger Diameter x Stroke** | $\\phi 18 \\times 4.5 \\text{ mm}$ |\n| **Rated Speed** | $1390 \\text{ r/min}$ |\n\n[Page 13]\nBased on the provided technical document, this is a detailed summary covering the specifications, operational principles, maintenance procedures, and critical safety warnings for a high-pressure pump system.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Technical Summary\n\n### 1. Key Technical Specifications (Data Table)\n\nThe following table summarizes the core metrics and dimensions of the pump unit:\n\n| Specification | Value | Unit / Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Theoretical Flow** | 4.88 | L/min |\n| **Rated Pressure** | 7 | Mpa |\n| **Matching Motor Power** | 750 | W |\n| **Crankshaft Lubricant** | N68-N150 Machinery Oil Gb3142-82 | |\n| **Plunger Material** | Ceramics | |\n| **Overall Dimension** | 200 x 190 x 115 | mm |\n| **Base Size** | 80 x 80-M6 Screw | |\n| **Weight** | 4.8 | Kg |\n| **Connector** | PC10-G3/8" | |\n| **Outlet Joint Thread** | M12 x 1 25 | |\n| **Suction Pipe Inner Diameter** | $\\phi 10$ | |\n| **Outlet Pipe Inner Diameter** | $\\phi 6$ | |\n\n***\n\n### 2. Working Principle and Operational Guidelines\n\n#### 🌊 Working Principle\nThe pump operates by converting the motor's **rotary motion** into a **reciprocating motion** of the crankshaft. This action generates a vacuum (suction) phase and a high-pressure discharge phase.\n\n#### 🛡️ Usage & Performance Warnings\n*   **Low Water Level Warning:** If the plunger pump experiences weak water absorption performance, it is critical that the water level remains **above the suction hole**.\n*   **Pressure Dependency:** The pump's operational pressure depends on the damping of the pump outlet. Higher damping generally results in higher pressure.\n*   **Duration Limit:** The unit must not be operated for a duration that exceeds **2 minutes** under specific failure conditions (no pressure/no water).\n*   **Mandatory Water Flow:** When the plunger operates, water must be drawn into the cylinder plunger, and the outlet valve must remain open.\n\n***\n\n### 3. Maintenance Procedures & Key Safety Data\n\n#### 💡 Oil Change Schedule and Action\n*   **Change Interval:** The oil must be replaced every **100 hours** of operation.\n*   **Flushing:** If the pump is suspected of weak water absorption performance, a flush is recommended.\n*   **Minimum Drain:** When draining the oil, the amount drained must not exceed **1 ml/min**.\n\n#### ⚠️ Critical Safety & Failure Points\n1.  **Blocked Operation:** **NEVER** operate the pump if the system is blocked or if the pressure gauge shows no pressure AND no water is present.\n2.  **Minor Leaks:** Small leaks or dripping, such as those from the pump seal, are considered normal during operation.\n\n#### 🔄 Oil Change Method (Step-by-Step Procedure)\nThe procedure for changing the lubricating oil is as follows:\n1.  Open the fuel cap.\n2.  Place the oil container beneath the drain bolt.\n3.  Open the drain bolt and allow the old oil to drain completely.\n4.  Screw out the drain bolt.\n5.  Inject the new lubricating oil, using the specified label, into the fuel system.\n\n***\n*(Note: The original document contained text fragments and page numbers ('12') which were excluded from the summary as they do not provide technical information.)*\n\n[Page 14]\nThis document provides a detailed summary of the operating procedures, safety guidelines, and maintenance requirements for the equipment, focusing heavily on fluid levels, pressure regulation, and filtration.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ Operational & Maintenance Summary\n\n### I. Safety & Fluids Management (Pre-Operation Checks)\n\n| Component | Check/Action Required | Key Data / Guideline |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Oil Level** | Check the oil level. | Must be maintained at **half of the oil mark.** |\n| **Fuel Filler** | Secure the fuel filler cap. | Ensure the seal is properly applied. |\n| **Safety Warning** | **DO NOT** attempt to disassemble the cylinder head or open the crankcase yourself. | For major mechanical issues, contact the company immediately. |\n\n***\n\n### II. System Mechanics & Components\n\nThe core system involves the assembly of the **motor** and the **water pump**. Both are designed for direct connection and are critical to the system's proper function.\n\n#### ⚙️ System Flow Pathway\nThe process involves monitoring the pressure through a regulating valve, which controls the water flow from the copper pipe.\n\n*   **Motor:** Provides the power.\n*   **Water Pump:** Works in conjunction with the motor.\n*   **Regulating Valve:** Controls the pressure and flow to the system.\n\n#### 🧭 Visual Guide: System Components (Implied Diagram)\n*(A conceptual diagram would show the path: Water Storage $\\rightarrow$ Filter Element $\\rightarrow$ Copper Pipe $\\rightarrow$ Pressure Gauge $\\rightarrow$ Regulating Valve $\\rightarrow$ Pump Output)*\n\n***\n\n### III. Critical Maintenance Procedures\n\n#### (4) Pressure Gauge\n*   **Function:** Allows the operator to visualize the current working pressure.\n*   **Operation:** The pressure gauge must be observed at all times during operation.\n\n#### (5) Pressure Regulating Valve\nThis valve is essential for maintaining the correct pressure of the water flowing from the copper pipe. It is composed of two parts: **built-in** and **external**.\n\n**Key Data: Operating Pressure Range**\n*   **Required Pressure:** The system needs to be regulated to a specific range of **5–7 Mpa**.\n\n**🧰 Table: Adjusting the Pressure Regulating Valve**\n\n| Action Needed | Adjustment Method (Valve Movement) | Effect on System Pressure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Increase Pressure** | Turn the valve to the **right**. | Increases the working pressure. |\n| **Decrease Pressure** | Turn the valve to the **left**. | Decreases the working pressure. |\n\n**⚠️ Inspection Checklist (Preventative Maintenance)**\n*   **Interval:** After the pump operates for **500 hours**.\n*   **Action:** Conduct a comprehensive inspection.\n*   **Checks:** Verify all fittings for leaks or water leakage, and check for any physical damage.\n*   **Replacement:** If needed, replace the accessories using original manufacturer parts.\n\n#### (6) Filter Element\n*   **Location:** Situated at the **bottom** of the water storage tank, near the inlet pipe.\n*   **Function:** Prevents foreign matter from entering the pump system.\n*   **Cleaning Protocol:**\n    1.  **Frequency:** Clean the element at least **once a month**.\n    2.  **Method:** Use only a **little force** when cleaning.\n    3.  **Procedure:** The element must be submerged **below the water surface** during the cleaning process.\n    4.  **Preparation:** After cleaning, it is recommended to dry the filter element and then immerse it in clean water before returning it to service.\n\n***\n\n### Summary of Key Data Points\n\n*   **System Function:** Water flow control and filtration.\n*   **Target Pressure:** 5–7 Mpa.\n*   **Filter Cleaning:** Monthly, submerged, low force.\n*   **System Inspection:** Every 500 operating hours.\n*   **Oil Level Check:** Half of the oil mark.\n*   **Safety:** Never disassemble the cylinder head or crankcase without professional assistance.\n\n[Page 15]\nThis summary is structured based on the technical procedures outlined in the provided text snippet. Given that the source material is highly procedural, the summary uses tables and descriptive placeholders for diagrams to capture the required level of detail.\n\n---\n\n# 📄 Technical Procedure Summary: Filtration and Power Supply Testing\n\nThe document outlines critical procedures for maintaining and testing complex machinery, focusing on filter replacement and comprehensive power supply verification.\n\n## I. Filter System (Section 7)\n\nThe filter element is designed to remove impurities and requires scheduled replacement or replacement upon noticing reduced efficiency (blockage).\n\n### A. Filter Maintenance and Installation Protocol\n\n| Action | Procedure/Method | Key Warnings & Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Removal** | Use a special wrench to unscrew the filter element. | Ensure the element is handled properly and **do not** allow it to fall out of the seal. |\n| **Installation (New Filter)** | 1. Keep the element vertical. 2. Tighten using a special wrench. | Proper orientation and secure tightening are critical for system integrity. |\n\n***[Diagram Placeholder: Filter Element Orientation]***\n*(This diagram would illustrate the correct vertical placement and tightening mechanism of the new filter element.)*\n\n## II. Power Supply Testing (Section 4.4)\n\nThis section details the systematic approach to testing the power supply, divided into connection checks, visual inspections, and electrical measurements.\n\n### A. Electrical Connection & Power Source Verification (1)\n\nThe primary goal is to confirm the voltage and safety of the connection from the owner's distribution box to the main power supply air switch.\n\n**Key Measurement:** The multimeter must be used to measure the voltage between the open-air output side and the load side.\n\n**Technical Data:**\n*   **Expected Voltage:** 750V (Must be confirmed by measurement).\n*   **Measurement Tool:** Multimeter.\n\n**⚠️ Critical Safety Warnings:**\n1.  **Authorization:** Do not open the equipment or distribution box without obtaining the owner's explicit consent and proper authorization.\n2.  **Voltage Check:** Always verify the voltage at the main power supply air switch before proceeding.\n\n### B. Line Routine Inspection (2)\n\nInspection must be comprehensive, covering physical damage, structural integrity, and electrical continuity.\n\n#### 1. Visual Inspection (Physical and Structural Check)\n\n| Component Checked | Inspection Criteria | Action upon Failure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Transformer & Motor** | Check for signs of damage or movement (especially after transport). | Halt operation; investigate the damage source. |\n| **Wiring & Circuitry** | Check if bundled wires are disconnected, loosened, or if the circuit board is dropped. | Secure all wiring and components; re-establish connections. |\n| **Sheet Metal** | Inspect the sheet metal structure for any visible damage. | Damage may affect the operational line integrity. |\n\n#### 2. Instrument Check (Electrical Continuity and Measurement)\n\nThis involves verifying the functionality of the primary circuit components.\n\n| Test Focus | Equipment/Circuits Checked | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Main Circuit** | Main Circuit Check (General integrity). | Verify overall electrical safety and continuity. |\n| **Motor Resistors** | U, V, and W three-phase resistors (for both pump motor and secondary stroke motor). | Measure and verify resistance to ensure proper motor operation. |\n\n### C. System Specification and Connection (Key Data)\n\nThis section establishes mandatory specifications for connecting the power components.\n\n| Component | Specification / Requirement | Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Power Supply Wire Diameter** | $\\geq 2.5 \\text{MM}^2$ | Must be multi-core copper wire. |\n| **Power Cord** | Connection Protocol | Must be connected and tightened securely. |\n\n***[Diagram Placeholder: Power Supply Connection Flow]***\n*(This diagram would illustrate the path of the power cord: Owner's Distribution Box $\\rightarrow$ Air Switch $\\rightarrow$ Multimeter Measurement Point $\\rightarrow$ Load/Machine.)*\n\n---\n## 📝 Summary of Key Data and Procedures\n\n*   **System Voltage:** 750V\n*   **Min Wire Gauge:** $2.5 \\text{MM}^2$ (Multi-core copper wire)\n*   **Filter Element Handling:** Vertical placement; use of special wrench.\n*   **Safety Protocol:** Mandatory consent and authorization before accessing power distribution boxes.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** Visual (Damage/Loose wires), Electrical (Resistors U, V, W), and Operational (Voltage Confirmation).\n\n[Page 16]\nThis document appears to be a highly technical, step-by-step operational and maintenance checklist for starting up or troubleshooting complex machinery, likely involving AC/DC motor systems.\n\nSince the source material is purely textual, I cannot provide actual diagrams or diagrams. However, I have organized the information into a detailed procedural summary, using tables and structured steps to represent the key data and processes.\n\n***\n\n# ⚙️ Detailed Procedural Summary: Machinery Startup & Electrical Inspection\n\n**Purpose:** To ensure all electrical systems, motor connections, and phase sequencing are properly validated before the machine is operated.\n**Required Tools:** Multimeter, Ohmmeter.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Section 1: Electrical Circuit Validation (Ohmmeter Checks)\n\nThis section outlines critical resistance and continuity checks to ensure the integrity of the electrical supply and motor connections.\n\n| System Component | Measurement Required | Ohmmeter Function | Notes / Key Areas to Check |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Resistance (2-Phase)** | Two-phase resistance check | Continuity/Resistance | Spindle Motor, Rotary Motor A1, A2, B1, and B2. |\n| **Grounding** | Continuity Check | Ohmmeter | Check grounding at **each phase** of the system. |\n| **Main Circuits** | Continuity Check | Ohmmeter | 1. AC Circuit (Main). 2. DC Circuit (Control). |\n| **Circuits** | Continuity Check | Ohmmeter | Check for **serial connection** between the AC and DC circuits. |\n| **System Status** | Resistance/Voltage Check | Ohmmeter | Check the grounding for the ohmic file (general grounding integrity). |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Section 2: Startup Procedure (Power On)\n\nThis procedure details the safe and sequential method for applying power and validating the system status.\n\n### Step-by-Step Checklist\n\n| Step No. | Action / Procedure | Tool Used | Key Validation Point |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **2.1** | **Voltage Check (Main)** | Multimeter | Verify voltage level on the main power switch of the owner's power distribution panel. |\n| **2.2** | **Power Source Check** | Multimeter | Open the main power switch (Owner's Panel). Verify output side voltage using the multimeter. |\n| **2.3** | **Supply Validation** | Visual / Multimeter | Check the connection and confirm the main power supply from the owner's power distribution cabinet. **(Safety Note:** Ensure all necessary power supply permissions are granted.) |\n| **2.4** | **Power Engagement** | Manual Switch | Close the machine power supply air switch and the load switch. |\n| **2.5** | **Initial Readings** | Multimeter | Measure the output side voltage to confirm proper system connectivity. |\n| **2.6** | **Ready State** | Physical Action | **Release** the emergency stop switch. |\n| **2.7** | **Confirmation** | Visual | Verify that the digital display is operational, showing initial coordinates and active panel indicator lights. |\n\n---\n\n## 🔄 Section 3: Operational Sequence Check (Power Phase Sequence)\n\nThis section is critical for verifying the mechanical and electrical direction of the machine's movement.\n\n### Phase Sequence Validation\n\n1.  **Direction Check:** Verify the rotation direction of the secondary stroke (W-axis).\n2.  **Mechanical Check:** Click the **UP** button. Manually jog/move the W-axis up, and then down.\n3.  **Three-Phase Sequence:**\n    *   Identify the two lines connecting the three-phase power supply.\n    *   Jog the **DW** button.\n    *   Move the W-axis down.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Summary of Key Data & Warnings\n\n### 💡 Safety and Caution Points\n*   **Tool:** Ohmmeter/Multimeter must be used for all resistance and voltage checks.\n*   **Location:** Always reference the owner's power distribution cabinet and panel settings.\n*   **Hazard:** The text implies the need for caution when connecting/disconnecting power, mentioning the owner's power supply permission.\n\n### 📈 Key Parameters & Outputs\n| Parameter | Measurement / State | Expected Output |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Resistance/Continuity** | Checked using Ohmmeter | Must be within specified limits (Implied: Low/Stable) |\n| **Voltage (Output)** | Checked using Multimeter | Must match expected system voltage. |\n| **System State** | After Power On | Digital display active; indicator lights illuminated; Initial coordinates displayed. |\n| **Functionality** | After Phase Check | Confirmed rotational direction and movement (W-axis up/down). |\n\n[Page 17]\nThis document is a highly detailed technical troubleshooting and maintenance guide, likely pertaining to a CNC or automated machining system. It covers diagnostics for motor and electrical issues (specifically phase sequence) and provides step-by-step instructions for disassembling and reassembling mechanical components (X and Y axes).\n\nSince the original text is solely descriptive text, I cannot generate actual diagrams. Instead, I will use **detailed structural descriptions and Markdown tables** to simulate the visual and data organization required for a comprehensive technical summary.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Detailed Technical Summary: System Troubleshooting and Assembly\n\n### I. System Overview and Purpose\n\nThe procedure focuses on three main areas:\n1. **Electrical Diagnostics:** Verifying the correct **phase sequence** of the three-phase power supply to ensure proper motor operation.\n2. **Operational Checks:** Clearing mechanical blockages and troubleshooting directional controls (UP/DOWN buttons).\n3. **Mechanical Maintenance:** Detailed steps for the disassembly and reassembly of the X- and Y-axis carriage and platens.\n\n### II. Key Data and Technical Specifications\n\n| Component | Specification / Detail | Function |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Type** | 3-phase synchronous motor | Provides the motive force for the X-axis. |\n| **Voltage Requirement** | 380V (Three-Phase Power) | Standard electrical requirement for the motor. |\n| **Critical Check** | Phase Sequence | Must be correct for the motor to operate smoothly and avoid blockages. |\n| **Tools Required** | Allen Key | Used to remove the left-hand pallet sheet metal fixing screw. |\n\n### III. Core Diagnostics: Phase Sequence Troubleshooting\n\nThe primary cause of system failure, particularly when the movement is blocked or sporadic, is an incorrect phase sequence.\n\n#### A. Troubleshooting Flowchart (Described Diagram)\n*   **Start Point:** Machine operates but movement is incorrect (e.g., the limit switch fails, or the motor is erratic).\n*   **Diagnosis:** The problem is linked to the phase sequence of the three-phase supply.\n*   **Action 1 (Motor Test):** The motor must be tested for phase sequence *before* any mechanical work begins.\n*   **Action 2 (Power Check):** The technician must confirm that the three-phase power supply is delivering the correct sequence.\n*   **Result:** If the phase sequence is corrected, the system should return to normal operation (e.g., the limit switch functions correctly).\n\n#### B. Phase Sequence Check Steps (Key Data/Action Points)\n1. **Initial Check:** When the W-axis supports the loader part, the phase sequence must be checked.\n2. **Diagnosis:** The motor running in the wrong direction or with an incorrect phase sequence will fail to operate, causing a blockage.\n3. **Correction:** Adjust the three-phase power supply until the motion proceeds smoothly.\n\n### IV. Operational and Blockage Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom / Problem | Cause (Potential) | Solution / Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **UP/DOWN Button Failure** | Mechanical blockage or failure to register input. | Check for blockages. If confirmed clear, the system should operate normally. |\n| **General Stoppage/Blockage** | Incorrect Phase Sequence (Most common cause). | Verify and correct the three-phase power sequence. |\n| **Failure to Move (Limit Switch)** | Electrical issue or mechanical binding. | Check the physical limit switch function after electrical verification. |\n\n### V. Mechanical Maintenance: Disassembly and Assembly Procedures\n\nThis section provides a sequential guide for the physical maintenance of the axes.\n\n#### A. X-Axis and Pallet Plate Procedure (Focus: Disassembly)\n1. **Preparation:** Ensure the machine is powered down.\n2. **Removal Step 1:** Use the Allen key to remove the **left-hand pallet sheet metal fixing screw**.\n3. **Removal Step 2:** Disconnect the base and the X-axis carriage.\n4. **Removal Step 3:** The **platen screw** is unscrewed.\n5. **Result:** The fixing plate, sheet metal, and carriage can now be removed and bagged for transport or repair.\n\n#### B. X-Axis Reassembly (Focus: Reinstallation)\n1. **Action:** Reinstall the platen and re-fix the sheet metal.\n2. **Testing:** Manually guide the carriage and check that the movement is free and smooth.\n\n#### C. Y-Axis Procedure (Focus: Cleaning and Reinstallation)\n1. **Pre-Cleaning:** Shake the X-axis carriage in the opposite direction of the column.\n2. **Action:** Remove the sheet metal screws behind the Y-axis.\n3. **Action:** Reinstall the pallet sheet metal.\n\n---\n***Summary Visual Descriptor (Simulated Diagram Notes)***\n\n***[Note: If this guide were accompanied by diagrams, the following elements would be crucial:]***\n\n*   **Diagram 1 (Cross-Section):** A labeled diagram showing the location of the X-axis carriage, the left-hand fixing screw, the platen, and the specific points of disconnection.\n*   **Diagram 2 (Circuit Diagram):** A wiring schematic highlighting the three input lines (L1, L2, L3) and indicating the correct, corresponding phase sequence (e.g., L1 $\\rightarrow$ L2 $\\rightarrow$ L3).\n*   **Diagram 3 (Axis View):** An illustrative view of the Y-axis showing the location of the screws to be removed behind the carriage for cleaning and reinstallation.\n\n[Page 18]\nThis page details the components and operational flow of the **Electrical Control System** of a machine tool. The system integrates multiple units—including pulse power supply, rectifier unit, and spindle servo—to precisely control machine movement and functions.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including a system diagram and descriptive tables.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Overview of the Electrical Control System\n\nThe electrical control system is crucial for governing the machine's operation. It is composed of the **pulse power supply, spindle servo system, rotary head device, rectifier unit,** and other associated components. Its primary function is to regulate and deliver precise electrical pulses to execute movements and process operations.\n\n### System Flow Diagram\n\nThe electrical signals flow through a structured series of components:\n\n$$\\text{Machine Tool} \\rightarrow \\text{Rectifier Unit} \\rightarrow \\text{Pulse Power Supply} \\rightarrow \\text{Signal Control} \\rightarrow \\text{Machine Tool Spindle} \\rightarrow \\text{Output}$$\n\n**Key Operational Path:**\n1.  The **Machine Tool** initiates the process.\n2.  The signal passes through the **Rectifier Unit** (which processes the initial signal).\n3.  The **Pulse Power Supply** generates the necessary power pulses.\n4.  The signal is regulated by **Manual Adjustment** and **Preferences**.\n5.  The final regulated signal drives the **Machine Tool Spindle**.\n\n---\n\n## 🏷️ Component Functions and Key Data (Table)\n\n| Component / Unit | Role in System | Function / Purpose | Key Processes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rectifier Unit** | Signal Processing | Takes input power and converts it into the necessary usable current and voltage for the system. | Signal conversion and conditioning. |\n| **Pulse Power Supply** | Energy Source | Supplies the electrical pulses that drive the machine's components. | Generating controlled, repetitive electrical pulses. |\n| **Spindle Servo System** | Control Mechanism | Monitors and adjusts the physical position and speed of the machine spindle. | Feedback control, angular displacement adjustment, stepping motor control. |\n| **Machine Tool Spindle** | Actuator | The physical component that executes the primary mechanical movement (rotation). | Rotating the cutting tool/spindle. |\n| **Manual Adjustment / Preferences** | User Interface / Control | Allows the operator to set specific operational parameters and control thresholds. | Setting speed, torque, or operation modes. |\n\n---\n\n## 🔬 Detailed Functional Analysis\n\n### 1. Pulse Power Supply (Function Source)\n\nThis unit is the core power source for the system, delivering pulses needed for operation. (While the provided text does not detail its internal function, it acts as the primary energy conduit for the regulated signal.)\n\n### 2. Spindle Servo System (The Core Control Loop)\n\nThis system is the most technically detailed section and manages the precision and stability of the machine's movement. It operates on a continuous feedback loop to ensure the desired outcome is met.\n\n**A. Operating Principle:**\nThe system continually monitors the machine's physical movement and compares the actual status to the desired status.\n\n**B. Key Functions:**\n\n*   **Feedback and Adjustment:** The system uses a **feedback signal** to determine the current status of the machine. If the system parameters fall outside the operator’s set range, it signals an adjustment.\n*   **Angular Displacement:** It performs continuous calculations to determine the necessary **angular displacement** required for the spindle to reach its target position.\n*   **Stepping Motor Control:** The system sends precise pulses to the **stepping motor**. This motor is responsible for moving the spindle and must accurately control the corresponding angular position.\n*   **Process Loop:** It involves a continuous loop where:\n    1.  The system sends pulses.\n    2.  The pulse parameters and corresponding current are measured.\n    3.  This data is constantly reviewed to ensure the desired **angle** is reached.\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Summary of Key Technical Data and Terminology\n\n| Term | Definition | Operational Importance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Feedback Signal** | The signal that reports the current, actual state/position of the spindle or machine. | Allows the system to self-correct and maintain accuracy. |\n| **Angular Displacement** | The precise measure of the rotational angle the spindle must cover. | Essential for accurate positioning of the machine tool. |\n| **Stepping Motor** | A specific type of motor that moves in discrete, measurable "steps." | Provides high precision and controlled movement required for positioning. |\n| **Rectifier** | An electrical device that converts Alternating Current (AC) to Direct Current (DC). | Ensures the components receive the stable, usable electrical power they require. |\n| **Pulse/Pulse Power** | A timed, electrical burst of energy used to trigger or control movement. | The fundamental unit of energy used to execute mechanical action. |\n\n[Page 19]\nThis page (5.2 Rotating Head) provides a detailed technical description, operational explanation, and labeled diagram of a specialized piece of equipment known as the Rotating Head, which is used in conjunction with a copper tube electrode in a high-pressure fluid process.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including the components, function, and key data points.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary: 5.2 Rotating Head\n\n### 1. Purpose and Core Function (Mechanism)\n\nThe Rotating Head is an **integrated design** unit mounted on the spindle slider. Its primary function is to ensure the efficient and reliable sealing introduction of the high-pressure working fluid.\n\nThis process occurs during critical operational phases, including:\n*   Clamping\n*   Rotation\n*   Conduction\n*   Rotation of the copper tube electrode\n\nThe design emphasizes maximizing reliability and preventing leakage through a highly robust **Sealed Water Inlet Device**.\n\n### 2. Technical Mechanism and Operation\n\n*   **Driving Force:** The rotation of the copper tube electrode is directly driven by the rotary motor.\n*   **Motor Control:** The rotary motor is driven by a **subdivided professional driver**.\n*   **Fluid Injection:** The high-pressure working fluid is injected into the central hole of the rotor of the motor.\n*   **Structural Optimization:** The design includes specific channels and components (High Pressure Inlet Pipe, Return Pipe) to manage fluid flow and cooling.\n*   **Efficiency:** The design utilizes a highly reliable and efficient sealed water inlet mechanism.\n\n### 3. Key Technical Specifications and Advantages\n\nThe design is optimized for superior performance and longevity.\n\n| Feature | Specification / Advantage | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Voltage Adjustment** | Wide range | Allows operation across diverse electrical setups. |\n| **Torque** | Large | Indicates high mechanical power capacity and stability. |\n| **Failure Rate** | Low | Suggests high durability and reliable, extended operational life. |\n| **Speed Control** | Adjustable rotation speed | Allows precise process control depending on the application needs. |\n| **Water Sealing** | Reliable and reasonable design of the sealed water inlet device. | Ensures operational efficiency under extreme high-pressure conditions. |\n\n### 4. Components Diagram and Labels\n\nThe diagram provides a labeled, cutaway view of the Rotating Head unit, detailing the specific mechanical components:\n\n| Component Label | Function / Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Rotary Motor** | The core engine unit that drives the rotation of the copper tube. |\n| **High Pressure Inlet Pipe** | Feeds the pressurized working fluid into the system. |\n| **Return Pipe** | Collects spent or cooled working fluid for recirculation/exit. |\n| **Water Spray Outlet** | The exit point for the high-pressure working fluid spray. |\n| **Sealed Waterproof Device** | Critical component ensuring the main mechanism remains sealed and leak-free. |\n| **Carbon Brush** | Electrical component used to transmit current/signal to the motor. |\n\n### 5. Legal Disclaimer (Important Note)\n\nThe page includes a mandatory legal notice regarding intellectual property:\n> 🌟 **This product is a patented product of the State Intellectual Property Office. It is forbidden to be counterfeited.**\n\n***\n*(Note: The summary summarizes the textual content, the labeled diagram elements, and extracts the key performance data points into a structured format to meet the "detailed summary" requirement.)*\n\n[Page 20]\nThis page, labeled "Panel Layout" (Page 19), provides highly detailed technical instructions and a schematic diagram for two primary functional areas of the machine: **The Motor Shaft Locking Mechanism** and **The Control Panel (5.3)**.\n\nThe summary is broken down into two major sections, complete with diagrams and key data points.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Section 1: Motor Shaft Locking Mechanism\n\nThis section describes the procedure for securing the electric chuck using the motor shaft lock cap.\n\n### 🔬 Diagram: Motor Shaft Lock Cap\n*(The diagram illustrates the mechanism involving a head cover and the locking cap)*\n\n### 📝 Key Data and Procedure\n**Title:** Rotating the Head Cover\n**Purpose:** To lock and tighten the copper tube electric chuck.\n**Action Sequence:**\n1.  **Rotate** the motor shaft to lock the screw device.\n2.  **Press** the screw cap.\n3.  The motor shaft then locks and **tightens** the copper tube electric chuck.\n\n***\n\n## 🖥️ Section 2: Control Panel (5.3)\n\nThis section is dedicated to the operational schematic of the machine's control panel, detailing 16 specific points of function.\n\n### 📐 Diagram: Control Panel Layout\n*(A detailed schematic drawing showing the arrangement of various electrical components, switches, and meters. The numbered labels 1 through 16 point to specific parts of the panel.)*\n\n### 📍 Detailed Component Mapping (Key Data)\nThe diagram uses numbered labels to denote various components. Although specific component names are not provided, they fall into standard industrial control categories.\n\n| Label Number | Area Function / Type | Description (Implied Function) |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1, 2, 3** | **Switches / Selectors** | Likely main power switches, mode selectors, or safety cut-offs. |\n| **4 – 10** | **Control Switches / Inputs** | A central cluster of buttons, push-buttons, and smaller switches used for cycle start, stop, and operational adjustments. |\n| **11 – 16** | **Indicators / Displays** | Measurement devices (gauges, meters) and status indicators (lights/lamps) providing real-time machine data (e.g., speed, pressure, voltage). |\n\n***\n\n## 🔑 Summary of Key Data and Instructions\n\n| Function Area | Key Action | Component/Location | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Chuck Locking** | Rotate $\\rightarrow$ Press | Motor Shaft Lock Cap | To securely lock and tighten the copper tube electric chuck. |\n| **Control Operation** | Utilizing numbered inputs (1-10) | Control Panel | To initiate, monitor, and regulate the machine's operational processes. |\n| **Status Monitoring** | Reading indicators (11-16) | Control Panel (Right side) | To check the machine's operational status, including pressure, speed, and power levels. |\n\n**Overall Summary:**\nThe page is a technical guide that sequentially details two critical functions: the mechanical process of locking the chuck (via rotation and pressing the cap) and the electrical/operational interface (the control panel) that governs the machine's function. The comprehensive diagram of the control panel provides a functional map, requiring the use of 16 labeled components for system control and monitoring.\n\n[Page 21]\nThis document describes the operational controls and status indicators of a specialized piece of machinery (likely a high-frequency machining or cutting apparatus). The summary is organized by function, incorporating tables and highlighting critical operational data.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of Machine Controls and Operation\n\n### I. Overview of Status Monitoring Controls\n\nThe machine utilizes three primary indicators to provide real-time status of the machining process.\n\n| Control | Purpose | Displayed Data |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Indicator Light (1)** | Power Status | Indicates if the machine power is currently **on**. |\n| **Voltmeter (2)** | Voltage Monitoring | Displays the **machining gap voltage**. |\n| **Ammeter (3)** | Current Monitoring | Displays the **machining current**. |\n\n### II. Power and Energy Control (Key Data)\n\nThe machine provides precise control over the applied electrical power, specifically the voltage and the frequency of the pulse.\n\n#### A. Voltage Switch (4)\nThis switch is used to adjust the working voltage based on the required intensity of the operation.\n\n*   **High Voltage:** **90V** (Used for high-power operations).\n*   **Low Voltage:** **60V** (Used for lower-intensity operations).\n\n#### B. High Frequency Switch (8)\nThis control manages the pulse power output.\n*   **Function:** Turning this switch on allows the user to monitor and control the machine's pulse processing power using the Voltmeter.\n\n### III. Operational Controls and Procedure\n\nThe function of the machine relies on a series of sequenced controls, detailed below.\n\n#### A. Water Pump Switch (7) - **Pre-Operational Checklist**\nBefore starting the machine, mandatory checks must be completed:\n1.  Turn on the water pump.\n2.  Verify that the copper electrode is wet.\n3.  Check the supply of copper pipe water before commencing processing.\n\n#### B. Tool Switch (5) - **Process Activation and Setup**\nThis switch initiates and manages the entire machining process.\n\n**1. Startup Sequence:**\n*   Press the Tool Switch $\\rightarrow$ Motor starts $\\rightarrow$ Spindle starts.\n\n**2. Operational Adjustment:**\n*   **Potentiometer Control:** The potentiometers must be adjusted to ensure stable and flexible operation.\n*   **Spindle Rotation Control:** Adjusting the potentiometer controls the spindle's movement:\n    *   Potentiometer adjusted **to the left** $\\rightarrow$ Spindle rotates **upwards**.\n    *   Potentiometer adjusted **to the right** $\\rightarrow$ Spindle rotates **downwards**.\n*   **Warning:** The buzzers issue a loud noise when the copper pipe water is insufficient.\n\n#### C. Rotary Head Switch (6)\n*   **Function:** Controls the rotational movement of the head.\n*   **Action:** When the switch is pressed, the rotary motor starts to run.\n\n### IV. Key Operational Processes\n\n| Process Area | Switch/Control | Action / Status |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **General Operation** | **Tool Switch (5)** | Activates the entire machining process and controls spindle movement. |\n| **Rotary Motion** | **Rotary Head Switch (6)** | Initiates the rotational movement of the head. |\n| **Electrical Power** | **Voltage Switch (4)** | Selects operational voltage (90V or 60V). |\n| **Pulse Power** | **High Frequency Switch (8)** | Enables and monitors the machine's pulse processing capabilities. |\n\n***\n\n### 📐 Diagrammatic Description (Conceptual)\n\nWhile actual diagrams cannot be provided, the machine's console layout is conceptualized as a central panel organized around three key functional areas:\n\n1.  **Upper Panel (Status):** Located at the top, displaying Indicator Light, Voltmeter, and Ammeter.\n2.  **Mid-Panel (Controls):** Housing the main switches (Tool Switch, Rotary Head Switch, Voltage Switch) and the Water Pump Switch.\n3.  **Lower Panel (Adjustment/Input):** Containing the potentiometers for fine-tuning spindle and operational stability, and the High Frequency Switch.\n\n### ⚡ Summary of Key Data Points\n\n*   **High Voltage Setting:** 90V\n*   **Low Voltage Setting:** 60V\n*   **Critical Warning Signal:** Loud buzzer noise indicates insufficient copper pipe water.\n*   **Prerequisites:** Water pump must be on, and the copper electrode must be wet before processing starts.\n\n[Page 22]\nThis document provides a detailed summary and technical overview of the operating procedures, control systems, and key data associated with the machine, based on the provided operational manual segment.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Machine Control and Operation Summary\n\nThe machine utilizes multiple integrated switches and controls (Potentiometer, Knobs, Switches) to precisely manage machining parameters, power, and safety.\n\n### I. Key Operational Procedures\n\n| Section | Component | Function / Action | Purpose / Effect |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **9** | **Processing Switch** | Turn off the high-frequency switch $\\rightarrow$ Turn off the water pump. | Initiates the processing cycle. |\n| **10** | **Manual Switch** | Pressing Up or Down. | Controls the spindle height (rise/lower) and determines the machining effect. |\n| **14** | **Emergency Stop** | Release the knob. | Energizes the entire machine immediately (Safety feature). |\n\n### II. Detailed Control Systems and Parameters\n\n#### **[11] Potentiometer (Voltage & Current Control)**\n\nThe potentiometer is the primary control for regulating voltage and machining current. Its operation is based on rotational direction, providing fine-tuning control.\n\n**💡 Key Data: Voltage Range**\nThe processed voltage is generally stable between **20V and 25V**.\n\n**🗺️ Conceptual Diagram: Potentiometer Operation**\n*(Imagine a rotary dial with two defined paths)*\n\n| Direction | Action/Input | Effect on Voltage/Current | Physical Result |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Clockwise (CW)** | Rotation | Voltage **increases** $\\&$ Machining current **reduces**. | The gap voltage increases. |\n| **Counter-Clockwise (CCW)** | Rotation | Voltage **decreases** $\\&$ Machining current **increases**. | The gap voltage decreases. |\n\n***\n\n#### **[12] Parameter Adjustment Knob**\n\nThis knob is used to adjust fundamental machine processing parameters, which must be tailored based on the job requirements.\n\n**⚙️ Parameters Controlled:**\n*   Voltage\n*   Processing Materials\n*   Copper Tube Size (Diameter)\n\n**⚠️ Operational Guideline:** The selection of parameters depends critically on the **diameter of the copper tube** and the **processing materials**.\n\n***\n\n#### **[13] Amplifier Switch (Power Output)**\n\nThis switch manages the machine's power source and output.\n\n**🔢 Table: Amplifier Identification**\nThe amplifier switch has a total of **four** distinct identification slots (I, II, III, IV).\n\n| Slot | Identification | Notes |\n| :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **I** | Amplifier Identification | - |\n| **II** | Amplifier Identification | - |\n| **III** | Amplifier Identification | - |\n| **IV** | Amplifier Identification | - |\n\n**⚡ Key Power Data:**\n*   If the **number of power amplifier tubes** is used, the overall machining current **increases**, and thus the loss is also increased.\n*   All power amplifier tubes are **all turned off** when the machine is inactive.\n\n***\n\n### 📋 Summary of Controls and Functions\n\n| Control / Switch | Primary Function | Primary Control Action | Parameter Affected |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Potentiometer** | Voltage and Current Control | CW / CCW Rotation | Voltage (20-25V), Current |\n| **Parameter Knob** | Global Parameter Tuning | Manual Adjustment | Copper Tube Diameter, Material, Voltage |\n| **Amplifier Switch** | Power Management | Tube Selection (I-IV) | Overall Machining Power (Increased Current) |\n| **Manual Switch** | Spindle Height Control | Push Up / Push Down | Machining Depth / Spindle Position |\n| **Processing Switch** | Start/Stop Cycle Management | De-selection (Water Pump/High Frequency) | Processing State |\n| **Emergency Stop** | Safety Shutdown | Knob Release | Machine Power (Immediate Shutoff) |\n\n[Page 23]\nThis page provides detailed technical instructions and operational parameters for setting up and initiating a machining process, focusing on machine setup, coordinate verification, and safety procedures.\n\nBelow is a detailed, structured summary, including tables, descriptions of diagrams, and key data.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of Machine Operation and Setup\n\n### 1. Key Machine Functions and Display Areas\n\nThe machine interface includes specialized display and control areas:\n\n| Code | Function Name | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **[15]** | Light Ruler Function Area | (Reference area for ruler functions.) |\n| **[16]** | XY Axis Coordinate Display Area | Displays the precise coordinate position of the machining head on the X and Y axes. |\n\n***\n\n### 2. Gap Capacitance Measurement and Operation\n\nThis section addresses the relationship between capacitance values and the physical operation box.\n\n**Key Data:**\n*   $C_1$, $C_2$, and $C_3$ represent three distinct capacitance values.\n*   The side of the operation box corresponds to these capacitance readings.\n\n**Diagrammatic Representation (Conceptual Diagram):**\nThe diagrams illustrate three setups (C1, C2, C3) where the physical configuration relates to the measured capacitance.\n\n| Setup | Capacitance Value | Associated Action/Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **C1** | Low Capacitance | Represents the first measurement point. |\n| **C2** | Medium Capacitance | Represents the second measurement point. |\n| **C3** | High Capacitance | Represents the third measurement point. |\n\n**Important Note:** The button switch cannot be placed in the neutral position during processing.\n\n***\n\n### 3. 🛠️ Processing Order (The Setup Procedure)\n\nThe operational setup is divided into five mandatory steps, detailing how the machine must be prepared before actual machining begins.\n\n#### **6.1 Process Before Starts**\n\n| Step | Procedure | Key Action / Equipment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Placement of the work-piece** | Secure the piece to be machined. | Place the work-piece on the **table balance bar**. |\n| **2. Electrode installation** | Mount the tool electrode. | The **copper tube electrode** is mounted in the chuck, and then subsequently mounted onto the machined rotary head shaft. |\n| **3. Confirmation of working fluid** | Ensure the coolants/cutting fluid system is operational. | Turn on the pump and confirm that the working fluid can be **ejected normally** from the front end of the electrode. |\n| **4. Adjustment of machining position** | Calibrate the movement axes to the target machining zone. | **A. X & Y Axes:** Move to the machining position using the hand wheel. **B. W Axis:** Adjust the position so that the lower end of the guide maintains a distance of **3 to 5 mm** from the work-piece machining surface. |\n| **5. Processing condition setting** | Set initial parameters for the cycle. | Set the required **machining parameters** and turn the potentiometer to the **far left** position. |\n\n***\n\n### Summary of Key Operational Parameters\n\n*   **Safety Feature:** In an unexpected situation, press the knob to cut off the power.\n*   **Initial Workpiece Position:** On the table balance bar.\n*   **Critical Gap:** The lower end of the guide must be positioned **3–5 mm** from the work-piece surface.\n*   **Pre-Cycle Setting:** The potentiometer must be set to the **far left**.\n\n[Page 24]\nThis document provides detailed technical instructions for the setup, inspection, and preparation of the copper tube electrode for processing.\n\nBelow is a comprehensive summary, organized by section, incorporating the operational steps, technical details, and structural components.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Detailed Summary: Electrode Preparation and Processing Setup\n\nThe procedure begins with the initiation of processing (Section 6) and involves three major steps: setting the guide, inspecting the electrode, and preparing/cutting the electrode tube.\n\n### I. Operational Workflow (Section 6)\n\n| Step | Action | Detail |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Start** | Processing Begins | The initial step marks the beginning of the operational process. |\n| **Action** | Initial Setup | The guide must be set up on the rotary head, pump, and high-frequency machine before machining can start. |\n\n### II. Setting the Electrode Guide (Section 6.2)\n\n**Objective:** To correctly mount the guide to ensure proper electrode placement.\n\n*   **Requirement:** Use an electrode guide that is specifically sized for the diameter of the copper tube electrode.\n*   **Procedure:** Install the guide firmly into the designated hole on the guide plate.\n\n### III. Copper Tube Electrode Handling and Installation (Section 6.3)\n\nThis section is divided into inspection, assembly, and preparation/cutting.\n\n#### A. Electrode Inspection (Section 6.3 - part a)\n\nThe quality check is critical before assembly.\n\n1.  **Selection:** Select a copper tube electrode.\n2.  **Damage Check:** Ensure the electrode is free from damage or bends.\n3.  **Cleaning:** The electrode must be thoroughly inspected. A simple method is to open one end and blow into the other.\n4.  **Contamination Check:** Inspect for any blisters or debris inside the tube.\n    *   **Key Data:** If the blisters are appropriate (i.e., small, non-critical), no action is needed.\n\n#### B. Assembly and Installation (Diagram Reference)\n\nThe guide structure is assembled onto the machine using the parts shown in the schematic.\n\n**Components Identified:**\n*   Guide Plate\n*   Guide Lock Screw\n*   Electrode Director\n*   Copper Tube Electrode\n\n**Procedure:** The guide components are mounted to receive the copper tube electrode.\n\n#### C. Preparation and Cutting (Section 6.3 - part b)\n\nThis step details how the electrode is cut to the required length for processing.\n\n1.  **General Method:** If the copper tube electrode is suspected to be plugged or obstructed, special attention is required.\n2.  **Cleaning (Sludge/Blockage):** If the tube opening is blocked, gently turn the copper tube and cut the front end of the copper tube using a few millimeters of cutting action.\n3.  **Positioning:** The electrode must be held while cutting (cannot be held by bending or damaging the tube).\n4.  **Advanced Technique:** If the setup is still difficult, it is possible to install the copper tube onto the machine first, and then use a specialized jig or special scissors to cut the end of the copper tube by a few millimeters.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Schematic Diagram: Electrode Assembly\n\nThe operational setup relies on the following hardware components, mounted on the machine frame:\n\n**(Conceptual Diagram Reproduction based on the visual source)**\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Guide Plate] -->|Mounts To| B(Machine Base)\n    B --> C[Guide Lock Screw]\n    C --> D[Electrode Director]\n    D -->|Receives| E(Copper Tube Electrode)\n```\n\n**Key Data from Diagram:**\n*   **Guide Plate:** The mounting base.\n*   **Guide Lock Screw:** Fastens the guide components securely.\n*   **Electrode Director:** Holds and guides the electrode.\n*   **Copper Tube Electrode:** The primary functional component.\n\n## 📋 Summary of Key Technical Data\n\n| Aspect | Detail / Specification | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Guide Sizing** | Must match the diameter of the copper tube electrode. | Ensures proper fit and stable operation. |\n| **Inspection Data** | Check for blisters, blockages, or bends. | Prevents machine malfunction and ensures material integrity. |\n| **Cleaning Action** | Blow through the tube or use cutting action. | Removes debris and ensures full flow. |\n| **Cutting Precision** | Cut the end of the copper tube by **a few millimeters**. | Provides the precise working length for the process. |\n| **Handling Note** | Cannot be held by bending or damage the tube. | Mandatory safety and handling precaution. |\n\n[Page 25]\nThis page provides detailed instructions for the **(2) Electrode Mounting Method**, outlining the assembly and installation process for connecting an electrode copper tube to a motor chuck assembly.\n\nThe summary is structured into three parts: Component Identification, Detailed Procedure, and Critical Specifications/Cautions.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Overview: Electrode Mounting Method\n\nThis procedure details the sequence for mounting the copper tube electrode using specialized hardware (sealing rings, chucks, etc.) to ensure a secure and functional connection to the motor shaft.\n\n### 📑 I. Component Identification (Diagrams)\n\nThe assembly utilizes several key components:\n\n| Component Name | Function/Purpose | Key Feature |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **ELECTRODE COPPER TUBE** | The main electrode material. | Must be handled carefully (Do not bend). |\n| **SEALING RING** | Provides a seal, fitted around the copper tube. | Must be suitable for the specific diameter of the electrode. |\n| **ELECTRODE CHUCK** | The primary mounting assembly that holds the electrode. | Contains the central passage for connection. |\n| **KNURLED NUT** | Used for securing and adjusting the assembly. | Part of the tightening mechanism. |\n| **CONCENTRIC AXIS** | Provides alignment and structural support. | Forms part of the central connection path. |\n| **THREE-SIDED CLAW** | Used in the detailed chuck assembly view. | Indicates a specific gripping or mounting feature. |\n\n### 🛠️ II. Assembly Procedure (Step-by-Step)\n\nThe mounting process follows a specific sequence:\n\n1. **Sealing Ring Installation:**\n    *   Install the sealing ring onto the copper tube electrode, following the configuration shown in the diagrams.\n2. **Passing Through Chuck:**\n    *   Pass the electrode holder (including the electrode) from top to bottom through the center hole of the electrode chuck.\n3. **Final Assembly:**\n    *   The rest of the chuck components (Knurled Nut, Concentric Axis) are fitted around the assembled electrode shaft.\n4. **Integration (Motor Connection):**\n    *   The tapered head of the copper tube seal must fit upwards onto the inner tapered surface of the motor shaft, ensuring proper mechanical sealing.\n\n### 📈 III. Key Specifications and Operational Data\n\nThis section highlights mandatory dimensional requirements and safety precautions.\n\n#### A. Dimensional Requirements (Crucial Data)\n\n| Specification | Requirement | Purpose/Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sealing Ring Fit** | Must be suitable for the specific diameter of the copper tube electrode. | Ensures a tight, effective seal. |\n| **Upper End Length** | The upper end of the copper tube must **exceed 10 mm** of the sealing ring. | This is a minimum dimensional requirement for proper assembly or seating. |\n| **Tapered Fit** | The tapered head of the copper tube seal must fit onto the inner tapered surface of the motor shaft. | Ensures axial alignment and mechanical lock. |\n\n#### B. Operational Cautions (Safety/Maintenance)\n\n*   **Handling:** Be careful not to bend the copper tube electrode at any point during the operation.\n*   **Sealing:** Use a sealing ring that is appropriate for the exact diameter of the copper tube electrode.\n\n***\n\n### 📌 Summary Diagrammatic Flow (Conceptual)\n\n**Copper Tube Electrode** $\\xrightarrow[\\text{Install}]{\\text{Sealing Ring}}$ **(Electrode + Ring)** $\\rightarrow$ **(Pass Through)** $\\rightarrow$ **Electrode Chuck** $\\xrightarrow[\\text{Secure}]{\\text{Knurled Nut, Axis}}$ **Assembly** $\\xrightarrow[\\text{Fit}]{\\text{Tapered Head}}$ **Motor Shaft**\n\n**Minimum Length Check:** Copper Tube upper end $> 10 \\text{ mm}$ (measured from the sealing ring).\n\n[Page 26]\nThis document outlines detailed operational procedures for the installation, handling, and machining/polishing of electrodes and workpieces, typically related to precision electrical or mechanical machining.\n\nBecause the source text is highly procedural, the summary is presented using checklists, flow diagrams (described), and tables to maintain the structure and detail required for technical documentation.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Comprehensive Procedural Summary\n\n### I. Electrode Handling and Pre-Operation Checks\n\n**Critical Safety & Operational Warnings:**\n*   **Electrode Holder:** The electrode holder is a three-sided claw.\n*   **Copper Tube Handling:** Use extreme caution when handling the copper tube. Never force or bend the tube, as this can cause damage.\n*   **Operation Constraint:** When using the electrode chuck, ensure that the copper tube remains straight and undamaged.\n\n**Key Action Points:**\n*   **Initial Inspection:** Verify that the copper tube is attached to the electrode holder.\n*   **Mandatory Step:** Do not use force when operating the chuck; rely on proper alignment.\n\n### II. Electrode Chuck Installation and Operation (Steps 3)\n\nThis section details the physical setup of the electrode.\n\n| Step | Procedure | Key Action / Data | Warning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Setup** | Open the chucking switch and adjust the potentiometer. | Adjust the spindle to the lowest limit position at the fastest speed. | N/A |\n| **2. Chucking** | Hold the electrode chuck knurled nut. | **Action:** Turn the rotary motor switch on the right hand. | N/A |\n| **3. Sealing** | Position the electrode by turning the chuck nut. | **Action:** Wait until the rotary motor switch is fully attached and locked in place. | N/A |\n| **4. Operation** | Prepare for processing. | **Data:** The chuck can be secured using a special wrench for electrode holder setup. | N/A |\n| **5. Initial Test** | Check the copper tube rotation. | **Critical Check:** Confirm the copper tube can rotate freely and without strain. | Avoid over-forcing or damaging the tube. |\n\n### III. Work-Piece Installation (6.4)\n\nThis procedure focuses on securing the material to be processed.\n\n1.  **Surface Preparation:** Place the work-piece on the **stainless steel balance bar** or suitable surface.\n2.  **Clamping:** Secure the work-piece using a clamp.\n3.  **Alignment:** The magnetic table of the dial gauge must be attached to the lower end and cover of the spindle.\n4.  **Positioning:** After securing the work-piece, confirm the process setup by clamping the work-piece in place.\n\n### IV. Determining Processing Position (6.5)\n\nThis section describes the precise setup for the machining or polishing action.\n\n**Setup and Alignment (Initial Process):**\n1.  **Switching:** Open the tool setting switch, the rotary head switch, and the processing switch.\n2.  **Positioning:** Adjust the copper tube so that the copper electrode exceeds the lower end surface of the electrode guide by **20 to 30 mm**.\n3.  **Surface Cleaning:** The work-piece end face should be thoroughly cleaned before starting.\n4.  **Initial Check (Fluid/Contaminants):** Check the work-piece for contaminants (oil, water, garbage, etc.). If present, ensure the work-piece is wiped clean.\n\n**Operation Checklist (Machining/Polishing):**\n| Step | Action | Outcome / Focus | Critical Data |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Approach** | Move the work-piece into contact with the end face of the work-piece. | Confirmation of initial contact. | N/A |\n| **2. Contact Confirmation** | Observe the contact between the electrode and the workpiece. | Ensure the copper electrode is in contact with the work-piece surface. | N/A |\n| **3. Execution** | The contact buzzer will activate when the electrode and the work-piece are ready for the machining process. | Indicates the optimal moment to begin the process. | **Buzzer Activation** |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Key Data and Measurements Summary\n\n| Component / Measurement | Detail / Specification | Purpose / Significance |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Holder** | Three-sided claw design. | Mechanism for securing the electrode. |\n| **Copper Tube Damage** | Must avoid bending, tearing, or excessive force. | Critical preventative maintenance warning. |\n| **Electrode Guide Length** | Copper electrode must exceed guide by **20 to 30 mm**. | Ensures proper reach and process positioning. |\n| **Work-Piece Surface** | Use **stainless steel** balance bar. | Ensures stable and appropriate work-piece support. |\n| **Contaminants** | Oil, water, garbage, etc. | Must be **wiped clean** prior to processing to ensure accuracy. |\n| **Process Indicator** | Contact buzzer. | Signals when the electrode and work-piece are correctly positioned and ready for machining. |\n\n## 🔄 Process Flow Diagram (Descriptive)\n\n*(If a diagram were available, it would follow this sequence):*\n\n**[START] $\\rightarrow$ (Prepare Workpiece) $\\rightarrow$ (Install Electrode Chuck) $\\rightarrow$ (Check Parameters) $\\rightarrow$ (Adjust Electrode Position: 20-30mm) $\\rightarrow$ (Clean Workpiece) $\\rightarrow$ (Move Workpiece to End Face) $\\rightarrow$ (WaitFor Contact Buzzer) $\\rightarrow$ (Execute Machining/Polishing) $\\rightarrow$ [END]**\n\n[Page 27]\nThis page contains detailed operational instructions and a comprehensive parameter selection table, likely pertaining to a machine or process involving electrode movement and processing conditions.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including the key operational steps and the data presented in the table.\n\n---\n\n## 📖 Detailed Summary of Processing Operations\n\nThe text describes several procedural steps related to controlling the equipment and setting parameters:\n\n### Key Operational Steps and Procedures:\n\n*   **Initial Movement:** When discussing the "ring," the operation requires monitoring the XY axis.\n*   **XY Axis Control:** If the copper tube hits the end surface of the workpiece, the copper tube will bend. Therefore, when moving the XY axis to move the board, extreme care must be taken, paying close attention to the process.\n*   **Process Observation:** It is important to observe the process using the digital display.\n*   **Processing Angle:** The location of the end face is determined, and the copper tube is raised without colliding with the workpiece.\n*   **Final Action:** The process requires moving the XY axis to the workpiece processing position.\n\n### Processing Condition Setting:\n\nThe document specifies that processing parameters must be set according to:\n1.  The electrode diameter.\n2.  The material of the electrode workpiece.\n3.  The roughness of the machined surface.\n4.  The processing efficiency.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Processing Parameter Selection Table\n\nThe core of the page is a detailed table that provides selected parameters based on the electrode diameter ($\\text{mm}$) and specified processing environments.\n\n**Table Title:** Processing Parameter Selection Table\n**(For different processing environments, for reference only)**\n\n| Electrode Diameter ($\\text{mm}$) | Speed | Gear Position | Power Sub-type | Processing Gap Voltage ($\\text{V}$) | Processing Gap Current ($\\text{A}$) | Gap Capacitance | Recommended Process Condition |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.3$ | Low | 3 | 2 | 40 | 2 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.3$ | High | 4 | 3 | 25 | 4 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.3$ | Low | 3 | 3 | 25 | 5 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.5$ | Medium | 4 | 2 | 20 | 5 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.5$ | High | 3~4 | 5 | 15 | 8 | C1 | Low Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.5$ | Low | 3 | 3 | 15 | 7 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.7$ | Medium | 3 | 4 | 15 | 8 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}0.7$ | High | 5 | 3 | 15 | 8 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}1.0$ | Medium | 5 | 4 | 15 | 14 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}1.5$ | High | 6 | 5 | 15 | 14 | C1 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}2.0$ | High | 7 | 5 | 10 | 20 | C1C3 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}2.5$ | High | 9 | 7 | 10 | 25 | C2C3 | High Pressure |\n| $\\text{\\Phi}3.0$ | High | 9 | 8 | 10 | 30 | C2C3 | High Pressure |\n\n***Note:** The columns represent the following parameters:*\n*   **Speed:** Operational speed setting (e.g., Low, Medium, High).\n*   **Gear Position:** The numerical setting for the gear position.\n*   **Power Sub-type:** The specific power mode utilized.\n*   **Processing Gap Voltage ($\\text{V}$):** The required voltage setting.\n*   **Processing Gap Current ($\\text{A}$):** The required current setting.\n*   **Gap Capacitance:** The capacitance setting (e.g., C1, C2C3).\n*   **Recommended Process Condition:** The required pressure condition (Low Pressure or High Pressure).\n\n[Page 28]\nThis summary provides a detailed, structured breakdown of the technical procedures for **Machining** and the necessary steps for addressing **Abnormal Processing Concerns** related to the electrode guide.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Operational Summary: Machining Procedures & Maintenance\n\n### 6.7 Machining Procedure Checklist\n\nThe machining process is a sequential procedure involving parameter setting, activating multiple switches, and ensuring proper flow control.\n\n| Step No. | Action Required | Key Parameter / Component | Operational Detail |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | **Set Initial Parameters** | Machining Parameters | Set the appropriate parameters (e.g., speed, feed rate). |\n| **2** | **Activate Switches** | Power Switches | Turn on the rotary head switch, water pump switch, and high frequency switch. |\n| **3** | **Verify Flow & Discharge** | Cooling Water & Flushing | Ensure cooling water is activated. The water pressure must be maintained to initiate the processing switch. |\n| **4** | **Monitor Process Flow** | Electrode Guide / Flushing | Observe the system. If the workpiece is moved, the electrode must move simultaneously. Check for excessive debris buildup. |\n\n#### 🔑 Key Data Point (Critical Observation)\n*   **Workpiece Movement:** When moving the workpiece at the end of machining, it is crucial that the electrode moves simultaneously to maintain stability and prevent uneven finishing.\n\n***\n\n### ⚡ 7.0 Abnormal Processing Concerns\n\nThis section addresses the maintenance and checking of the equipment, specifically focusing on the electrode guide.\n\n#### 7.1 Electrode Director\n\n---\n\n#### 🛠️ 7.1.1 Electrode Guide Inspection (Cleaning & Checking)\n\nThe guide requires regular inspection to ensure proper material flow and prevent blockages.\n\n| Checkpoint | Inspection Area | Action / Procedure | Critical Goal |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **General Check** | Guide Surface | Inspect for burnt marks, nicks, or excessive material buildup. | Ensure guide integrity and smooth operation. |\n| **Cleaning (Debris)** | Guide Slots / Guides | Use fine sandpaper or gentle abrasion to remove foreign debris. | Maintain clear pathways for the copper tube electrode. |\n| **Verification** | Guide Bore | Confirm the bore size and fit by passing the copper tube electrode through it. | Guarantee the electrode moves smoothly and accurately. |\n\n---\n\n#### 🔩 7.1.2 Electrode Guide Fixing (Secure Installation)\n\nThe guide must be securely fastened to maintain dimensional accuracy.\n\n**Procedure:**\n1.  **Fixing Mechanism:** The electrode guide is secured using **stainless steel screws**.\n2.  **Torque/Force Management:** The force applied to the screws must be appropriate.\n    *   **WARNING:** If the force is too long, or if the guide is fixed too tightly, the following issues may occur:\n        *   The accuracy of the guide may be inaccurate.\n        *   The vertical hole of the hole may not be good.\n\n***\n\n## 📋 Summary of Key Operational Parameters\n\n| Parameter / Area | Condition / Goal | Problem Indication (What to look for) | Corrective Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Movement** | Continuous, Stable | Workpiece or electrode stalls; irregular discharge. | Check all switches (rotary, water, high frequency); ensure simultaneous movement. |\n| **Guide Surface** | Smooth, Clean | Burnt marks; residue; visible damage. | Sandpaper/Abrade the area; inspect for material stress. |\n| **Guide Fixing** | Secure, Optimal Tension | Guide wobbles; loose screws; vibration. | Re-torque stainless steel screws; ensure force is sufficient but not excessive. |\n| **System Flow** | Constant pressure & flow | Low water pressure; intermittent process switch activation. | Verify the water pump switch is fully functional and operating correctly. |\n\n[Page 29]\nThis document provides detailed instructions and quality control checklists for the setup, adjustment, and processing of copper tube electrodes, focusing specifically on achieving perpendicularity and ensuring stable operations.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, incorporating tables and describing the necessary diagram.\n\n***\n\n## ⚙️ Summary of Electrode Setup and Processing Guide\n\n### **Diagram Reference**\n\nThe central mechanism shown is the **Perpendicularity Adjustment Method of Electrode Guide**, which utilizes a **Vertical Adjustment Screw**. This diagram illustrates how the electrode guide structure can be precisely adjusted in two axes (X and Y) to ensure the electrode is properly aligned and perpendicular to the work piece.\n\n### **(3) Perpendicularity Adjustment Method of Electrode Guide**\n\nThis section details the procedure for physically adjusting the guide mechanism to ensure proper perpendicularity.\n\n#### **Step-by-Step Adjustment Procedure**\n\n| Step | Action | Goal/Focus |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(a)** | **Electrode Protrusion Check** | Verify the electrode protrusion depth. |\n| **(b)** | **Guide Block Positioning** | Use a square or right-angled block to position the electrode guide. |\n| **(c)** | **Final Fastening** | Tighten all vertical adjustment screws. |\n\n#### **Key Data and Measurements (Table 1)**\n\n| Parameter | Specification / Dimension | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Protrusion (Minimum)** | 40 to 50 mm | Depth of protrusion into the lower end surface of the guide. |\n| **Electrode Diameter ($\\Phi$)** | 1.5 mm | The size requirement for the electrode protrusion. |\n| **Electrode Holder Diameter ($\\Phi$)** | 12 mm | The size of the shaft/holder. |\n| **Adjustment Axis** | X-direction and Y-direction | Adjustment is made by utilizing the vertical adjustment screw to adjust the distance between the square and the guide. |\n\n---\n\n### **Operational Quality Checks**\n\nThe document includes two crucial sections for mandatory pre-process and post-process inspections: Unstable Processing and Copper Tube Electrode Bending.\n\n### **7.2 Unstable Processing Checkpoints (Mandatory Checklist)**\n\nBefore processing, the following seven points must be confirmed to ensure stable and efficient operation:\n\n1.  **Confirmatory Check:** Process all guiding conditions.\n2.  **Fluid Check:** Ensure that the working fluid is ejected from the front end of the copper pipe.\n3.  **Electrode Status:** Verify that the copper tube is not bent or eccentric.\n4.  **Workpiece Fixture:** Confirm that the fixture of the workpiece is fixed.\n5.  **Guide Integrity:** Confirm that the installation of the copper tube electrode, especially the electrode holder, is **not abnormal**.\n6.  **Electrode Wear:** Confirm that the copper tube electrode is **not abnormally consumed** (excessively worn).\n7.  **Alignment:** Check that the installation position of the electrode is verifiable.\n\n### **7.3 Copper Tube Electrode Bending Checkpoints (Mandatory Checklist)**\n\nThese three points ensure the setup is correctly aligned for bending operations:\n\n1.  **Process Confirmation:** Confirm all processing conditions are met.\n2.  **Connection Check:** Ensure there is no insulation between the copper tube electrode and the workpiece.\n3.  **Wire Connection:** Check if the workpiece and electrode output wires are connected.\n\n***\n***\n**Summary Conclusion:** The guide mandates a precise, multi-step adjustment process using a vertical adjustment screw to achieve perpendicularity. Furthermore, it establishes critical safety and operational checklists focusing on measurement specifications (40-50 mm, $\\Phi$ 1.5 mm), guide integrity, electrode wear, and proper electrical connectivity to guarantee successful and stable processing.\n\n[Page 30]\nThis document provides a detailed technical guide focusing on the operational procedures, maintenance, and troubleshooting of electro-discharge machining (EDM) using copper tube electrodes.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Summary Overview: EDM with Copper Electrodes\n\nThe primary focus is ensuring optimal machining performance by adhering to strict procedural checks regarding the electrode, the machining environment, and the processing speed.\n\n### 1. Operational Checks and Setup Requirements\n\nOperational efficiency depends on maintaining proper spacing, preventing blockages, and ensuring adequate cooling fluid.\n\n| Check Point | Condition/Requirement | Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Alignment** | Ensure there is **not too much space** between the electrode copper tube and the workpiece. | Adjust setup to maintain close, stable contact. |\n| **Blockage** | Confirm that the copper tube electrode is **not blocked**. | Clean or inspect the electrode thoroughly. |\n| **Working Fluid** | The working fluid must be sprayed **smoothly** and adequately. | Check fluid pressure and nozzle integrity. |\n| **Electrode Length** | The copper tube electrode must **not extend** out of the electric chuck too long. | Adjust the electric chuck setting to the proper depth. |\n\n### 2. Troubleshooting Operational Issues\n\nThe document details specific issues that necessitate procedural checks:\n\n*   **7.4 Abnormal Discharge:** Requires checking processes and ensuring correct operational conditions.\n*   **7.5 Raise The Knife Sharply:** General process confirmation is required.\n*   **7.6 Low Processing Speed:** Indicates issues with fluid delivery and electrode setup.\n\n### 3. Key Procedural Guidelines: Electrode Use (7.5)\n\nCare must be taken when using and inspecting the copper electrode to ensure consistent performance:\n\n1.  **Initial Check:** Confirm the general processes are followed.\n2.  **Electrode Condition:** The copper tube electrode itself must be confirmed to be operational.\n3.  **Damage/Wear Warning:** If the copper tube is processed again, special attention is required, as repeated processing might result in the tube being **too long** or having **damaged ends**.\n4.  **Minimum Specification (Key Data):** For single-hole type copper tube electrodes, the minimum functional diameter ($\\Phi$) must be **$0.1 \\text{mm}$ or more**.\n\n### 4. Diagram and Physical Evidence\n\nThe provided diagram illustrates the result and components involved in the machining process:\n\n**[Diagram: Single Hole Copper Tube Electrode vs. Workpiece]**\n\n*   **Components:** The diagram shows a **Single Hole Copper Tube Electrode** being used to machine a **Workpiece**.\n*   **Resulting Feature:** The area between the electrode and workpiece shows **Processing Residue**, indicating the material being removed or machined.\n\n### 5. Key Data and Safety Notes\n\n*   **Minimum Diameter:** $\\Phi \\geq 0.1 \\text{mm}$ (for single-hole type electrodes).\n*   **Handling Protocol:** The text advises that if the copper tube is spotted with water, it will *easily cause* the copper tube to discharge water or open the polarity switch, necessitating immediate corrective action.\n*   **Purpose of Observation:** These protocols are crucial because neglecting them can lead to **machining residue** and **unstable processing speed**.\n\n[Page 31]\nThis document is an excerpt from a highly technical maintenance and inspection manual, detailing troubleshooting steps for machine movement, mandatory lubrication schedules, and general electrical and fluid system checks for a machining tool.\n\nSince the source material is purely text, the summary will organize the information using detailed tables and highly structured checklists to fulfill the requirement of including "tables and key data."\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Detailed Summary of Machine Maintenance and Inspection\n\n### 📍 I. Operational Checks (General Observations)\n\nThese are critical checks for identifying immediate physical discrepancies or failures:\n\n*   The copper tube and the electric chuck must be confirmed as **identical** components.\n*   The electrode guide must be confirmed to be **perpendicular**.\n\n### 🔴 II. Troubleshooting: W-Axis Movement Failure (7.7)\n\nIf the W-Axis cannot move Up and Down, the following five potential causes must be checked systematically:\n\n| # | Component/System Checked | Action Required/Confirmation |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | Three-Phase Power Supply | Confirm whether the power is **out of phase**. |\n| **2** | Electrical System | Confirm that the three-phase electrical **phase sequence is correct**. |\n| **3** | Manual Controls | Check if the manual up and down buttons are in **poor contact** or if the contacts are off. |\n| **4** | Limit Switch / Machine Status | Confirm if the limit switch is **damaged** or if the machine is stuck. |\n| **5** | Intermediate Relay | Confirm whether the relay works normally or if the **conduction is poor**. |\n\n***\n\n### 🔧 III. Mandatory Maintenance and Inspection (Section 8)\n\n#### A. 💧 8.1 Lubrication Parts of the Machine Tool (Key Data Table)\n\nThe following table details the specific components requiring lubrication, the required material, amount, and frequency/method of application.\n\n| Lubrication Position | Lubricating Oil Type | Quantity/Amount | Application Frequency/Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Worldbench Carriage Guide Plane** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Drag Plate Screw And Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Z-Axis Linear Guide, Lead Screw and Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n| **Machine Head Secondary Screw Rod and Nut** | Rail Oil | Moderate amount | Hand pump automatic injection (1 time / 100 hours) |\n\n#### B. 🔩 8.2 General Machine Checks (Inspection Checklist)\n\nThis section outlines detailed inspection points for the machine's core systems.\n\n**1. Power Distribution Connector**\n*   **Check Point:** Verify the connection of the three-phase power cable to the grid and ensure the machine is properly secured/fixed.\n\n**2. Electrical Installation**\n*   **Wiring Check:** Inspect all contact wiring for looseness.\n*   **Component Integrity:** Inspect for signs of ablation marks on the contact points.\n*   **High-Current Component Check:** Inspect capacitors, shunts, work-pieces, electrode wires, and other high-current components for any looseness.\n*   **Switch/Plug Check:** Verify the switch usage panel components and ensure the plug-in connection is tight and the connection is thoroughly eliminated of any looseness.\n\n**3. Water Circulation Device**\n*   **System Requirement:** Since the machining fluid operates under high pressure, the entire water circulation system is high-requirement.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** Check the **looseness of all joints** during the inspection process.\n\n***\n\n### 📝 Summary Key Data Points\n\n*   **Preventative Maintenance Cycle:** Critical components (Worldbench, Z-Axis, etc.) require lubrication using **Rail Oil** at a **Moderate amount**.\n*   **Standard Lubrication Schedule:** $1$ injection per $100$ operating hours.\n*   **Primary Failure Points:** Electrical phase sequence errors, poor contact in manual buttons, and high-pressure joint looseness.\n*   **Critical Materials:** Rail Oil.\n*   **System Requirement:** Maintaining electrical integrity (tight connections, no ablation) and high-pressure fluid system integrity.\n\n[Page 32]\nThis document is an excerpt from a technical manual, likely detailing operating and maintenance procedures for specialized industrial or laboratory equipment (given the focus on working fluids, ventilation, and electromechanical components).\n\nAs the source material is only text, I cannot include actual diagrams or highly structured data tables. However, I have summarized the information in a highly detailed, organized format using tables and lists to categorize the critical procedures, maintenance cycles, and warnings.\n\n***\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary of Operational and Maintenance Procedures\n\n### 💧 Section 8.2: Piping and Fluid Flow (Influent/Effluent)\n\nThis section focuses on inspecting the system's plumbing and flow paths (the flume).\n\n| Component | Inspection Focus | Action/Warning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Piping/Flume** | Structural Integrity | Check the flume/pipe for leaks, damage, or deterioration. |\n| **Flow Monitoring** | Influent to Effluent | Ensure the flow path from the influent to the effluent is properly monitored. |\n\n### 💨 Section 8.3: Machine Ventilation Inspection\n\nRegular inspection of the ventilation system is critical for machine longevity and safety.\n\n*   **Procedure:** Check the cooling fan regularly.\n*   **Inspection Focus:** Check the fan and the overall ventilation system.\n*   **Timing:** Inspection must be performed **every 1 to 3 months**.\n\n### 🚰 Section 8.4: Working Fluid Management\n\nThis section governs the type, usage, and replenishment of the fluid used in the process.\n\n#### **1. Fluid Specifications (Working Fluid)**\n*   **Type:** Use **tap water or distilled water** and special machining fluid.\n*   **Purpose:** The fluid must be appropriate for small hole machines.\n*   **Caution:** Do **NOT** use tap water directly into the filler pipe if hard mineral deposits are a risk.\n\n#### **2. Fluid Depletion and Replenishment**\n*   **Observation:** Mineral buildup/crystallization (effluent) is expected, especially in the high-pressure pipe within the column.\n*   **Mitigation:** Always replenish the working fluid.\n*   **Warning:** The fluid will decrease during processing, and evaporation will occur.\n\n#### **3. Fluid Contamination**\n*   **Prohibited Action:** Do not let process water or distilled water accumulate or mix with the machine's circulating fluid.\n*   **Caution:** The user must ensure that **tap water is not directly collected from underground sources** (potential for contamination).\n\n### 🧼 Section 8.5: Cleaning of Various Components\n\nThis section provides specific, cyclical cleaning protocols for various machine parts.\n\n#### **A. Table and Operation Panel (Daily Maintenance)**\n*   **Action:** The table, waste tray, and operation panel must be cleaned **daily**.\n*   **Caution:** Ensure that the panel is easily soiled.\n\n#### **B. Electrical and Connectors (Periodic Maintenance)**\n*   **Circuit Board:** If the circuit board needs cleaning, it must be done *before* the machine is used.\n*   **Connectors:**\n    *   **Requirement:** Check the circuit board/connectors regularly.\n    *   **Warning:** If the board is soiled, cleaning must be done in a specific, controlled manner, as indicated by the manufacturer.\n*   **High Voltage:** The main control power must be turned off **before** performing any electrical inspections or cleaning.\n\n#### **C. Working Fluid Components (Cyclical Cleaning)**\n| Component | Cleaning Frequency | Key Procedure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Filter** | Once a week | Check the filter inside the inlet pipe. |\n| **Working Fluid Line** | Once a week | Check the fluid line itself. |\n| **General Area** | Periodically | Ensure that discharge lines for chips and shavings are properly cleaned and kept free of foreign matter. |\n\n#### **D. Carriage Base and Movement (Operational Care)**\n*   **Purpose:** Preventing rust and ensuring smooth movement.\n*   **Cleaning:** Clean the carriage base regularly.\n*   **Movement Direction:** When cleaning, sweep the Y-axis *away* from the column, and remove the base (the dirty area) before storing or moving.\n\n***\n\n### 🔑 Key Summary Points (Action Checklist)\n\n*   **Daily:** Clean the table, waste tray, and operation panel.\n*   **Weekly:** Inspect and clean the filter and the working fluid lines.\n*   **Monthly:** Inspect the machine ventilation fan (1 to 3 months).\n*   **Pre-Use/Before Service:** Turn off the main power supply, and clean/inspect circuit boards and connectors.\n*   **Safety Priority:** Always maintain physical cleanliness to prevent chip accumulation and ensure smooth movement of the carriage base.\n*   **Fluid Handling:** Use distilled/tap water (if suitable) and ensure constant replenishment to prevent operational slowdown due to evaporation or buildup.\n\n[Page 33]\nThis page is a technical manual segment detailing routine machine maintenance and, most extensively, a detailed **Troubleshooting Process** for mechanical failures, particularly concerning the operating ability of a rotating head.\n\nHere is a detailed summary, including structural tables and flow diagrams, outlining the necessary procedures.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Detailed Summary of Page Content\n\n### 1. Routine Maintenance and Cleaning Procedures\n\nThe text outlines mandatory cleaning and lubrication steps for optimal machine function.\n\n| Item | Area to Be Cleaned | Procedure / Action | Key Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **(3)** | Head Storage Tube | In order to maintain full lubrication of the second stroke of the machine head, the oil storage pipe must be installed in the lower part of the screw. | **Action:** Check and clean once a month. Refill the right amount of oil. |\n| **(4)** | Rotating Head & Guide Plate | This cleaning is crucial as the machine component is installed. | **Caution:** Do not let processing speed be affected by poor conductivity; also be sure to keep it clean. |\n\n***\n\n### 2. ⚙️ Section 9: Troubleshooting Process (Flow Diagram)\n\nThis section is the core of the page, presenting a step-by-step diagnostic flow chart. If the rotating head fails to operate, the user must follow these sequential checks to diagnose the source of the problem.\n\n#### 🔹 Troubleshooting Flowchart Logic (Decision Tree)\n\nThe process starts with the symptom: **"Rotating head does not work."**\n\n| Step | Diagnostic Question (Check) | Result: NO (Failure Found) | Result: YES (System OK) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1** | **Is the connector in good contact?** | **Action:** Check and fix the motor connector, driver connector, and pulse power board connector. | Proceed to Step 2. |\n| **2** | **Is the indicator light normal?** | **Action:** If the light is off, check the **power board** and **pulse power board**. | Proceed to Step 3. |\n| **3** | **Is the rotary switch on and off normally?** | **Action:** Replace the rotary switch. | Proceed to Step 4. |\n| **4** | **Does the motor have a sound?** | **Action:** Replace the motor. | Proceed to Step 5. |\n| **5** | **Motor speed is abnormal or stuck?** | **Diagnosis:** The issue is physical/mechanical. | **END.** (The motor speed is operating correctly). |\n\n#### ⚡️ Key Diagnostic Data for Motor Speed Issues\n\nIf the motor speed is abnormal or stuck (Step 5 failure), the potential causes are categorized as follows:\n\n1.  **Poor Conductivity:** The connector is not in good contact.\n2.  **Mechanical Wear:** Motor bearing wear.\n\n***\n\n### 3. 🔑 Summary of Key Data and Components\n\nThis table compiles all the technical components and specific actions mentioned throughout the page.\n\n| System/Component | Potential Problem | Diagnostic Action (Check) | Repair/Solution (Action) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Power/Signal** | Loose Electrical Connection | Check connector status (Step 1). | Fix motor connector, driver connector, pulse power board connector. |\n| **Electrical System** | Operational Fault | Check indicator light status (Step 2). | Check power board and pulse power board. |\n| **Control Input** | Mechanical Failure | Check rotary switch function (Step 3). | Replace the rotary switch. |\n| **Motor Unit** | Complete Failure | Listen for sound (Step 4). | Replace the motor. |\n| **Motor Operation** | Abnormal/Stuck Speed | Observe physical operation (Step 5). | Inspect for poor conductivity (connector issue) or replace due to motor bearing wear. |\n\n### 💡 Summary Checklist (Flow Chart Logic)\n\nTo summarize the overall process, troubleshooting must follow this strict order:\n\n1.  **Electrical Check** (Connector $\\rightarrow$ Indicator Light $\\rightarrow$ Switch).\n2.  **Motor Check** (Sound $\\rightarrow$ Speed).\n3.  **Mechanical Check** (Bearing/Connection).\n\n[Page 34]\nThis page represents a detailed **Troubleshooting Flowchart** for diagnosing the mechanical and electrical issues associated with a spindle or motor system that is experiencing difficulty moving up and down.\n\nThe summary is structured into an **Overview,** a **Diagnostic Flowchart Table,** and a **Key Findings Data Sheet** to meet the requirement for tables and detailed information.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Summary: Spindle Movement Diagnostic Guide\n\n### 🔍 Overall Purpose\n\nThis guide outlines a systematic, step-by-step process to isolate the root cause when the primary failure symptom is: **"The spindle cannot move up and down."** The checks range from simple physical inspections (loose cables, debris) to complex electrical diagnostics (voltage, board processing).\n\n### 📊 Diagnostic Flowchart Table\n\nThe most effective way to understand this process is through a structured decision table:\n\n| Step | Condition Tested | Outcome (YES) | Outcome (NO) | Next Step |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **START** | **Spindle cannot move up and down** | N/A | Proceed to Check 1 | N/A |\n| **Check 1** | Is the connector loose? | (Action needed: Tighten/Repair) | Proceed to Check 2 | Check 2 |\n| **Check 2** | Is the servo motor voltage abnormal? | **ACTION:** Ensure the speed adjustment button is not left too far to the left during machining. | Proceed to Check 3 | Check 3 |\n| **Check 3** | Is the connector loose? | (Action needed: Inspect/Repair specific connector) | Proceed to Check 4 | Check 4 |\n| **Check 4** | Is the servo motor voltage abnormal? | (Further specific action/diagnosis required based on preceding check) | Proceed to Check 5 | Check 5 |\n| **Check 5** | Is the limit switch damaged? | (Action needed: Replace/Repair limit switch) | Proceed to Check 6 | Check 6 |\n| **Check 6** | Is the servo board processing abnormal? | **DIAGNOSIS:** (See Key Data for details on voltage/board status). | Proceed to Check 7 | Check 7 |\n| **Check 7** | Is the motor hot? | **CAUSE:** 1. Motor is bad. 2. Motor leakage. | Proceed to Check 8 | Check 8 |\n| **Check 8** | Are there interferences in each part of the main shaft? | **ACTION:** 1. Coupling loose. 2. Clearing interference (Debris removal). | **SUCCESS:** Spindle should move freely. | Diagnosis Complete |\n\n***\n\n### ⚡ Key Data and Electrical Diagnostics (Check 6 Details)\n\nIf the investigation reaches **Check 6 (Servo Board Processing)**, specific electrical components must be checked:\n\n#### 1. High Voltage/Power Diagnostics\n\n| Component Checked | Condition/Value | Action/Diagnostic Check |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Servo Board** | Power Supply (DC Voltage) | Check if the servo board receives **DC32V**. |\n| **Key Components** | Capacitors, Voltage Transformer | Visually inspect for damage. |\n| **Indicator Status** | H3 and H4 | Check if H3 and H4 are *not* lit under normal conditions. |\n| **Power Output** | Supply Signal | Confirm that the AC output is normal. |\n\n#### 2. Servo Motor Processing Issues\n\n| Condition | Diagnosis/Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Failure Mode 1** | Check the primary motor connector and servo board connector for proper fixing. |\n| **Failure Mode 2** | Check the discharge resistance (This area may need specialized testing). |\n| **Failure Mode 3** | If the board is faulty, the **servo board must be replaced** for maintenance. |\n\n***\n\n### 🚧 Step-by-Step Troubleshooting Summary\n\n#### 1. Physical Inspection (Mechanical Blockage)\n*   **Focus:** Check the coupling and main shaft for external resistance.\n*   **Symptom Found:** Spindle jamming.\n*   **Action:** Check for loose coupling or foreign debris/interference on the main shaft.\n\n#### 2. Electrical Checks (Voltage & Power)\n*   **Focus:** Servo motor and power supply integrity.\n*   **Key Alert:** If the servo voltage is abnormal, the machine operator must be warned to ensure the speed adjustment button is not repeatedly moved to the far left during operation.\n\n#### 3. System Component Checks (Limit Switches & Boards)\n*   **Limit Switch:** If suspected of damage, it must be replaced.\n*   **Servo Board:** This is the most complex diagnostic step. Check for:\n    *   Proper **DC32V** input power.\n    *   Correct indicator status (H3 and H4 must be off).\n    *   If the board is the source of the fault, replacement is necessary.\n\n#### 4. Motor Status Check\n*   **Motor Hot:** If the motor is overheated, the cause is likely a bad motor unit or internal electrical leakage.\n\n### 💡 Summary of Key Action Points\n\n*   **Initial Fixes:** Always start by checking loose cables and physical debris (Coupling/Shaft).\n*   **Voltage Criticality:** The stability and correct reading of **DC32V** for the servo board is a critical point.\n*   **Failure Hierarchy:** The troubleshooting moves systematically from *Mechanical* ($\\rightarrow$ Shaft/Coupling) to *Power* ($\\rightarrow$ Voltage/Connectors) to *Electronic* ($\\rightarrow$ Board/Switches).\n\n[Page 35]\nThis document is a detailed troubleshooting guide, presented in a flowchart format, designed to diagnose operational issues related to a high-pressure fluid system (likely involving a working fluid, pump, and electric motor).\n\nBelow is a detailed summary, organized by diagnostic phase, using tables and structured text for maximum clarity.\n\n---\n\n## 🔧 Detailed Troubleshooting Summary\n\nThe guide initiates when the primary symptom is observed: **The working fluid is not normally sprayed.**\n\n### I. Initial System Checks (Motor and Pump)\n\nThe guide first verifies the mechanical integrity of the system components.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Motor Rotation** | Is the motor rotating normally? | If NO: 1. The fuse is burnt out. 2. The contactor is broken. 3. The motor is broken. |\n| **Pump Sound** | Does the pump have a rotary sound? | If NO: 1. The shaft connection key is detached or worn seriously. 2. The pump is broken. |\n| **Pump Maintenance** | *(General Advice)* | **⚠️ Important:** Please don't forget to change the pump oil regularly. |\n\n### II. Operational Line Checks (Water and Pressure)\n\nThese steps verify the status of the fluid lines and required pressure levels.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **High Pressure Line** | Is the high pressure water line normal? | If NO: Check the joints for water leakage. |\n| **Pressure Output** | Does the working fluid pressure reach the set pressure? | If NO: 1. Adjust the built-in and external pressure regulator to the set value of **6.0 MPa**. 2. Working fluid with tap water or distilled water. |\n\n### III. Filtration and Fluid Supply Checks\n\nThese sections focus on the blockages and availability of the fluid.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Inlet Filter** | Whether the inlet pipe filter element are blocked? | If YES: Clean the filter and filter. |\n| **Working Fluid Level** | Is there enough working fluid? | If NO: 1. Fill the working fluid. 2. Whether the inlet filter sinks to the bottom of the container. |\n\n### IV. Final Component Check (Electrode)\n\nThe final operational check focuses on the primary spraying component.\n\n| Condition Checked | Symptom/Question | Diagnosis / Action Required |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Electrode Hole** | Whether the copper tube electrode hole is blocked? | If YES: Special scissors cut the front of the copper tube electrode. |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Summary Flowchart Diagram (Conceptual)\n\nThis structure represents the troubleshooting logic flow:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Start: Working Fluid is Not Normally Sprayed] --> B{Is the motor rotating normally?};\n    B -- No --> B1(Check: Fuse, Contactor, Motor);\n    B -- Yes --> C{Does the pump have a rotary sound?};\n    C -- No --> C1(Check: Shaft Key, Pump);\n    C -- Yes --> D{Is the high pressure water line normal?};\n    D -- No --> D1(Action: Check joints for leakage);\n    D -- Yes --> E{Does the working fluid pressure reach set pressure?};\n    E -- No --> E1(Action: Adjust regulator to 6.0 MPa);\n    E -- Yes --> F{Are the inlet pipe filter element blocked?};\n    F -- Yes --> F1(Action: Clean filter);\n    F -- No --> G{Is there enough working fluid?};\n    G -- No --> G1(Action: Fill fluid; Check filter sinking);\n    G -- Yes --> H{Is the copper tube electrode hole blocked?};\n    H -- Yes --> H1(Action: Cut electrode with scissors);\n    H -- No --> I[End: System operational];\n```\n\n***\n\n### Key Data and Action Points\n\n| Area | Critical Data / Value | Action Priority |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pressure Adjustment** | **6.0 MPa** (Standard Set Value) | High (If pressure is low, this is the required adjustment). |\n| **Maintenance** | Routine oil changes | Critical (Must be done regularly). |\n| **Motor Faults** | Fuse, Contactor, Motor replacement | Electrical Checks (Follow specific unit failure diagnosis). |\n| **Fluid Faults** | Inlet filter cleaning; Filling fluid | Physical Checks (Ensure proper fluid level and filtration). |\n\n[Page 36]\nThis page is a detailed troubleshooting flowchart designed to diagnose the root cause of **Insufficient Processing Performance** on a machine tool. The flow follows a systematic series of checks, starting with general operational parameters and progressing to specific mechanical components.\n\nDue to the nature of the content (a sequential decision tree), the summary is best presented using a structured process flow diagram and detailed tables.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Detailed Summary of Troubleshooting Flowchart\n\n**Main Problem:** Insufficient Processing Performance\n\n### I. Process Flow Overview (Conceptual Diagram)\n\nThe troubleshooting process is a multi-stage decision tree:\n\n1.  **Start:** Insufficient Processing Performance\n2.  **Check 1:** Processing Conditions $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n3.  **Check 2:** Workpiece Suitability $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n4.  **Check 3:** Working Fluid Pressure $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n5.  **Check 4:** Electrode Condition $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n6.  **Check 5:** Workpiece Discharge $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n7.  **Check 6:** Rotating Head Conduction $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n8.  **Check 7:** Spindle Abnormality $\\rightarrow$ (Yes/No)\n9.  **End:** Identified Root Cause and Corrective Action\n\n---\n\n### II. Troubleshooting Steps and Key Data (Table Format)\n\nThe following tables detail each decision point, the associated corrective action for "No" or "Yes" outcomes, and the key findings.\n\n#### 1. Operational and Setup Checks\n\n| Decision Point | Condition Check | Outcome (Answer) | Action / Observation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Processing Conditions** | Were the processing conditions normal? | **N (No)** | View the machining parameters to determine the set value. |\n| **Workpiece Suitability** | Was the workpiece suitable? | **N (No)** | *(No explicit action given for "No", implies reviewing standards).* |\n| **Super Hard Material** | Was the workpiece suitable (Super Hard)? | **Yes (Y)** | 1. The same materials come in different categories and may produce different performance than the data. |\n| | | | 2. Super hard materials should be chosen from copper. |\n\n#### 2. Fluid and Tooling Checks\n\n| Decision Point | Condition Check | Outcome (Answer) | Action / Observation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Working Fluid Pressure** | Is the working fluid pressure normal? | **N (No)** | 1. Adjust to the set value. 2. The working fluid is tap water or distilled water. |\n| **Electrode Condition** | Is the electrode abnormal? | **N (No)** | *(No explicit action given for "No").* |\n| | | **Y (Yes)** | 1. The electrode is bent or deflected too much. 2. The electrode quality is poor. |\n\n#### 3. Mechanical and System Checks\n\n| Decision Point | Condition Check | Outcome (Answer) | Action / Observation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Workpiece Discharge** | Was the abnormal workpiece discharge addressed? | **N (No)** | *(No explicit action given for "No").* |\n| | | **Y (Yes)** | 1. The electrode wire and the workpiece wire are firmly fixed. 2. The workpiece is in full contact with the balance bar and clamped. |\n| **Rotating Head Conduction** | Is the rotating head conduction abnormal? | **N (No)** | *(No explicit action given for "No").* |\n| | | **Y (Yes)** | 1. The carbon brush is in good contact with the collector ring. |\n| **Spindle Abnormality** | Is spindle abnormality present? | **N (No)** | *(No explicit action given for "No").* |\n| | | **Y (Yes)** | 1. Servo board aging. 2. Mechanical failure. |\n\n---\n\n### III. Summary of Key Data and Findings\n\nThis troubleshooting guide covers seven main areas of potential failure:\n\n1.  **Parameters/Setup:** Checking if the machining settings need adjustment (if conditions are not normal).\n2.  **Material/Workpiece:** Guidance on handling super hard materials (preferring copper sources).\n3.  **Coolant/Fluids:** Ensuring proper pressure and confirming fluid type (tap or distilled water).\n4.  **Electrodes:** Diagnosing issues like bending, deflection, or poor quality.\n5.  **Workpiece Fixation:** Checking that both the electrode and the workpiece are properly fixed and clamped to the balance bar.\n6.  **Electrical Components:**\n    *   **Rotating Head:** Ensuring the **carbon brush** makes good contact with the **collector ring**.\n    *   **Spindle:** Identifying potential failure points: **Servo board aging** or **Mechanical failure**.\n\n**Key Technical Recommendations:**\n\n*   **Super Hard Materials:** Use materials sourced from **copper**.\n*   **Electrode Status:** Check for **bent/deflected** state or **poor quality**.\n*   **Workpiece Clamping:** Must be in **full contact** with the balance bar and **clamped**.\n*   **Power Source:** If spindle failure is suspected, investigate **servo board aging** or **mechanical failure**.\n\n[Page 37]\nThis page presents two highly detailed electrical schematics: the **10. ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM** (a general power distribution map) and the **Panel Wiring Diagram** (a detailed, internal control and signal flow map).\n\nThe purpose of these diagrams is to illustrate the complete electrical assembly, detailing how power is distributed, how signals are processed, and how various control modules are interconnected to operate the system.\n\n***\n\n## 🔌 1. Summary of the Electrical Wiring Diagrams\n\n### A. 10. ELECTRICAL WIRING DIAGRAM (System Overview)\n\nThis diagram provides a high-level view of the system's power flow, demonstrating how external power sources are routed through protective and switching elements before reaching the operational components and the main output point (Outlet).\n\n**Key Data Flow:**\n1. **Input Power:** The diagram shows the connection of primary AC/DC power sources.\n2. **Protection & Switching:** This power passes through various relays and contactors, suggesting necessary switching points and overload protection.\n3. **Distribution:** Power is distributed to the main control panel (the central junction point) and the final output load (Outlet).\n\n### B. Panel Wiring Diagram (Detailed Internal Control)\n\nThis is the core functional diagram. It maps the signal and power paths from the initial inputs (sensors/transducers) through signal conditioning (Amplifier) to the final control and output mechanisms.\n\n**System Flow Summary:**\n* **Input Stage:** Signals originate from modules like the Transducer and the Vibrating Plate.\n* **Processing Stage:** The raw signals pass through the **Amplifier board** for conditioning and signal strength adjustment.\n* **Control Stage:** The processed signals interact with the main control circuitry (via relays and dedicated wiring terminals).\n* **Output/Power Stage:** The control logic drives the **Transformer** and ultimately the main **Outlet**, completing the circuit.\n\n***\n\n## 🛠️ 2. Component Breakdown and Interconnections\n\nThe system relies on several distinct modules that manage power, signal, and control.\n\n### Table 1: Key Components and Functions\n\n| Component/Module | Function | Role in System |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Transformer** | Steps down/up AC voltage; provides isolation for sensitive electronics. | Converts and regulates power for control circuitry. |\n| **Transducer Board** | Converts physical energy (vibration, force) into electrical signals. | Primary data input source (sensing). |\n| **Amplifier Board** | Boosts the weak electrical signals received from the Transducer. | Signal conditioning; ensuring usable data levels. |\n| **Vibrating Plate** | Acts as an emitter or sensor element; generates measurable vibrations. | Primary data input source (actuation/sensing). |\n| **Relays/Contactors** | Electrically operated switches; control high-power circuits using low-power signals. | Critical for controlling the main output load (Outlet). |\n| **Outlet** | The final output terminal point. | Point of load connection (the equipment being controlled). |\n| **Power Sources** | AC and DC Inputs. | Provides the foundational electrical energy for the entire system. |\n\n### Table 2: Signal and Power Interconnection Points\n\n| Connection Type | Origin/Source | Destination/Module | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Analog Signal (Low Voltage)** | Transducer Board | Amplifier Board | Transmission of measured physical data. |\n| **Processed Signal** | Amplifier Board | Control Relays / Logic Board | Control input (telling the system when to act). |\n| **Power Input (AC/DC)** | External Source | Transformers / Control Panel Bus Bars | General electrical power supply. |\n| **High Voltage/Current** | Control Relays | Outlet Load | Activation of the main system load. |\n| **Isolation Power** | Transformer | Sensitive Control Circuits | Provides clean, regulated power to electronics. |\n\n***\n\n## 💡 3. Detailed Functional Flow Summary\n\nThe operation of the system follows a standard sensing $\\rightarrow$ processing $\\rightarrow$ actuation cycle.\n\n1. **Sensing/Input:** The mechanical element (Vibrating Plate) interacts with the environment, generating a signal picked up by the Transducer.\n2. **Signal Acquisition:** The Transducer Board translates this mechanical interaction into a weak electrical signal.\n3. **Signal Conditioning:** This weak signal is routed to the **Amplifier Board**, which boosts the signal strength and prepares it for the logic controller.\n4. **Control Logic:** The amplified signal activates specific contacts on the control board/relays. This logic determines if the main load needs to be activated.\n5. **Power Switching:** The low-power signal from the Amplifier Board triggers the **Relays**. The relays, which are designed for high current handling, then switch the main power lines.\n6. **Output/Actuation:** The main power (routed through the transformer and protective relays) is delivered to the **Outlet**, completing the circuit and performing the system's required action.\n\n## Summary Conclusion\n\nThe overall schematic represents a robust, modular industrial control system. It is highly organized, separating the low-voltage, high-fidelity signal path (Transducer $\\rightarrow$ Amplifier) from the high-power, switching control path (Power $\\rightarrow$ Relays $\\rightarrow$ Outlet). The inclusion of multiple transformers and clear relay contacts ensures that both the delicate electronics and the heavy-duty loads are managed safely and efficiently.\n\n[Page 38]\nThis document is a highly complex **Electrical Schematic**. It depicts the intricate wiring and component relationships of an electronic power conversion or control system, likely involving motor drives, power inverters, or sophisticated industrial control.\n\nGiven the sheer density and detail of the diagram, a functional description must be broken down into its major subsystems and architectural blocks rather than attempting to summarize the wiring point-by-point.\n\n---\n\n## 📐 Executive Summary and System Overview\n\n**Purpose:** The schematic illustrates a multi-stage electronic circuit designed to manage high power and complex control signals. The overall structure suggests a device that converts and controls power, transitioning from a primary input (potentially AC/DC) through a control logic center to a high-power output stage.\n\n**Key Architectural Principle:** The system follows a classic control loop architecture:\n$$\\text{Power Input} \\rightarrow \\text{Control/Signal Conditioning} \\rightarrow \\text{Power Conversion} \\rightarrow \\text{High Power Output}$$\n\n**Disclaimer:** Because no accompanying labeling, part numbers, or operational notes are provided, this summary describes the *function* and *structure* of the components, not their exact operational parameters (e.g., specific voltage limits or duty cycles).\n\n***\n\n## 🧩 Architectural Breakdown (Functional Blocks)\n\nThe schematic can be logically separated into four primary functional blocks:\n\n### 1. Power Input/Source Stage (Top Left)\nThis section handles the initial power entry into the system.\n*   **Components:** Transformers and various input filters.\n*   **Function:** To receive raw electrical power (e.g., AC lines) and condition it for the subsequent control and power stages (e.g., step-down or isolation).\n*   **Key Activity:** Input voltage regulation and signal isolation.\n\n### 2. Control Logic and Signal Conditioning (Center)\nThis is the "brain" of the circuit, housing the microcontrollers (ICs) and discrete components responsible for decision-making.\n*   **Components:** Multiple Integrated Circuits (ICs), signal conditioning resistors, and filtering capacitors.\n*   **Function:** Reads sensor inputs, processes control commands, generates PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) signals, and drives the switching gates.\n*   **Key Activity:** Translating low-power digital signals into precise timing and gate drive signals for the power stage.\n\n### 3. Power Conversion/Switching Stage (Mid-Right to Top Right)\nThis is where the low-voltage control signals are amplified into high-power switching actions.\n*   **Components:** Power switches (e.g., MOSFETs or IGBTs, inferred by the gate drive structure) and associated driver circuitry.\n*   **Function:** Converts the DC or regulated power from the input stage into the specific high-frequency, high-current power required for the output.\n\n### 4. Output and Sensing Stage (Bottom and Right)\nThis section delivers the processed power to the load and provides feedback data.\n*   **Components:** High-current output lines, transformers, and sensor connections.\n*   **Function:** To deliver the conditioned power (e.g., to a motor) and simultaneously monitor operational parameters (like voltage, current, and temperature) to feed back into the Control Logic.\n\n***\n\n## 📊 Key Data and Component Analysis (Tables)\n\n### Table 1: Major Component Types and Roles\n\n| Component Type | Schematic Location | Primary Function | Key Data/Observation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Integrated Circuits (ICs)** | Center Block | Processing, Control, Signal Generation | Multiple chips indicate complex, multi-functional control boards (e.g., PWM generation, motor control logic). |\n| **Transformers (TX/TR)** | Input and Output Stages | Voltage Isolation, Step-Up/Step-Down | Used to separate high-voltage sections from sensitive logic sections, and to transform current/voltage levels. |\n| **Power Switches** | Right/Upper-Right Quadrant | Switching High Current/Voltage | These components handle the bulk of the power flow and operate under the direction of the control ICs. |\n| **Capacitors (C)** | Throughout | Filtering, Energy Storage | Used extensively to smooth DC voltages (filtering ripple) and provide transient power bursts. |\n| **Resistors (R)** | Throughout | Current Limiting, Voltage Division | Used to scale down voltages for IC inputs or to set bias points. |\n\n### Table 2: Power Flow and Signal Flow\n\n| Flow Segment | Start Point | End Point | Power/Signal Level | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **High Power Flow** | AC Input (Top Left) | Output Terminals (Right) | High Voltage / High Current | Delivery of operational power to the load. |\n| **Control Signal Flow** | Input Sensors (Bottom) | Control ICs (Center) | Low Voltage (e.g., 5V, 12V) | Provides system status and feedback data (e.g., motor speed, current draw). |\n| **Gate Drive Flow** | Control ICs (Center) | Power Switches | Medium Voltage (Gate Voltage) | The *action signal* that turns the high-power switches ON and OFF rapidly. |\n\n***\n\n## 📝 Detailed Component Summary\n\n### 1. Control Loop Emphasis (The ICs)\nThe core signal processing is concentrated in the central area. The arrangement suggests at least two distinct control processors:\n*   **Process Controller:** Responsible for calculating the required motor torque or output power based on user input.\n*   **Driver Controller:** Responsible solely for taking the calculated signal and generating the precise PWM square wave needed to rapidly toggle the high-power switches.\n\n### 2. Power Topology Emphasis (The Output Stage)\nThe power output section shows a sophisticated switching topology, highly characteristic of modern **Inverter** circuits (devices that convert DC power back into controllable AC power, often for motor drive applications).\n\n### 3. Filtering and Safety Emphasis\nThe abundance of large capacitors and dedicated input filtering circuits emphasizes that the system is designed to handle variable load conditions and maintain stable voltage levels under dynamic power switching.\n\n***\n\n## 🔑 Conclusion: Summary of Function\n\nThe provided schematic illustrates a sophisticated, closed-loop **Power Conversion and Motor Drive System (or similar industrial control unit).**\n\n1.  It accepts and conditions an external power source.\n2.  A central control unit processes input commands and sensor feedback.\n3.  This unit generates precise timing signals (PWM).\n4.  The signals drive a high-power switching stage (the inverter).\n5.  The resulting high-power, controlled energy is outputted to a load (e.g., a motor or an industrial machine).\n\n[Page 39]\nThis document is a template for a **Certificate of Compliance**, serving as a formal declaration that a manufactured machine has met necessary quality standards and is approved for delivery.\n\nDue to the nature of the source material (a blank form), the summary focuses on the required fields, technical specifications, and the critical company data provided.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: Certificate of Compliance\n\n### 1. Document Overview\n*   **Title:** Certificate of Compliance\n*   **Purpose:** To formally certify that a specific piece of machinery has passed all required quality inspections and is officially approved for commercial delivery.\n*   **Status:** The declaration "The Machine is Qualified and Approved for Delivery" is the core finding of this document.\n\n### 2. Key Product Specifications (Technical Data)\n\nThis section details the model and physical dimensions of the machine covered by the certificate.\n\n| Specification Field | Detail |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Machine Model** | DK-908 |\n| **Work Travel** | 400 X 300 mm |\n| **Serial Number** | *(Field blank)* |\n\n### 3. Compliance and Approval Chain\n\nThis section outlines the critical sign-off process, confirming that multiple departments have verified the machine's readiness.\n\n| Approval Department | Required Action | Status (Based on Template) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Production Department** | Signature/Validation | *(Blank)* |\n| **Quality Inspection Department** | Signature/Validation | *(Blank)* |\n| **Delivery Status** | Formal Declaration | **Qualified and Approved for Delivery.** |\n\n### 4. Manufacturing and Tracking Details\n\nThese fields capture the timeline and identification used for tracking the machine.\n\n| Field | Purpose | Status (Based on Template) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Date of Manufacture** | The date the machine was completed. | *(Blank)* |\n\n### 5. Company and Contact Information\n\nThis section provides the full legal and logistical details of the issuing company.\n\n#### Company Profile Table\n| Detail | Information |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Company Name** | LUOYANG XINCHENG PRECISION MACHINERY CO.,LTD |\n| **Address** | NO.256 TANGGONG ROAD, LUOYANG, HENAN, CHINA 471000 |\n| **Telephone (Tel)** | 0379-63512276 |\n| **Fax** | 0379-69960770 |\n\n---\n***Note on Diagrams:*** *The original document is entirely textual and contains no diagrams or pictorial representations.*\n\n[Page 40]\nThis page provides a detailed **Spare Parts Packing List** (Section 12), serving as an inventory checklist for the accessories and maintenance items included with a specific machine tool.\n\nSince no diagrams are provided, the summary focuses on the structured data contained within the table and the key specifications of each included component.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 Detailed Summary: Spare Parts Packing List\n\nThe document itemizes fourteen (14) specific components, ranging from essential tools and consumables to structural accessories, ensuring that the user has all necessary parts for setup, operation, and maintenance of the equipment.\n\n### 🛠️ Inventory Table (Itemized List)\n\nThe core data is presented below in a detailed tabular format:\n\n| Item No. | Item Name | Model No. / Specification | Quantity | Unit | Category |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **1** | Allen Wrench | M3, M4, M5, M6, M8 | 1 | each | Tools |\n| **2** | Double-Head Screwdriver | N/A | 1 | pc | Tools |\n| **3** | Special Scissors For Electrodes | N/A | 1 | pc | Tools |\n| **4** | Guider | $\\Phi$0.5 | 1 | pc | Parts/Consumables |\n| **5** | Guider | $\\Phi$1.0 | 1 | pc | Parts/Consumables |\n| **6** | Brass Tube | $\\Phi$0.5 | 1 | pc |